// What CQC found, quoted from each report: QUOTES[council][statement].q = [page, sentence].
const QUOTES = {"Bradford":[{"q":[[12,"The local authority had waiting lists in place for Care Act assessments and assessments were not always completed in a timely manner."],[17,"An advisor from the IAH triaged initial contacts with the local authority, and if people were not eligible for Care Act assessment, or if they had needs which could be supported by VCFSE organisations – or both - they were signposted to them."],[19,"The local authority had a target of 4 weeks for the completion of financial assessments, however, due to the backlog they were prioritising cases based on risk."]],"m":"The local authority had waiting lists in place for Care Act assessments and assessments were not always completed in a timely manner. The local authority had recognised that people had not always received an annual care review. While other people told us they lacked support from social workers and had no support following their carers assessment. Leaders told us that within its own data, carers assessments had not always been recorded as a specific carers assessment and were often recorded as a "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Buckinghamshire":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[51,"The local authority, in partnership with the ICB, used investment to support the safe management of care and support during hospital discharge."],[52,"Staff told us if a hospital discharge failed, resulting in readmission or a deterioration at home, the team manager was notified."],[53,"The local authority had also introduced dedicated transitions social workers within the mental health team. Despite this, staff, people and partners told us most young people’s transition process did not begin until the age of 18 and often, they found the transitions process was fragmented and took too long."]],"m":"Staff told us if a hospital discharge failed, resulting in readmission or a deterioration at home, the team manager was notified. The local authority had also introduced dedicated transitions social workers within the mental health team. Despite this, staff, people and partners told us most young people’s transition process did not begin until the age of 18 and often, they found the transitions process was fragmented and took too long."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Blackburn with Darwen":[{"q":[[11,"In line with this review some staff did not always use person-centred language terminology, describing people as ‘service users’."],[12,"Purpose-built data dashboards provided up-to-date monitoring of timeliness of assessments, including oversight of when assessments were overdue of the target timescale of 28 days."],[13,"The local authority’s data from 27 March 2025 showed 7.07% of annual reviews as overdue."]],"m":"In line with this review some staff did not always use person-centred language terminology, describing people as ‘service users’. Purpose-built data dashboards provided up-to-date monitoring of timeliness of assessments, including oversight of when assessments were overdue of the target timescale of 28 days. The local authority’s data from 27 March 2025 showed 7.07% of annual reviews as overdue. Carers also reported limited opportunities for breaks from their caring roles, Feedback highlighted i"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[42,"Partners, staff and people highlighted a lack of in-borough housing with care options including supported living. This included for people with learning disabilities and people with mental health needs."],[43,"The local authority was seeking to address gaps in service provision but due to the size of the borough and a more recent focus on strategic commissioning, these remained areas for development. This had resulted in increased numbers of people requiring services outside of the borough."],[43,"Homecare was a particular strength within the borough, with a tiered approach of 8 tier 1 providers, with further tier 2 providers which were used when tier 1 providers did not have capacity."]],"m":"While intentions were clear, a staff team also told us as roles were new, the impact of the team was still embedding. As a result, there was a lack of delivery details of the commissioning service at this stage. An ageing population and lack of options for people with mental health needs were specific challenges for the local authority. The local authority recognised that housing options for people with mental health needs were underdeveloped locally, due in part to a lack of strategic commissio"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[63,"The local authority Safeguarding Adults Board (SAB) had transitioned from wider Pan-Lancashire arrangements to a place-based focus."],[66,"However, some staff we spoke with were not always confident in relaying broad learning points from SARs. Despite this, some staff told us SAR learning was part of team meetings and told us the transitions policy was influenced following the SAR."],[69,"As of 27 March 2025, there were 261 DoLS applications waiting to be approved, with 182 of these assessments not yet started."]],"m":"However, some staff we spoke with were not always confident in relaying broad learning points from SARs. However, some staff we spoke with were not always confident in relaying broad learning points from SARs. Despite this, some staff told us SAR learning was part of team meetings and told us the transitions policy was influenced following the SAR. As of 27 March 2025, there were 261 DoLS applications waiting to be approved, with 182 of these assessments not yet started."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Bolton":[{"q":[[18,"However, the range of services available remained unclear. They said assessments varied in quality and timeliness, and while some carers received support through the carers organisation, access to services was not consistent."],[18,"A refresh of the Carers Strategy was underway, focusing on promoting carers’ roles in local communities, identifying unpaid carers who were not known to services, improving information through GP practices, and working with a wide range of organisations to support working carers."],[21,"However, in a record, some eligibility decisions were inconsistent. For example, going to the toilet was assessed as an eligible need, but the assessment stated the person could carry out this task independently."]],"m":"The local authority had effective systems for assessment and care planning, but delays in reviews and inconsistent eligibility recording highlighted areas for improvement. Records included what worked well and what did not for each person. Unpaid carers said they were included in assessments. They were invited to meetings and, when unable to attend, received updates and copies of the assessment, which they said reflected the person’s needs. Staff told us they were embedding strengths-based asses"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[39,"One partner told us coproduction in areas like the Carers Strategy lacked diversity and inclusivity. They said tailored engagement approaches such as facilitated conversations and accessible formats were necessary to strengthen equity and broaden representation in service planning."],[40,"However, some staff said there were sometimes delays in accessing British Sign Language (BSL) interpreters and Somali interpreters. They said they escalated these concerns to senior leaders to maintain service quality."],[41,"One partner said they were not aware of any seldom-heard communities that the local authority struggled to reach, due to effective community networks and well-established groups."]],"m":"One partner told us coproduction in areas like the Carers Strategy lacked diversity and inclusivity. One partner told us coproduction in areas like the Carers Strategy lacked diversity and inclusivity. They said tailored engagement approaches such as facilitated conversations and accessible formats were necessary to strengthen equity and broaden representation in service planning. There was evidence of strategic commitment to equity, coproduction and inclusion, but limited assurance that this wa"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[70,"One partner told us that some unpaid carers had previously raised safeguarding concerns, including misinformation about funding, financial vulnerability and inconsistent support during hospital discharge."],[71,"The local authority told us that while forced marriage had been raised in professional discussions, this did not indicate a high level of prevalence locally. They said the topic had been raised to reflect professional awareness rather than frequency of occurrence."],[73,"Staff said the safeguarding team was not consistently meeting the 2-day target for initial reviews due to complexity and volume. In response, the local authority advertised 2 permanent posts and added a member of staff from the learning disability team to strengthen capacity and reduce risk to people waiting."]],"m":"One partner told us that some unpaid carers had previously raised safeguarding concerns, including misinformation about funding, financial vulnerability and inconsistent support during hospital discharge. The local authority told us that while forced marriage had been raised in professional discussions, this did not indicate a high level of prevalence locally. The local authority told us that while forced marriage had been raised in professional discussions, this did not indicate a high level of"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Birmingham":[{"q":[[11,"However, pathways and processes did not always ensure people’s support was planned and co-ordinated across different services and partners."],[12,"The local authority was acting to manage and reduce waiting times for Care Act 2014 assessments. This included actions to reduce risks to people's wellbeing, whilst they waited for an assessment."],[17,"However, some service providers reported delays in the allocation of social workers and the processing of reassessment outcomes for people who fell below financial thresholds after being assessed as needing to self-fund their support previously."]],"m":"However, pathways and processes did not always ensure people’s support was planned and co-ordinated across different services and partners. Staff and leaders told us of a lack of understanding of the remit of adult social care leading to high levels of inappropriate referrals adding pressure to referral and pathway systems. Combined with staffing capacity issues, some people experienced delays in getting help. Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely or up to date. Senior"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[46,"Data showed there were no delays in hospital discharge due to lack of service capacity."],[47,"Equipment could not be issued out-of-hours without a service order number, though community nurses and providers could facilitate this."],[51,"There were 77 providers suspended due to contract compliance issues, with 92% of those suspensions due to delays in care providers notifying the local authority of their updated insurance. These suspensions were removed as soon as evidence was provided and validated."]],"m":"Unpaid carers told us there had been several meetings conducted which included the local authority, partners, and people with lived experience of carers needs looking at gaps or areas where there were limited opportunities or services. Data showed there were no delays in hospital discharge due to lack of service capacity. Partners told us despite progress some challenges remained around balancing limited capacity between hospital discharge priorities and preventative work. There were gaps in spe"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[79,"The number of safeguarding concerns progressing to an S42 enquiry were proportionately low and concerns were not always addressed in a timely way."],[74,"Partners told us safeguarding communication was an area for development, with incomplete safeguarding referrals sometimes returned because of missing personal details such as a person’s date of birth. Where partners did not hold this information, this led to delays and concerns some risks might go unaddressed."],[80,"Whilst this meant the local authority were not following the guidance set out in their policies on S42 enquiry thresholds, this was still in line with their duties under the Care Act 2014."]],"m":"Systems, processes, practices were not always effective in making sure appropriate safeguarding concerns were reported and people were protected from abuse and neglect. Where partners did not hold this information, this led to delays and concerns some risks might go unaddressed. The number of safeguarding concerns progressing to an S42 enquiry were proportionately low and concerns were not always addressed in a timely way. Thresholds followed a 3-stage test which meant any concern which identifi"},{"q":[[84,"There were governance, management and accountability arrangements at all levels within the local authority. These did not always provide visibility and assurance on the delivery of Care Act 2014 duties, quality and sustainability, and risks to delivery of people’s care, and supported people’s experiences and outcomes."],[84,"The delays in planned annual reviews of people needs, combined with safeguarding process which required further development to ensure robust and timely interventions and learning, meant there was an increased risk of potential harm to people using services due to delayed opportunities to provide early interventions."],[86,"Senior leaders told us the local authority’s main governance principle was the recognition prevention and early intervention could not be delivered by adult social care alone."]],"m":"There were governance, management and accountability arrangements at all levels within the local authority. These did not always provide visibility and assurance on the delivery of Care Act 2014 duties, quality and sustainability, and risks to delivery of people’s care, and supported people’s experiences and outcomes. The delays in planned annual reviews of people needs, combined with safeguarding process which required further development to ensure robust and timely interventions and learning, "},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Bracknell Forest":[{"q":[[13,"The local authority was unable to provide waiting times prior to the implementation of the new model in October 2023. They told us since then, people who contacted the Hub did not have to wait to start assessments if their needs were simple or short term."],[14,"When this assessment was not done in time to determine longer term needs, it impacted on the provider of interim care services and could cause funding problems."],[12,"Some people told us they had waited a long time for assessments and had not had reviews. For example, one person told us they had requested an assessment for a family member due to a deterioration in mobility in February 2024 and had not received a response."]],"m":"Some people reported that they did not often speak to the same person and had to ‘retell their story' each time they made contact. Some people told us they had waited a long time for assessments and had not had reviews. In contrast, one person said they did not feel listened to in the assessment process and had disputed the information documented. The person and their family did not understand how or why this decision was reached. The local authority was unable to provide waiting times prior to "},{"q":[[25,"The local authority used free to access public meetings such as the Happiness Hub, as well as the Hub to identify people with needs for care and support that were not being met."],[26,"Data for January to March 2024 showed people going home with a package of care on average waited 2.62 days as compared to 4.14 days on average across the Frimley Integrated Care System (ICS) as a whole."],[26,"Over the same period, people being discharged with an interim package or short-term care home placement under discharge to assess, where care was provided pending a full assessment of needs, waited on average of 2.2 days as compared to 8.27 days across the ICS."]],"m":"Many carers were offered one off payments for recreation or respite however, this was not always suitable or achievable. We heard from carers who felt unable to leave the person they cared for, or that the person they cared for would just contact them and become distressed meaning these options were not beneficial. Many carers we spoke with told us there was a lack of respite/short break provision and support available and did not feel they had access to short breaks, especially in an emergency."},{"q":[[32,"A senior analyst was tasked with interpreting data sourced from Adult Social Care, NHS and the census, to identify inequalities so work could take place to look at addressing gaps in the system and improving outcomes for people."],[33,"Feedback was used to influence plans and look at impact and outcomes. These were open opportunities, but might be inaccessible if people were working, or did not know about them, due to language or digital barriers."],[34,"Some areas for improvement which required addressing included inconsistent access to dementia services within Bracknell Forest, increase in reviews/contact, improved joint working between organisations."]],"m":"A senior analyst was tasked with interpreting data sourced from Adult Social Care, NHS and the census, to identify inequalities so work could take place to look at addressing gaps in the system and improving outcomes for people. Feedback was used to influence plans and look at impact and outcomes. These were open opportunities, but might be inaccessible if people were working, or did not know about them, due to language or digital barriers. Some areas for improvement which required addressing in"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[58,"An audit of 5 transitional cases relating to young people in a variety of circumstances, including youth justice and being a care leaver with mental health needs, was undertaken in February 2023 by the Bracknell Forest Safeguarding Board (BFSB). This found inconsistent performance against 6 principles which ensured effective, person-centred planning for a safe and smooth transition to adulthood."],[58,"Staff did not consistently use processes and guidance such as Mental Capacity Act."],[59,"One person described that the local authority entered a dispute over funding with the host local authority after a person had been placed in a home out of borough, which resulted in a long wait for the person to receive financial support for the care placement when they were no longer self-funding."]],"m":"An audit of 5 transitional cases relating to young people in a variety of circumstances, including youth justice and being a care leaver with mental health needs, was undertaken in February 2023 by the Bracknell Forest Safeguarding Board (BFSB). This found inconsistent performance against 6 principles which ensured effective, person-centred planning for a safe and smooth transition to adulthood. Staff did not consistently use processes and guidance such as Mental Capacity Act. One person describ"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Bristol":[{"q":[[10,"Some areas which were identified could be improved were in relation to people not always having a named social worker and feeling some staff lacked knowledge in certain areas such as in relation to complex conditions or autism."],[11,"Feedback was there could also be significant delays in allocating work into the receiving teams."],[12,"Some partners explained once a person had a named worker the system worked well, referrals to their services were not always appropriate and sometimes they made referrals to the local authority but had to chase up or resubmit these again."]],"m":"Some areas which were identified could be improved were in relation to people not always having a named social worker and feeling some staff lacked knowledge in certain areas such as in relation to complex conditions or autism. Feedback was there could also be significant delays in allocating work into the receiving teams. Some partners explained once a person had a named worker the system worked well, referrals to their services were not always appropriate and sometimes they made referrals to t"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[35,"The local authority acknowledged recording around equality and diversity needed to improve. They shared they did not yet know enough about all the people who attempted to access their services."],[35,"In June 2024, local authority data showed 7.7% of people receiving care and support in Bristol did not have their ethnicity recorded on the client record system. These gaps in recording had also been consistently noted in the local authority quality assurance audits."],[35,"Improvements could be seen in the use of a new software data tool which allowed the data team to identify gaps in data and give feedback to relevant teams if improvements in recording were needed."]],"m":"The local authority acknowledged recording around equality and diversity needed to improve. They shared they did not yet know enough about all the people who attempted to access their services. In June 2024, local authority data showed 7.7% of people receiving care and support in Bristol did not have their ethnicity recorded on the client record system. In June 2024, local authority data showed 7.7% of people receiving care and support in Bristol did not have their ethnicity recorded on the clie"},{"q":[[44,"From this, efforts had been made to improve areas such as patient discharge pathways from hospital. It was noted that despite increased demand for community reablement and rehabilitation, capacity gaps persisted, leading to longer wait times."],[44,"Staff gave one example of where they wanted to support a person who was HIV positive, and they faced difficulty in finding services to support them due to a lack of understanding around HIV."],[52,"Around 75% of the providers on the Single Framework were new providers, not yet known to them."]],"m":"From this, efforts had been made to improve areas such as patient discharge pathways from hospital. It was noted that despite increased demand for community reablement and rehabilitation, capacity gaps persisted, leading to longer wait times. Staff gave one example of where they wanted to support a person who was HIV positive, and they faced difficulty in finding services to support them due to a lack of understanding around HIV. One care provider told us a strategic partner group used to be hel"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[76,"Following a Safeguarding Adults Review, practice was updated to ensure other local authorities were notified when placements were made outside of Bristol."],[77,"However, we did not find that staff understanding of the Mental Capacity Act 2005 or safeguarding was noted as an area of concern during our assessment."],[74,"Consistent feedback from a number of care providers was that communication could be better in relation to safeguarding as they often had to chase up outcomes. It could be difficult to get hold of certain staff at times and other staff did not seem to be able to update in their absence, which partners felt was a risk."]],"m":"Some staff told us they did not feel they always had the resources or time to do the work they would like to do. Decisions taken by partners agencies had an impact on their work, for agencies not always responding to mental health calls which created a knock-on effect. Consistent feedback from a number of care providers was that communication could be better in relation to safeguarding as they often had to chase up outcomes. It could be difficult to get hold of certain staff at times and other s"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Bury":[{"q":[[13,"The framework ensured people received the necessary support while they waited to maintain their health, well-being and independence, through regular well-being checks, access to clear information, emergency support, and community engagement."],[14,"Due to the multiple roles in the services that performed this function, the local authority were unable to give the data on the waiting times for occupational assessment for equipment."],[16,"However, in contrast we were told carers were not always offered a carers assessment and given information on how to contact the Carers Hub."]],"m":"Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely. The local authority told us, the high maximum waiting time was due to a person who had received active caseload management and updates to their support plan, however a review document had not been completed on the system. The local authority told us, the high maximum waiting time was due to a person who had received active caseload management and updates to their support plan, however a review document had not been completed on th"},{"q":[[22,"Preventative support to people over 50 and who were not in receipt of adult social care services, were offered services such as blue badge applications to maintain independence and mobility."],[26,"The local authority was unable to provide data to show the overall waiting times for an assessment for equipment, due to the Trusted Assessor model for equipment assessment, which was done in real-time as part of other adult social care pathways. For example, alongside the completion of My Life, My Way Care Act assessments, where the allocated worker made recommendations and ordered appropriate equipment."],[28,"Some people reported they had been given limited information and not made aware of services available in their area, although they were offered choices for support options."]],"m":"Preventative support to people over 50 and who were not in receipt of adult social care services, were offered services such as blue badge applications to maintain independence and mobility. Overall feedback from people was positive, with only isolated issues reported around delays in equipment provision. The local authority was unable to provide data to show the overall waiting times for an assessment for equipment, due to the Trusted Assessor model for equipment assessment, which was done in r"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[44,"We were told that access to emergency respite could be challenging as the local authority did not have a formal process in place for providers who have been commissioned to deliver services out of hours."],[44,"Staff had to use other measures such as calling emergency services if safety was a concern or consider if family members could bridge the gap in services."],[45,"In the 3 months which preceded information submitted by the local authority in September 2025, it evidenced people in Bury waited an average of 3 days for their care at home package to start from the date it was brokered by the local authority."]],"m":"There was not always sufficient capacity for unpaid carers to have access to replacement care for the person they cared for, in both planned and unplanned situations. We were told that access to emergency respite could be challenging as the local authority did not have a formal process in place for providers who have been commissioned to deliver services out of hours. Staff had to use other measures such as calling emergency services if safety was a concern or consider if family members could br"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[62,"There were concerns about reduced involvement from other professional agencies, a lack of mental health liaison officers, and difficulties accessing Section 12 doctors at night, leading to delays in Mental Health Act assessments and hospital admissions."],[63,"We heard although health services helped, their capacity remained inconsistent."],[64,"The review highlighted several risks, one of which was a disconnect in multi-agency working between acute and community teams, legal literacy was applied inconsistently and transfers of care lacked coordination between acute and community services."]],"m":"Risks were identified and monitored although this was not always effective. There were concerns about reduced involvement from other professional agencies, a lack of mental health liaison officers, and difficulties accessing Section 12 doctors at night, leading to delays in Mental Health Act assessments and hospital admissions. We heard although health services helped, their capacity remained inconsistent. Staff told us workers prioritised cases based on risk and ensured a risk plan was in place"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Barnsley":[{"q":[[11,"Additionally, a small number of staff in focus groups held in early 2025 suggested that pathways to assessments could be unclear at times, and some reported using discretion when accepting referrals beyond their team’s eligibility criteria."],[14,"In the year prior to July 2025, the median time for overdue reviews was 2 days."],[20,"The local authority had frameworks and criteria for assessing eligibility for adult social care, however, feedback from staff, people and partners indicated they were not consistently applied in a number of service areas."]],"m":"Additionally, a small number of staff in focus groups held in early 2025 suggested that pathways to assessments could be unclear at times, and some reported using discretion when accepting referrals beyond their team’s eligibility criteria. In the year prior to July 2025, the median time for overdue reviews was 2 days. Local authority data indicated 44% of people’s reviews were 1 to 3 months overdue, 39% were 3 to 6 months overdue, and 17% were over 6 months overdue. Additionally, some people ha"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[37,"There was an aim to use this intelligence to guide service and resource planning, and work was ongoing to identify existing gaps in the local authority’s understanding of the population. For example, leaders told us improvements were needed around capturing information about younger people approaching adulthood and transitioning to adult services to meet their specific needs."],[38,"However, some partners told us access to support persisted for people who had been diagnosed with neurodegenerative conditions such as Alzheimer’s and dementia. They said people with dementia were not consistently well supported to complete forms, which were sometimes online, or understand the local authority’s charging policy, which were barriers to accessing support."],[41,"Where face to face interpreters were not immediately available (for example, outside normal office hours) it used virtual interpreting services."]],"m":"There was an aim to use this intelligence to guide service and resource planning, and work was ongoing to identify existing gaps in the local authority’s understanding of the population. However, some partners told us access to support persisted for people who had been diagnosed with neurodegenerative conditions such as Alzheimer’s and dementia. They said people with dementia were not consistently well supported to complete forms, which were sometimes online, or understand the local authority’s "},{"q":[[48,"There was adequate homecare, residential and nursing care provision to meet demand in Barnsley, however, people waited longer for supported living services due to a lack of appropriate accommodation options for people requiring this care and support."],[49,"Leaders were working with brokerage and frontline teams to understand reasons people waited for supported living services, which included difficulty in finding appropriate accommodation for people with care needs."],[53,"However, some VCS partners we spoke with did not think the existing arrangements supported sustainability within the sector. For example, some told us they and the communities they supported were seldom heard as staff rarely visited them."]],"m":"Additionally, this data was also not yet being used to identify whether certain demographics were underrepresented at the front door. Some partners said they had not been involved or engaged in discussions by the local authority about how to adapt their support offers as the population became more culturally diverse. This suggested a lack of collaborative action by the local authority in response to demographic changes. Staff and leaders told us there were gaps in provision and support for young"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[64,"The local authority understood where there were risks to people’s wellbeing across their care journeys. These included unplanned interruptions in service provision caused by provider failure, people becoming ‘lost’ between services due to gaps in access or referral criteria, and timely planning for transition between services, agencies or across local authority boundaries."],[69,"In line with the local authority’s integrated Home First approach, work was ongoing to understand existing delays in procuring adaptations and/or appropriate properties that could be adapted for people with Care Act needs."],[70,"To accommodate for this trend, a crisis support service could be contacted by unpaid carers for support at short notice, for example, if they could not fulfil their caring duties for a period of time. This solution, while highly responsive and tailored to the tendencies of the unpaid carer population, was reactive and leaders were working towards more proactive ways of supporting unpaid carers' wellbeing."]],"m":"The local authority understood where there were risks to people’s wellbeing across their care journeys. These included unplanned interruptions in service provision caused by provider failure, people becoming ‘lost’ between services due to gaps in access or referral criteria, and timely planning for transition between services, agencies or across local authority boundaries. Similarly, staff who supported young people approaching adulthood or adult services did not reject referrals on the basis of"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[80,"To address these knowledge gaps in the social workforce, the PSW implemented Social Work Online Team (SWOT) training toolkits to support reflective practice and team reflection."],[83,"Despite high levels of positive staff feedback from most staff, some staff groups expressed a lack of parity compared with other local authority teams. For example, some staff said they were not included in key communications about changes implemented in the directorate or local authority, which led them to feel undervalued."],[83,"Where there were risks or shortfalls to delivery of Care Act duties, for example, provider quality issues, risk mitigations were in place, and these were effective in keeping people safe. For example, staff and leaders took a consistent approach to addressing quality and/or safeguarding issues identified in care provided by commissioned agencies, including supporting these partners to improve."]],"m":"To address these knowledge gaps in the social workforce, the PSW implemented Social Work Online Team (SWOT) training toolkits to support reflective practice and team reflection. Despite high levels of positive staff feedback from most staff, some staff groups expressed a lack of parity compared with other local authority teams. At the time of the CQC assessment, staff told us a team’s structure had been under review since 2022 and said they lacked clarity around the outcome of the review or plan"},{"q":[[87,"The Principal Social Worker (PSW) oversaw the Professional Practice Development team, who supported staff to find courses, training and other development opportunities to progress their knowledge and help them support people in the ways they wanted."],[89,"The local authority used multiple ways to obtain feedback from people, staff and partners about their experiences of care and support and delivery of Care Act duties. This informed strategy, improvement activity and decision making."],[90,"Leaders said the low number of complaints and the lack of a formalised approach to extracting learning from informally resolved issues made it difficult to demonstrate how learning from issues/ complaints was used to improve practice."]],"m":"Leaders said the low number of complaints and the lack of a formalised approach to extracting learning from informally resolved issues made it difficult to demonstrate how learning from issues/ complaints was used to improve practice."}],"Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole":[{"q":[[8,"People could access the local authority’s care and support services through multiple channels, but people sometimes experienced delays to accessing an assessment."],[9,"At the time of the assessment, the three conversations strengths-based approach was embedded across most community-based teams, though not consistently in all areas."],[16,"Factsheets such as “Paying the full cost of your care” and “When your money is running out” signposted people to further guidance but did not advise on their right to a Care Act assessment or explain deferred payment processes and timescales. This reduced transparency for people planning care and support."]],"m":"People could access the local authority’s care and support services through multiple channels, but people sometimes experienced delays to accessing an assessment. At the time of the assessment, the three conversations strengths-based approach was embedded across most community-based teams, though not consistently in all areas. People experienced clearer planning and felt involved, but variability in review timeliness and funding decisions meant consistency was not always achieved. People’s exper"},{"q":[[21,"Partners told us that whilst there was a prevention strategy in place, they felt prevention work was not always properly resourced and plans were implemented in a crisis, such as around winter."],[22,"The local authority identified gaps in early support and responded by developing a short term community reablement offer. This included piloting time limited support prior to long term care, and benchmarking its approach with other local authorities."],[27,"People could not always easily access information and advice on their rights under the Care Act and ways to meet their care and support needs. This included unpaid carers and people who fund or arrange their own care and support."]],"m":"Partners told us that whilst there was a prevention strategy in place, they felt prevention work was not always properly resourced and plans were implemented in a crisis, such as around winter. The local authority identified gaps in early support and responded by developing a short term community reablement offer. The local authority identified gaps in early support and responded by developing a short term community reablement offer. This included piloting time limited support prior to long term"},{"q":[[30,"Mapping life expectancy against deprivation scores showed men in the most deprived areas died on average 6.3 years earlier than those in the least deprived areas, and women 5.4 years earlier. Despite this insight, there was limited evidence of clear plans to meet the cultural and accessibility needs of older people and those with protected characteristics."],[33,"Frontline teams also struggled to source BSL interpreters locally, commissioning distant providers that caused delays and reduced equitable access. These issues meant people with sensory needs sometimes waited longer for support, increasing stress and limiting choice."],[33,"Healthwatch feedback and partner meetings highlighted that online-only systems excluded blind and partially sighted people and older carers, leaving them unable to obtain support without external intervention. For example, people could not complete online appointment forms due to lack of technology, credit, or confidence in using online systems."]],"m":"The local authority understood its population profile and demographics but had gaps in planning for diverse needs. Mapping life expectancy against deprivation scores showed men in the most deprived areas died on average 6.3 years earlier than those in the least deprived areas, and women 5.4 years earlier. Despite this insight, there was limited evidence of clear plans to meet the cultural and accessibility needs of older people and those with protected characteristics. Local authority audits als"},{"q":[[36,"However, providers also reported gaps in services, including respite for unpaid carers, dementia support, and provision for people with complex needs. This meant some people and carers had limited options and could not always access support locally or in the right type of setting, which increased stress and risk of placement far from home."],[37,"The local authority’s Market Sustainability Plan outlined how this was an ongoing challenge because it reduced capacity in care home and homecare provision, which had led to more ‘hand backs’ from providers and increased pressure on hospitals. This meant people sometimes experienced delays in receiving care and had to accept interim residential placements."],[38,"The programme was being implemented at the time of assessment, so was not yet fully embedded."]],"m":"However, providers also reported gaps in services, including respite for unpaid carers, dementia support, and provision for people with complex needs. However, providers also reported gaps in services, including respite for unpaid carers, dementia support, and provision for people with complex needs. This meant some people and carers had limited options and could not always access support locally or in the right type of setting, which increased stress and risk of placement far from home. The loc"},{"q":[[55,"Local VCSE representatives told us engagement often happened only at “need-must” points or once situations had escalated into crisis. For instance, voluntary groups reported being contacted too late for winter planning, often in November, which meant funding intended for prevention was used for crisis response."],[53,"The local authority did not consistently monitor and evaluate the impact of its partnership working on the costs of social care and the outcomes for people, although there were examples of improvement and positive practice. This informed ongoing development and continuous improvement."],[53,"There were examples where collaboration improved outcomes for people but monitoring and evaluation were not consistently evident. For example, provider representatives said restructures within the local authority led to the loss of key relationships, creating shortterm gaps in communication and making joint work harder until new links were established."]],"m":"Partnership working showed clear arrangements for governance, accountability, monitoring, and information sharing, but these were not always consistent. However, some providers said co-production with local care sector partners was still at an early stage and they did not always see a clear link between their input and changes in commissioning. Tthe Future Care programme had also trialled a Transfer of Care hub in hospital wards to improve discharge planning Data provided by the local authority "},{"q":[[58,"Information sharing protocols supported safety, but delays and unclear accountability sometimes undermined outcomes. For example, staff told us there was sometimes confusion between the role of reablement which led to regular inappropriate referrals which highlighted a lack of clarity about the most appropriate pathways for people."],[59,"Arrangements for safe transitions from children’s to adult services were inconsistent and did not always ensure continuity for people."],[61,"A Safeguarding Adult Review highlighted a lack of proactive move-on planning and joinedup working when a person was admitted to a hostel, which increased risks."]],"m":"However, staff told us there was sometimes a lack of clarity about access to reablement between partners and partners said people sometimes faced delays accessing step-down services when discharged from hospital which meant people could encounter gaps. Information sharing protocols supported safety, but delays and unclear accountability sometimes undermined outcomes. However, delays in safeguarding referrals and deprivation of liberty safeguard (DoLS) applications showed that information sharing"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[81,"Concerns were raised that the local authority sometimes arrived with pre-formed plans rather than genuine coproduction, particularly when savings were part of the agenda. This meant some people felt their voices were not fully heard."],[81,"Comments from young people and families in that strategy highlighted the need for better partnership between children’s and adult services and more conversations about independence and employment. This suggested progress but also gaps in fully involving people in shaping their future."],[83,"However, evidence suggested feedback did not always influence decisions. For instance, a partner reported that when the local authority changed plans to close half of the day centres, co-production was not used appropriately."]],"m":"Concerns were raised that the local authority sometimes arrived with pre-formed plans rather than genuine coproduction, particularly when savings were part of the agenda. This meant some people felt their voices were not fully heard. Comments from young people and families in that strategy highlighted the need for better partnership between children’s and adult services and more conversations about independence and employment. This suggested progress but also gaps in fully involving people in sh"}],"Bath and North East Somerset":[{"q":[[11,"People may not always be able to easily access the local authority’s care and support services."],[11,"Data provided evidenced a lack of recording regarding people’s requests for Care Act assessments and any signposting of services for people with non-eligible needs."],[11,"There were no data records regarding how many people had contacted the first response team and had been given advice and information or signposted to a voluntary and community sector organisation. This meant the local authority could not monitor whether people had accessed advice, care and support before and whether signposting to community and voluntary organisations was meeting their needs."]],"m":"People may not always be able to easily access the local authority’s care and support services. However, lack of recording and staffing shortages could mean people were unable to access the local authority in a timely manner. Data provided evidenced a lack of recording regarding people’s requests for Care Act assessments and any signposting of services for people with non-eligible needs. There were no data records regarding how many people had contacted the first response team and had been given"},{"q":[[22,"However, there was a lack of data or recording from the first response team to evidence whether this was conducted effectively and how this impacted the outcomes for people."],[23,"The current interim care process was that people using this service would be placed where a care home had a vacancy, and the appropriate staff would visit the person in the care home for example, occupational therapist, physiotherapist and social worker. This meant people could be placed in care homes that did not fully meet their needs or promote their independence."],[25,"The local authority provided information and advice to people in accessible formats but recognised the need to improve the accessibility of resources for example, for people for whom English was not their first language."]],"m":"However, support groups were not always accessible due to the unpaid carers caring responsibilities, and some felt support was not always flexible to meet their needs. Unpaid carers told us they found it difficult to access emergency respite when needed and found it difficult at times to contact their named worker for advice and support to prevent carer breakdown. However, there was a lack of data or recording from the first response team to evidence whether this was conducted effectively and ho"},{"q":[[27,"Whilst there was an understanding of the needs of most of the local communities there were still some shortfalls in working with and identifying seldom heard voices."],[27,"There were gaps in the knowledge of some staff and leaders with regards to Equality Diversity and Inclusion."],[28,"Data collected by the local authority did not currently record people’s ethnicity, race, religion as a prerequisite when contacting the first response team, this meant the local authority had missed an opportunity to further identify seldom heard and hard to reach people and may impact on the local authorities understanding of the needs of the community when commissioning new services."]],"m":"Whilst there was an understanding of the needs of most of the local communities there were still some shortfalls in working with and identifying seldom heard voices. There were gaps in the knowledge of some staff and leaders with regards to Equality Diversity and Inclusion. Data collected by the local authority did not currently record people’s ethnicity, race, religion as a prerequisite when contacting the first response team, this meant the local authority had missed an opportunity to further "},{"q":[[31,"Staff identified there was a gap in specialist learning disability provision, whilst they tried to always keep people in the area this was more difficult for more specialist services."],[36,"The local authority identified following conversations with the Parent Carer Forum that there was a lack of provision for young adults following transitions to adult social care in relation to their housing needs."],[37,"Providers identified some services not yet rated by CQC and therefore the quality of services was not always clear."]],"m":"Staff identified there was a gap in specialist learning disability provision, whilst they tried to always keep people in the area this was more difficult for more specialist services. Providers told us that the cost of care was also a key factor when choosing a provider. This meant people may not always receive care and support best suited to their individual needs. Unpaid carers told us there was not always appropriate provision in place regarding emergency care, the local authority had identif"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[47,"The local authority had a clear process for transitions, however, current staffing levels meant there could be delays in supporting people transitioning from children to adult services."],[48,"Commissioning processes ensured access to services in a timely manner, however, access to specialist services was more limited."],[48,"The local authority had identified there was a lack of local provision for young people moving into adult services."]],"m":"The local authority understood its role in keeping people safe, however, systems were not fully embedded to allow full oversight and monitoring of risk. Leaders told us the introduction of a newer version of their Power BI Data monitoring system would help improve knowledge and oversight across adult social care. However, this system was not yet in full use. Leaders and staff told us knowledge of risk and the themes and trends relating to risk was often held by managers of individual teams howev"},{"q":[[49,"Data provided by the local authority showed the expected timescale of 4 days for a safeguarding referral was not always achievable."],[51,"As per the Safeguarding Adults Board SAR protocol, all organisations known to be involved with the individuals were notified."],[52,"Some providers told us there was a lack of clarity regarding what constituted a safeguarding concern and when S42 safeguarding enquiries were required."]],"m":"The local authority did not consistently have robust systems and processes in place to protect people from abuse and neglect. Leaders told us staff had access to the duty chair if needed and cover arrangements for managers would be made if the manager was not available. However, staff told us there was not a clear process if the safeguarding managers were off work and staff would often self-allocate work with a lack of decision-making oversight. However, staff told us there was not a clear proce"},{"q":[[55,"Some improvements had been made since the appointment of a new PSW such as providing support to staff to understand the principles of the MCA 2005 and identifying gaps in eligibility following a Care Act assessment and quality of records and assessments."],[56,"The oversight and management of risk was inconsistent and varied from team to team, for example, there was no mechanism for risk review in the manager’s absence in some teams which could mean work may not be prioritised effectively and could result in harm to a person with care and support needs."],[56,"The ways that waiting lists were managed was also inconsistent across teams."]],"m":"The local authority identified gaps in overall governance which were planned to be addressed after bringing statutory functions back in-house after several years of these being managed by an external commissioned company. Leaders told us they recognised the local authorities’ oversight was limited when services were externally commissioned and had recognised that more work was needed to clearly define the standards of practice for example, better oversight and improved quality assurance. Some im"},{"q":[[58,"Though the strategy was clear and was known by senior leaders, it was not fully embedded at operational levels."],[59,"However, some staff reported limited opportunity for career progression and others from front-line teams experienced difficulty finding time to access training due to low staffing levels."],[62,"The local authority gathered people’s feedback about their experiences of care and support, and feedback from staff and partners. However, this was not consistently used to learn and improve services."]],"m":"Though the strategy was clear and was known by senior leaders, it was not fully embedded at operational levels. However, some staff reported limited opportunity for career progression and others from front-line teams experienced difficulty finding time to access training due to low staffing levels. Co-production was not yet fully embedded and senior leaders highlighted this as an area for improvement. However, some carers told us they were not aware of or had not been involved in any coproductio"}],"Bromley":[{"q":[[11,"Figures provided by the local authority in September 2025 showed the median time people waited for a Care Act assessment to begin from first contact with the local authority had significantly reduced between September 2024 from 166 days to 14 days in August 2025."],[13,"The median waiting times in August 2025 were similar to the previous 12 months; however maximum waiting times had increased over the previous 6 months."],[15,"People were not routinely contacted after they had been signposted to support, however, the local authority had recently started sending out an online questionnaire to people to ask them about their experience with the front door service."]],"m":"Figures provided by the local authority in September 2025 showed the median time people waited for a Care Act assessment to begin from first contact with the local authority had significantly reduced between September 2024 from 166 days to 14 days in August 2025. The local authority was working to better align data with the health partner, to improve how overdue reviews were tracked and overseen. The median waiting times in August 2025 were similar to the previous 12 months; however maximum wait"},{"q":[[20,"Senior leaders told us they did not routinely collect data from VCFSE groups about people’s outcomes; therefore, it was difficult to measure the impact of preventative services at a more granular level."],[20,"However, the impact of some individual projects or interventions for particular groups was measured. This limited the full reach and impact of preventive services, particularly for seldom heard populations."],[21,"Senior leaders also told us the local authority had the highest settled and travelling Gypsy, Roma, and Traveller population in London. Despite this, the local authority’s strategies such as the Adult Social Care Strategy did not include plans to engage with these groups to ensure any unmet needs for prevention or care and support were identified and considered in strategy and planning."]],"m":"Senior leaders told us they did not routinely collect data from VCFSE groups about people’s outcomes; therefore, it was difficult to measure the impact of preventative services at a more granular level. However, the impact of some individual projects or interventions for particular groups was measured. This limited the full reach and impact of preventive services, particularly for seldom heard populations. Senior leaders also told us the local authority had the highest settled and travelling Gyp"},{"q":[[28,"The full impact of these services for people was not yet known. For example, senior leaders and partners told us they were aware of some gaps remained to ensure all people received equitable access to care and support, particularly for the LGBTQ+ community, veterans, unpaid carers, in substance misuse and HIV in older populations."],[29,"Outcomes and impact of this group were not yet clear."],[29,"Some partners told us the authority monitored their ethnicity data and challenged them to improve outreach to minority groups. This approach to shared data and challenge showed the local authority could be responsive and were aware of the changing demographics, they were aware of the gaps in provision and showed some commitment to reaching seldom heard voices through proactive measures."]],"m":"The full impact of these services for people was not yet known. However, there were gaps in the local authority’s understanding of people’s experiences of drawing on support. However, senior leaders recognised some gaps in data capture remained, particularly for people from the LGBTQ+ community. Outcomes and impact of this group were not yet clear. Some partners told us the authority monitored their ethnicity data and challenged them to improve outreach to minority groups. This approach to share"},{"q":[[33,"Staff told us there was a lack of provision for working aged adults who required 24-hour care and those with more complex needs."],[33,"A range of quality services were available in the area to meet most people's needs; however, staff and partners told us about gaps in the local care market, particularly residential care for those aged 65 or over."],[37,"Where processes and standards did not meet local authority standards, the team agreed an action plan with the care provider accompanied by follow up visits as required to ensure the action plan was completed and standards were maintained."]],"m":"Staff told us there was a lack of provision for working aged adults who required 24-hour care and those with more complex needs. A range of quality services were available in the area to meet most people's needs; however, staff and partners told us about gaps in the local care market, particularly residential care for those aged 65 or over. Staff and partners told us about further gaps in service provision in the area. Staff and partners told us about further gaps in service provision in the are"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[47,"Staff told us they were concerned about the lack of adult management support during the night, particularly from 10pm when the EDT manager went off duty."],[49,"Practice review meetings authorised assessments and proposed transition care plans. This was designed to ensure young people were supported through both pathways; however, this was not reflected in both the experiences we heard from young people and their unpaid carers."],[50,"However, they told us when people with more complex needs went into hospital and needed 1:1 support to access hospital services, social workers did not always understand these needs when arranging care and support."]],"m":"Staff told us they were concerned about the lack of adult management support during the night, particularly from 10pm when the EDT manager went off duty. Staff told us there was a children's manager available, however, this was not formalised management cover. Staff told us there was often a lack of care agency and care home provision out of hours, especially if the referral came in for a person after late evening. People could sometimes be admitted to hospital as a place of safety. They told us"},{"q":[[54,"In 2024 to 2025, 2 SAR referrals did not meet the criteria, however a learning event was facilitated by the local authority and health partners for practitioners in February 2025."],[54,"There was a clear rationale and outcome from initial information gathering, including those which did not progress to a s42 enquiry."],[55,"The median number of days it took for section 42 enquiries to be completed was 49 days over the past 12 months between 2024 and 2025 which inside of the local authority timescale of 60 days. This target timescale meant that people and partners waited for the outcomes of safeguarding enquiries."]],"m":"In 2024 to 2025, 2 SAR referrals did not meet the criteria, however a learning event was facilitated by the local authority and health partners for practitioners in February 2025. There was a clear rationale and outcome from initial information gathering, including those which did not progress to a s42 enquiry. The median number of days it took for section 42 enquiries to be completed was 49 days over the past 12 months between 2024 and 2025 which inside of the local authority timescale of 60 da"},{"q":[[59,"The wider local authority had been going through a period of significant transformation which was also occurring within adult social care. This included the arrival of a new Director of Adult Social Services (DASS) and major changes to how services were structured within the department, particularly at the initial point of contact and Safeguarding areas."],[61,"There was a clear vision and adult social care strategy and there were multiple other strategies in place, such as the Adult Social Care Digital Transformation Programme, some of which were coproduced."],[61,"There was further work to do to ensure all staff were engaged with the strategy and vision as there was limited evidence of how this impacted their work with people and their outcomes."]],"m":"There was further work to do to ensure all staff were engaged with the strategy and vision as there was limited evidence of how this impacted their work with people and their outcomes."},{"q":[[64,"The local authority acknowledged more work was needed around coproduction as this had not been embedded throughout the organisation. This meant people’s experiences were not consistently being used to shape service provision."],[65,"The most common theme of complaints was the quality of the service, with other common themes being lack of action and information sharing."],[66,"The local authority acknowledged the delays in responding to complaints which had improved in recent years and had taken several steps to improve timeliness and accountability."]],"m":"The local authority acknowledged more work was needed around coproduction as this had not been embedded throughout the organisation. This meant people’s experiences were not consistently being used to shape service provision. The most common theme of complaints was the quality of the service, with other common themes being lack of action and information sharing. The local authority acknowledged the delays in responding to complaints which had improved in recent years and had taken several steps "}],"Brighton and Hove":[{"q":[[8,"People could access the local authority’s care and support services via telephone, online or through self-assessment, but people told us it was not easy to access an assessment and there were significant delays to accessing an assessment or review."],[11,"However, these improvements were recent and the improved waiting times were not consistent across all teams."],[11,"However, data dashboards did not provide oversight of the frequency of contact with people who were waiting."]],"m":"People could access the local authority’s care and support services via telephone, online or through self-assessment, but people told us it was not easy to access an assessment and there were significant delays to accessing an assessment or review. Feedback about ease of access to assessments was mixed; Two voluntary and community sector (VCS) partners told us people were sometimes unable to find the right information about assessments. People often experienced extended delays to assessment and "},{"q":[[21,"Staff described how there had been delays to reablement starting which had affected hospital discharge. In response, the local authority had set up a bridging service to support people after discharge from hospital and before reablement could start."],[22,"Local authority data for March 2025 showed people waited an average of 164.5 days for OT assessment."],[23,"VCS partners said it could sometimes be difficult to access information and advice, particularly for people at risk of digital exclusion. They told us the removal of the helpdesk at the local authority meant people had to seek information and advice by phone or through the website and could not attend in person."]],"m":"The local authority has taken steps to identify people with needs for care and support that were not being met. Health partners and staff cited limited access to reablement as an issue which could sometimes affect the timeliness of discharge from hospital. Staff and leaders told us there was sometimes limited capacity in community reablement and improving this was a strategic priority. Staff described how there had been delays to reablement starting which had affected hospital discharge. Staff d"},{"q":[[25,"The local authority had recently developed dashboards to look at demographic data, inform any gaps in service and identify seldom heard voices."],[27,"The work was recent so there were limited examples of positive impact at the time of assessment."],[29,"Staff described good access to translators where people did not speak English or where people used British Sign Language (BSL)."]],"m":"The local authority had recently developed dashboards to look at demographic data, inform any gaps in service and identify seldom heard voices. The work was recent so there were limited examples of positive impact at the time of assessment. Two VCS partners told us digital exclusion was a current issue, because the local authority website was sometimes difficult to use and options to attend in-person were limited. Staff described good access to translators where people did not speak English or w"},{"q":[[31,"Whilst these datasets did not directly replace ONS projections data, we heard how they had been used to inform commissioning decisions. For example, leaders described how data had shown increases in the population of people aged over 65 and this had informed the commissioning of the ‘ageing well’ service."],[32,"However, whilst the local authority clearly set out their approach to commissioning, the commissioning strategy documents and the market position statement lacked detail such as how much of each service they would develop, or by when."],[35,"Local authority data showed that over the three months to February 2025, people waited an average of 25 days for homecare support, and 54 days for supported living."]],"m":"Leaders told us this was due to gaps in the latest census data, which had not yet all been published by the Office for National Statistics (ONS). Leaders told us this was due to gaps in the latest census data, which had not yet all been published by the Office for National Statistics (ONS). This impacted the local authority’s ability to forecast to potential changes to the population to inform commissioning strategies and plans. Leaders told us how they sourced data from other places, such as fr"},{"q":[[44,"Leaders and health partners described positive working relationships but also acknowledged they were not where they wanted to be when it came to delivering on some shared objectives, such as delays in hospital discharge pathways or access to reablement."],[44,"There was collaborative work underway in these areas to improve hospital discharge and access to community reablement, but these had not yet had a significant impact."],[46,"A 2023/24 report on the BCF identified a number of successful metrics but also noted BCF projects had not yet fully impacted on reducing the use of residential care or improving access to reablement."]],"m":"Leaders and health partners described positive working relationships but also acknowledged they were not where they wanted to be when it came to delivering on some shared objectives, such as delays in hospital discharge pathways or access to reablement. There was collaborative work underway in these areas to improve hospital discharge and access to community reablement, but these had not yet had a significant impact. A 2023/24 report on the BCF identified a number of successful metrics but also "},{"q":[[49,"The local authority had a waiting list for reviews and on average reviews were over 12 months overdue and the numbers of overdue reviews were increasing."],[50,"The local authority and partners had started to pilot approaches to share information more effectively, which received positive feedback from staff because it gave them a rounded view of people’s needs and any risks. However, this pilot was not yet embedded into practice and staff feedback showed the sharing of information was not yet consistent."],[51,"However, there were delays to hospital discharge and we heard about inconsistencies in information sharing when people moved between services."]],"m":"The local authority had systems and processes in place to ensure safety in the system, but in some areas plans to mitigate risks were not fully implemented which meant known risks had gone unresolved for a number of years. The local authority had a waiting list for reviews and on average reviews were over 12 months overdue and the numbers of overdue reviews were increasing. Partners told us they regularly saw examples of people needing a review because of changes in need and that there were ofte"},{"q":[[55,"There was a safeguarding hub that undertook safeguarding investigations where people were not known to a team."],[59,"There was clarity on what constituted a safeguarding concern and when a Section 42 enquiry was required, but there was not always a shared understanding amongst partners about what constituted a safeguarding."],[60,"Staff had been trained as best interest assessors (BIAs) within this team, but we also heard from staff from outside this team that BIA training was not currently available."]],"m":"There were clear systems and processes to make sure people were protected from abuse and neglect, but feedback showed partners did not always receive information about safeguarding after making referrals. Feedback showed this was not consistent with every partner’s experience. There was a safeguarding hub that undertook safeguarding investigations where people were not known to a team. Safeguarding enquiries were not always resolved in a timely way. Staff also said they sometimes felt safeguardi"},{"q":[[63,"However, we noted dashboards did not provide oversight of how teams responded to risks within their waiting lists by capturing how frequently people received calls to ensure they were safe and check if their needs had changed."],[63,"We heard multiple examples of staff making regular contact with people who were waiting, but where we heard about instances where people had not received contact the dashboards did not provide assurance that calls were taking place in line with the local authority’s policy, which meant leaders relied on assurance from teams and could not monitor this area of performance through dashboards."],[64,"The local authority used information about risks, performance, inequalities and outcomes to inform strategy, but strategies to address significant challenges had made limited progress."]],"m":"Staff told us having new leaders in new leadership roles created a lack of clarity about who to raise issues with. However, systems did not capture information about people’s experiences of waiting lists. However, we noted dashboards did not provide oversight of how teams responded to risks within their waiting lists by capturing how frequently people received calls to ensure they were safe and check if their needs had changed. Staff also told us they did not use performance data to understand i"},{"q":[[66,"However our findings showed that despite the local authority using data or engaging with people to understand their performance, that actions taken to respond to risks in areas such as waiting times or commissioning had not yet been fully implemented."],[67,"Staff and leaders also described various routes to a professional qualification for staff who were not registered as social workers, occupational therapists or nurses."],[68,"However, VCS partners said there were opportunities for more co-production and that approaches to co-production were not advanced in some areas."]],"m":"However our findings showed that despite the local authority using data or engaging with people to understand their performance, that actions taken to respond to risks in areas such as waiting times or commissioning had not yet been fully implemented. Staff and leaders also described various routes to a professional qualification for staff who were not registered as social workers, occupational therapists or nurses. However, VCS partners said there were opportunities for more co-production and t"}],"Bedford Borough":[{"q":[[13,"The local authority was also in the process of implementing a data strategy designed to address these shortfalls."],[14,"The local authority provided a dataset showing the current waiting lists across teams on 15 September 2025, including how long each person had waited for their assessment."],[14,"The data showed that across the social work teams, excluding OT teams and transition work, 70% of people on the waiting lists had waited over 28 days, 49% had waited over 50 days and 26% had waited more than 90 days for an assessment."]],"m":"Staff said they were not always sure how the local authority supported people who had no recourse to public funds in mental health teams. The local authority told us there was not a formal process or pathway for people with no recourse to public funds, but that they dealt with very few people who required this support. People faced delays to having their needs assessed or reviewed and the local authority’s oversight of waiting list performance was limited by shortfalls in their data systems. Som"},{"q":[[23,"There were services in place to reduce, prevent and delay need for unpaid carers, but unpaid carers did not always feel they had access to the right services to promote their wellbeing."],[23,"The offer included peer support groups and activities, but feedback we received showed unpaid carers were not always able to access the preventative support they needed."],[23,"The local authority was working with unpaid carers to understand their needs through co-production and engagement activity, but this work was not yet fully implemented."]],"m":"There were services in place to reduce, prevent and delay need for unpaid carers, but unpaid carers did not always feel they had access to the right services to promote their wellbeing. Unpaid carers said they faced challenges around their mental wellbeing and they did not always know where to seek support. The offer included peer support groups and activities, but feedback we received showed unpaid carers were not always able to access the preventative support they needed. The local authority w"},{"q":[[31,"However, it did not detail how the local authority would do this and there was an absence of a plan or strategy which findings from reviews could feed into."],[32,"There was not an overarching equality strategy in place."],[32,"The local authority made use of the data available to it to understand how people and communities accessed adult social care services, but strategic approaches to tackling inequalities were not matured."]],"m":"Partners described being involved in a variety of initiatives with the local authority to understand barriers and inequalities, but also shared examples where this work was not fully evolved. However, partners also said people did not always get the opportunity to share their experiences and that some groups could remain hidden, such as people from deprived communities or male carers. However, it did not detail how the local authority would do this and there was an absence of a plan or strategy "},{"q":[[39,"Whilst the MPS outlined commissioning priorities and drew upon data, there was not always a strategy aligned to commissioning priorities. For example, there were commissioning priorities in relation to prevention but there was not a specific strategy that outlined how the local authority would achieve them."],[39,"The MPS gave a high-level summary which would inform the local market but there was not an underpinning strategy about how the local authority intended to shape the market, such as how they would identify and address gaps in their prevention offer."],[39,"There was not a strategy or plan providing more detail on the local authority’s approach to this."]],"m":"Whilst there had been commissioning activity to respond to this, there were some areas where this was not yet fully evolved. However, staff, leaders and partners also told us about areas where the strategy had not yet fully addressed need, such as homeless people who had non-eligible needs under the Care Act. However, staff, leaders and partners also told us about areas where the strategy had not yet fully addressed need, such as homeless people who had non-eligible needs under the Care Act. Thi"},{"q":[[50,"Whilst the tool itself showed clear action to address the identified need, the evidence of how it would be embedded within wider systems was unclear."],[50,"Partner agencies were named within the tool but we did not see evidence of co-production or collaboration in how the tool was developed."],[51,"When the local authority worked in partnerships with other agencies, there were not always clear arrangements for governance, accountability, monitoring, quality assurance and information sharing."]],"m":"Reports were shared with partners to support public health campaigns, track key indicators and ensure all partners were notified of emerging issues. Whilst the tool itself showed clear action to address the identified need, the evidence of how it would be embedded within wider systems was unclear. Partner agencies were named within the tool but we did not see evidence of co-production or collaboration in how the tool was developed. Feedback from leaders and staff about gaps in provision for peop"},{"q":[[55,"The local authority understood the risks to people across their care journeys, but there was often a lack of services for people with mental health needs who were not eligible under the Care Act, which posed risks to the safety of the system."],[56,"There was not a clear strategy to address the multiple gaps in services and provision for people with non-eligible mental health needs."],[56,"However, feedback from staff showed this issue was ongoing and there was not a focused strategy on improving access to early help services within mental health."]],"m":"The local authority understood the risks to people across their care journeys, but there was often a lack of services for people with mental health needs who were not eligible under the Care Act, which posed risks to the safety of the system. Staff and leaders told us how people with mental health needs which were not eligible under the Care Act, or autistic people with mental health needs, sometimes lacked a suitable service to meet their needs. Staff also described challenges in finding the ri"},{"q":[[61,"The local authority was also reliant on support from staff from across the service to support their safeguarding team in reducing backlogs of referrals."],[62,"Local authority data from between April and June 2025 showed 3.2% of referrals progressed to a section 42 enquiry. This meant for the majority of people whose referrals did not lead to a section 42 enquiry, safeguarding work took place in the triage stage."],[68,"There was not always clarity on what constituted a section 42 safeguarding concern because gaps in oversight limited the local authority’s ability to understand how thresholds were being applied."]],"m":"The local authority had limited oversight of key areas of safeguarding performance and risk, which meant they were not easily able to understand safeguarding performance and outcomes for people. Feedback about the approach of staff was positive, but staff and leaders told us about challenges in systems and processes that meant the ability to use data and understand performance was limited. The local authority was also reliant on support from staff from across the service to support their safegua"},{"q":[[74,"The local authority was working to overcome challenges in how they used data to maintain oversight of Care Act duties, but improvements had not been fully implemented at the time of this assessment. This meant there were gaps in the local authority’s understanding of key Care Act duties such as assessment and safeguarding."],[75,"Improvement work had not yet addressed limitations to how the local authority understood performance against core Care Act duties."],[75,"Safeguarding data was also an area of focus, but at the time of our assessment the majority of work carried out by the safeguarding team was captured on a local spreadsheet, which significantly limited the local authority’s ability to understand performance against this Care Act duty in a clear, timely way."]],"m":"The local authority was working to overcome challenges in how they used data to maintain oversight of Care Act duties, but improvements had not been fully implemented at the time of this assessment. This meant there were gaps in the local authority’s understanding of key Care Act duties such as assessment and safeguarding. Improvement work had not yet addressed limitations to how the local authority understood performance against core Care Act duties. Work to ensure waiting list data accurately "},{"q":[[81,"The local authority had a variety of processes in place to develop a culture of continuous learning, but shortfalls in the use of performance data limited their ability to understand and learn from people’s experiences of adult social care through data."],[81,"Coproduction was not yet embedded in the local authority’s work, but the local authority was working to enhance how it collaborated with people and partners to develop strategy."],[82,"However, the examples of the use of co-production within the framework document were limited to informing commissioning activity and specific pieces of work such as developing a new transition protocol."]],"m":"The local authority had a variety of processes in place to develop a culture of continuous learning, but shortfalls in the use of performance data limited their ability to understand and learn from people’s experiences of adult social care through data. Coproduction was not yet embedded in the local authority’s work, but the local authority was working to enhance how it collaborated with people and partners to develop strategy. People and unpaid carers told us opportunities to inform strategy we"}],"Blackpool":[{"q":[[13,"However, there was no formal monitoring or internal assurance audits for the quality of the contacts and responses to ensure people were consistently directed to the right team or service."],[13,"There was more to do to ensure any actions taken did not have negative outcomes for people such as understanding an unmet need or risks."],[14,"Following the CQC site visit in March 2025, senior leaders updated staff guidance in response to concerns raised. This clarified the role of business support staff was not to make a decision around a person’s needs, risks, care planning."]],"m":"Staff also told us if a person did not appear to have an adult social care need, business support could signpost the referrer to another service and the information would not need to be reviewed by a duty worker. However, there was no formal monitoring or internal assurance audits for the quality of the contacts and responses to ensure people were consistently directed to the right team or service. There was more to do to ensure any actions taken did not have negative outcomes for people such as"},{"q":[[37,"People waited a median of 41 days; however, we were told this had come down from 52 weeks (around 364 days) waiting."],[37,"The length of time people waited varied depending on geography meaning people had inequitable responses depending on where they lived. For example, the South team had an average wait of 120 days."],[38,"There were concerns about the lack of resource to progress local authority aspirations including to reduce formal care needs through specialist assessment and equipment. For example, senior leaders and partners told us about a ‘moving with dignity’ project."]],"m":"The local authority's efforts to support those at risk did not sufficiently promote healthier lives or reduce future care needs. Whether or how a person’s needs could be reduced or other needs could be delayed from arising risks was not considered at every interaction with a person. Timescales within improvement plans had repeatedly not been met. However, it did not provide detail of how ‘making every contact count’ would be embedded in social care practice and how prevention, delay or reduction"},{"q":[[43,"However, there was a lack of local strategy and action to reduce inequalities in people’s access to care and support, their experiences and outcomes."],[45,"People’s accounts highlighted the need for more mental health support in Blackpool that catered for LGBTQIA+ needs and people did not feel that services were tailored for them."],[50,"People told us their preferred inclusion and accessibility arrangements were not always followed. For example, a person told us they had significant reading difficulties and preferred to have information shared with them verbally as opposed to in writing, but they continued to get letters in the post rather than telephone calls as agreed from the local authority."]],"m":"People were not being treated equally, standards of care and support differed depending on people’s individual and multiple needs. People were not being treated equally, standards of care and support differed depending on people’s individual and multiple needs. These were not new issues for the local authority to address. However, there was a lack of local strategy and action to reduce inequalities in people’s access to care and support, their experiences and outcomes. The local authority lacked"},{"q":[[56,"However, there was a lack of alignment with health and public health to meet local needs. For example, there was no reference to equality or prevention in the draft Commissioning Strategy submitted to us."],[56,"Although the local authority aspired within the draft Commissioning Strategy to support research and work more closely with partners (including providers) this had not yet been implemented."],[58,"However, the local authority did not have plans to increase extra care housing and instead was progressing development of traditional housing accommodation such as sheltered schemes."]],"m":"Teams told us they supported care home providers to understand that someone's learning difficulty or disability did not mean they would need support much different from anybody else. Another partner told us a senior leader ‘had consultations with commissioners to promote their work’ but did not reference any direct work with them. However, there was a lack of alignment with health and public health to meet local needs. Although the local authority aspired within the draft Commissioning Strategy "},{"q":[[72,"There were inconsistent experiences and outcomes for people and unpaid carers. Senior leaders, staff and partners told us more needed to be done to integrate with health, the voluntary and charity sector and progress preventative approaches to make a difference to people’s outcomes."],[74,"There were health neighbourhood teams aligned to primary care networks and health led priorities. These were not specifically aligned to local authority community or specialist adult social care teams, but they did have a local authority social worker assigned to each to support partnership working such as taking referrals, and sharing expertise and information as required."],[77,"There was a lack of market shaping with the voluntary and charity sector groups in Blackpool and missed opportunities around shaping a prevent, reduce and delay offer."]],"m":"Challenges were not limited to the local authority within partnership working arrangements. There were inconsistent experiences and outcomes for people and unpaid carers. However, adults within this board told us their impact was limited. There were health neighbourhood teams aligned to primary care networks and health led priorities. These were not specifically aligned to local authority community or specialist adult social care teams, but they did have a local authority social worker assigned "},{"q":[[83,"However, there was more to do to ensure young people known to services received holistic and person-centred planning from age 14 and to ensure transitional safeguarding processes included young adults without obvious care and support needs (such as care leavers) are well supported and do not fall through gaps in services."],[83,"Young adults who had an Education, Health and Care Plan (EHCP) and parent carers were requesting assessment and support that current prevention, assessment and equipment services were not prepared for."],[87,"People and their unpaid carers gave negative feedback about individual contingency plans within the local authority. For example, unpaid carers told us the local authority had not considered their caring role and future plans. Most had planned for family to step in if there was a crisis, but this had not been discussed with the local authority."]],"m":"However, where staff had autonomy or flexible responsibilities the local authority was unable to provide assurances that there was robust and specialist induction and training, regular and quality supervision, and evidence audit and learning to ensure this was safe and effective practice. However, due to other priorities this had not yet been progressed and was not in place at the time of the CQC assessment. There was a lack of evidence that processes were effective and being routinely monitored"},{"q":[[88,"Section 42 enquiries could not be closed without management approval on the digital system, however concerns or risks raised that did not reach enquiry stage did not receive the same oversight or assurance."],[93,"There were recognised discrepancies or gaps in data to inform strategic understanding, however improvements were beginning to be evidenced. For example, a partner told us 12 months ago the SAB did not have a data set informed by local authority safeguarding data."],[99,"There was mixed feedback from partners about responses and support from the local authority in relation to rationale and outcome from safeguarding enquiries, including those which did not progress to a section 42 enquiry."]],"m":"Section 42 enquiries could not be closed without management approval on the digital system, however concerns or risks raised that did not reach enquiry stage did not receive the same oversight or assurance. However, an equal number did not receive any explanation, resulting in uncertainty and confusion. A person told us they had raised financial abuse concerns but had not been listened to and they were not offered support until a different social worker became involved at a later date. Staff tol"},{"q":[[108,"The leadership approach had resulted in lack of clear strategic direction and delayed pace around necessary actions needed to ensure effective governance, management and accountability arrangements. For example, the local authority had been rolling out a new adult social care assessment model for over 2 years."],[108,"However, there was more to do, as not all staff were clear about how the local authority was working towards reducing risks to keeping people safe where there were shortfalls in carrying out Care Act duties."],[109,"There were plans to develop a newsletter and an adult services library for staff to improve communication, however this had not yet been implemented."]],"m":"The DASS told us there had been some changes to improve leadership capacity and address gaps in resources to carry out key actions needed over the past two years. However, they also told us there was ambiguity and a lack of staff engagement. The leadership approach had resulted in lack of clear strategic direction and delayed pace around necessary actions needed to ensure effective governance, management and accountability arrangements. Senior leaders consistently told us this had taken longer t"},{"q":[[114,"Senior leaders told us the delays in progressing actions within the adult services improvement plan had been impacted by a number of issues."],[114,"Engagement sessions with 17 unpaid carers were held to understand what was working and what was not working, how needs could be best met and what potential gaps in current services there was."],[120,"There were aspirations to actively promote the training across adult social care services, however this had not taken place at the time of the CQC assessment and therefore there was not yet conclusive evidence of any effective impact this could have."]],"m":"Whilst we found there had been a more recent priority to resource improvement around safeguarding responsibilities, there was a clear lack of resource and pace needed to prioritise shortfalls around prevention, early intervention, strength based approaches and equity in peoples experiences. Senior leaders told us the delays in progressing actions within the adult services improvement plan had been impacted by a number of issues. A senior leader also told us there was a lack of engaging staff in "}],"Calderdale":[{"q":[[10,"Staff followed the \"Waiting Safe and Well Call Back Conversation\" guidance and escalated concerns through their supervisor before the final risk assessment was completed for allocation. This process ensured that individuals received timely and appropriate support, reducing risks and safeguarding their wellbeing while they awaited further care."],[11,"Integrating occupational therapy at the front door and streamlining hospital assessment pathways had helped improve efficiency through a smoother transition for people requiring support and reduction in delays to access reablement services."],[12,"However, the local authority experienced recruitment challenges in sensory teams. As a result, a significant backlog of people awaited specialist assessments."]],"m":"Staff followed the \"Waiting Safe and Well Call Back Conversation\" guidance and escalated concerns through their supervisor before the final risk assessment was completed for allocation. This process ensured that individuals received timely and appropriate support, reducing risks and safeguarding their wellbeing while they awaited further care. Integrating occupational therapy at the front door and streamlining hospital assessment pathways had helped improve efficiency through a smoother transiti"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[62,"Carers expressed concerns about abrupt transitions, highlighting gaps in service provision. For example, parent carers likened the transition process to \"dropping off a cliff,\" demonstrating the sudden shift in support."],[63,"Weekly multidisciplinary team discussions improved coordinated care, and targeted initiatives reduced inequalities and prevented gaps in support. These measures ensured people facing multiple disadvantages received appropriate interventions, aligning with legal responsibilities to promote well-being and safety."],[65,"The lack of clarity regarding transition barriers and service access criteria underscored gaps in effective planning."]],"m":"Carers expressed concerns about abrupt transitions, highlighting gaps in service provision. Weekly multidisciplinary team discussions improved coordinated care, and targeted initiatives reduced inequalities and prevented gaps in support. Weekly multidisciplinary team discussions improved coordinated care, and targeted initiatives reduced inequalities and prevented gaps in support. These measures ensured people facing multiple disadvantages received appropriate interventions, aligning with legal "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Cambridgeshire":[{"q":[[12,"However, we were aware that there had been an upward trend in overdue reviews at the local authority in the last 12 months from 1,121 in February 2024 to 1,691 in February 2025."],[12,"The median wait for overdue reviews completed in the 12 months to February 2025 was 162 days."],[13,"An emergency planning service was available if needed for unpaid carers to access a sitting service, plus rapid response care workers, if unpaid carers were unable to provide care for any reason unexpectedly."]],"m":"Some were invited to review meetings, however others were not, when they felt this would have been beneficial. However, we were aware that there had been an upward trend in overdue reviews at the local authority in the last 12 months from 1,121 in February 2024 to 1,691 in February 2025. The median wait for overdue reviews completed in the 12 months to February 2025 was 162 days. An emergency planning service was available if needed for unpaid carers to access a sitting service, plus rapid respo"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[29,"Staff told us about some gaps in services and areas currently. For example, for young people in full time education, equipment could currently be provided to them at home but not in educational settings."],[29,"Additionally, some basic health care needs such as administering eye drops would not be covered by health, nor be covered by social care, unless this was part of a wider package of care, which then left a gap in support for some people."],[31,"Other feedback from partners was there was some limited choice in terms of support for the deaf community and gaps in other services, for example, housing for people with serious mental health problems. It was felt advocacy services were also needed to support people with mental health needs better, for example, to prevent people from becoming homeless."]],"m":"Staff told us about some gaps in services and areas currently. Additionally, some basic health care needs such as administering eye drops would not be covered by health, nor be covered by social care, unless this was part of a wider package of care, which then left a gap in support for some people. Other feedback from partners was there was some limited choice in terms of support for the deaf community and gaps in other services, for example, housing for people with serious mental health problem"},{"q":[[36,"There had also been a reduction in placements which had experienced delays due to a lack of capacity in the market from 12%, to 7%."],[37,"Overall, 7% of placements were delayed due to lack of capacity and were delayed by 31 days on average."],[37,"There were some minor shortfalls within East Cambridgeshire and Fenland, but it was expected the county would have an oversupply of residential care beds within the next 2 years."]],"m":"Partners gave us mixed feedback about whether the local authority had a good understanding of demographics in Cambridgeshire with some saying this could be improved whereas others felt that the local authority did understand local people’s needs, but this did not always translate into services well. There had also been a reduction in placements which had experienced delays due to a lack of capacity in the market from 12%, to 7%. In these situations, incounty providers may have had capacity but w"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[52,"The team members were all senior social workers and Approved Mental Health Professionals (AMHPs), covering work out of hours, emergencies and dealing with issues that could not wait until the next working day."],[53,"Emergency respite and pet care were other issues which arose, as well as some delays in getting access to s12 doctors."],[53,"Additional funding had been invested into EDT 18 months ago for relief workers, but this needed to be reviewed again. They were aware it was not sustainable to use relief staffing currently so were exploring permanent staffing now."]],"m":"The team members were all senior social workers and Approved Mental Health Professionals (AMHPs), covering work out of hours, emergencies and dealing with issues that could not wait until the next working day. Staff told us the team were resourceful, however felt the current model of working was not sustainable, and a review of this was underway which had been led by Children’s services who manage this service. Staff feedback about EDT was there were issues with staffing which meant that sometim"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Camden":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Central Bedfordshire":[{"q":[[11,"Staff in FRT contacted people based on risk: immediate priorities were scenarios such as where unpaid carers were overwhelmed by their caring responsibilities or hospital discharge was not successful."],[14,"Where people had urgent need, such as in an unpaid carer emergency, this was prioritised. This meant, where people waited, waiting lists were regularly considered and people’s needs and changing circumstances were proactively taken into account and their assessments or reviews were reprioritised."],[15,"A proportionate assessment means providing the right level of response to meet the needs of the person, the situation or the level of risk. This was available at an individual level using the interactive waiting list but was not reportable to senior managers for oversight of a person’s journey through Central Bedfordshire’s system, from point of contact to the completion of the assessment."]],"m":"Staff in FRT contacted people based on risk: immediate priorities were scenarios such as where unpaid carers were overwhelmed by their caring responsibilities or hospital discharge was not successful. The local authority had taken action to remind staff about the importance of sharing documentation and work was ongoing to develop an online portal to share information with people, though this was not in place at the time of our assessment. Some providers said they were not always involved in revi"},{"q":[[27,"Staff received specific enhanced training, including in situations where there were limited assessments, in order to support people effectively at this point. This intervention played a key role in easing hospital discharge and reducing pressure on ongoing care services."],[28,"Local authority data indicated where care was delivered by a provider who was not a part of the reablement offer people either had no formal support following the reablement period (50%) or short- or long-term homecare support (50%) between April and October 2025."],[29,"There were no reported delays in accessing TEC; local authority data indicated there were no delays to installation, no stock shortfalls and the referral to installation rate was 98%."]],"m":"Staff received specific enhanced training, including in situations where there were limited assessments, in order to support people effectively at this point. Staff received specific enhanced training, including in situations where there were limited assessments, in order to support people effectively at this point. This intervention played a key role in easing hospital discharge and reducing pressure on ongoing care services. Local authority data indicated where care was delivered by a provider"},{"q":[[37,"Another partner described remaining vigilant about areas of known higher deprivation with the local authority so people’s needs were not lost considering the general affluence of the area."],[38,"Some staff however said this could sometimes be challenging, especially in an emergency situation and not always when needed face to face."],[40,"The local authority had a map-based directory of services available to the public, which included care services and local authority and partner provision, such as libraries, pharmacies and children’s centres. It did not extend to community sector services which could have further supported access to resources within isolated areas."]],"m":"Another partner described remaining vigilant about areas of known higher deprivation with the local authority so people’s needs were not lost considering the general affluence of the area. Some staff however said this could sometimes be challenging, especially in an emergency situation and not always when needed face to face. The local authority had a map-based directory of services available to the public, which included care services and local authority and partner provision, such as libraries"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Cheshire East":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Cheshire West and Chester":[{"q":[[11,"Some teams lacked formal review systems and instead provided people with information booklets."],[12,"Interim and long-term solutions were available while people waited, including input from the promoting independence and community teams."],[13,"Data provided by the local authority for carer assessments (including themes if people waited longer) indicated at the time of submission in January 2025, there were 38 people awaiting carer assessments."]],"m":"Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely and up to date. Some teams lacked formal review systems and instead provided people with information booklets. However, the local authority had acknowledged the long waits and increased staffing to improve this. Interim and long-term solutions were available while people waited, including input from the promoting independence and community teams. Data provided by the local authority for carer assessments (including themes if people"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[43,"Efforts to manage future demand reflected an understanding of the need for systemic change but highlighted gaps in operational planning and collaboration with service providers."],[43,"Carers highlighted challenges in balancing their responsibilities and personal wellbeing due to insufficient support services."],[45,"As of January 2025, 337 people were placed OOA, with 91 placements made in the last year due to personal choice or the lack of local specialist services."]],"m":"The local authority shared the potential of data intelligence to enhance its initiatives though this was not yet fully embedded. However, some progress was noted in care market and housing market engagement. However, plans remained broad and lacked specifics on implementation, governance, and accountability. Efforts to manage future demand reflected an understanding of the need for systemic change but highlighted gaps in operational planning and collaboration with service providers. Carers highl"},{"q":[[54,"However, providers told us there were delays in Care Act assessments which affected patient flow in inpatient units, and integrated service, joint commissioning and pooled resources remained unestablished in some areas."],[55,"Health providers acknowledged the lack of a formal s75 agreement and limited funding for core initiatives like the rapid response service."],[57,"Community groups highlighted the pressures within the sector and insufficient support from the local authority. They observed that, while these pressures were often selfimposed, there was a lack of management regarding the impact of their work."]],"m":"However, providers told us there were delays in Care Act assessments which affected patient flow in inpatient units, and integrated service, joint commissioning and pooled resources remained unestablished in some areas. Staff also told us some health partner relationships were strained which made work to reduce significant delays in some discharge pathways challenging. Health providers acknowledged the lack of a formal s75 agreement and limited funding for core initiatives like the rapid respons"},{"q":[[62,"Efforts included addressing hoarding and homelessness, minimising delays in the west, and increasing homecare services to prevent hospital admissions."],[63,"Staff highlighted insufficient respite facilities and the reliance on out-of-area placements for residential support."],[65,"Carers told us they felt unable to consider the future/contingency plans."]],"m":"Efforts included addressing hoarding and homelessness, minimising delays in the west, and increasing homecare services to prevent hospital admissions. Staff highlighted insufficient respite facilities and the reliance on out-of-area placements for residential support. Funding decisions or disputes with other agencies did not lead to delays in the provision of care and support. Carers told us they felt unable to consider the future/contingency plans."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Doncaster":[{"q":[[15,"In June 2025, 636 people needing a review of their needs were waiting to be allocated a social worker, and in the year prior to this time, the median time for overdue reviews was 89 days."],[15,"Over this time, the longest period a review was overdue was 404 days."],[15,"At the time of assessment, the local authority was working to improve its approach to assessing the needs of unpaid carers and working with them to meet their needs. In response to poor feedback and outcomes for this population group, including a lack of support and declining wellbeing, the local authority had made and was continuing to make improvements to how it identified and assessed the needs of unpaid carers."]],"m":"The local authority had undertaken focused work to reduce the backlog of people waiting for a review of their care and support needs and leaders were aware of the need to continue this work. In June 2025, 636 people needing a review of their needs were waiting to be allocated a social worker, and in the year prior to this time, the median time for overdue reviews was 89 days. Over this time, the longest period a review was overdue was 404 days. At the time of assessment, the local authority was "},{"q":[[25,"The local authority offered community reablement provision (Short Term Enablement Programme, STEPS) and a bedded provision for people unable to remain at home safely (Positive Steps Unit, PSU)."],[27,"Arrangements were in place to reduce the time people waited for equipment, for example, equipment could be ordered in preparation for an assessment of a person’s needs."],[29,"Carer feedback also indicated that, without information on what was available to support their wellbeing, there was a perception of a lack of choice in the borough for this population group."]],"m":"The local authority offered community reablement provision (Short Term Enablement Programme, STEPS) and a bedded provision for people unable to remain at home safely (Positive Steps Unit, PSU). Arrangements were in place to reduce the time people waited for equipment, for example, equipment could be ordered in preparation for an assessment of a person’s needs. Carer feedback also indicated that, without information on what was available to support their wellbeing, there was a perception of a lac"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[41,"Staff corroborated these gaps in provision, saying that when people came into the area or service from other areas, lack of capacity meant emergency respite was sometimes used to keep people safe while appropriate care placements became available."],[43,"Between December 2024 and January 2025, the average time people waited for a home care support package to start from the date of referral was 10 days."],[43,"During the same time period, 3 people needed to wait for care from supported living services to begin due to lack of capacity in the market."]],"m":"People we spoke with told us that even when specialist care was available in Doncaster, limited options meant they could still be placed far from their homes and/or families in the borough. Staff corroborated these gaps in provision, saying that when people came into the area or service from other areas, lack of capacity meant emergency respite was sometimes used to keep people safe while appropriate care placements became available. Leaders were aware of the need to address gaps in provision an"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[64,"We were assured arrangements were in place to facilitate sharing of safeguarding and quality themes internally and at multi-agency forums with other system partners. However, this gap in understanding indicated work was needed by leaders to ensure these arrangements were properly communicated and understood by the workforce."],[66,"Mixed feedback from partners regarding the local authority’s approach to safeguarding indicated pathways and processes were not always understood throughout the system, which could leave people at risk of harm. This was an area for focus by leaders."],[68,"Leaders were working towards reducing the backlog of people waiting for assessments of their Deprivation of Liberty Safeguards (DoLS) applications so their liberty was not restricted unnecessarily."]],"m":"Additionally, some staff described available safeguarding training as outdated and said they did not feel supported by the safeguarding hub. Additionally, some staff described available safeguarding training as outdated and said they did not feel supported by the safeguarding hub. This gap signalled areas of focus by leaders. Leaders told us tools such as peer support, feedback, and coaching would be implemented to support staff not yet using the new safeguarding approach. We were assured arrang"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"City of London":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[31,"Staff and partners told us the Bangladeshi community was a key focus for engagement, as traditional service models did not always meet their needs. This community used translated materials and language translation services to provide support."],[33,"Examples were provided of how staff drew on their own cultural knowledge and second languages to support people, and where this was not possible, they accessed professional interpreters."],[34,"The local authority commissioned a language service to support people whose first language was not English. This included British Sign Language provision, and data showed that 35 people accessed the service in 2023-2024."]],"m":"Staff and partners told us the Bangladeshi community was a key focus for engagement, as traditional service models did not always meet their needs. Staff and partners told us the Bangladeshi community was a key focus for engagement, as traditional service models did not always meet their needs. This community used translated materials and language translation services to provide support. A voluntary sector partner told us many ethnic minority unpaid carers did not identify as carers and were the"},{"q":[[43,"An example was provided of staff not recommending services in Hackney due to the location, whilst near in miles, lack of public transport made access difficult."],[47,"However, there were gaps in provision identified in some areas, such as short breaks."],[48,"While the local authority had an action plan to ensure sustainable services, gaps in services and feedback from partners and people demonstrated that there was a need for more localised services to manage people’s individual needs."]],"m":"Senior leaders told us a future commissioning strategy was under consideration and involved exploring residential care models, including shared lives, extra care provision and alternatives but that although current data did not provide a detailed picture of current demand the local authority was mindful of the large provision within a 5-mile radius. Unpaid carers told us there was a lack of access to respite, sitting services, and befriending services locally. Staff told us where support could n"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[67,"The discretionary review identified how both the police and health services worked in isolation and did not share information."],[67,"The discretionary review highlighted gaps in inter-agency communication and missed opportunities for follow-up after discharge from mental health services."],[67,"The review identified the need for face-to-face engagement within 24 hours post-discharge and raised concerns about categorising homeless people as “no fixed abode,” which limited access to services."]],"m":"The discretionary review identified how both the police and health services worked in isolation and did not share information. The discretionary review highlighted gaps in inter-agency communication and missed opportunities for follow-up after discharge from mental health services. The review identified the need for face-to-face engagement within 24 hours post-discharge and raised concerns about categorising homeless people as “no fixed abode,” which limited access to services. Although this did"},{"q":[[74,"There were arrangements for governance and quality assurance which supported the delivery of the Adult Social Care Strategy."],[75,"There was a potential gap in how this role was strategically aligned and embedded within the leadership structure, stronger strategic oversight could support a stronger alignment with corporate strategy and objectives."],[77,"While the strategy was co-produced with carers, they told us that they did not always feel that their views were fully reflected within the strategy. It was felt that co-production would benefit from a formal governance structure and coproduction strategy that would support and ensure consistency in utilising the voices of people with lived experience."]],"m":"There was a potential gap in how this role was strategically aligned and embedded within the leadership structure, stronger strategic oversight could support a stronger alignment with corporate strategy and objectives. While the strategy was co-produced with carers, they told us that they did not always feel that their views were fully reflected within the strategy. While the strategy was co-produced with carers, they told us that they did not always feel that their views were fully reflected wi"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Wolverhampton":[{"q":[[13,"The local authority confirmed that OneWolverhampton was supporting a review of all hospital pathway discharges with the aim of developing a project called ‘Home2Assess’. This project was not live at the time of the assessment."],[15,"At the time of the assessment in June 2025, the backlog for KIT conversations remained high, with 910 overdue reviews and a maximum waiting time of 27 months beyond the 12-month due date."],[15,"A dedicated KIT team was established to address overdue reviews, and a test and learn pilot involving in-house provider staff conducting reviews had shown promising results in improving quality and timeliness."]],"m":"People reported that they were not always provided with the contact details of their social worker. People told us that in the absence of direct contact information for a named social worker, they had to call the main switchboard and request to be connected to the relevant team or staff member whenever they had concerns or queries. This lack of direct access created barriers to communication and support. Unpaid carers specifically described not always receiving meaningful support from the local "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[40,"Others highlighted gaps in engagement and delivery for example one partner told us that people struggle to contact the local authority by telephone and spend time on the line waiting for someone to answer however after 30 minutes the call is ended automatically."],[40,"People who are digitally excluded were not able to communicate with the Local Authority when needed."],[40,"Concerns were raised about digital exclusion, with some partners reporting that people struggled to access services due to limited digital skills or connectivity."]],"m":"However, the impact of these actions had not yet been measured. Senior leadership told us an EDI plan existed, but it was not yet visible across the workforce. Others highlighted gaps in engagement and delivery for example one partner told us that people struggle to contact the local authority by telephone and spend time on the line waiting for someone to answer however after 30 minutes the call is ended automatically. People who are digitally excluded were not able to communicate with the Local"},{"q":[[46,"However, data sharing across the partnership was still in its early stages, and the Market Position Statement had not yet been developed into a commissioning strategy to inform future intentions."],[51,"Staff identified gaps in provision for people leaving prison and gaps in services for young people with physical disabilities, which sometimes led to out-of-area placements. This issue was set to be addressed as part of the disability framework review, with a new disability contract expected by 2026."],[52,"Carers made specific reference to the lack of service provision for people with complex nursing needs in the local area."]],"m":"Another gap was the lack of ground-floor accessible accommodation, particularly for younger adults with disabilities. Some staff said that the absence of a formal Market Position Statement had created a lack of strategic direction in assessing whether services met needs and addressed access barriers. Others identified the absence of population demographic data sharing as an area for improvement. They said health services held valuable intelligence that the local authority could not access, which"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[70,"Staff acknowledged it that people were not always allocated a social worker six months before their 18th birthday, as expected."],[70,"If provision was not available locally, commissioning teams explored options across the wider Black Country footprint."],[71,"Staff told us that there were very small delays for all pathways despite the increased demand that the service experienced."]],"m":"Staff acknowledged it that people were not always allocated a social worker six months before their 18th birthday, as expected. If provision was not available locally, commissioning teams explored options across the wider Black Country footprint. Staff told us that there were very small delays for all pathways despite the increased demand that the service experienced. Staff felt confident that these cases were seen, and managed appropriately, although staff were unable to confirm that this was n"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"York":[{"q":[[13,"Whilst there were management checks for the quality of the contacts and responses there were no formal audits to assure senior leaders that people were consistently given the right advice and directed to the right service including those outside of the local authority."],[13,"People, staff and partners gave mixed feedback about peoples’ experiences of being supported by teams whilst they waited for assessment."],[16,"There were delays in carrying out a Care Act assessment."]],"m":"Staff told us if a person did not appear to have an adult social care need, they could signpost the person or referrer to another service and the information would not need to be reviewed by a duty worker or manager. Whilst there were management checks for the quality of the contacts and responses there were no formal audits to assure senior leaders that people were consistently given the right advice and directed to the right service including those outside of the local authority. People, staff"},{"q":[[28,"Early intervention linked with the Health and Wellbeing Strategy, however, there was no clear, co-produced prevention policy, strategy or delivery plan informed by local data and aligned with strategies and priorities of local partners."],[28,"There were some challenges in delivery such as gaps in workforce but a clear enthusiasm to make the changes to the way the local authority and partners delivered these services to improve outcomes for people."],[34,"However, priority 2 and 3 which included identified risk but being managed were overdue up to 17 weeks compared to targets set (between 2-12 weeks)."]],"m":"However, at the time of CQC assessment there were delays in this being progressed. Early intervention linked with the Health and Wellbeing Strategy, however, there was no clear, co-produced prevention policy, strategy or delivery plan informed by local data and aligned with strategies and priorities of local partners. There were some challenges in delivery such as gaps in workforce but a clear enthusiasm to make the changes to the way the local authority and partners delivered these services to "},{"q":[[42,"The local authority funded a self-advocacy forum, however senior leaders recognised this did not represent all people and they wanted to see greater diversity in the forums."],[42,"Previously, they had been supported by staff within the specific learning disabilities team, which in their opinion had the wrong skills and as a result did not meet their needs, which negatively impacted their wellbeing."],[43,"The strategy set out the need to collect data about people taking part in engagement activities, and analysis to identify gaps in representation and ensure inclusive and diverse engagement."]],"m":"Staff had limited confidence and understanding of their local communities and wider themes that negatively impacted people who were most likely to experience inequality. Most people from seldom heard groups told us they didn’t feel listened to, and the local authority did not engage with them in ways that worked for them, this resulted in issues such as stigmatisation and a need to ‘change culture’, ‘peoples attitudes’ and ‘people to be accepted for who they are’ which people told us was not bei"},{"q":[[50,"The local authority aimed to become a health generating city and reduce the gap in healthy life expectancy over the next 10 years by tackling the causes of ill health in poorer communities."],[50,"Senior leaders identified a need to have a Housing with Support Strategy, this had been delayed due to lack of capacity and progress was being monitored within their ambition board and plan."],[52,"Within York over 1,500 people were registered as having a learning disability. This was 0.4% of all people compared to national estimates that suggest 2% of people in the United Kingdom had a learning disability and the local authority acknowledged potential gaps in health reporting of this."]],"m":"The local authority aimed to become a health generating city and reduce the gap in healthy life expectancy over the next 10 years by tackling the causes of ill health in poorer communities. A key challenge was the lack of specialist housing and support options particularly for people with multiple and, or additional needs. Senior leaders identified a need to have a Housing with Support Strategy, this had been delayed due to lack of capacity and progress was being monitored within their ambition "},{"q":[[66,"People wanted to be involved in the planning of bringing together a partnership board and shaping future strategy and plans. They had previously been involved in an AllAge Learning Disability strategy 2019-2024 but were not involved or aware of any review or new strategy consultation, involvement, or co-production."],[67,"Additional concerns were raised about hospital discharge processes, especially in relation to 'home first' approaches and trusted assessment principles, as well as duplication or lack of clarity around the roles of health and social care occupational therapy services."],[69,"Due to gaps in senior leadership at the time of the CQC assessment the Director of Adult Social Care had taken a key role to support improved ways of working including being a point of operational escalation over the past 6-9 months."]],"m":"People wanted to be involved in the planning of bringing together a partnership board and shaping future strategy and plans. They had previously been involved in an AllAge Learning Disability strategy 2019-2024 but were not involved or aware of any review or new strategy consultation, involvement, or co-production. Additional concerns were raised about hospital discharge processes, especially in relation to 'home first' approaches and trusted assessment principles, as well as duplication or lack"},{"q":[[86,"There were challenges around people who were unable to access support and struggle to engage with services."],[87,"There was also a lack of effective processes to support people who arranged and funded their own care, this meant people could be misinformed of what could happen when there was a depletion of a person’s finances and could lead to a move between services of their choice."],[87,"People who self-funded their own care did have a choice to utilise local authority assessment and planning functions, but this came at a cost of £600 per interaction and often deterred people and partners from referring into the local authority, along with long waits to seek the support they may need."]],"m":"Another partner told us the local authority had a significant lack of staff resources, and it took staff 3 days to come back to them about an urgent situation even after leaving 4 voicemails. Another partner told us the local authority had a significant lack of staff resources, and it took staff 3 days to come back to them about an urgent situation even after leaving 4 voicemails. They said staff turnover resulted in poor training; lack of experienced staff and people were being left at risk. Th"},{"q":[[90,"However, staff and partners told us there could be ‘bouncing back and forth’ of referrals back to Customer Contact Workers if there was not the right information to determine if the referral met the threshold for a safeguarding enquiry."],[97,"However, the maximum wait times demonstrated the local authority was not meeting its own targets. They also set ‘Outcome Reviews’ targets which should be held regularly to check if the agreed actions in the Safeguarding Plan had taken place and whether any further action was needed."],[99,"Therefore, between March 2025 until the CQC site visit the local authority did not have any updated data to share with us."]],"m":"The local authority had a complaint that had gone to ombudsman that showed a person was not getting a response in relation to a safeguarding concern they had. Partners told us there were difficulties relating to the local authority interpretation of statutory thresholds of safeguarding and they did not always feel supported to manage risks to people if it was screened as not meeting criteria or they were waiting for a response. However, staff and partners told us there could be ‘bouncing back an"},{"q":[[102,"In contrast, unpaid carers in York told us services felt stretched and gaps in the system were filled by carers, this was having a negative impact on unpaid carers wellbeing and reduced their confidence in the leadership of the local authority."],[105,"There had been gaps in quality assurance due to the vacant PSW role, however some senior leaders had shared aspects out between them such as the transformation lead and heads of service."],[106,"Previous poor planning and lack of evaluation of projects and initiatives had a negative impact on some staff trust and confidence in adult social care strategic planning."]],"m":"In contrast, unpaid carers in York told us services felt stretched and gaps in the system were filled by carers, this was having a negative impact on unpaid carers wellbeing and reduced their confidence in the leadership of the local authority. There had been gaps in quality assurance due to the vacant PSW role, however some senior leaders had shared aspects out between them such as the transformation lead and heads of service. Senior leaders told us this work was not being rushed as it was the "},{"q":[[109,"Staff were not receiving feedback and had no way of learning from panel decisions and outcomes."],[110,"However, there was a gap in practice development framework particularly for specialist teams, commissioning staff, and occupational therapy."],[114,"Over the past 6 months they made changes to processes that were not needed or had 'lesser value' to make job more effective to support people in a timelier way. As a result of improved ways of working senior leaders were able to strategically use some of the additional workforce to benefit demand in other teams with the aim to meet Care Act duties."]],"m":"Some staff were frustrated with gaps in management impacting the demands they faced and told us gaps in workforce meant ideas for improvements were not always successful. Staff were not receiving feedback and had no way of learning from panel decisions and outcomes. However, there was a gap in practice development framework particularly for specialist teams, commissioning staff, and occupational therapy. Over the past 6 months they made changes to processes that were not needed or had 'lesser va"}],"Cornwall":[{"q":[[11,"Some partners told us they were not consistently informed by social workers when a planned review was being arranged but had received this information through the people they were supporting."],[13,"We found some services and projects, for example hotel breaks had been introduced but uptake remained limited."],[19,"If informal support was not available an advocacy referral would be completed and was normally in place within 5 days."]],"m":"We heard from people that the local authority remained in contact whilst they waited for an assessment. Some partners told us they were not consistently informed by social workers when a planned review was being arranged but had received this information through the people they were supporting. Staff recognised that the reviews had a long waiting time and explained that managers triaged and risk rated those waiting. Whilst many carers felt listened to, some reported receiving no support or irrel"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[67,"The local authority advised that due to information sharing and data protection it was not possible to provide updates to partners in some instances."],[67,"The local authority recognised that there were high numbers of referrals that did not result in a section 42 enquiry."],[73,"However, people who lacked capacity were not always adequately supported through safeguarding procedures, meaning the best interests of the person concerned may not always have been at the centre of decision making."]],"m":"However, partners also told us updates on safeguarding referrals raised were not always provided. The local authority advised that due to information sharing and data protection it was not possible to provide updates to partners in some instances. The local authority recognised that there were high numbers of referrals that did not result in a section 42 enquiry. The local authority told us there had been concerns about learning on SARs and system learning, which led to an internal audit which i"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Isles of Scilly":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[22,"Staff used hardship funds after the Covid-19 pandemic to reach more people, including those who didn’t typically engage with formal services."],[22,"However, the local authority acknowledged gaps in fully understanding inequalities and said they needed to improve analysis and collaboration with public health teams."],[22,"While data on age, deprivation, and health was available, it wasn’t clear how these insights were used to shape strategy or measure impact. For example, the local authority’s selfassessment identified the need to bridge inequalities more effectively, and staff said improving access to specialist services, especially on smaller islands, was still a challenge."]],"m":"Staff used hardship funds after the Covid-19 pandemic to reach more people, including those who didn’t typically engage with formal services. However, the local authority acknowledged gaps in fully understanding inequalities and said they needed to improve analysis and collaboration with public health teams. While data on age, deprivation, and health was available, it wasn’t clear how these insights were used to shape strategy or measure impact. Staff said where communication needs were greater,"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[51,"Leaders said there was some limited capacity and system strain due to ongoing change, however."],[53,"People said they saw evidence of planning in action through targeted initiatives, like renovations at Park House, the carer support offer, and improvements to transport and technology-enabled care."],[53,"However, some carers said they were contacted only sporadically or lacked consistent engagement, suggesting that feedback mechanisms were not yet fully embedded."]],"m":"Leaders said there was some limited capacity and system strain due to ongoing change, however. However, some carers said they were contacted only sporadically or lacked consistent engagement, suggesting that feedback mechanisms were not yet fully embedded."},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Coventry":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[34,"A staff team told us about recent research involving people from Black, Asian and Mixed ethnicity communities who were engaged to understand barriers to accessing ASC, but the impact of this was not yet evident."],[37,"Staff told us they identified communication and cultural needs during assessments and arranged appropriate support for people who could not speak English. These included visual tools and face to face visits, enabling people with reduced literacy to share their views."],[39,"Additionally, the local authority ensured non-digital routes remained available such as leaflets, in person events and telephone support, recognising increasing digital options could impose a risk to people with limited digital confidence."]],"m":"A partner highlighted, however, migrant communities could still feel underserved, especially where English was not their first language. A staff team told us about recent research involving people from Black, Asian and Mixed ethnicity communities who were engaged to understand barriers to accessing ASC, but the impact of this was not yet evident. The local authority was developing its understanding of barriers to equitable experiences of ASC but the impact of this work was not yet embedded. Staf"},{"q":[[44,"The local authority had capacity to support people to access residential and homecare services, but some services, including services for independent living and specialist services for people with higher or multiple associated needs were not always available."],[45,"Information provided by the local authority stated residential and nursing care was accessible within 2 days but could be longer for where a specific service had been identified by the person or their family and it did not have availability."],[47,"The Market Position Statement also outlined the need to address gaps in local provision to reduce out of city placements which were not people’s choice."]],"m":"The local authority had capacity to support people to access residential and homecare services, but some services, including services for independent living and specialist services for people with higher or multiple associated needs were not always available. Information provided by the local authority stated residential and nursing care was accessible within 2 days but could be longer for where a specific service had been identified by the person or their family and it did not have availability"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Croydon":[{"q":[[15,"Staff regularly re-assessed people’s risks while they waited to check if their needs had changed. This provided opportunities for signposting to other services, prevention and enablement support."],[15,"Care coordinators worked closely with placement teams to identify appropriate placement or reablement services, and they completed reviews. This indicated the local authority’s processes kept people safe while they waited."],[17,"However, it was recognised that an assessment for an unpaid carer in combination with a Care Act assessment for a person with care needs was not always appropriate. For example, if there were risks of domestic abuse, staff scheduled an unpaid carer assessment separate to the assessment of the person they cared for."]],"m":"Some progress was being made towards reducing the backlog of people waiting for assessments, but many people were still waiting too long, which presented risks to their safety and wellbeing. The local authority had systems to mitigate risks while people waited for assessments. Staff regularly re-assessed people’s risks while they waited to check if their needs had changed. Staff regularly re-assessed people’s risks while they waited to check if their needs had changed. This provided opportunitie"},{"q":[[25,"However, the AEISS team did not have access to non-urgent budgets to arrange shortterm support provision. This impacted their ability to provide preventative support."],[25,"We heard an example of staff working with the Anti-Social Behaviour team and a person over a longer period to reduce their risk of homelessness because they did not meet criteria for support from other teams."],[25,"The Public Health team was developing outcome measures for the local authority’s health and care plan, but this work was relatively new and there was not yet shared agreement of the desired outcomes."]],"m":"However, the AEISS team did not have access to non-urgent budgets to arrange shortterm support provision. However, the AEISS team did not have access to non-urgent budgets to arrange shortterm support provision. This impacted their ability to provide preventative support. We heard an example of staff working with the Anti-Social Behaviour team and a person over a longer period to reduce their risk of homelessness because they did not meet criteria for support from other teams. The Public Health "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[45,"ASC staff worked closely with the housing team to analyse local data, identify the level of housing with care need, and address blockages in the system. This fed into the local authority’s strategic housing review, which had identified insufficient capacity in step down beds."],[46,"There was insufficient provision of community mental health services, support for autistic people and support for people with challenging behavioural needs."],[47,"Whilst almost of a quarter of people were placed outside of Croydon, in the 12 months up to our assessment 137 new placements (8%) were not local, indicating the rate of people placed outside the borough was reducing."]],"m":"ASC staff worked closely with the housing team to analyse local data, identify the level of housing with care need, and address blockages in the system. This fed into the local authority’s strategic housing review, which had identified insufficient capacity in step down beds. Staff gave examples of ending contracts with providers that did not meet people’s needs or align with local authority priorities. There was insufficient provision of community mental health services, support for autistic pe"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[65,"However, some people and unpaid carers reported a general sense that support was lacking, not having a named contact, generic assessments not tailored to people’s needs, and limited access to information. This highlighted inconsistencies in people’s experiences."],[66,"The team did not hold cases but would provide continuity of support at weekends."],[71,"The commissioning team demonstrated a recent example of home care provider failure and the actions taken to support people and source alternative providers, which was managed well with limited disruption to people’s care."]],"m":"However, some people and unpaid carers reported a general sense that support was lacking, not having a named contact, generic assessments not tailored to people’s needs, and limited access to information. However, some people and unpaid carers reported a general sense that support was lacking, not having a named contact, generic assessments not tailored to people’s needs, and limited access to information. This highlighted inconsistencies in people’s experiences. The team did not hold cases but "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Cumberland":[{"q":[[10,"Referrals for assessments were made through the Single Point of Access (SPA) via telephone, self-referral forms and referrals from partners. It had been recognised there was a lack of self-assessment options and there was ongoing development of self-assessment toolkits which were planned to reduce waits for assessments."],[12,"As of June 2025, 811 people were waiting for a review, with a median wait of 210 days and maximum wait of 1675 days over the last 12 months. This included 51 people who were overdue by over 1000 days."],[16,"While there had been sessions for carers organised by the local authority, such as an OT session on falls prevention, this was not a regular offer."]],"m":"Referrals for assessments were made through the Single Point of Access (SPA) via telephone, self-referral forms and referrals from partners. It had been recognised there was a lack of self-assessment options and there was ongoing development of self-assessment toolkits which were planned to reduce waits for assessments. However, some partners reflected that this approach was inconsistent and there was an opportunity to make assessments more holistic and empowering. People did not always have acc"},{"q":[[27,"The reablement service did not include OTs, with a leader telling us the service was not therapy-led."],[30,"The local authority had acted to reduce delays to OT assessments and equipment. For example, the local authority was utilising mobile and static clinics."],[35,"Direct payment systems and oversight were included in the local authority’s Transformation and Service Improvement Plan 2024-2026, but this was in its early phases, and it was not yet clear what a new model might look like."]],"m":"The reablement service did not include OTs, with a leader telling us the service was not therapy-led. A senior leader also told us there were plans for a fully integrated therapy-led reablement and intermediate care service. They told us OT vacancies were mitigated using health and social care OT colleagues and they felt the current structure was not problematic. People faced delays to OT assessments and delivery of equipment. A leader told us there had been problems in getting people the equipm"},{"q":[[39,"The local authority had examples of engagement with communities across Cumberland to help better understand inequalities and barriers, but evidence of impact of these approaches were not yet embedded. For example, in a localised approach, there were community panels across Cumberland to gather local peoples’ voice for their distinct areas."],[39,"Action was being taken to reduce long waits for reviews for people with learning disabilities and an update from the local authority confirmed the longest waiting outstanding reviews had been completed by the end of July 2025."],[42,"There were, however, systems to support people who were visually impaired, such as through larger print documentation or braille. This was not always consistently applied, with a person telling us, for example, they had not received a copy of their assessment in larger font despite it being identified as an accessibility need on their assessment."]],"m":"The local authority had examples of engagement with communities across Cumberland to help better understand inequalities and barriers, but evidence of impact of these approaches were not yet embedded. Action was being taken to reduce long waits for reviews for people with learning disabilities and an update from the local authority confirmed the longest waiting outstanding reviews had been completed by the end of July 2025. The local authority had inclusion and accessibility arrangements in plac"},{"q":[[50,"Data provided by the local authority for February 2025 stated the average wait time for homecare to commence was 7.6 days, which showed a more limited impact on people waiting."],[51,"However, the Market Position Statement outlined learning disability placements, mental health placements and dementia nursing placements were limited in areas of Cumberland. This could impact on people’s choice of service, with a relative of a person telling us their loved one was placed a distance away as there was no local provision and they wanted them closer to them."],[51,"The local authority had provision of day opportunities, with both in-house and independent sector services, but there were gaps in this area."]],"m":"A partner also told us they had been asked by the local authority to support information gathering from people about gaps in local services and what they felt was needed before services were set up. A partner also told us they had been asked by the local authority to support information gathering from people about gaps in local services and what they felt was needed before services were set up. This aligned clearly to the local authority’s direction in including stakeholder and people’s voice to"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[69,"A leader also told us support for staff in this area was more informal. This meeting was for internal staff and was not attended by key partner agencies, but the local authority highlighted this was used as a complementary tool for staff alongside statutory safeguarding processes which included a multi-agency approach."],[72,"Some specific pathways for hospital discharge were not always clear. For example, the local authority often worked with a different health trust for people living in the South of the Cumberland area, in Millom."],[72,"Partners told us they had worked with the local authority to improve discharge processes in this area, but staff teams said there still could be some confusion with this pathway. They told us, for example, there were occasions where care was being sourced for people on discharge without the local authority being aware and so this was not recorded on local authority systems."]],"m":"A leader also told us support for staff in this area was more informal. This meeting was for internal staff and was not attended by key partner agencies, but the local authority highlighted this was used as a complementary tool for staff alongside statutory safeguarding processes which included a multi-agency approach. Some specific pathways for hospital discharge were not always clear. Partners told us they had worked with the local authority to improve discharge processes in this area, but sta"},{"q":[[79,"Although the strategy did link to a self-neglect practice tool and the detailed SAB multiagency guidance, the strategy did not include robust localised guidance including responsibilities, timescales and oversight."],[80,"However, a staff team felt there was still a gap in training around self-neglect and drug and alcohol use."],[82,"The audit included a dedicated section on safeguarding, although the local authority did not have a specific safeguarding audit. This meant there had not been specific focus on safeguarding work and quality assuring practice across teams."]],"m":"Although the strategy did link to a self-neglect practice tool and the detailed SAB multiagency guidance, the strategy did not include robust localised guidance including responsibilities, timescales and oversight. The lack of localised guidance increased the risk of an inconsistent approach and there was scope for the local authority to review this further, following the more recent SARs. Further work to look at challenges in relation to self-neglect had also been identified in the bespoke safe"},{"q":[[92,"While there was clear strategic oversight of risks, there was opportunity to focus resources to further support key service areas. For example, there was ongoing work to reduce waits for assessments, reviews, OT assessments, equipment and DoLS authorisations but waits were still significant for people."],[92,"Some staff teams told us it was difficult to manage their workloads, especially with increased complexity. They also told us there were a lack of experienced or advanced practitioner staff embedded within some teams, who they would usually access for case support."],[92,"There was a separate advanced practice lead (APL) team, which could be contacted for support, but this expertise was not embedded within teams."]],"m":"While it was not yet clear on the impact of this work, it demonstrated a direction for ASC. Some staff teams told us it was difficult to manage their workloads, especially with increased complexity. They also told us there were a lack of experienced or advanced practitioner staff embedded within some teams, who they would usually access for case support. There was a separate advanced practice lead (APL) team, which could be contacted for support, but this expertise was not embedded within teams."},{"q":[[97,"The local authority sought to learn from feedback from partners to learn and improve practice, but some partners felt feedback was not always acted upon."],[97,"While some of these changes were not yet embedded, it showed the local authority’s openness to learn from challenge and external resource."],[98,"However, there had been a low number of upheld or partially upheld complaints which meant identification of practice themes was limited."]],"m":"The local authority sought to learn from feedback from partners to learn and improve practice, but some partners felt feedback was not always acted upon. However, some other partners told us although they felt heard, they felt feedback was not always acted upon as issues re-occurred. While some of these changes were not yet embedded, it showed the local authority’s openness to learn from challenge and external resource. However, there had been a low number of upheld or partially upheld complaint"}],"Derby":[{"q":[[9,"Staff were unclear about the application of the strength-based approach, some struggled to provide the details of application and did not always have the knowledge of the key principles or knowledge of the tools used."],[11,"Data shows that there were 1454 overdue reviews equating to 42.11% of total cases."],[11,"There was a waiting list of 6-8 months to be assigned for ongoing visual impairment rehabilitation after initial screening. This was due to lack of capacity."]],"m":"Staff were unclear about the application of the strength-based approach, some struggled to provide the details of application and did not always have the knowledge of the key principles or knowledge of the tools used. Providers felt that a person-centered approach was not always evident to promote a person's wellbeing. Others told us they didn’t always feel involved in the assessment process as the accommodation was selected because the other providers were unable to meet their support needs, th"},{"q":[[19,"However, some staff told us that there was a gap in analysis which would support the local authority to ensure an agenda was developed to work with the community to ensure the right service provision was in place."],[19,"Carers told us there had been a lack of support identified for them, illustrating those preventative measures such as respite care was often full, this meant that many carers felt that a lack of timely support affected their mental health."],[22,"Staff recognised that information wasn’t always readily accessible for people of different languages, including printed and translation services. It was also recognised that more accessible information was also needed for the deaf community, this was an area which they were currently working to improve."]],"m":"There were evident gaps in some areas of provision, demonstrating the need for a consistent approach across specific areas. However, some staff told us that there was a gap in analysis which would support the local authority to ensure an agenda was developed to work with the community to ensure the right service provision was in place. Carers told us there had been a lack of support identified for them, illustrating those preventative measures such as respite care was often full, this meant that"},{"q":[[25,"However, despite this, concerns were raised by partners about the lack of representation across partnership Boards specifically the carers group and the learning disability partnership Board."],[26,"Partners felt that meeting the needs of deaf impaired people is a recognised challenge throughout Derby. This was having an adverse effect on the deaf community as it meant that there were a number of services which were not accessible."],[26,"The capacity of assessment beds meant that people with autism and the Deaf community were often excluded due to lack of service provision which matched their needs. This was also an issue for people who were partially sighted who experienced barriers of access to services."]],"m":"However, despite this, concerns were raised by partners about the lack of representation across partnership Boards specifically the carers group and the learning disability partnership Board. Both providers and staff identified that there were gaps in relation to the provision for the established deaf community. Both providers and staff identified that there were gaps in relation to the provision for the established deaf community. It was unclear whether targeted work specific to adult social ca"},{"q":[[28,"There were noted disparities in health inequalities across minority groups affecting access to services."],[28,"There was an acknowledged gap in provision in the deaf community and for those living with dementia, where the authority was unable to provide local services and placements, including limited respite care, due to a lack of sufficiency to meet people’s needs with the rising demand."],[28,"We were told that focusing on the gaps in provision was a priority for commissioners who were working with the market to overcome this. It is unclear how long there has been acknowledged gaps in service provision."]],"m":"There were noted disparities in health inequalities across minority groups affecting access to services. There was an acknowledged gap in provision in the deaf community and for those living with dementia, where the authority was unable to provide local services and placements, including limited respite care, due to a lack of sufficiency to meet people’s needs with the rising demand. We were told that focusing on the gaps in provision was a priority for commissioners who were working with the ma"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[39,"Staff told us that the preparing for adulthood team had been able to manage any negative impact on waiting lists due to their early access to preparing for adulthood team working with young people from the age of 14."],[39,"Partners told us that the transition from children’s to adult service provision was not always a smooth process as services for young people are more bespoke than adults service provision. It was felt that some people at the age of 18 had no support in place, for others an assessment had been completed, however, their needs were not met."],[40,"Providers felt the local authority did not always work with them to ensure people received coordinated safe support when moving between different services."]],"m":"Partners told us that the transition from children’s to adult service provision was not always a smooth process as services for young people are more bespoke than adults service provision. Partners told us that the transition from children’s to adult service provision was not always a smooth process as services for young people are more bespoke than adults service provision. It was felt that some people at the age of 18 had no support in place, for others an assessment had been completed, howeve"},{"q":[[41,"There had been a recent successful focus on reducing the backlog, this had been achieved by using staff to support with the workload as well as reviewing a number of areas of the process."],[44,"The local authority had identified they had a high backlog in the number of DoLS applications awaiting assessment. This stood at over 600 in the second half of 2023."],[44,"Partners told us that further work was needed which specifically focused on working with seldom heard groups within the community to understand the rationale behind lack of referrals coming in from these groups."]],"m":"Safeguarding remains a challenging area for the local authority with evident high backlogs in managing both safeguarding enquiries and DoLS assessments. A large backlog of referrals had accumulated historically before action was taken. A large backlog of referrals had accumulated historically before action was taken. As a result of the action the backlog had been reduced prior to our assessment. Safeguarding was seen to be a priority for the authority with a notable commitment from all levels of"},{"q":[[46,"The local authority had experienced a recent change to the political leadership of the council, which has not impacted the delivery of services or strategic priorities for adult social care."],[46,"The new section 75 around hospital discharge was a positive example of a robust governance structure which oversaw all activities provided by the NHS Trust on behalf of the local authority. This included Scrutiny Boards, contractual assessment meetings, key performance indicators (KPI), and daily discussions between departments."],[47,"There was a lack of clarity in the principal social worker’s role specially around a functional plan of prioritisation."]],"m":"There was a lack of clarity in the principal social worker’s role specially around a functional plan of prioritisation."},{"q":[[49,"Staff were unclear on how the learning from SARs were shared to support and improve practice in front line teams."],[50,"Staff told us there was limited specialist training for the deaf services team, with them receiving generic training."],[50,"The local authority acknowledged that more work was needed around co-production, there were pockets of co-produced work, but this had not been embedded throughout the organisation. This had meant peoples experiences were not consistently being used to shape service provision."]],"m":"Staff told us that there was no principal OT, with no plans to create the role. Staff told us that there was no principal OT, with no plans to create the role. They felt this left no one to drive improvement and innovation in practice in occupational therapy. Partners identified gaps in safeguarding, specifically around the lack of monitoring of whether learning from safeguarding concerns had been effective to drive improvement. Staff were unclear on how the learning from SARs were shared to sup"}],"Derbyshire":[{"q":[[13,"A tiered-approval panel arrangement was in place to consider extra funding if there were concerns that the package of care did not fully meet the needs of the person."],[13,"There was a backlog in financial assessments which impacted payments to services."],[13,"We also heard some families and carers didn’t understand the reason for increases in charges, for example when a family member was moving into a nursing home."]],"m":"One person told us they were able to be discharged from hospital and return home following the support they were given, whereas others said reviews were not regular. Some partners reported reviews and assessments were not easy to plan or request and levels of involvement with partners was dependent on the social worker. There was also consistent feedback from carers there were not sufficient options or resources for them to access respite or breaks. A tiered-approval panel arrangement was in pla"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[21,"Leaders, staff and partners mainly described addressing equity in terms of service delivery and digital-equality for the rural population and the deaf community, with very little description about other groups that could be affected."],[22,"Derbyshire local authority had not always communicated and coproduced with groups effectively and some seldom-heard groups reported the relationship with social workers could be improved."],[22,"A workstream for each of the priorities had been established, however progress on this was not clear. It was positive to see neuro-development hubs had been provided in the county to support autistic people."]],"m":"Derbyshire local authority had not always communicated and coproduced with groups effectively and some seldom-heard groups reported the relationship with social workers could be improved. A workstream for each of the priorities had been established, however progress on this was not clear. A workstream for each of the priorities had been established, however progress on this was not clear. It was positive to see neuro-development hubs had been provided in the county to support autistic people."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[44,"However, some staff reported mental capacity training was not always available and at times the process was confusing."],[45,"Demand for safeguarding was high, we were told there were many repeat referrals due to poor quality referrals and lack of knowledge from referrers."],[46,"We heard reference to local approaches by local team practitioners which sometimes led to an inconsistent approach across the county."]],"m":"However, some staff reported mental capacity training was not always available and at times the process was confusing. Demand for safeguarding was high, we were told there were many repeat referrals due to poor quality referrals and lack of knowledge from referrers. The risk of impacts from delays in completion of DoLS, was included on the adult social care risk register. The outcomes of safeguarding enquiries were not routinely received by providers, and they described ‘chasing’ outcomes. We he"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Devon":[{"q":[[9,"However, feedback from some people we spoke to highlighted that the self-help tool could present barriers for people who needed more nuanced options than yes/no answers, which may have limited accessibility for some."],[9,"Although strengths-based, person-centred practice was embedded across services, people continued to experience long waits for assessments and reviews. This meant that, despite positive progress, the approach was not yet consistently delivering timely support for everyone."],[10,"However, feedback from partners and complaints highlighted gaps in considering equality and diversity, such as assumptions about cultural needs and inconsistent application of the Mental Capacity Act."]],"m":"However, feedback from some people we spoke to highlighted that the self-help tool could present barriers for people who needed more nuanced options than yes/no answers, which may have limited accessibility for some. Although strengths-based, person-centred practice was embedded across services, people continued to experience long waits for assessments and reviews. Although strengths-based, person-centred practice was embedded across services, people continued to experience long waits for assess"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[36,"The local authority also maintained a housing website with an A–Z guide, ensuring staff and people could access consistent information. This improved navigation and understanding of housing options across districts, supporting informed decisions and reducing delays in finding suitable homes."],[40,"Hospital discharge delays were also linked to lack of service availability and capacity. To address this, the local authority launched a Rapid Support Service in July 2025 to provide short-term reablement support for up to six weeks for people discharged from hospital."],[43,"When a provider faced financial difficulty because the local authority was not paying the correct amount, the team intervened, resolved care planning, and backdated payments. This proactive approach prevented service failure and ensured people continued receiving care without interruption."]],"m":"The local authority also maintained a housing website with an A–Z guide, ensuring staff and people could access consistent information. This improved navigation and understanding of housing options across districts, supporting informed decisions and reducing delays in finding suitable homes. Where arrangements could not be made promptly, the local authority assessed risks and put short‑term contingencies in place to ensure people’s needs were met until permanent support could be sourced. In addi"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[54,"In some hospitals, social care staff were not involved early enough in discharge planning. For example, one team only became involved after discharge, which delayed people’s return home because tasks such as arranging deep cleans were not completed in time."],[56,"The local authority developed a protocol for person-led care planning, including escalation mechanisms to the 16–17 Transitions Forum where ownership of actions was unclear."],[57,"Volunteer initiatives such as Neighbourhood Friends supported equipment delivery to prevent hospital admissions or delays in discharge, helping people stay safe and recover at home."]],"m":"In some hospitals, social care staff were not involved early enough in discharge planning. The local authority developed a protocol for person-led care planning, including escalation mechanisms to the 16–17 Transitions Forum where ownership of actions was unclear. Volunteer initiatives such as Neighbourhood Friends supported equipment delivery to prevent hospital admissions or delays in discharge, helping people stay safe and recover at home. Funding decisions or disputes with other agencies did"},{"q":[[65,"Previously, three separate teams applied safeguarding criteria inconsistently, which created delays and uncertainty."],[66,"Delays in DoLS processes persisted throughout the same period."],[66,"In February 2025 local authority data still showed significant backlogs in safeguarding and DoLS, prompting the service to request additional investment from the council."]],"m":"Previously, three separate teams applied safeguarding criteria inconsistently, which created delays and uncertainty. Delays in DoLS processes persisted throughout the same period. Although improvements were reducing the time people waited, these delays meant individuals could remain in restrictive care arrangements without timely legal authorisation, which affected their rights and wellbeing. In February 2025 local authority data still showed significant backlogs in safeguarding and DoLS, prompt"},{"q":[[70,"The local authority had effective governance structures in place, but this assessment identified improvements to assessment waiting times and safeguarding were not yet fully embedded."],[70,"Technology Enabled Care was identified as requiring improvement following an internal audit that provided limited assurance due to the absence of a published plan."],[73,"An internal audit in 2024 highlighted limited assurance in this area, indicating the need for a published plan and clearer implementation framework which were being progressed at the time of this assessment."]],"m":"The local authority had effective governance structures in place, but this assessment identified improvements to assessment waiting times and safeguarding were not yet fully embedded. Technology Enabled Care was identified as requiring improvement following an internal audit that provided limited assurance due to the absence of a published plan. The commissioning model for technology-enabled care was revised to support ongoing provision, following identification of gaps in planning. An internal "},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Dorset":[{"q":[[18,"The local authority did not currently have any appeals relating to Care Act assessments but there was an appeals process in place."],[19,"Of these, 49 were not upheld, 13 were partially upheld and 8 were upheld, demonstrating that clear processes were in place for reviewing decisions."],[20,"Staff provided examples where advocacy, translation services, and wider voluntary sector support were brought together for people with significant communication barriers, complex needs, or limited informal support, ensuring they were able to participate fully and receive coordinated help."]],"m":"However, some people experienced inconsistencies, including instances where written plans did not reflect the support agreed during assessment, which created anxiety and uncertainty. Many providers described effective communication and involvement, but others reported limited consultation during assessments or felt reviews resembled paper exercises rather than meaningful engagement. Some providers of older people’s services reported that assessments for people with complex needs lacked sufficien"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[34,"The local authority showed awareness of the unique challenges posed by the rurality of the county, and its population. This understanding had been enhanced by a commitment to wide-ranging coproduction including examples of effective engagement with local diverse communities such as the Gypsy, Roma, Traveller, farming and military communities."],[34,"Access to transport in rural areas and lack of availability of services in these communities were repeatedly cited as barriers to accessing care and support, creating pockets of isolation, and limiting opportunities for social connection."],[37,"Appropriate inclusion and accessibility arrangements were in place to ensure individuals could engage in ways that suited their communication needs. This included access to British Sign Language (BSL) interpretation, enabling deaf or hard-of-hearing people to videocall council staff via a BSL interpreter."]],"m":"Access to transport in rural areas and lack of availability of services in these communities were repeatedly cited as barriers to accessing care and support, creating pockets of isolation, and limiting opportunities for social connection."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[65,"The local authority had seen an increase in the number of safeguarding referrals it received, from 70 per week to 180 per week over the 5 years to the end of 2024. As a result, a waiting list had been implemented but the continued rise to 239 by September 2024 had raised significant concern over the level of both known and unknown risk and that practice was not sustainable."],[66,"The local authority told us it did not currently have a direct phone line as demand was so high it detracted from the team's ability to respond to concerns."],[70,"Staff told us if a person had substantial difficulty being involved and lacked a suitable person to support them, the local authority would provide a Care Act Advocate or an Independent Mental Capacity Advocate (IMCA) to ensure their voice was heard."]],"m":"The local authority had seen an increase in the number of safeguarding referrals it received, from 70 per week to 180 per week over the 5 years to the end of 2024. As a result, a waiting list had been implemented but the continued rise to 239 by September 2024 had raised significant concern over the level of both known and unknown risk and that practice was not sustainable. The local authority told us it did not currently have a direct phone line as demand was so high it detracted from the team'"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Dudley":[{"q":[[12,"Data provided by the local authority showed there were 1123 people waiting for a review in July 2025, with a median of 160 days overdue and a maximum of 899 days overdue."],[11,"Data provided by the local authority showed in July 2025 there were 106 people waiting for a Care Act assessment with a median wait time of 61 days and the longest wait being 182 days."],[15,"For those unable to attend the hub, there were alternative ways to be supported through home visits and telephone calls for information to be shared."]],"m":"An example was unpaid carers who often did not consider themselves as carers or recognise the extent of the support they provided. Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely and up to date. Others told us that in some cases, people experienced long waits before being assessed which meant people were having longer periods of time without any social care or support. Data provided by the local authority showed there were 1123 people waiting for a review in July 2025, with a me"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[40,"There was sufficient capacity to meet demand in areas such as homecare and older adult residential nursing placements, however, there was a lack of placements for people under 65 within the borough."],[41,"People shared concerns about a lack of mental health support within the borough with a lack of specialised services for specific needs. For example, hoarding was identified as an area where support was lacking, with no dedicated services available to provide tailored intervention or long-term support."],[41,"Staff had worked closely with the voluntary sector particularly around vulnerable people who were not eligible under the Care Act, however there was an urgent need to support those who were alcohol dependent and at risk of homelessness."]],"m":"The Dudley All Age Mental Health Needs Assessment Final Report 2025 and the Market Sustainability Plan told us there were challenges and gaps in provision for adults for example a lack of specialised services for specific needs such as hoarding, unmet needs relating to neurodivergent people and adults with co-occurring substance misuse. There was sufficient capacity to meet demand in areas such as homecare and older adult residential nursing placements, however, there was a lack of placements fo"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[66,"The Think Family approach was a reminder that people did not live in isolation, and so when there were concerns about possible abuse or neglect within a 'family' situation, professionals needed to think about the needs of the whole family."],[68,"The local authority had revised the Multi-Agency Risk Management (MARM) guidance and process, which gave a framework for professionals to work together effectively with adults who had care and support needs and were at risk of harm, who were deemed to have mental capacity for specific decisions that may result in serious harm or death through severe self-neglect, risk taking behaviours and refused or were unable to engage with services."],[70,"Audits had identified the lack of proportionate, robust, risk-based section 42 enquiries. In response to this, MASH facilitated a workshop detailing the expectations of section 42 enquiries to increase consistent and appropriate risk management."]],"m":"The local authority said concerns reported through the Portal were classified as safeguarding upon receipt, however many of those were quickly triaged to other areas of adult social care and 68-75% of concerns did not meet the safeguarding criteria and were redirected. If the person were not open to a worker, the safeguarding concern would be screened by the MASH who used a RAG (Red, Amber, Green) system to review and process safeguarding concerns. The Think Family approach was a reminder that p"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Durham":[{"q":[[13,"The local authority had taken further action, due to start after our assessment, to complete overdue reviews from internal resource and an external agency, aiming to clear outstanding reviews by March 2025."],[13,"Home care packages were monitored for any delays in start date, with only one person so far in 2024 waiting for at least one day."],[15,"Some carers were unaware of support groups and forums. This was reflected in national data where 16.77% of carers were accessing a support group or someone to talk to in confidence in County Durham, which was much lower than the England average of 32.37% (Survey of Adult Carers, published June 2022)."]],"m":"Staff were encouraged to disseminate their learning with colleagues who were unable to attend. However, we did not see much reflection of how an individuals’ care assessments, plans or reviews understood their protected characteristics. Some carers provided care themselves because they did not feel the care and support required could be provided by the local authority. Some staff felt they did not have enough time to fully understand and plan for the complexity of people’s needs, especially when"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[29,"There were some examples of where the local authority had responded to information that indicated people were not experiencing the same quality of outcomes. For example, the implementation of the Enable service, which had been successfully supporting increasing number of people with a learning disability into employment."],[29,"There was limited understanding or focus on communities of interest and where people’s identity characteristics intersected. For example, we heard that older people who were also LGBT+ did not have their needs met, especially those in more rural areas."],[31,"One partner we spoke with was pleased the local authority had retained phone lines and customer services points to facilitate appropriate access for people who found the website difficult to navigate or who were unable to access it."]],"m":"There were some examples of where the local authority had responded to information that indicated people were not experiencing the same quality of outcomes. Other characteristics of people’s experiences were not always used to understand themes and trends on a deeper level to target work with communities. There was limited understanding or focus on communities of interest and where people’s identity characteristics intersected. There was a lack of data collected systemically across all services "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[55,"Frontline staff could not always describe learning from serious incidents or Safeguarding Adults Reviews, outside of where they had been specifically involved in an enquiry or investigation."],[56,"The team often had to describe their roles because Adult Protection was not always a well understood or modern term to describe their function."],[57,"When safeguarding enquiries were conducted by another agency, for example a care or health provider, it was not clear how the local authority assured the robustness or quality of planning or outcomes for the person involved in line with their Care Act duties."]],"m":"Some staff described a two-tier system, where some people received a high standard of safeguarding enquiry, and others didn’t due to the range of staff skills and experience. Frontline staff could not always describe learning from serious incidents or Safeguarding Adults Reviews, outside of where they had been specifically involved in an enquiry or investigation. We heard from senior leaders that safeguarding enquiries were not always dealt with in a timely way following allocation and these had"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Darlington":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[23,"The local authority had some data on equality issues and demographics but did not use data to plan services around inequalities in experiences or identify people at risk of having unmet needs, systematically."],[23,"The Joint Strategic Needs Assessment (JSNA) showed the life expectancy gap in the borough had worsened in recent years."],[25,"Some people said it was not easily accessible and there were limited options in different formats such as British Sign Language (BSL) or Talking Mats."]],"m":"The local authority had some data on equality issues and demographics but did not use data to plan services around inequalities in experiences or identify people at risk of having unmet needs, systematically. The Joint Strategic Needs Assessment (JSNA) showed the life expectancy gap in the borough had worsened in recent years. Some people said it was not easily accessible and there were limited options in different formats such as British Sign Language (BSL) or Talking Mats."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Gateshead":[{"q":[[12,"The team was not integrated with the health Trust under a formal arrangement, but staff worked closely with health partners in areas such as discharge from hospital or providing support to people in the community."],[19,"Staff said there were some areas where they felt there were gaps in community provision for people with non-eligible needs, such as mental health. Despite this, staff told us about multiple examples where they had worked creatively to meet non-eligible need."],[20,"From April to December 2023 there had been 14 complaints related to charging and the local authority had noted a theme that people who had complained were not aware the services put in place were chargeable. In response the local authority introduced a new charging leaflet in September 2023 and carried out work to improve understanding amongst staff."]],"m":"The team was not integrated with the health Trust under a formal arrangement, but staff worked closely with health partners in areas such as discharge from hospital or providing support to people in the community. The local authority had been undertaking improvement work to reduce their waiting lists and waiting times for assessment which had started to address the shortfalls we heard about. There were sometimes delays to home adaptations, but access to equipment and minor adaptations was usuall"},{"q":[[24,"We heard feedback from staff and partners that there were sometimes gaps in community provision for low-level support for people with mental health needs."],[27,"Staff described how OT cases were allocated in a risk-based way and people who were waiting for adaptations would usually have other interventions in place to keep them safe whilst they awaited a full assessment and adaptation works."],[27,"We also heard that sometimes people did not know where to go for information about what was available to them."]],"m":"We heard feedback from staff and partners that there were sometimes gaps in community provision for low-level support for people with mental health needs. Staff described how OT cases were allocated in a risk-based way and people who were waiting for adaptations would usually have other interventions in place to keep them safe whilst they awaited a full assessment and adaptation works. We also heard that sometimes people did not know where to go for information about what was available to them."},{"q":[[31,"The new systems had been in place since January 2024 but had not yet become embedded in practice, so examples of changes they had informed was limited."],[31,"The local authority was reaching and engaging with groups that sat below high-level demographic data, but this had not yet led to development of specialist provision. For example, the JSNA highlighted that the local authority was home to a large orthodox Jewish population that made up a minority of the overall demographics."],[33,"Another person also told us they had said on their initial contact with the local authority that they would require a BSL interpreter, but the staff who contacted them about their assessment did not know this. Despite this feedback from partners and people, staff described good access to BSL interpreters in locality teams and at the front door."]],"m":"The new systems had been in place since January 2024 but had not yet become embedded in practice, so examples of changes they had informed was limited. The local authority was reaching and engaging with groups that sat below high-level demographic data, but this had not yet led to development of specialist provision. However, we also heard from partners and staff how there was a lack of specialist provision to meet their needs which meant people sometimes had to move out of borough to have their"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Gloucestershire":[{"q":[[17,"Local authority data for June 2024 showed 12.5% of cases in the two city localities of Cheltenham and Gloucester had waited 100 days or more."],[17,"Data also showed that by September 2024 no person waited longer than 3 months, including within the two localities which had faced longer wait times previously."],[22,"People’s feedback about provision to meet non-eligible needs was mixed, we heard about good links with community partners and voluntary groups that meant people were able to access support where their needs were not eligible under the Care Act 2014."]],"m":"However, we heard that sometimes people who had received an assessment were not aware they had been assessed and people told us they did not always feel they had been given choices when developing their care plans. Care planning was carried out in a way that involved the person, but we heard feedback there was sometimes limited choice available to people. Staff said this usually worked well but we heard feedback from staff that there were sometimes delays in finding care for people in certain di"},{"q":[[27,"People and partners told us there were sometimes gaps in this, which the local authority had identified and were addressing through their strategy."],[31,"Data to monitor the performance of the OT contract was limited and was another area the local authority was working to improve as part of their data strategy."],[33,"We heard feedback from unpaid carers that they thought they were not entitled to support because of having assets or income above the threshold at which they would be expected to fund their own care, despite the local authority’s fairer contributions policy stating unpaid carers’ services were exempt from charging."]],"m":"People and partners told us there were sometimes gaps in this, which the local authority had identified and were addressing through their strategy. Unpaid carers gave mostly positive feedback on the support available to them in the community through the commissioned carers hub, but most of the unpaid carers we spoke with told us they felt unable to continue with hobbies or interests. People and partners said that in some districts, community access could be an issue, because of a lack of transpo"},{"q":[[36,"One partner said there were minority ethnic communities they worked with who would be fearful of being contacted by a social worker. They were not aware of any work to reach out to these communities to understand their concerns about accessing services."],[37,"The local authority acknowledged that improvements to co-production work was an area of recent focus and some of the impacts of this work had not yet been realised."],[40,"There were gaps in the local authority’s understanding of people’s experiences through data and engagement which meant the local authority’s ability to measure how much progress had been made against the public sector equality duty (PSED) under the Equality Act 2010 was limited."]],"m":"One partner said there were minority ethnic communities they worked with who would be fearful of being contacted by a social worker. They were not aware of any work to reach out to these communities to understand their concerns about accessing services. Another community partner said people from minority backgrounds had been asked to attend repeated co-production sessions but did not hear any feedback about what had been done with the information they had shared at previous meetings. The local a"},{"q":[[45,"Providers spoke positively about the work the local authority had done to involve them in strategic planning through co-production and engagement, but we heard that concerns they raised were not always acted upon by the local authority and some mistrust had developed."],[45,"The 2024 MPS outlined feedback from providers and presented these issues openly, but the feedback we received from providers showed they did not yet feel like issues they raised with the local authority would always be addressed."],[46,"We heard some people were provided with choices when planning their care, but we also heard from unpaid carers and partners that provision for unpaid carers and from community services was sometimes limited."]],"m":"The local authority recognised where there were gaps in available data and their understanding. The MPS also described a lack of information about people who funded their care privately, which limited the local authority’s understanding of the demand for community care. The local authority carried out impact reports to understand gaps in the community and voluntary sector so they could target grant funding to these areas. The local authority carried out impact reports to understand gaps in the c"},{"q":[[54,"We heard positive feedback about working arrangements and relationships in integrated teams, but there were gaps in oversight of data and understanding performance."],[54,"However, there were gaps in strategic oversight of these agreements and this meant impacts on people in areas such as waiting list were not addressed promptly."],[56,"In other areas we heard about work having started, but the impacts not yet fully realised. For example, the integrated approach to workforce development to aid better recruitment and retention had led to gradual improvements in workforce within the local authority, but there was a lack of data to show its impact on the wider system."]],"m":"We heard positive feedback about working arrangements and relationships in integrated teams, but there were gaps in oversight of data and understanding performance. Recording systems were often incompatible and did not share data easily between partners. However, there were gaps in strategic oversight of these agreements and this meant impacts on people in areas such as waiting list were not addressed promptly. Our findings showed that use of the BCF had not yet achieved its aim around increasin"},{"q":[[59,"However, gaps in data and provision meant oversight of safety was inconsistent."],[59,"We also heard from staff and leaders how lack of provision could sometimes cause people to be readmitted to hospital shortly after discharge and the measures being taken to address this through improving community provision had not yet been fully implemented."],[62,"Partners said there were sometimes challenges in finding the right provision where people required residential care for complex needs, such as for people living with dementia. This meant people on these pathways could sometimes face delays to their discharge home."]],"m":"However, gaps in data and provision meant oversight of safety was inconsistent. Staff and leaders felt there was oversight of safety, but they told us they sometimes had to work around barriers presented to them by shortfalls in data. We also heard from staff and leaders how lack of provision could sometimes cause people to be readmitted to hospital shortly after discharge and the measures being taken to address this through improving community provision had not yet been fully implemented. The l"},{"q":[[66,"The new case management system did not currently have the capacity to hold DoLS information so the team worked from a spreadsheet but there were plans to improve this and migrate DoLS to the new system after our assessment."],[66,"However, staff said they did not use data to inform their practice, for example in looking at any themes in the types of abuse or the outcomes of safeguarding for people from any minority groups."],[67,"Local authority data showed concerns moved from the single point of access team to the safeguarding team in between 2 and 4 days, this had come down to below 2 days by June 2024, but this did not show how promptly cases were picked up or their risk levels once they went to the safeguarding team."]],"m":"Whilst this showed some assurance, it was limited and we did not see evidence of a robust analysis by the local authority to understand the potential risk that safeguarding concerns were not being effectively reported or that people at risk of or experiencing abuse and neglect were not always being identified. The document showed there was work undertaken to analyse SAR referrals and learn from cases which were not taken forward as SARs. The new case management system did not currently have the "},{"q":[[70,"However, the local authority’s use of data to understand their performance under Part 1 of the Care Act 2014 was not consistent."],[70,"Performance was scrutinised by lead elected members of the council but we heard from elected members with roles in the opposition and scrutiny functions that they did not receive the same level of insights about performance. This impacted on their ability to fully scrutinise the local authority’s performance in relation to adult social care and their duties under the Care Act 2014."],[71,"Our findings showed there were numerous areas where work had not yet had its desired impact because of improvements to data not yet becoming embedded and teams not routinely using data to inform performance in areas such as safeguarding or monitoring commissioned functions."]],"m":"However, the local authority’s use of data to understand their performance under Part 1 of the Care Act 2014 was not consistent. Performance was scrutinised by lead elected members of the council but we heard from elected members with roles in the opposition and scrutiny functions that they did not receive the same level of insights about performance. Performance was scrutinised by lead elected members of the council but we heard from elected members with roles in the opposition and scrutiny fun"},{"q":[[75,"In other cases, we heard that some minority ethnic groups had not yet been involved in co-production work."],[76,"A 2023 LGA peer challenge review identified areas the local authority was working to improve, such as a need to improve the link between strategic commissioning and operational teams or make better use of data. These were both areas of which were subject to a lot of improvement activity at the time of our assessment but had not yet been fully implemented."],[78,"The local authority told us how the 2021/22 results had picked up on themes around lack of homecare which had informed strategies in these areas."]],"m":"In other cases, we heard that some minority ethnic groups had not yet been involved in co-production work. A 2023 LGA peer challenge review identified areas the local authority was working to improve, such as a need to improve the link between strategic commissioning and operational teams or make better use of data. These were both areas of which were subject to a lot of improvement activity at the time of our assessment but had not yet been fully implemented. The local authority told us how the"}],"East Riding of Yorkshire":[{"q":[[14,"There were also concerns that documents were not always shared with people as they should, limiting people’s ability to effectively understand and contribute to their own assessment, care plan, or review. This had been a theme of complaints which had been acknowledged by relevant staff, including through the Practice Development Board, and additional guidance had been shared with staff to improve this."],[16,"The local authority did not set target timescales for the completion of assessments to track the performance of teams and identify any concerns or deviations that warranted further investigation and improvement."],[16,"Those rated at the lowest level waited approximately 4 weeks for triage, according to some staff. They could then go on to wait longer once the need for further assessment was identified due to allocation delays."]],"m":"However, people’s care and support had not always reflected their right to choice and reviews could be difficult to instigate. Communication with people about the progress of their assessment or review had been limited. The local authority did not have a practice framework in place at the time of our assessment to support a unified and cohesive adult social care system in the local authority. In another example, a carer said their family member was placed out of area and there was no considerati"},{"q":[[25,"There were some services that supported the local authority’s prevention duty but there were shortfalls particularly in the provision of reablement that significantly impacted people’s outcomes."],[25,"The local authority did not have a prevention strategy in place to clearly set out its intentions, objectives, and actions on arrangements to prevent, delay or reduce the needs for care and support in the East Riding."],[28,"Provision of reablement services was limited by significant capacity constraints and limited dedicated provision to deliver reablement care within the wider adult social care market."]],"m":"There were some services that supported the local authority’s prevention duty but there were shortfalls particularly in the provision of reablement that significantly impacted people’s outcomes. The local authority did not have a prevention strategy in place to clearly set out its intentions, objectives, and actions on arrangements to prevent, delay or reduce the needs for care and support in the East Riding. The prevention board was not currently running, having been recognised as needing a ref"},{"q":[[38,"While we heard that another service within the local authority had developed anti-racist practice, this had not been developed more widely or specifically for adult social care and was not in place at the time of our assessment."],[40,"A lack of the voices of people with a wide variety of experiences in co-production reduced the ability of these groups to meet the needs of the breadth of the population."],[40,"The local authority did not have a centralised strategy or outlined published vision for the voluntary and community sector, including grassroots organisations, to support equality and diversity goals."]],"m":"While the local authority undertook some work to understand and identify barriers to care and support, actions to reduce inequalities from recommendations were limited. There were significant shortfalls in the identification and recording of people’s protected characteristics which limited the local authority’s ability to ensure equity in experience and outcomes. While we heard that another service within the local authority had developed anti-racist practice, this had not been developed more wi"},{"q":[[47,"Between March 2025 and the end of May 2025, local authority data indicated 7 people waited for extra care housing for their internally operated services, with an average waiting time of 120 days."],[47,"There were 2 private providers in the local authority area of extra care housing, but the local authority did not have data at the time of our assessment regarding waits for these services."],[49,"There was not sufficient care and support available to meet demand for home care, nursing care or for younger adults with complex needs in East Riding."]],"m":"The document did not include information about the future number of unpaid carers in the area in line with national carer estimates, or further demographic information about carers which would support providers in the area to understand local need. The document did not include information about the future number of unpaid carers in the area in line with national carer estimates, or further demographic information about carers which would support providers in the area to understand local need. Th"},{"q":[[61,"The impact of the previous Dementia Strategy was not outlined in the newly launched version, in favour of a more streamlined approach to clearly state agreed actions, timescales, and objectives."],[61,"In one example, there had been positive work to develop micro-providers in part of the county, but this was separate from adult social care commissioning approaches, meaning a lack of join up for the sector to ensure the delivery of Care Act duties."],[62,"There were some gaps in the sector identified by staff due to the rural geography of the county which meant, for example, accessing groups or services to support wellbeing and quality of life, were difficult."]],"m":"A community sector partner told us there were lots of strategies that didn’t get implemented, citing further work needed to use health inequalities funding as an example. Some of the people we spoke to were clear they had experienced a joined-up approach that meant they didn’t need to keep repeating their stories, as information was shared appropriately. The impact of the previous Dementia Strategy was not outlined in the newly launched version, in favour of a more streamlined approach to clearl"},{"q":[[64,"The local authority understood where there were risks to people’s wellbeing across their care journeys, however, these risks were not always consistently assessed across teams."],[64,"The Information and Advice Hub gathered initial information to triage. It was not always possible to gather enough information to make an accurate assessment of risk."],[66,"Some staff, such as those in the mental health teams, were using two recording systems. These systems and external tracking documents, including for the DoLS team, hospital discharge, reablement, and the community wellbeing teams, did not communicate with each other or allow for the easy transfer of information across systems."]],"m":"The local authority understood where there were risks to people’s wellbeing across their care journeys, however, these risks were not always consistently assessed across teams. Information sharing protocols were in place with partners, though local authority recording systems were limited and not sufficiently robust. The Information and Advice Hub gathered initial information to triage. It was not always possible to gather enough information to make an accurate assessment of risk. Staff said it "},{"q":[[79,"A weekly report was shared with us showing how leaders tracked safeguarding activity, but this primarily focused on volume and did not track how long activity took or what risk level it related to. For example, the report asserted all concerns were triaged within 24 hours to ensure the immediate safety of the person was attended to, but no data in the report indicated this detail."],[79,"Their case management system only recorded key episodes which did not always reflect the level of work underway as part of the safeguarding process, or account for when enquiries paused, such as the progression of police investigations, or additional delays that were outside of the control of the practitioner."],[81,"The information linked to safety in the community and protection from crime and prevention of abuse alongside clear ways to contact services about concerns. This supported people to understand safeguarding and how to raise concerns when they didn’t feel safe or they had concerns about the safety of other people."]],"m":"However, safeguarding enquiries were not always prioritised and there were challenges in staff confidence and delays in processes which meant enquiries were not always timely. There were some concerns from staff there was a gap in a shared understanding of all safeguarding roles and a level of uncertainty in the process that was challenging to manage. Some staff told us the right level of support around safeguarding concerns and completing safeguarding enquiries was not in place. Some staff told"},{"q":[[86,"Performance data in this risk profile tended to focus on financial spending and provider activity. It was not clear how wider assessment activity and internal provision was considered within this risk profile, for example."],[86,"However, risks were not aligned against the legal framework the local authority operated under. This made it difficult to assess which key controls were mitigating the more detailed areas of risk as it related to delivery of Care Act and other functions."],[87,"In one example, the local authority were responding to backlogs in the Disability Resource Team by exploring a relationship with a private provider to take on assessments and support in order to reduce people’s waits for services. This represented an understanding of risk and capacity."]],"m":"Partners described a lack of a clear vision and strategy. One partner said this had resulted in conflicting priorities and directions, while another talked about a lack of continuity which had made the implementation of long-term strategies difficult. People with lived experience of services told us there had been a lack of strategic vision and direction as a result of restructures and unstable leadership. People with lived experience of services told us there had been a lack of strategic vision"},{"q":[[90,"Quality assurance primarily focused on case file audit, and did not appear to include elements such as where thematic reviews, senior leadership audits, supervision audits, external reviews, or performance data were used collectively to contribute regularly to practice quality improvement."],[91,"Some bitesize learning was in place, for example, to support safeguarding activity but this was not always well attended."],[94,"There were practice and engagement forums in place for leaders such as the Principal Social Worker to gather staff feedback about their practice needs, however these were limited in the size of the groups."]],"m":"The lack of a clear practice framework and a minimal quality assurance framework limited opportunities for learning and improvement within the local authority. There was mixed feedback about the availability of training to support ongoing skills development, with some staff sharing they had limited to no access to appropriate training. However, the local authority did not have a standalone overarching quality assurance framework indicating how all quality assurance activity came together in plac"}],"East Sussex":[{"q":[[10,"There were 86 adults who had waited more than 6 months for an assessment."],[10,"Local authority data provided showed there were 1368 people with an overdue care review by 0-6 months, 503 people who were overdue by 6 to 12 months and 703 people were more than 12 months overdue for a review."],[12,"If a carer contacted the local authority requesting that their overdue review was completed, the team would complete a review on the phone or refer to a locality team if the review could not be completed over the phone."]],"m":"Assessments, care planning and reviews were not always completed in a timely manner or up to date and the local authority had waiting lists in place. Some people told us they had initial contact with the local authority in February 2024, and an assessment was not started until June 2024. There were 86 adults who had waited more than 6 months for an assessment. Local authority data provided showed there were 1368 people with an overdue care review by 0-6 months, 503 people who were overdue by 6 t"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[35,"The local authority Market Position Statement acknowledged there was a gap in provision in the county for people with particularly complex and challenging conditions."],[36,"The local authority provided a range of community mental health support services which were free and available to any adults living in East Sussex who were living with mental health challenges. These services included but were not limited to, 7 Wellbeing centres providing community-based support, peer support services and staying well spaces."],[36,"Data provided by the local authority for the number of time people had waited for their residential or nursing home service to begin due to lack of capacity was 11.5 days for residential general, 10.9 days for residential dementia, 10.9 days for nursing general, and 12.8 days for nursing dementia."]],"m":"Data was divided into different areas, these included but were not limited to health and social care, deprivation, housing and crime and community safety. Partners told us the local authority had a good understanding of the needs of the local community and that they would seek input from stakeholders to understand local needs if there were any gaps in their knowledge. Partners told us the local authority had a good understanding of the needs of the local community and that they would seek input "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[52,"Hospital discharge was not always completed in a timely manner for people and there were delays with discharging people."],[52,"There had been more than a 10% reduction in hospital delays and there were low delays in terms of NHS community services."],[52,"The local authority was working with partners to find a solution to the delays with discharge; however, this issue had not yet been resolved at the time of assessment."]],"m":"Hospital discharge was not always completed in a timely manner for people and there were delays with discharging people. There had been more than a 10% reduction in hospital delays and there were low delays in terms of NHS community services. The local authority was working with partners to find a solution to the delays with discharge; however, this issue had not yet been resolved at the time of assessment."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Essex":[{"q":[[8,"Assessments, care planning and reviews were not always completed in a timely manner and there were some waiting lists in place."],[9,"Partners told us reviews were not always carried out in a timely manner, however, when a review was requested due to a change of needs the local authority were responsive to this request."],[11,"The local authority policy and framework processes were clear for when people did not meet the eligibility criteria under the Care Act 2014."]],"m":"Assessments, care planning and reviews were not always completed in a timely manner and there were some waiting lists in place. Partners told us reviews were not always carried out in a timely manner, however, when a review was requested due to a change of needs the local authority were responsive to this request. Some people told us that sometimes choice was not offered to carers when looking at support offers, and they did not feel supported by the local authority with access to regular carers"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Hackney":[{"q":[[12,"Feedback from some community groups and staff was that people who need services could sometimes get caught between teams when referrals were not accepted or services were not available. For example, with support for autism, some teams felt provision was good, other teams expressed that there were no services available to meet needs."],[16,"The Principal Social Worker gave an example of how staff audits had flagged a gap in knowledge relating to energy-limiting conditions like chronic fatigue syndrome, and so there was training planned for this area in 2025."],[16,"The local authority had delays in assessments and reviews but was working to ensure they gave high-quality information and advice at the front door and that people were triaged appropriately depending on risk. To support this, Hackney Council had a ‘Waiting Well’ policy which set out the process of triage based upon risk and how teams were to actively manage their waiting lists."]],"m":"Feedback from some community groups and staff was that people who need services could sometimes get caught between teams when referrals were not accepted or services were not available. The Principal Social Worker gave an example of how staff audits had flagged a gap in knowledge relating to energy-limiting conditions like chronic fatigue syndrome, and so there was training planned for this area in 2025. The local authority had delays in assessments and reviews but was working to ensure they gav"},{"q":[[25,"An attempt had been made to integrate the priorities of the ‘Hackney Joint Health and Wellbeing Strategy’ (2022-26) under the governance of the City and Hackney Partnership board; in doing so, it had lost reporting lines to the Health and Wellbeing board, so there was no clear action plan linked back to the Health and Wellbeing Strategy 2022-2026."],[26,"There was a disconnect between the neighbourhood model and Hackney Council's planning, meaning that neighbourhood community engagement work and lessons learnt were not feeding into the learning and development of adult social care’s preventative approaches, and some of the neighbourhood approaches were health-led."],[31,"People could access information and advice on some of their rights under the Care Act and ways to meet their care and support needs, but this was not comprehensive. This included carers’ information for unpaid carers."]],"m":"There was limited data on how many people had been supported using the local multidisciplinary approach and whether this had led to a reduction in needs. An attempt had been made to integrate the priorities of the ‘Hackney Joint Health and Wellbeing Strategy’ (2022-26) under the governance of the City and Hackney Partnership board; in doing so, it had lost reporting lines to the Health and Wellbeing board, so there was no clear action plan linked back to the Health and Wellbeing Strategy 2022-20"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[40,"There was evidence that the supported employment programme commissioned from Hackney Works had supported 20 people with Learning Disabilities into employment in 2023/24, the local authority did not gather information about number of the people in employment as a percentage of the population to show the impact of this programme."],[41,"The local authority started to address these sustainability issues in June 2024, when an independent Housing with Care needs assessment was published. This identified a current and predicted shortfall of housing with care units, which informed a capital building programme and contract redesign."],[42,"The local authority recognised that spot contracting reduced their oversight of quality, buying power, and limited the influence of the local authority to make placements within certain provisions. This was behind the aim to transition to framework contracts and dynamic purchasing systems that would cover the whole of the Northeast London footprint."]],"m":"Both reports indicated that there was not enough housing with care in the borough, especially for people with learning disabilities, mental health and complex physical health conditions. Several strategies spoke to commissioning intentions, and although in some cases there were positive outcomes, due to lack of governance they were not always directly attributable to the strategic intent. But they did not report Autism separately so could not track if there was an improvement in respite take up."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[55,"The out of hours team talked about working in partnership with the emergency housing team which worked well but reported partnerships with the police were not always as effective, as they struggled to get support if welfare calls were needed. This indicated that more work was needed in partnership to understand boundaries of responsibilities and joint arrangements for welfare calls."],[58,"There was no separate waiting list for people who were waiting for assessment for transition, but there were indications that some young people had a timely assessment, while others did not have an assessment until their 18th birthday."],[59,"There was evidence that consideration was given to protecting the safety and well-being of people who were using services which were located away from their local area, through provider selection and contract management, and through the quality assurance activity, but there was an acknowledgement that overdue care reviews reduced oversight."]],"m":"The out of hours team talked about working in partnership with the emergency housing team which worked well but reported partnerships with the police were not always as effective, as they struggled to get support if welfare calls were needed. The out of hours team talked about working in partnership with the emergency housing team which worked well but reported partnerships with the police were not always as effective, as they struggled to get support if welfare calls were needed. This indicated"},{"q":[[62,"The safeguarding adults’ team did not triage all enquiries; some enquiries were sent directly to East London Foundation Trust’s (ELFT) mental health team and the learning disability Team to triage."],[62,"The ELFT had a safeguarding policy, but it did not include managing safeguarding referrals and making section 42 decisions, so there was a gap in operational guidance in this area."],[62,"Some staff were also unclear about the role of the safeguarding team and when they held referrals or sent them on, and there were multiple waiting lists for safeguarding across the system."]],"m":"The safeguarding adults’ team did not triage all enquiries; some enquiries were sent directly to East London Foundation Trust’s (ELFT) mental health team and the learning disability Team to triage. The ELFT had a safeguarding policy, but it did not include managing safeguarding referrals and making section 42 decisions, so there was a gap in operational guidance in this area. Some staff were also unclear about the role of the safeguarding team and when they held referrals or sent them on, and th"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Halton":[{"q":[[10,"The Complex Care team and mental health teams did not use a named worker approach, and some people told us they could have multiple social workers which resulted in them telling their story numerous times."],[12,"Staff in frontline teams completed first and annual reviews with people, and a reviewing team had been set up to provide additional support in completing annual reviews due to the local authority identifying they were not successfully completing all reviews annually with the current staff resource."],[14,"There were also dedicated carer’s assessors within the Prevention and Wellbeing Service (PWS) who completed a carers assessment if there was not an allocated social worker."]],"m":"The Complex Care team and mental health teams did not use a named worker approach, and some people told us they could have multiple social workers which resulted in them telling their story numerous times. Staff in frontline teams completed first and annual reviews with people, and a reviewing team had been set up to provide additional support in completing annual reviews due to the local authority identifying they were not successfully completing all reviews annually with the current staff reso"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[32,"Halton Borough Council was part of the Liverpool City Region Combined Authority which enabled them to access the Liverpool City region Flexible Purchasing System (FPS) to procure services for people with a range of needs and commission services from providers which were not available within Halton."],[33,"The local authority recognised that some of the commissioned models of care and support were not in line with best practice, such as building based day services, and were looking to make changes to provision where appropriate to ensure they were proportionate to a person’s level of need."],[36,"There was some need for people to use services or support in places outside of their local area due to lack of local provision."]],"m":"There was limited choice of home care providers for people whose care was arranged by the local authority. There was limited choice of home care providers for people whose care was arranged by the local authority. This was because the local authority contracted with only one principal home care provider which operated across the borough although there was an additional provider sub-contracted. The local authority had recognised the limited choice and capacity risks with this arrangement, and the"},{"q":[[47,"Partners gave an example of where they had given the local authority feedback about a lack of understanding of sensory processing, and the local authority worked with partners to run workshops for families, professionals, and social care staff to increase their understanding of sensory processing."],[47,"The local authority worked collaboratively with Voluntary, Community and Faith Sector Enterprises (VCFSE) to understand and meet local social care needs."],[48,"Staff told us some VCFSE had closed during covid and there were not as many as there used to be, which limited options for people to socialise outside of formal care services, but the community centres were providing good outcomes for people."]],"m":"Partners gave an example of where they had given the local authority feedback about a lack of understanding of sensory processing, and the local authority worked with partners to run workshops for families, professionals, and social care staff to increase their understanding of sensory processing. Staff told us some VCFSE had closed during covid and there were not as many as there used to be, which limited options for people to socialise outside of formal care services, but the community centres"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[63,"There was not a clear structure and process in place to implement the large-scale changes which were needed at pace to drive a sustainable, prevention, and strengths-based service and extend this beyond the current Transformation Programme."],[64,"There was consistent feedback from leaders who recognised while the current model was not thought to be sustainable, there needed to be system-wide change, co-produced with people, to deliver effective transformation and provide a greater focus on prevention and independence."],[64,"The local authority had identified an increase in overdue annual reviews for people and had implemented a reviewing team to ensure people had a review of their care."]],"m":"There was not a clear structure and process in place to implement the large-scale changes which were needed at pace to drive a sustainable, prevention, and strengths-based service and extend this beyond the current Transformation Programme. There was consistent feedback from leaders who recognised while the current model was not thought to be sustainable, there needed to be system-wide change, co-produced with people, to deliver effective transformation and provide a greater focus on prevention "},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Hammersmith and Fulham":[{"q":[[10,"The local authority had not made experts available for the assessment of people who are Deafblind or autistic people. It was found the sensory team who supported the Deafblind did not carry out Care Act assessments, and the learning disability team did not feel they had the depth of training to provide effective assessment and support to autistic people."],[12,"The local authority was unsure that the waiting lists were reflective of current needs, for example in the Mental Health team they did not know if people waiting were health or social care responsibility due to recording across 2 databases."],[12,"The local authority had hired agency social work staff for a review project team to complete the backlog of annual reviews. This team had found over and under provision of support, as well as people who had not been spending their direct payments as anticipated."]],"m":"Some staff told us they were not confident about practice and process and some people in need of care and support found care pathways confusing. The local authority had not made experts available for the assessment of people who are Deafblind or autistic people. It was found the sensory team who supported the Deafblind did not carry out Care Act assessments, and the learning disability team did not feel they had the depth of training to provide effective assessment and support to autistic people"},{"q":[[22,"Between August 2023 and September 2024, the median wait for Occupational Therapy assessment was 107 days and the maximum wait was 819 days, the reason for the wait was that the current service could not tackle the backlog of referrals."],[22,"There was a lack of strategic direction to define the role of occupational therapy and how it fitted in with the Care Act assessment pathway, and this meant there was a missed preventative opportunity in the borough."],[23,"The contract was awarded in 2013; data from September 2023 to August 2024 showed that they were not meeting their contracted timescales. For example, same-day orders took a median of 2 working days for delivery which impacted care pathways like hospital discharge as people could not go home till equipment was in place."]],"m":"People were not receiving equipment and adaptations in a timely way, and this meant there was a missed opportunity to promote independence. Between August 2023 and September 2024, the median wait for Occupational Therapy assessment was 107 days and the maximum wait was 819 days, the reason for the wait was that the current service could not tackle the backlog of referrals. There was a lack of strategic direction to define the role of occupational therapy and how it fitted in with the Care Act as"},{"q":[[26,"The local authority had multiple communities in the borough representing a rich diversity, however, the depth of equalities analysis did not always reflect this in adult social care. For example, we heard about the lesbian, gay bisexual, and trans community, asylum seekers community, and traveller community but these communities were not identified in the adult social care data or population profiles."],[27,"However, there was no specific equality plan for adult social care and though it had some information about people receiving services captured in its care records system, this did not cover all protected characteristics and was not analysed to understand if inequality existed in the provision of social care."],[27,"Within these events led by community leaders people opened up about issues of racism and unconscious bias, and gave reasons why they struggled to engage, especially over the phone, with services due to the language barriers and lack of translators."]],"m":"The local authority had multiple communities in the borough representing a rich diversity, however, the depth of equalities analysis did not always reflect this in adult social care. Partners recognised that adult social care was seeking to reach out to a range of communities but felt sometimes it did not have a depth of understanding about different cultures and differences within groups. However, there was no specific equality plan for adult social care and though it had some information about"},{"q":[[30,"Hammersmith & Fulham understood the local needs for future care and support but did not show how they had developed their market position statement and sustainability plan with local stakeholders, including people who were most likely to experience poor care."],[33,"Where there were specific needs that could not be met by the contracted home care provider, for example, a specific language or a cultural need, these were sought, and if possible, provided through spot purchase."],[37,"In its market sustainability plan Hammersmith and Fulham said that there was a shortage of care workers and were supporting care providers with recruitment and offering training to new care workers, but there was no strategy or training programme in place."]],"m":"Hammersmith & Fulham understood the local needs for future care and support but did not show how they had developed their market position statement and sustainability plan with local stakeholders, including people who were most likely to experience poor care. The local authority was proud that they did not charge unpaid carers for replacement home care to have a break. The carers’ support contract was receiving one-year extensions, which created sustainability issues for the provider in meeting "},{"q":[[39,"However, they reported that despite multiple meetings about data among partners, no progress was made meaning the board was unable to set key performance indicators for the strategy."],[40,"The local authority had Better Care Fund (BCF) arrangements with the Northwest London Integrated Care Board (ICB) but there were limited funds spent on joining up health, social care and housing services."],[40,"In the bridging service, contracts were put out to the open market for spot contracting when the local authority could not meet the needs under contract within targeted timescales. This meant people were not delayed in hospital waiting for care provision if it was available on the open market."]],"m":"All partners agreed relationships at the borough level needed to be developed, and the local health and social care system did not have a shared vision for health and social care support in Hammersmith the Fulham. However, they reported that despite multiple meetings about data among partners, no progress was made meaning the board was unable to set key performance indicators for the strategy. The local authority had limited systems arrangements with health partner agencies. In the past there ha"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[49,"Key risks identified included fluctuating capacity, fire safety, lack of learning, substance misuse, self-neglect, racism and multi-agency communication."],[50,"There was no guidance on what constituted poor practice and what was a Section 42 (s42) safeguarding concern which needed an enquiry."],[50,"Frontline staff were not aware of the quality audits taking place or wider changes in the safeguarding improvement plan."]],"m":"Key risks identified included fluctuating capacity, fire safety, lack of learning, substance misuse, self-neglect, racism and multi-agency communication. There was no guidance on what constituted poor practice and what was a Section 42 (s42) safeguarding concern which needed an enquiry. Frontline staff were not aware of the quality audits taking place or wider changes in the safeguarding improvement plan."},{"q":[[53,"Following the restructure and appointment of senior leaders the team had developed positive relationships, and though the Director of Adult Social Care (DASS) appointed in June 2024 was not a direct line report to the Chief Executive they had regular meetings to agree priorities in adult social care."],[53,"In the past implementing recommendations had been slow; plans, people’s feedback and board documents showed that there was not a culture of identifying performance indicators so that projects and boards could be held accountable."],[54,"Where there were shortfalls in delivery of Care Act duties these had been identified and actions were in place to mitigate the shortfalls, for example there had been investment in the review project team to reduce waiting times for review of Care Act assessments."]],"m":"Following the restructure and appointment of senior leaders the team had developed positive relationships, and though the Director of Adult Social Care (DASS) appointed in June 2024 was not a direct line report to the Chief Executive they had regular meetings to agree priorities in adult social care. In the past implementing recommendations had been slow; plans, people’s feedback and board documents showed that there was not a culture of identifying performance indicators so that projects and bo"},{"q":[[57,"There was a reliance on temporary contracts and indirectly employed staff, who accounted for 39% of the workforce. This impacted the stability of the workforce and there were not enough experienced or senior staff to support apprenticeship or newly qualified staff, this meant at times these staff felt unsupported."],[58,"The data provided on the local authority workforce showed that 32% of frontline staff were Black, Black British or Black other and for senior roles, this was 14%. This reflected a lack of representation from these communities in senior roles."],[60,"We saw developments had commenced around these areas, however, were not yet embedded and some staff felt they lacked the opportunity to feedback on the new initiatives or changes to leaders. For example, the safeguarding changes had been made and communicated to staff by email and staff felt they had not been consulted on the pathway or how the changes might affect the flow or risks."]],"m":"There was a reliance on temporary contracts and indirectly employed staff, who accounted for 39% of the workforce. This impacted the stability of the workforce and there were not enough experienced or senior staff to support apprenticeship or newly qualified staff, this meant at times these staff felt unsupported. The data provided on the local authority workforce showed that 32% of frontline staff were Black, Black British or Black other and for senior roles, this was 14%. This reflected a lack"}],"Hampshire":[{"q":[[12,"At the time of our assessment there were some delays in Care Act assessments, reviews, and carers’ assessments."],[14,"The strategy included a target to ensure no person waited longer than 2 months for assessment by December 2025, with an ambition to eliminate all waits in the future."],[15,"One of the strategy’s key aims was to identify and recognise carers, acknowledging that many people did not realise they were carers. As a result, through changing practice, there had been a significant increase in carers identified and recorded from 19,626 (2022/23) to 23,796 (2024/25)."]],"m":"Assessments and care planning arrangements were not consistently timely. At the time of our assessment there were some delays in Care Act assessments, reviews, and carers’ assessments. Most said that in an emergency reviews would be done quickly but generally they were not completed on time and that the outcome of reviews was not always sent to the provider in a timely manner. The strategy included a target to ensure no person waited longer than 2 months for assessment by December 2025, with an "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[55,"Sensory teams also triaged referrals and reviewed waiting lists every 2 weeks to re-prioritise people whose circumstances had changed, helping to maintain safety while people waited for assessment."],[55,"Staff reviewed people waiting for assessment weekly, 4 or 8 weekly, depending on need, and visiting officers carried out home checks when people could not be contacted, enabling early identification of deterioration or safeguarding concerns."],[61,"Contingency arrangements for unpaid carers were established, with the commissioned carers service developing 1,844 contingency and emergency plans in the first nine months of their contract, giving those unpaid carers reassurance that alternative support was identified should they become unexpectedly unable to provide care."]],"m":"Sensory teams also triaged referrals and reviewed waiting lists every 2 weeks to re-prioritise people whose circumstances had changed, helping to maintain safety while people waited for assessment. Staff reviewed people waiting for assessment weekly, 4 or 8 weekly, depending on need, and visiting officers carried out home checks when people could not be contacted, enabling early identification of deterioration or safeguarding concerns. However, staffing levels meant they had limited capacity for"},{"q":[[63,"However, some partners reported delays or lack of communication pathways. For example, escalation routes were used when responses were not received through standard channels."],[64,"However, clear timescales for responding to safeguarding concerns were lacking, and 19% of people involved in section 42 enquiries had no recorded ethnicity, indicating gaps in completeness of data."],[65,"Key risks identified included poor-quality safeguarding referrals from a local health partner which often lacked sufficient detail or were inappropriate. To address this, referrals from the health partner were sent to the community teams for review, and senior leaders actively engaged with the local health partner to improve reporting standards."]],"m":"However, some partners reported delays or lack of communication pathways. Partners reported inconsistent application of eligibility criteria, and adult safeguarding processes were said to require improvement in timeliness and communication. However, clear timescales for responding to safeguarding concerns were lacking, and 19% of people involved in section 42 enquiries had no recorded ethnicity, indicating gaps in completeness of data. Key risks identified included poor-quality safeguarding refe"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Hartlepool":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[29,"However, despite this, a voluntary and community partner was concerned seldom heard groups were not accessing local authority services through the hubs."],[31,"The local authority collected data but did not always analyse equality data on social care users to use it to identify and reduce inequalities in people’s access to care and support, their experiences and outcomes."],[36,"Some local people living with sensory loss, deaf British Sign Language users and parent carers experienced difficulties in the ways services communicate, there was a lack of British Sign Language, deaf awareness and communication skills training, which meant many experienced barriers to communication."]],"m":"However, despite this, a voluntary and community partner was concerned seldom heard groups were not accessing local authority services through the hubs. The community risk mapping would enable an accurate picture of geographical areas, demographics and key statistics. This would also enable key decision making relating to gaps in services, investment need, targeted interventions and environmental profiles. The Socio-Economic Duty had been adopted by the local authority as a protected characteris"},{"q":[[42,"In relation to gaps in care provision some partners said the local authority were proactive in including the voluntary sector and identifying gaps. For example, additional funding had been provided around commissioned carer services which meant unpaid carers did not need to wait for support."],[42,"However, other organisations felt commissioning of the voluntary sector lacked funding and resulted in less choice for people."],[45,"Whilst there was an adult social care workforce strategy for the local authority’s workforce, we did not see a strategy for the recruitment, training and retention for the provider market."]],"m":"In relation to gaps in care provision some partners said the local authority were proactive in including the voluntary sector and identifying gaps. However, other organisations felt commissioning of the voluntary sector lacked funding and resulted in less choice for people. The local authority described adult social care as not always seen as an attractive career option and they were seeking to address challenges with recruitment and retention in the social care sector. Whilst there was an adult"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Havering":[{"q":[[12,"While the impact of this approach varied and there was no evidence people did not receive support due to panels, there remained a risk of impact people’s choice."],[14,"The impact of this team continued to embed, and it was unclear if this would help to reduce delays in accessing services, but initial feedback from staff was positive."],[14,"The local authority outlined frontline community teams contacted people by telephone at least monthly or more frequently when required, but this was not reflected in the Waiting List Management Process document."]],"m":"Another person’s support had enabled them to build their independence and take part in activities they could not do previously. However, another person told us while initially they also had a positive experience with an empathetic and understanding staff member, a further staff member did not take time to obtain their viewpoints, and they felt it was not a strength-based approach. Another person’s relative felt the assessment completed did not reflect the person’s level of need and opportunity t"},{"q":[[22,"While there was not an overarching prevention strategy, a new Health and Wellbeing (HWB) Strategy, along with a Place-Based Partnership Strategy were being developed following the previous strategies concluding in 2024."],[28,"Of the 34 people referred by GPs to support admission avoidance, 68% did not have a hospital admission within 3 months of reablement support."],[32,"The Havering Mental Health Needs Assessment (2025), developed with contributions from the local authority alongside health and VCSE partners, identified that community-based VCSE services could sometimes be underrecognised, meaning people and some professionals were not always aware of the full range of support available."]],"m":"While there was not an overarching prevention strategy, a new Health and Wellbeing (HWB) Strategy, along with a Place-Based Partnership Strategy were being developed following the previous strategies concluding in 2024. However, it was not possible to determine the impact of this approach as it was in early stages of implementation. The LACs did not work across all areas of Havering, but resource was focused in areas of higher need and a staff team also told us people were encouraged to attend h"},{"q":[[36,"There was not yet, however, a clearly defined approach to reaching specific seldom-heard communities to support their access to ASC services."],[36,"Some partners particularly highlighted the local authority was not working well with the VCSE effectively to better support seldom heard groups."],[38,"The local authority further outlined these included enquiries, informal and formal complaints, with 9 out of 26 being formal complaints. They also welcomed increases in complaints from people with learning disabilities as it suggested this was more accessible and enabled learning, even where complaints were not upheld."]],"m":"A partner told us they were confident there were no hidden communities the local authority was not aware of. The local authority recognised the diversity of people accessing ASC was not representative of the wider community. There was not yet, however, a clearly defined approach to reaching specific seldom-heard communities to support their access to ASC services. Some partners also told us there was not enough work being undertaken to reach and understand communities to address specific barrier"},{"q":[[43,"While the interim strategy was not co-produced, a strategy in consultation with key stakeholders and residents, was also being developed."],[46,"Specialist and personcentred dementia support, accessible accommodation and services to support people with mental health needs or needs relating to drug and alcohol use were all highlighted as being more difficult to access."],[47,"A review of respite services was also part of the Carers Strategy Action Plan. This included a focus on supporting carers to have the right information and advice on how to access respite care, but there was not a clear approach to improving the local respite offer."]],"m":"While the interim strategy was not co-produced, a strategy in consultation with key stakeholders and residents, was also being developed. A staff team told us there was close work between frontline and commissioning staff to develop the project including discussing the design and people who may have wanted to live there. They felt where this engagement approach was not always well developed, this was an example to be built upon in further commissioning developments. There was recognition from th"},{"q":[[58,"The Havering Mental Health Needs Assessment (2025) also highlighted only 46% of frontline staff who were surveyed understood referral procedures, with unclear thresholds, poor communication and service delays all referenced."],[61,"Initial evaluation of this approach showed 30% of people returning to their usual place of residence, where prior to these arrangements (2019), 98% of people remained in long term services. This approach was also extended out to residential beds, information provided by the local authority showed the block bed commissioned model did not work as effectively as anticipated."],[61,"However, the pilot was unable to evidence reduced length of stay in hospital and as a result had not been continued despite some encouraging impact."]],"m":"The Havering Mental Health Needs Assessment (2025) also highlighted only 46% of frontline staff who were surveyed understood referral procedures, with unclear thresholds, poor communication and service delays all referenced. The local authority recognised there could be challenges and delays to decision making around health funding for people’s care and support. Staff teams told us of the challenges of funding disputes with health, where they felt people were eligible for health funding. They to"},{"q":[[66,"We were told that there has never been a time when the service was not covered."],[67,"Referrals which were not appropriate for reablement, such as those for people accessing the discharge to assess pathway, received input from local authority staff."],[67,"However, there were some challenges of this approach, with a staff team telling us where the form was not filled out appropriately, people could remain in hospital for longer than needed. They told us trusted assessments sometimes did not accurately reflect people’s needs and required more information, which potentially delayed discharge."]],"m":"We were told that there has never been a time when the service was not covered. Referrals which were not appropriate for reablement, such as those for people accessing the discharge to assess pathway, received input from local authority staff. However, there were some challenges of this approach, with a staff team telling us where the form was not filled out appropriately, people could remain in hospital for longer than needed. However, there were some challenges of this approach, with a staff t"},{"q":[[76,"The local authority’s Quality Assurance Framework (April 2025) did not provide information on quality assurance processes for safeguarding work undertaken by the trust."],[76,"The local authority also outlined although regular specific quality audits had not been completed, there were systems for oversight such as monthly meetings with the mental health trust to provide monitoring of open safeguarding enquiries, updates on processes and reinforcement of best practice, for example."],[80,"There was an aim to complete s42 enquiries within a 28-day period. However, this did not always happen as this would depend on the nature of the risk to the person."]],"m":"The local authority’s Quality Assurance Framework (April 2025) did not provide information on quality assurance processes for safeguarding work undertaken by the trust. A partner also told us there had been an issue of a lack of ownership of s42 enquiries and a lack of full understanding what the trust was responsible for. A partner also told us there had been an issue of a lack of ownership of s42 enquiries and a lack of full understanding what the trust was responsible for. They told us there "},{"q":[[88,"The local authority did not have a specific ASC strategy and instead strategic direction was incorporated within the wider Corporate Vision (2022) and Corporate Plan (2024-2027) which supported an aligned approach with the rest of the local authority. This plan was put in place following a reassessment of what the local authority could deliver within their financial constraints."],[89,"Leaders were not always clear how the local authority were addressing these waits. For example, a leader was unable to tell us of any plans to reduce people’s waits for Care Act assessments."],[90,"Staffing pressures were recognised on the ASC risk register, which highlighted the potential impact if the service were unable to maintain sufficient staffing levels to meet demand."]],"m":"A senior leader recognised a lack of change in leadership roles could reduce innovation or new ideas, but they told us this was mitigated by leaders involvement in external networks so they understood best practice. However, at the time of the assessment, ASC was not being sufficiently scrutinised at scrutiny meetings with a senior leader acknowledging there had not been a recent focus on ASC across meetings. A senior leader also told us the current model of scrutiny was not allowing necessary f"},{"q":[[92,"The local authority recognised co-production was not fully embedded throughout their service design and delivery."],[92,"Another partner felt there ongoing changes to commissioning of services, but there was not meaningful engagement with people who would be impacted."],[94,"The local authority was developing innovation to support new ways to deliver services, but current examples of innovative practice were limited."]],"m":"The local authority recognised co-production was not fully embedded throughout their service design and delivery. Another partner felt there ongoing changes to commissioning of services, but there was not meaningful engagement with people who would be impacted. A staff team told us ability to access training could be impacted by their workload, but this view was not fed back from other teams. The local authority was developing innovation to support new ways to deliver services, but current examp"}],"Herefordshire":[{"q":[[15,"While processes were in place to quality assure decision making in ART, we received mixed feedback that decision making within the referral process was not always efficient and some staff highlighted referrals often came through to the wrong teams or prioritisation wasn’t clear."],[18,"People were signposted to the Talk Community Directory and community organisations to provide support while they waited."],[18,"Technology Enabled Care (TEC) solutions could also be progressed while people waited for an assessment."]],"m":"While processes were in place to quality assure decision making in ART, we received mixed feedback that decision making within the referral process was not always efficient and some staff highlighted referrals often came through to the wrong teams or prioritisation wasn’t clear. Staff said this meant some people experienced delays in receiving information, advice and guidance or an appropriate service at the right time. However, some staff and leaders told us they felt the lack of PSW. However, "},{"q":[[28,"In one example, an unpaid carer was referred to groups that didn’t exist, which had caused further stress."],[30,"However, reablement services were not sufficiently effective to support people to return to their optimal independence."],[32,"Some staff shared concerns that people who would benefit from a reablement service, but who came through a community route rather than a hospital discharge route, did not receive reablement care which would help them."]],"m":"In one example shared with us, health partners had identified unpaid carers were not attending their own preventative health appointments as they were concerned about care for their loved one. In some instances, however, unpaid carers told us there were not enough services available and their stress, anxiety and exhaustion were high. In one example, an unpaid carer was referred to groups that didn’t exist, which had caused further stress. However, reablement services were not sufficiently effect"},{"q":[[42,"We found some parts of the websites had good accessibility features, such as in-built translation, but that wasn’t the case in other parts, so there was some inconsistency."],[43,"Some partners reflected that services were not always accessible to people with complex needs, including care and support needs, due to the county’s rurality."],[43,"A 2022 survey highlighted 49% of people who used telecare services did not use the internet."]],"m":"One staff member, for example, told us some areas of the county had a lack of community infrastructure which made access and support from community agencies difficult. We found some parts of the websites had good accessibility features, such as in-built translation, but that wasn’t the case in other parts, so there was some inconsistency. Some partners reflected that services were not always accessible to people with complex needs, including care and support needs, due to the county’s rurality. "},{"q":[[48,"The local authority’s Market Sustainability Plan 2022-2025 identified capacity to meet demand for complex care in nursing environments in Herefordshire was limited."],[51,"A lack of respite and short breaks was reflected by several of the staff teams we spoke to."],[51,"However, there was a consistent concern around the limited capacity of volunteers, particularly in services supporting unpaid carers."]],"m":"The local authority’s Market Sustainability Plan 2022-2025 identified capacity to meet demand for complex care in nursing environments in Herefordshire was limited. Staff told us there were some gaps in provision for people with learning disabilities, particularly where people had complex needs, which were ongoing at the time of our assessment. Some providers told us respite services for unpaid carers had been insufficient. One partner, for example, told us unpaid carers were not entitled to res"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[67,"Data regarding who waited for services and how long was robust for the majority of adult social care services."],[67,"There was a lack of clarity about who was waiting for different services and how long for occupational therapy-based assessments."],[67,"Where people waited for a package of care, this was minimal and there were clear arrangements in place between relevant teams to consider risk, including carer crisis."]],"m":"Data regarding who waited for services and how long was robust for the majority of adult social care services. There was a lack of clarity about who was waiting for different services and how long for occupational therapy-based assessments. Waiting lists were prioritised and waiting well processes were in place, which mitigated the impact of this issue and reduced the risk safety diminished while people waited. Staff also told us there was a lack of guidance for how to access the relevant inform"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[83,"There were some leadership roles, such as the Principal Social Worker and Director of Public Health, which either had recent changes or were vacant, which were noted by some staff and partners."],[85,"There were areas where further work was needed to understand impact, such as reablement, in terms of outcomes for people that were not in fully place at the time of our assessment. This work would support the ongoing improvement of services."],[86,"Some staff recognised structural change was needed but these service changes had progressed slowly, reflecting some lack of clarity in strategic direction for some staff."]],"m":"Some staff felt this internally, with their voice not always well heard in contribution to service developments or transformation. Some staff felt this internally, with their voice not always well heard in contribution to service developments or transformation. This was supported by the employee survey in 2024 which indicated 45% of the staff who responded did not feel involved in making decisions about key changes and 39% did not feel able to influence change within the local authority. There w"},{"q":[[89,"More informal mechanisms, such as shadowing opportunities, could be limited or unavailable where workload was high, though these were available for some."],[91,"The local authority’s transformation plan outlined development of the area’s prevention strategy, for example, was in progress through multi-agency stakeholder groups that were identifying gaps in services, case study deep dives and community engagement."],[93,"Some staff identified recent improvements in feeling listened to, though they’d experienced an historical lack of consultation."]],"m":"More informal mechanisms, such as shadowing opportunities, could be limited or unavailable where workload was high, though these were available for some. In safeguarding, social workers’ information gathering was signed off by senior practitioners, although staff told us a formal audit process was not in place at the time of our assessment. The local authority’s transformation plan outlined development of the area’s prevention strategy, for example, was in progress through multi-agency stakehold"}],"Hertfordshire":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Hull":[{"q":[[10,"If a person’s needs were not urgent, they were transferred to be assessed by staff from long-term teams."],[11,"Pathways and processes did not ensure that people’s support was planned and coordinated across different agencies and services."],[12,"People’s protected characteristics under the Equality Act 2010 were not always understood and incorporated into care planning."]],"m":"However, these were not always effective for people who wanted to speak with the local authority. Partners told us online options for contacting the local authority were not always accessible due to digital deprivation in the area. People told us where they did not have an allocated worker from the local authority, they had contact details for duty teams but did not always receive a return call or spoke to a different worker each time they called which meant they had to tell their story multiple"},{"q":[[20,"Prevention was jointly owned across the local authority due to it covering several service areas however a lack of co-ordination limited the progress."],[20,"We heard about a disjointed approach where teams managed their own pressures and a lack of a ‘one council’ approach to issues such as waiting times for adaptations which were assessed for in one part of the service and then fulfilled by another."],[26,"People could not easily access information and advice on their rights under the Care Act and ways to meet their care and support needs. This included unpaid carers and people who fund or arrange their own care and support."]],"m":"Prevention was jointly owned across the local authority due to it covering several service areas however a lack of co-ordination limited the progress. We heard about a disjointed approach where teams managed their own pressures and a lack of a ‘one council’ approach to issues such as waiting times for adaptations which were assessed for in one part of the service and then fulfilled by another. We were told by leaders that there was oversight of repeat contacts and referrals to monitor the effect"},{"q":[[30,"The local authority had identified where corporate data had gaps and did not support them to identify where populations were facing inequalities in experiences, and was reviewing service-level data sets to ensure they had an adequate picture of their population and people’s outcomes."],[30,"However, due to poor recording of characteristics, this meant that some people may not have been identified through audits as their demographic information was not known."],[30,"Additionally, some data was not routinely scrutinised such as waiting lists which were not broken down by demographic to understand if people from particular groups faced longer waits or had a poorer experience."]],"m":"The local authority understood its local population profile and demographics however did not effectively use this information to understand and reduce inequalities in people’s experiences and outcomes. The local authority had identified where corporate data had gaps and did not support them to identify where populations were facing inequalities in experiences, and was reviewing service-level data sets to ensure they had an adequate picture of their population and people’s outcomes. Staff told us"},{"q":[[37,"Staff and partners told us there was a lack of planned respite provision within the area, an issue the local authority was considering while updating their Market Position Statement."],[37,"Where people required care within a residential or nursing home, approximately 10% people were also receiving 1-1 support, a large number of whom had been in receipt of this for over 6 weeks, according to data supplied by the local authority. This indicated a lack of provision which was unable to meet people’s more complex needs without additional restrictions placed upon people through 1-1 support."],[38,"Staff told us about difficulties sourcing residential care provision to meet people’s needs where they had complex mental health support needs or misused substances. This had been identified through the JSNA however staff told us there was limited supported accommodation willing to support people with these needs."]],"m":"Staff and partners told us there was a lack of planned respite provision within the area, an issue the local authority was considering while updating their Market Position Statement. Some staff gave examples of commissioning care to suit people’s interests and desired outcomes, however other staff gave examples of commissioning tasks and times of care calls which were not always a person’s preference. Where people required care within a residential or nursing home, approximately 10% people were "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[51,"Where people were not supported by the High Needs team they reported a poorer experience."],[51,"People told us they did not understand the differences between child and adult services and found the process confusing."],[52,"However, we also heard that despite this early planning there was sometimes difficulties in sourcing care placements where required for young people, leading to lack of proper planning for moves between services."]],"m":"The local authority had processes in place to manage risks to people across their care journey however risks were not always identified and managed proactively. While people who could not contact the local authority to inform them of any changes were prioritised, waiting lists presented an element of uncontrolled risk of people’s needs not being met appropriately or safely without oversight from the local authority. Partners told us there was shared ownership of hospital discharge pathways which"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[62,"However, use of this resource was not strategically prioritised, and staff were often redirected to meet other service demands. As a result, the intended impact was diluted, and the waiting list for annual reviews continued to grow."],[62,"High waiting lists for occupational therapy assessment had been identified by the local authority in 2023 however due to staff posts remaining vacant until recently, it had accepted that people’s experience would remain poor due to the existing backlog being addressed with the volume of requests incoming."],[63,"The senior leadership structure of the local authority meant the Director of Adult Social Services (DASS) did not report directly to executive leaders, although they were part of the corporate leadership board and had regular meetings with the Chief Executive to discuss Care Act duties and service performance."]],"m":"However, use of this resource was not strategically prioritised, and staff were often redirected to meet other service demands. However, use of this resource was not strategically prioritised, and staff were often redirected to meet other service demands. As a result, the intended impact was diluted, and the waiting list for annual reviews continued to grow. However, there was an acceptance that the local authority was not able to deliver on all its duties and resources could only be used to mee"},{"q":[[67,"We saw examples of coproduction activity but this was not fully embedded throughout the local authority’s work."],[67,"People were frustrated that their voice did not lead to meaningful change."],[69,"Partners gave us mixed feedback on the local authority’s response to learning when things wrong, with some telling us the local authority were responsive to issues, and others telling us they did not receive feedback about outcomes or measures being taken to ensure there would not be a reoccurrence."]],"m":"People told us senior leaders were detached from their feedback, and they did not have regular engagement with people who could make decisions and implement the changes they suggested. We saw examples of coproduction activity but this was not fully embedded throughout the local authority’s work. People were frustrated that their voice did not lead to meaningful change. People told us where they had raised complaints to the local authority, responses were fast, and issues did not reoccur followin"}],"Isle of Wight":[{"q":[[12,"Pathways and processes did not always ensure people’s support was planned and coordinated across different agencies and services."],[12,"However, the front door team did not have access to health recording systems to identify if people were open to secondary mental health services before transferring to a mental health social worker."],[12,"The local authority had processes in place which ensured people were not transferred multiple times and remained open to the front door team while awaiting acceptance by the mental health team, however staff feedback indicated some people experienced handoffs, or waits to receive assessments by specialist teams."]],"m":"People’s feedback told us responses from the local authority were not always timely, which sometimes led to people reaching crisis before they received support. Some people also told us they did not know who to contact at the local authority. Some people told us their assessments did not highlight what they could do for themselves and were incorrect. However, people’s assessments we reviewed were sometimes needs-based and lacked a person-centred approach which captured people’s personalities and"},{"q":[[19,"A senior leader told us about joint work between health partners and adult social care around falls prevention with strength and balance classes delivered through island leisure centres. These were well spread across the island to support people where they did not have access to transport."],[22,"The local authority had, in 2023, brought back in house its occupational therapy (OT) service and a backlog of people waiting for assessment existed."],[22,"There had also been recruitment difficulties for the local authority which limited staffing within the team and impacted on waiting times for people."]],"m":"A senior leader told us about joint work between health partners and adult social care around falls prevention with strength and balance classes delivered through island leisure centres. These were well spread across the island to support people where they did not have access to transport. The local authority had taken steps to identify people with needs for care and support that were not being met. Staff told us the local authority was proactive in identifying gaps in preventative support from "},{"q":[[27,"The data within the local authority’s Happy Healthy Lives Adult Social Care Strategy (2024-27) revealed significant challenges facing the Isle of Wight’s adult social care system, driven by demographic ageing, isolation and deprivation but equally did not analyse what this meant for people with different needs, such as those who are lesbian, gay, bisexual, trans and other identities (LGBTQ+), or people of different ethnicities."],[28,"People’s records we reviewed for this assessment did not contain demographic information outside of their age and sex, and staff told us information on additional protected characteristics was not always recorded. This was also demonstrated in local authority data which showed that of 4084 people accessing adult social care in December 2025, 2048 people did not have their sexuality recorded."],[30,"People using services and their unpaid carers did not always have equal opportunities to engage with the local authority in ways that worked for them."]],"m":"The data within the local authority’s Happy Healthy Lives Adult Social Care Strategy (2024-27) revealed significant challenges facing the Isle of Wight’s adult social care system, driven by demographic ageing, isolation and deprivation but equally did not analyse what this meant for people with different needs, such as those who are lesbian, gay, bisexual, trans and other identities (LGBTQ+), or people of different ethnicities. People’s records we reviewed for this assessment did not contain dem"},{"q":[[33,"The Independent Island Living Strategy 2023-2038 identified that using national modelling, there would be a potential shortfall in extra care housing with 526 additional units required by 2038."],[34,"However, the local authority had identified in its Market Position Statement that higher than average numbers of people aged 65+ were being admitted to permanent residential or nursing care homes, with the reason for this being identified as due to a lack of suitable alternative options such as extra care housing."],[34,"Local authority data showed in the 3 months to November 2025, 107 people waited for homecare services to begin due to lack of capacity within provisions."]],"m":"Partners told us about gaps in provision for people with mental health support needs, and residential care for people with advanced dementia, but an oversupply of general residential care homes which were not always suitable for people with dementia. The Independent Island Living Strategy 2023-2038 identified that using national modelling, there would be a potential shortfall in extra care housing with 526 additional units required by 2038. However, staff told us this need was known but limited "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[54,"The local authority reviewed data regularly to understand how the service was functioning, although there was scope for improvement as some data was unable to be reported on within the local authority system."],[54,"An example of this was a limited ability to monitor waiting times for Care Act assessments due to an issue within the recording system which created data inaccuracies."],[57,"There was scope to improve strategic planning to ensure people’s outcomes were equitable, and people who faced barriers to support were included in local authority planning. This was hampered by a lack of recording of people’s protected characteristics to provide oversight of who did and didn’t access adult social care."]],"m":"The local authority reviewed data regularly to understand how the service was functioning, although there was scope for improvement as some data was unable to be reported on within the local authority system. An example of this was a limited ability to monitor waiting times for Care Act assessments due to an issue within the recording system which created data inaccuracies. There was scope to improve strategic planning to ensure people’s outcomes were equitable, and people who faced barriers to "},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Islington":[{"q":[[9,"The team triaged contacts and referred to the First Point of Contact Team if people were not already known to adult social care for an initial Care Act assessment."],[10,"Leaders listened to frontline staff when processes were not working smoothly, and more resources had been allocated to support teams when required. For example, the First Point of Contact Team had vacancies within their team which was impacting on their workload."],[14,"Staff signposted people to other council services, partners and external agencies for help and support for needs that were not eligible under the Care Act."]],"m":"The team triaged contacts and referred to the First Point of Contact Team if people were not already known to adult social care for an initial Care Act assessment. Leaders listened to frontline staff when processes were not working smoothly, and more resources had been allocated to support teams when required. Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely and up to date. The local authority was acting to manage and reduce waiting times for assessment, care planning and reviews"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Kent":[{"q":[[10,"ASC Connect aimed to assess and intervene at the initial point of contact, understand emerging themes and gaps in provision, and connect individuals with the appropriate services to avoid unnecessary statutory intervention."],[12,"However, some staff worried they did not have the skills to support people with specialist support needs through the assessment process. For example, staff told us they were being asked to support assessments for people with learning disabilities and autistic people, which was not something they had done previously."],[12,"Audit findings identified 74% of people had a positive experience of adult social care, demographics of the cohort were not provided in the analysis we received. It was therefore not stated whether there were any similar characteristics for those who did not have a positive experience, which could have indicated additional learning and improvement."]],"m":"ASC Connect aimed to assess and intervene at the initial point of contact, understand emerging themes and gaps in provision, and connect individuals with the appropriate services to avoid unnecessary statutory intervention. Some partners raised concerns about the new, locality way of working of frontline teams which they said meant some staff did not always know or understand people’s specialist needs. Some partners raised concerns about the new, locality way of working of frontline teams which "},{"q":[[23,"Priorities included understanding where gaps in service delivery were, using technology to meet people’s needs, and ensuring capacity and resources could meet demand."],[25,"However, there was not always the organisations and groups available to direct people to. This had been fed back to senior leaders by partner organisations, but some partners told us they were not aware of any action being taken."],[27,"ASCS data for 2024 showed preventative services did not always have a positive impact on well-being outcomes for people, with 66.03% of people who had received short-term support no longer requiring support. This was significantly worse than the average for England of 79.39%."]],"m":"Priorities included understanding where gaps in service delivery were, using technology to meet people’s needs, and ensuring capacity and resources could meet demand. However, there was not always the organisations and groups available to direct people to. However, there was not always the organisations and groups available to direct people to. This had been fed back to senior leaders by partner organisations, but some partners told us they were not aware of any action being taken. ASCS data for"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[48,"The local authority’s Market Sustainability Plan in March 2023 indicated that 45.5% of homecare packages were purchased ‘off-contract’ in January 2023 compared to 18% in April 2022. This was due to a lack of capacity which resulted in more care packages being placed with providers outside the framework, often at a higher price and with less inbuilt scrutiny."],[50,"KCC had arrangements to monitor the quality and impact of the care and support services being commissioned for people and supported improvements where needed. However, this was not consistent across all localities."],[53,"Providers said there was a lack of understanding of the financial constraints providers were facing. The uplift offer in April 2024 for supported living and residential care was non-negotiable and well below the costs providers incurred."]],"m":"However, some providers did not always feel listened to or that their insight and feedback from their communities was utilised. However, partners said funding meant it was not always possible to be inclusive of people with lived experience. KCC told us there was an approximate 0.6% of invoices monthly which did not reconcile within the payments system. KCC were not always able to ensure there was sufficient care and support available to meet demand. People told us there was a lack of suitable, a"},{"q":[[56,"One person said they had to use services between health and social care, and found it was difficult to get them to talk to each other. They felt each organisation protected their culture and it would take time for them to become integrated."],[56,"In one example, one partner reflected positive improvements, such as joint work in East Kent between the local authority, integrated care partnership and primary care, needed more work to become countywide. They said at the top of the organisation there was a desire for the systems to be more integrated, but this didn’t always work operationally."],[57,"Partners told us engagement meetings with them had ‘dropped off’ since the teams’ restructure and there was a lack of insight into the outcomes of discussions during these meetings."]],"m":"Some partners told us the relationship between KCC and health was ‘poor’ with a lack of communication and engagement in joint priorities at all levels of the organisation and across the county, though there had been improvements at a strategic level. Some partners felt this had led to delays in hospital discharges in some parts of the county, with little or no explanation of the root cause or how delays were being addressed. One person said they had to use services between health and social care"},{"q":[[64,"In some instances, staff described a lack of guidance regarding implementation of new tools or team arrangements, leading to inconsistencies."],[65,"Paperwork needed to ensure continuity of care and safe transfers between services had to be sent manually and once a young person had transitioned there was a risk of gaps in information, for example, of a safeguarding nature."],[66,"The KCC Discharge Adult Social Care Commissioning Market Position Statement (2021-2026) identified disjointed discharge services across Kent due to a lack of coordination between KCC and the former NHS Kent and Medway Clinical Commissioning Groups which separately commissioned services in 2021."]],"m":"In some instances, staff described a lack of guidance regarding implementation of new tools or team arrangements, leading to inconsistencies. Staff told us transition panels worked well in highlighting transitions work but there were delays in picking up allocations in community teams. Staff generally thought the approach worked well but were not clear if earlier discussions about transitions in line with recognised good practice would be beneficial and support provision planning. Staff found el"},{"q":[[71,"As part of our assessment, we found a number of shortfalls which demonstrated safeguarding processes may not have always been effective in keeping people safe, for example the recent changes to practice and process that were being embedded and not well known at the time of the assessment by staff and partners."],[71,"The local authority’s move to community teams had created backlogs in safeguarding enquiries."],[73,"Partners had to navigate different approaches in the locality teams, such as inconsistent contact regarding outcomes and progress of concerns. This meant that people across the local authority risked different responses to safeguarding concerns, which may not have kept them safe."]],"m":"As part of our assessment, we found a number of shortfalls which demonstrated safeguarding processes may not have always been effective in keeping people safe, for example the recent changes to practice and process that were being embedded and not well known at the time of the assessment by staff and partners. The local authority’s move to community teams had created backlogs in safeguarding enquiries. Some were not fully clear on the impact of the changes as they felt there were remaining quest"},{"q":[[84,"However, the Cabinet Committees had no formal decision-making authority."],[84,"People and partners told us waiting times and communication of outcomes of assessments, poor communication of outcomes of safeguarding enquiries, and safe transitions between children’s and adult services were areas of learning and development."],[86,"Safeguarding hubs were also in development, with some staff concerned that unregistered staff had limited access to appropriate managerial support."]],"m":"However, the Cabinet Committees had no formal decision-making authority. People and partners told us waiting times and communication of outcomes of assessments, poor communication of outcomes of safeguarding enquiries, and safe transitions between children’s and adult services were areas of learning and development. Partners felt urgent work was needed on relationships and communication between KCC and service providers, including the voluntary and community sector. They felt there was a lack of"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Kirklees":[{"q":[[15,"Acknowledging that managing backlogs for assessments and reviews remained a concern, the local authority implemented changes at the front door, established an accommodation team focused on supporting people in long-term residential and nursing care, and introduced the waiting well policy."],[20,"Issues included unclear information about chargeable services, confusion about the financial assessment process, inaccurate details from health professionals, and delays. In response, they improved public communications, issued updated guidance for partners, initiated earlier assessments, and relocated resources."],[21,"Partners also shared there was not always a lot of clear information around the financial implications such as third-party top ups."]],"m":"Acknowledging that managing backlogs for assessments and reviews remained a concern, the local authority implemented changes at the front door, established an accommodation team focused on supporting people in long-term residential and nursing care, and introduced the waiting well policy. Despite these efforts, partners provided us with mixed feedback. They told us reviews were not always completed in a timely way, including both planned reviews and those triggered by a change in people's needs."},{"q":[[28,"Staff told us the integrated community equipment service supported timely delivery of equipment, maintaining continuity and reducing delays, with no waiting lists for equipment and good risk management in moving and handling despite a small backlog."],[29,"However, complaints were received due to limited assessor availability and contractor delays."],[29,"Although some people waited up to 8 weeks for installation, emergency referrals were prioritised within 24 hours, and senior managers reviewed any delays."]],"m":"Staff told us the integrated community equipment service supported timely delivery of equipment, maintaining continuity and reducing delays, with no waiting lists for equipment and good risk management in moving and handling despite a small backlog. However, complaints were received due to limited assessor availability and contractor delays. Although some people waited up to 8 weeks for installation, emergency referrals were prioritised within 24 hours, and senior managers reviewed any delays."},{"q":[[33,"People in Kirklees benefitted from the local authority’s focus on equity, inclusion and culturally responsive services."],[35,"Staff also shared autistic people often did not meet the criteria for learning disability services and were instead referred to mental health services. They highlighted a disconnect between mental health, learning disabilities, autism, and substance misuse services."],[38,"Staff confirmed concerns about the closure of face-to-face services, especially for those unable to use digital technology. They said the library offer was in its early stages and not all staff had adequate adult social care knowledge, though some could provide digital support when needed."]],"m":"Staff also shared autistic people often did not meet the criteria for learning disability services and were instead referred to mental health services. Staff also shared autistic people often did not meet the criteria for learning disability services and were instead referred to mental health services. They highlighted a disconnect between mental health, learning disabilities, autism, and substance misuse services. Staff confirmed concerns about the closure of face-to-face services, especially f"},{"q":[[41,"By comparing waiting lists with monitoring reports, the local authority improved accuracy and identified gaps in services for men. This improved inclusivity and resource allocation, ensuring support met people’s specific requirements and promoted earlier intervention."],[41,"A health partner told us population-level assessments had been strong, but due to limited integration of service-level data, services could not always be evaluated for how well they met population needs, affecting the ability to plan targeted interventions."],[45,"A gap in respite provision was corroborated by staff, who told us there were long waits for the unpaid carers respite sitting services, due to wait times."]],"m":"By comparing waiting lists with monitoring reports, the local authority improved accuracy and identified gaps in services for men. By comparing waiting lists with monitoring reports, the local authority improved accuracy and identified gaps in services for men. This improved inclusivity and resource allocation, ensuring support met people’s specific requirements and promoted earlier intervention. A health partner told us population-level assessments had been strong, but due to limited integratio"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[66,"Young people with a disability whose needs were being met solely by family did not meet the transitions pathway criteria, and one parent carer told us they had to wait until the young person was 18 before being able to access adult social care support, despite having informed the local authority that they would not be able to continue providing all support."],[66,"However, staff reported that limited capacity within assessment teams sometimes led to delays in allocating work, which could result in situations becoming unstable."],[68,"Both services were held in high esteem by staff, partners and leaders but we were told that the reablement service could be limited by its capacity to meet demand."]],"m":"However, some providers raised some concerns about the number of private providers entering the marketplace following the introduction of the new procurement system and whether limited commissioning and quality oversight of these providers could destabilise safety across the system. Young people with a disability whose needs were being met solely by family did not meet the transitions pathway criteria, and one parent carer told us they had to wait until the young person was 18 before being able "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[82,"People told us social workers did an amazing job with limited praise and recognition for such a challenging role. They raised concerns around the shortage in staff levels which they felt was having an impact on people waiting for assessment."],[86,"However, some told us they did not receive regular supervision, and any support from their line manager focused on managing workload. This inconsistency told us there is a gap in delivering reflective supervision across the whole workforce."],[86,"The local authority used data to help predict future demand and trends, which informed actions and decisions around service redesign, improvement planning, and workforce needs. They had a clear strategic vision embedded across departments, with a commitment to involve political leaders, partners, and people who used services in shaping improvements."]],"m":"People told us social workers did an amazing job with limited praise and recognition for such a challenging role. People told us social workers did an amazing job with limited praise and recognition for such a challenging role. They raised concerns around the shortage in staff levels which they felt was having an impact on people waiting for assessment. However, some told us they did not receive regular supervision, and any support from their line manager focused on managing workload. However, s"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Knowsley":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Barking and Dagenham":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Barnet":[{"q":[[10,"The local authority senior leaders had acknowledged a gap in people accessing information, advice and guidance and showed commitment to improving services by using a ‘mystery shopper’ exercise to gain further insight to the problem."],[15,"We were told that one carer had a 2-hour respite provision twice a week, but this was limited to specific times of the day, making it difficult for them to manage other responsibilities."],[15,"Another person had 12 nights of respite care per year, which was not being utilised due to the lack of available placements, leaving the unpaid carer without specific respite to meet their assessed needs."]],"m":"The local authority senior leaders had acknowledged a gap in people accessing information, advice and guidance and showed commitment to improving services by using a ‘mystery shopper’ exercise to gain further insight to the problem. There was a potential gap identified, where some people told us they did not receive a copy of their social care assessment and support plan, leaving them unsure on whether their preferences around care and support and choices were included in the assessment. Similar"},{"q":[[24,"Staff highlighted a potential gap regarding people’s ability to access services due to limited community transport, particularly for people who had mobility needs and limited support networks, and which potentially had an impact on their overall wellbeing and ability to maintain independence."],[29,"A recurring theme was the lack of guidance provided to unpaid carers. For example, one unpaid carer expressed frustration at not knowing how long it would take to arrange for a paid carer for the person they supported, and they felt extremely let down due to the absence of guidance."],[29,"Some unpaid carers were not aware of the services available, for example details for the contracted carers partner."]],"m":"Similarly, some partners told us many unpaid carers found accessing services confusing and many were not aware of all the services available to them. Partner organisations told us the main issues raised to them by people they support were related to employment and lack of social activities, however they told us the Bright Futures project funded by the local authority had been helpful. However, we were told by partner organisations that they were not always aware or informed about funding opportu"},{"q":[[36,"Partners organisations explained housing and homelessness was a concern due to limited housing availability, and the quality of available housing was not always adequate."],[40,"A partner told us they were not aware of the local authority using interpretation services to support contact with their community group. They felt language was not adapted to support the people and communities the local authority engaged with."],[41,"A partner fed back interpreters for people who had hearing loss could not be booked for urgent matters and the wait for support was at least 3 weeks. This meant people requiring BSL support were not receiving assessments in a timely manner."]],"m":"Partners organisations explained housing and homelessness was a concern due to limited housing availability, and the quality of available housing was not always adequate. Similarly, a person told us how they were supported with culturally appropriate food preparation, something they were not able to complete without support. The specific needs of people with hearing impairments were explained by the provider to the social worker undertaking each assessment, but feedback indicated that staffs lac"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[61,"However, a health partner told us about challenges around the shortage of specialist placements, which included supported accommodation, nursing and care home placements. As a result, this caused some delays to discharge, for example people ended up staying as mental health inpatients when they were clinically ready to be discharged."],[64,"We were also informed for some people, when transferred from one hospital to another, communication regarding their discharge planning was not transparent with either the person or their unpaid carer. As a result, they were not given information or choices about available support, sometimes equipment had already been installed in their home without their knowledge increasing the risk to their safety and well-being."],[65,"We heard contingency plans were not always documented. For example, an unpaid carer told us the person they were supporting had a known risk, but there was not a clear documented plan about how to manage this in their social care assessment."]],"m":"However, a health partner told us about challenges around the shortage of specialist placements, which included supported accommodation, nursing and care home placements. As a result, this caused some delays to discharge, for example people ended up staying as mental health inpatients when they were clinically ready to be discharged. We were also informed for some people, when transferred from one hospital to another, communication regarding their discharge planning was not transparent with eith"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Bexley":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[38,"The local authority told us they had responded to feedback highlighting gaps in provision for people with learning disabilities and autism, including extending support to families, such as specific carers support."],[40,"Staff and leaders told us about the accessible information available to people, for example, easy read formats, however staff went onto say accessibility did not extend to all people and leaders were aware of some gaps."],[41,"People told us communicating with the local authority as a person with learning disabilities was difficult: issues included long waits on the telephone and available options to reach a team were confusing."]],"m":"The local authority told us they had responded to feedback highlighting gaps in provision for people with learning disabilities and autism, including extending support to families, such as specific carers support. The local authority used grants to provide funding to small grassroots organisations to support communities when gaps in local support were identified and helped to meet needs. However, people’s experiences of these arrangements were not always consistent, with some people experiencing"},{"q":[[43,"The local authority acknowledged challenges such as limited extra-care accommodation and environmental constraints affecting new developments and worked with partners to explore alternative options."],[44,"Leaders demonstrated a commitment to improving access, personalisation, and integration of services, while seeking to address gaps in provision for unpaid carers, people with mental health needs and people affected by housing pressures."],[46,"The local authority worked to ensure there was sufficient capacity in local services to meet rising and increasingly complex demand, while addressing gaps in some areas of service provision, for example, nursing dementia care and supported housing for young people."]],"m":"Leaders recognised the complexity of issues across the borough, including gaps in mental health outcomes, increasing frailty, and housing constraints, and committed to finding local solutions in collaboration with people, providers, and professionals. The local authority acknowledged challenges such as limited extra-care accommodation and environmental constraints affecting new developments and worked with partners to explore alternative options. People did not always have access to a diverse ra"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[62,"However, some information sharing arrangements meant staff did not always have timely access to some essential health information. This meant people had to tell their stories twice, or health information was not known in order to inform holistic social care assessments."],[62,"Staff told us about effective handover processes between night and day staff to ensure information was not lost between services, with follow-up arranged by day staff where overnight intervention had taken place."],[65,"However, some contingency plans were not sufficiently robust, which meant risks were not always mitigated as effectively as intended."]],"m":"However, some information sharing arrangements meant staff did not always have timely access to some essential health information. However, some information sharing arrangements meant staff did not always have timely access to some essential health information. This meant people had to tell their stories twice, or health information was not known in order to inform holistic social care assessments. Staff told us about effective handover processes between night and day staff to ensure information"},{"q":[[67,"Safeguarding partnerships were not always effective, with a lack of clarity over roles and processes."],[69,"Several Safeguarding Adults Reviews (SARs) revealed safeguarding concerns were not escalated appropriately, even when indicators were present. For example, a SAR published in 2025 found repeated financial abuse went undetected across multiple agencies despite clear signs of isolation, anxiety, and unexplained debt."],[71,"There was not always clarity on what constituted a Section 42 (S42) safeguarding concern and when S42 safeguarding enquiries were required."]],"m":"Safeguarding partnerships were not always effective, with a lack of clarity over roles and processes. One person told us when their home-care provider was recording visits inaccurately, the social worker effectively supported them, and a new provider was found in a timely manner, which helped them feel safe and valued. This demonstrated where people were not safe, the local authority took action to resolve issues. The effectiveness of this oversight was not always clear based on the feedback fro"},{"q":[[75,"Data provided by the local authority demonstrated an improvement journey for safeguarding pathways and learning from SAR’s, however staff reported a lack of clarity in their roles."],[75,"Leaders identified that relatively low representation on some subgroups meant the voice of scrutiny was limited in some instances, for example direct payments, though they also described appropriate involvement to drive improvements."],[78,"Staff worked closely with housing teams to raise issues and influence decisions, aiming for greater alignment between housing, health, children’s services, and adult social care. This work was ongoing and was not yet fully embedded across all systems."]],"m":"Data provided by the local authority demonstrated an improvement journey for safeguarding pathways and learning from SAR’s, however staff reported a lack of clarity in their roles. Leaders identified that relatively low representation on some subgroups meant the voice of scrutiny was limited in some instances, for example direct payments, though they also described appropriate involvement to drive improvements. Leaders acknowledged improvements in ethnic representation and identified gaps in LGB"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Brent":[{"q":[[8,"People could not self-refer to be assessed through the local authority website however they could complete an enquiry form."],[10,"People confirmed the delays in relation to care provision. For example, one person explained an increase in a care package had been agreed but had not been actioned until some months later."],[11,"There were however delays in accessing beds in hospital and assessments being completed in a timely way. For example, out of hours in A & E there were delays in getting mental health assessments because of the number of cases and accessing appropriately qualified staff."]],"m":"People could not self-refer to be assessed through the local authority website however they could complete an enquiry form. Other comments included inconsistent information given following assessments and people having to repeat information due to high staff turnover. People confirmed the delays in relation to care provision. Staff told us about other challenges including requests for reviews of people with mental health needs in long-term placements when they were due for discharge. They did no"},{"q":[[21,"There was a lack of community services, and funding cuts in the community such as day centres and specific support groups, for example domestic violence support in the community."],[23,"Supply did not always keep up with new developments. For example, some analogue rather than digital equipment was still supplied, and available equipment did not always include up-to-date sensory equipment."],[24,"People could not always easily access information and advice on their rights under the Care Act and ways to meet their care and support needs. This included unpaid carers and people who funded or arranged their own care and support."]],"m":"There was a lack of community services, and funding cuts in the community such as day centres and specific support groups, for example domestic violence support in the community. Access to holistic support had reduced and partners were not able to meet broader needs. Supply did not always keep up with new developments. Staff told us the local authority gave them freedom to order bespoke equipment and did not question their professional judgement. Staff told us the local authority gave them freed"},{"q":[[30,"Staff had identified they did not receive safeguarding referrals from some under-represented groups for example, Roma, Gypsy and Traveller communities and Asian communities, which led to working with partners closely to raise awareness of this."],[32,"Information was not always accessible for people which could create anxiety. For example, letters sent to people, where English was not their first language, were not in simple language and this made the information difficult to understand."],[33,"In one case where translation services were not available for a person who was deaf, they had been able to use other means to support them in relation to housing challenges."]],"m":"Staff talked passionately about Brent, that it was an amazing place being one of the most diverse in Europe. They promoted social cohesion and told us the mobility of the community was a factor; however, many people were not mobile by choice but due to housing. Some staff felt there was a gap in support in the voluntary and community sector for women over 30 with long-term mental health needs. Staff had identified they did not receive safeguarding referrals from some under-represented groups for"},{"q":[[38,"The grant programme which provided funds to the voluntary sector was managed centrally and there was no clear connection with the commissioning function."],[39,"Feedback from local authority staff was there was a lack of understanding of autism spectrum disorder within the local authority and that there was also a gap in services to support people with this diagnosis. This had been fed back to leaders."],[40,"There was a middle group of people who were not in need of care support enough for specialist accommodation, but too ill for general housing and this was being reviewed through commissioning."]],"m":"The grant programme which provided funds to the voluntary sector was managed centrally and there was no clear connection with the commissioning function. Therefore, this lacked a focus on how the local authority were shaping the market for prevention and for reducing people’s needs for care and support. Some community partners felt there was a gap in provision for people living in hostels who were not in drug and alcohol related treatment and didn’t meet the threshold for intensive support. Ther"},{"q":[[49,"Some challenges were posed in accessing services, for example when a person did not have a formal mental health diagnosis which meant staff could be left trying to support people without the necessary tools or skills. This had been escalated to senior staff."],[49,"Partners confirmed there was strong partnership working with the local authority and health trusts. They explained day to day personal relationships were good and the day-to-day work was not affected by any specific challenges which arose."],[51,"Staff told us the lack of community and voluntary services available limited their ability to prevent needs for further services. Senior leaders told us they needed to do more to enable staff to consistently be part of the community."]],"m":"Sometimes assessments were not as joined up with health colleagues and there were delays. Other staff felt a closer link was needed and was a lack of collaboration at times. A partner suggested when the local authority attended meetings however roles could sometimes be better explained, and the language used less jargonistic, to ensure external people did not feel alienated. One partner told us there was limited strategic involvement with commissioners and funding was their main concern. One par"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Ealing":[{"q":[[10,"At the outset of the initiative, areas for improvement were noted in 60% of cases reviewed by the panel."],[13,"The local authority had over 550 reviews that had not been completed within 12 months at the time of our assessment."],[14,"A new review team had been implemented at the time of our assessment. It was not long established but intended to reduce waiting times for reviews."]],"m":"At the outset of the initiative, areas for improvement were noted in 60% of cases reviewed by the panel. Some staff felt it was difficult to genuinely support people to understand the choices available to them, especially when their first language was not English. Some staff felt it was difficult to genuinely support people to understand the choices available to them, especially when their first language was not English. This was easier for people supported by community teams but less so in prep"},{"q":[[24,"The provider of the contract changed in April 2023 and performance management information was limited at the time of our assessment."],[24,"Over 700 people were awaiting an occupational therapy assessment at the time of our assessment, and some people waited 20 weeks. This impacted on people’s experiences and outcomes."],[27,"The local authority provided data which showed improvements had been made in the last 12 months however, this data had not been published at the time of assessment."]],"m":"However, some carers told us that groups in their area had been stopped, and this was challenging as they were not able to travel to others. However, some carers told us that groups in their area had been stopped, and this was challenging as they were not able to travel to others. This affected people’s ability to engage in support in the community. The local authority provided us with data that showed improvements over the past 12 months however, this data was not yet published. Where people re"},{"q":[[31,"Some partners were worried that inequality would continue to rise and that services were not sufficient to meet the needs of communities."],[33,"Some partner organisations recognised they had few conversations about or limited representation from older Travellers. This may have limited the ability of care and support services to meet the needs of this community."],[33,"The direct payments booklet, for example, was not available in multiple languages."]],"m":"Some partners were worried that inequality would continue to rise and that services were not sufficient to meet the needs of communities. Some partner organisations recognised they had few conversations about or limited representation from older Travellers. Some partner organisations recognised they had few conversations about or limited representation from older Travellers. This may have limited the ability of care and support services to meet the needs of this community. The direct payments bo"},{"q":[[36,"Information was not always displayed in an easy-tounderstand way, such as infographics, which may have contributed to a communication gap between communities and services. This had been recognised by the local authority and improvements were being made in how information was presented to local communities."],[37,"Many carers had been part of consultation to develop the local authority’s carers’ strategy, but some organisations did not feel carers had been well consulted about other services, such as respite."],[42,"At the time of our assessment, the local authority did not have the analysis systems in place to outline the number of times people had to wait for their service to begin due to lack of capacity, or the average length of time people had to wait."]],"m":"Information was not always displayed in an easy-tounderstand way, such as infographics, which may have contributed to a communication gap between communities and services. Information was not always displayed in an easy-tounderstand way, such as infographics, which may have contributed to a communication gap between communities and services. This had been recognised by the local authority and improvements were being made in how information was presented to local communities. Many carers had been"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[59,"Where there were funding disputes, the local authority continued to provide funding and services until the dispute was resolved, ensuring young people were not left without services."],[59,"Staff were clear on actions to be completed following transfer between daytime teams into out of hours services, ensuring that people’s care and support was not disrupted."],[63,"Not all staff in adult services were clear on their role in identifying young carers. Where young people were carers, it was not clear how local authority services supported their transition to adult carer services. A partner organisation told us young carers were worried by the transition to adult services."]],"m":"Waiting lists contributed to confusion and administrative burden for other organisations, who felt unclear about progress or allocation. A voluntary sector provider told us that they felt that local services were disjointed and that they often supported people beyond their befriending remit because there was no one else to provide support for them. They told us that in particular, people found mental health services difficult to engage with and there was insufficient aftercare post discharge lea"},{"q":[[72,"The local authority told us in June 2024 that there were 42 section 42 safeguarding enquiries that were awaiting allocation to a social worker, once immediate risk issues had been addressed. People waited 7 weeks from the point of contact for an allocated social worker."],[67,"Once a safeguarding contact was assessed to have met the threshold for a section 42 safeguarding enquiry, these moved to an ‘awaiting worker allocation’ list. This was monitored daily, but systems were not in place at the time of our assessment to review and analyse the timeliness of this process and any trends over time."],[70,"Some staff felt there was a limited understanding of what constituted a safeguarding concern from partners in the borough, including within local authority services."]],"m":"We were told by leaders this was not a concern as the tool was most applicable to the ARC, who were the initial receivers of concerns where immediate actions are required. Once a safeguarding contact was assessed to have met the threshold for a section 42 safeguarding enquiry, these moved to an ‘awaiting worker allocation’ list. This was monitored daily, but systems were not in place at the time of our assessment to review and analyse the timeliness of this process and any trends over time. Some"},{"q":[[76,"Some partner agencies described several boards but told us that actions arising from them were sometimes limited, and that there were a lot of layers within the local authority that needed more joined-up working to be effective."],[77,"The PSW role included quality assurance of practice, in conjunction with the newly created role of safeguarding head of service. It was not always clear to staff how any practice findings collated through the PSW, for example through the Better Lives Review Panels, were analysed or aggregated from individual worker feedback to service level risk identification and improvement."],[77,"Analysis of data across some service areas was lacking which meant that oversight in those areas was more limited."]],"m":"The local authority was aware of areas where people were not achieving good outcomes and was in the process of implementing actions to resolve this. Many of these were in development or progress at the time of our assessment and the impact was not yet evident in all areas. Some partner agencies described several boards but told us that actions arising from them were sometimes limited, and that there were a lot of layers within the local authority that needed more joined-up working to be effectiv"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Enfield":[{"q":[[10,"Triage was generally completed within 24 hours and the team did not hold a caseload."],[10,"Staff did not impose their own values or opinions on people and allowed people to tell their own story. They spoke with people’s support networks to get a well-rounded picture of the person."],[12,"The local authority had established a dedicated review team to reduce the backlog of reviews."]],"m":"Triage was generally completed within 24 hours and the team did not hold a caseload. Staff did not impose their own values or opinions on people and allowed people to tell their own story. Staff did not impose their own values or opinions on people and allowed people to tell their own story. They spoke with people’s support networks to get a well-rounded picture of the person. However, they gave examples of inconsistent communication and lack of engagement, depending on which member of staff was"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[31,"The Enfield Joint Strategic Needs Assessment showed a large gap in life expectancy and healthy life expectancy between these areas."],[34,"However, resource or expertise in these areas was not always consistent. For example, staff told us there could be challenges sourcing deafblind interpretation."],[34,"We received some feedback from people using services that local authority information was not always accessible."]],"m":"However, there was limited evidence this was systematically reducing barriers and inequalities across the diversity of different communities in Enfield. The Enfield Joint Strategic Needs Assessment showed a large gap in life expectancy and healthy life expectancy between these areas. However, resource or expertise in these areas was not always consistent. Another partner told us that although they were involved in the production of the easy read material, they had not always understood the conte"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[59,"However, there were some gaps, particularly in accessing timely mental health support, and some service risk documents were not always completed."],[60,"However, multi-disciplinary safety systems were not always fully effective. For example, we received feedback from staff highlighting challenges with mental health practitioners not engaging with the local authority homelessness team, leading to cases of neglect and safeguarding concerns."],[60,"Risk management was not always recorded effectively. For example, some people’s assessment records did not always clearly outline if risks were present or had been mitigated."]],"m":"However, there were some gaps, particularly in accessing timely mental health support, and some service risk documents were not always completed. However, multi-disciplinary safety systems were not always fully effective. Risk management was not always recorded effectively. Some staff had access to both systems, but this was not universal. In cases where a young person has autism and/ or mental health support needs and it was unclear which was the best team to lead the assessment, a joint assess"},{"q":[[68,"Safeguarding concerns for people with mental health needs, who were open to mental health workers and in other specific cases, were actioned by the mental health trust as part of the local s75 agreement. This meant referrals were not always screened by the local authority MASH team and instead, referrals could go directly to the Mental HealthTrust through the local Single Point of Access (SPA)."],[73,"The safeguarding MASH, hoarding panel, mental health specialist hoarding support, and specialist cleaning providers ensured risks were managed around hoarding in cases where the person did not meet criteria for clinical mental health support."],[74,"The average time to process an application was 5 weeks, the median processing time was not provided."]],"m":"Safeguarding concerns for people with mental health needs, who were open to mental health workers and in other specific cases, were actioned by the mental health trust as part of the local s75 agreement. This meant referrals were not always screened by the local authority MASH team and instead, referrals could go directly to the Mental HealthTrust through the local Single Point of Access (SPA). Most care providers told us staff were skilled and knowledgeable with safeguarding, although experienc"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Haringey":[{"q":[[8,"People and partners told us contacting the local authority over the phone to request assessments or support was time consuming as it was difficult to get through to speak with staff."],[10,"Assessments and care planning arrangements were not always completed in a timely manner and there was a risk this would impact on people negatively. For example, a person told us they did not access day service support for 12 months due to delays in assessment processes."],[10,"The local authority had outsourced 600 assessments and reviews to reduce their backlog to coincide with their move to a locality model."]],"m":"Access to assessments and reviews was limited due to challenges contacting the local authority. People and partners told us contacting the local authority over the phone to request assessments or support was time consuming as it was difficult to get through to speak with staff. A person-centred approach was not always consistent, and this was reflected by data. Assessments and care planning arrangements were not always completed in a timely manner and there was a risk this would impact on people"},{"q":[[17,"The adult social care prevention strategy (2024-2026) was clear and concise but was not recorded as being coproduced."],[21,"Partners told us about the impact the waiting lists was having on people, for example, they were concerned for people’s welfare as there were delays in assessment and completion of home adaptations."],[23,"People could not always easily access information and advice on their rights under the Care Act and ways to meet their care and support needs. This included unpaid carers."]],"m":"The adult social care prevention strategy (2024-2026) was clear and concise but was not recorded as being coproduced. There were also plans for a refurbished mental health centre which would support people before they reached crisis point, but this project had experienced significant delays. However, the local authority told us these figures were not accurate due to an internal data recording issue. Partners told us about the impact the waiting lists was having on people, for example, they were "},{"q":[[29,"Impact of the EDI working group was not yet clear."],[31,"Another frontline team told us they had internally requested translated versions of documents, such as assessments, for people. This highlighted an inconsistent approach being taken by frontline teams."],[31,"A person’s relative told us communication needs were not considered before the person’s assessment, such as easy-read information, to prepare them for the process."]],"m":"Impact of the EDI working group was not yet clear. People’s experience of accessibility arrangements was not always positive, and this created barriers when accessing support. Partners told us there was a lack of information available in other accessible formats and said the local authority’s website did not include information in different languages for people whose first language was not English. The local authority’s website had a translation function for multiple languages, but the local aut"},{"q":[[34,"Due to its infancy, the impact of this approach was unclear, but there was some evidence the board had begun to influence processes such as quality assurance of services."],[34,"Care provision within the borough was limited, but the local authority worked closely with other North Central London (NCL) boroughs to provide support for people."],[35,"Available provision within the borough made a person-centred model more limited. For example, staff told us there was a lack of available placements for people at a higher risk of falls or needing 1:1 support."]],"m":"Due to its infancy, the impact of this approach was unclear, but there was some evidence the board had begun to influence processes such as quality assurance of services. People’s access to a diverse range of local support options which were effective, affordable, and high-quality was inconsistent. Care provision within the borough was limited, but the local authority worked closely with other North Central London (NCL) boroughs to provide support for people. Staff and partners told us about gap"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[52,"Advocacy services were accessed by staff where required, but assignment of advocates was not always timely."],[53,"The hospital discharge team told us they were now at full staffing capacity but recognised there could be delays to hospital discharges. They told us there were sometimes disagreements on the level of support required for people on discharge between staff and health partners."],[57,"However, details around how they planned with carers to minimise risk when they could not fulfil their caring duties were vague."]],"m":"Advocacy services were accessed by staff where required, but assignment of advocates was not always timely. Some partners felt communication concerns and delays to discharge were due to a high workload and a lack of availability of local authority workforce. The hospital discharge team told us they were now at full staffing capacity but recognised there could be delays to hospital discharges. The hospital discharge team told us they were now at full staffing capacity but recognised there could b"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[67,"There was not an up-to-date Carers Strategy however, with the previous strategy running between 2020-2023."],[67,"A lack of an up-to-date strategy for carers was significant due to the negative experiences of unpaid carers reflected in this assessment report."],[69,"While there were data reports and dashboards for leaders, a senior leader also told us they did not always receive regular performance information about adult social care. Despite this, they told us they did regularly meet other leaders, and they were proactive in seeking information which supported them in their role."]],"m":"However, systems and processes had not always supported people to have positive outcomes. However, systems and processes had not always supported people to have positive outcomes. This was particularly evident in the experiences of unpaid carers and people awaiting assessments. There was evidence of some improvement in outcomes, and this coincided with ongoing transformations, but the impact of these changes was not clear and some concerns remained. There was not an up-to-date Carers Strategy ho"},{"q":[[71,"Partners told us coproduction was not well embedded and this was recognised by the local authority. For example, a partner told us it was not clear how coproduction was influencing change."],[71,"We were told when there was effective coproduction, the local authority did not always follow through with projects."],[73,"Staff gave several examples of opportunities for progression, but some staff teams shared staff moving to more senior roles put pressure on services on the frontline as they were not replaced with permanent staff."]],"m":"Partners told us coproduction was not well embedded and this was recognised by the local authority. We were told when there was effective coproduction, the local authority did not always follow through with projects. Staff gave several examples of opportunities for progression, but some staff teams shared staff moving to more senior roles put pressure on services on the frontline as they were not replaced with permanent staff. People and partners did not always feel listened to at a strategic le"}],"Harrow":[{"q":[[11,"Interpreter and translation services were readily available to support teams and people when working with people for whom English was not their first language."],[12,"A new borough wide planning and reviewing team had been introduced to help tackle a backlog of incomplete annual reviews."],[17,"People were not always confident their views would be heard by the panel. They expressed that they believed they should be involved in it themselves, given the impact the decisions could have on them."]],"m":"Local support organisations told us some people found the pathway to gain access to support was not always clear and that they often struggled to find information about local authority services. By contrast, some unpaid carers told us they felt they were not always consulted on their loved one's assessments, and carer’s assessments were not always carried out sensitively. People also told us they often had to repeat their stories when speaking with local authority staff as they did not have a si"},{"q":[[21,"We heard from some people however that reablement services team had not improved their independence, reduced or delayed their further health and social care needs and there were concerns that poor communication between partners were leading to safeguarding concerns."],[22,"The leadership team were aware of the issues, which had affected much of Greater London, and that work was underway to address the current shortfall."],[22,"The service worked with people for a time limited period (12 weeks) to provide one to one personalised support to achieve goals focused on improving their mental health."]],"m":"We heard from some people however that reablement services team had not improved their independence, reduced or delayed their further health and social care needs and there were concerns that poor communication between partners were leading to safeguarding concerns. Local authority staff told us there had been issues with the jointly commissioned community equipment service. This had led to some delays in discharging people home from hospital while they waited for occupational therapy equipment "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[34,"However, there was currently a lack of suitable housing for emergency placements, for example to facilitate discharge for homeless people who presented at hospital and needed short term care support with their mental health, rather than an acute admission."],[35,"The local authority’s Market Sustainability and Improvement plan demonstrated they were aware of capacity gaps in the market. These were for suitable local placements in nursing, residential and nursing dementia, specific cultural needs and suitable provision for those with complex needs."],[40,"The local authority collaborated with care providers to ensure that the cost of care was transparent and fair. They had not had any contracts handed back relating to care homes, home care or supported living during the previous 12 months and we were not made aware of any providers who had left the market in the last 12 months."]],"m":"The local authority’s Market Sustainability and Improvement plan demonstrated they were aware of capacity gaps in the market. The local authority’s Market Sustainability and Improvement plan demonstrated they were aware of capacity gaps in the market. These were for suitable local placements in nursing, residential and nursing dementia, specific cultural needs and suitable provision for those with complex needs. However, there was currently a lack of suitable housing for emergency placements, fo"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[50,"Systems were in place to access referral pathways and safeguarding teams out of hours. For example, the Emergency Duty Team accessed referral systems and had support from Careline Personal Alarm Service and on-call managers."],[50,"Care and support were planned and organised with people and the people who were important to them, to improve their safety across their care journeys and ensure continuity in care. This included referrals, admissions and discharge, and where people were moving between services."],[51,"When people moved from one local authority area to another, the local authority took steps to ensure there was no gap in service."]],"m":"When people moved from one local authority area to another, the local authority took steps to ensure there was no gap in service."},{"q":[[53,"The board used one set of data to inform about timeliness, which did not align with the council’s performance data following changes to the triage process."],[55,"The local authority did not have any section 42 enquires awaiting allocation as they were immediately assigned to staff for investigation."],[55,"The safeguarding team triaged all referrals from partners which included high volumes of referrals which did not meet the safeguarding threshold and were signposted elsewhere."]],"m":"The board used one set of data to inform about timeliness, which did not align with the council’s performance data following changes to the triage process. The local authority did not have any section 42 enquires awaiting allocation as they were immediately assigned to staff for investigation. There was a clear rationale and outcome from initial enquiries, including those which did not progress to a section 42 enquiry. The safeguarding team triaged all referrals from partners which included high"},{"q":[[59,"One team told us they were not confident the data for waiting lists was accurate."],[59,"Whilst the local authority accepted that data was an area for improvement, this shortfall meant that local authority processes were not working well at the time of the assessment. This did not always provide consistent oversight and produce good outcomes for people overall."],[60,"Staffing levels and funding were however impacting on frontline services and actions were not always met. For example, postdiagnosis dementia support for carers was not always available."]],"m":"One team told us they were not confident the data for waiting lists was accurate. Another told us they did not believe all their work was being captured by the local authority’s data system and data the leaders used did not reflect the breadth of the work they did. Whilst the local authority accepted that data was an area for improvement, this shortfall meant that local authority processes were not working well at the time of the assessment. Whilst the local authority accepted that data was an a"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Lambeth":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Lancashire":[{"q":[[13,"However, we heard frequent feedback from local people, providers and partners that it was difficult to access the local authority’s care and support services by telephone. They told us the local authority telephone system was difficult to navigate with confusing menus."],[15,"The service had invested additional funding to address the backlog of annual reviews and had made steady progress."],[15,"In 2023 the local authority conducted a pilot of OTs and OTSOs in the customer access service to provide more direct and timely support people with OT needs. It was successful in resolving most equipment needs before people required a social care assessment but was not continued beyond the pilot."]],"m":"However, we heard frequent feedback from local people, providers and partners that it was difficult to access the local authority’s care and support services by telephone. However, we heard frequent feedback from local people, providers and partners that it was difficult to access the local authority’s care and support services by telephone. They told us the local authority telephone system was difficult to navigate with confusing menus. There were concerns that some call operatives did not have"},{"q":[[30,"However, there were long waits for Occupational Therapy assessments, and people from different districts across Lancashire had divergent experiences of home adaptations."],[32,"The local authority was not a Housing Authority and had no statutory responsibility under the Housing Act or the Care Act to provide suitable housing for the population."],[32,"We received consistent feedback that current housing stock limited options for adaptations, and this was limiting some people’s lives. For example, people being restricted to certain rooms in their home because of equipment needs."]],"m":"Some partners felt preventative initiatives were not yet showing impact and local hospitals were seeing an increase in the number of ‘social’ admissions. People unable to be discharged directly from hospital to home were offered intermediate care beds if possible, or a spot purchased care home bed. There was a single intermediate care offer across Lancashire, however partner organisations and staff reported that the intermediate care offer differed across localities and capacity did not always m"},{"q":[[39,"The local authority had provided training on the care needs of older LBGTQ+ people to help staff to be more aware of people's needs, but there was limited involvement of local LGBTQ+ groups in service development or co-production."],[41,"There were recognised gaps in inclusion and accessibility arrangements to support people to engage with the local authority in ways that worked for them, for example British Sign Language (BSL) translation and accessible publications."],[42,"Some information and support services were online only, and we heard examples of people’s frustrations if they did not have access to a computer or email address to contact the local authority."]],"m":"However, there was limited evidence it was systematically reducing barriers and inequalities across different communities in Lancashire. However, most partner NHS trusts reported limited collaboration with the local authority around inequalities and instead were working directly with grassroots VCSE organisations at district level. The local authority had provided training on the care needs of older LBGTQ+ people to help staff to be more aware of people's needs, but there was limited involvement"},{"q":[[46,"The local authority understood specific challenges for different populations in different geographical areas and used data to identify gaps in services."],[46,"However, some local partners reported insufficient attention to more rural areas, with limited public transport links or services, which impacted on people’s access in those areas."],[47,"However, many local VCSE organisations highlighted a need for improved partnership working in the planning, development and delivery of services. They reported limited involvement in identifying service gaps where people needed them."]],"m":"The local authority understood specific challenges for different populations in different geographical areas and used data to identify gaps in services. However, some local partners reported insufficient attention to more rural areas, with limited public transport links or services, which impacted on people’s access in those areas. However, many providers reported a lack of communication and collaboration by the local authority. However, many providers reported a lack of communication and collab"},{"q":[[58,"Practitioners in some teams highlighted limited engagement, communication and information sharing by healthcare staff was a barrier to joined up working."],[60,"A history of challenged relationships between the local authority and local health partners had legacy implications which limited integration and partnership working."],[61,"Social care staff carried out assessments and care packages ‘without prejudice’, with funding agreed afterwards to reduce Section 117 delays so people were not left waiting for the care they needed."]],"m":"However, the integration agenda was not working well in all areas, and there was a need to improve relationships between the local authority, health partners and some VCSE organisations. Partner organisations highlighted insufficient oversight of some long-established pooled budget arrangements. However, other trusts highlighted complex and reactive discharge pathways, and insufficient local capacity for care hours, recovery placements and housing, which delayed discharges. Practitioners in some"},{"q":[[74,"People and providers reported difficulties getting through to an appropriate member of staff to seek support if they did not have a point of contact such as a named social worker."],[75,"Records access varied and only some teams were able to access health partner systems with limited access rights. This did not support good overview of people’s recent interventions or risks."],[75,"Long waiting lists for assessments and reviews were recorded as a ‘red’ risk on the service risk register."]],"m":"The local authority’s systems and processes did not ensure consistent support and safety management for all people. We received feedback from people, their carers and local partners about limited continuity of care and regular change of practitioners, which impacted on risk management for individuals. Some people and families found it difficult to access practitioners in the service and felt unsupported as a result. People and providers reported difficulties getting through to an appropriate mem"},{"q":[[70,"However, many local providers and partners told us they struggled to engage with the local authority safeguarding team and get feedback about safeguarding concerns. They found the local authority’s online portal and self-reported risk rating system was inflexible and did not support professional autonomy or consistency."],[70,"Senior leaders had approved the commissioning of an external agency and recruited 54 Best Interest Assessors (BIA) to reduce the backlog of DoLS applications awaiting assessment. This had reduced wait times and improved the number of people waiting well with regular calls and triaging to prioritise people correctly."],[72,"There were systems and processes to make safeguarding personal, however these were not consistently applied in practice."]],"m":"However, many local providers and partners told us they struggled to engage with the local authority safeguarding team and get feedback about safeguarding concerns. They found the local authority’s online portal and self-reported risk rating system was inflexible and did not support professional autonomy or consistency. Many partners and providers stated they were not informed of the outcomes of their safeguarding referrals and they considered safeguarding was done to, not with providers. Others"},{"q":[[83,"However, governance and oversight systems did not provide sufficient information or transparency to support all elected members in their scrutiny of the local authority’s Care Act obligations."],[83,"Opposition elected members told us scrutiny functions were not working effectively and senior leaders reported insufficient scrutiny of adult social care services in council meetings. For example, elected members told us they had received limited information about long DoLS waiting lists, which meant this had not been discussed in council meetings."],[86,"A 2024 Serious Incident review identified poor record-keeping practices, with case notes written months after events and a lack of completed assessments."]],"m":"Some felt their concerns about implementation of new processes were not listened to. However, governance and oversight systems did not provide sufficient information or transparency to support all elected members in their scrutiny of the local authority’s Care Act obligations. Opposition elected members told us scrutiny functions were not working effectively and senior leaders reported insufficient scrutiny of adult social care services in council meetings. There was frustration amongst some opp"},{"q":[[88,"However, local authority Academy reports identified some newly recruited staff were not receiving regular formal supervision and support, although informal supervision through case progression was readily available in most teams."],[88,"The most common reasons given for this were manager changes, high staff sickness rates and vacancies, and high numbers of newly qualified practitioners requiring higher frequency of supervision that could not be supported in some teams."],[88,"There was recorded feedback of insufficiently experienced staff in some service areas, and managers being too busy with their own work to be available to support new staff. This was impacting on staff retention, with some new recruits leaving because of stressful working environment and lack of support."]],"m":"However, local authority Academy reports identified some newly recruited staff were not receiving regular formal supervision and support, although informal supervision through case progression was readily available in most teams. The most common reasons given for this were manager changes, high staff sickness rates and vacancies, and high numbers of newly qualified practitioners requiring higher frequency of supervision that could not be supported in some teams. There was recorded feedback of in"}],"Leeds":[{"q":[[9,"The Contact Centre provided a 'Front Door' access to adult social care and when social work intervention was not required, the Contact Centre signposted people to other services."],[11,"Data provided by the local authority in May 2025 showed there were 906 people waiting for a Care Act assessment with a median wait time of 19 days and the longest wait being 357 days."],[12,"A dedicated integrated reviewing team was in place, reducing waiting times for reviews and to address the backlogs, focusing on those with the longest wait."]],"m":"The Contact Centre provided a 'Front Door' access to adult social care and when social work intervention was not required, the Contact Centre signposted people to other services. Most unpaid carers had been involved in the assessment process although we also heard examples where there had been a lack of communication with unpaid carers, and they had not been consulted about the plans for their family member. Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely and up to date. Staff t"},{"q":[[20,"We received some positive feedback from people about the care and support they received, but this was not always the case."],[28,"However, we also heard from a partner some information on the Leeds Directory website was not always easy to find."],[30,"There was not a good uptake of direct payments in Leeds."]],"m":"We received some positive feedback from people about the care and support they received, but this was not always the case. Several unpaid carers told us their health had been adversely affected by caring responsibilities, and they lacked the adequate support for this. Another unpaid carer told us they did not have enough support for their mental health which made them feel alone and isolated. However, we were told some people with a learning disability received financial assessment documents in "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Leicester":[{"q":[[10,"Information in nonEnglish languages was not easily accessible, especially on the local authority's website. This lack of accessibility posed a challenge for the city's residents, especially for those whose main language was not English; this group comprised 30% of Leicester City's population in 2023."],[10,"The city's high levels of deprivation further exacerbated access to care and support services with many residents facing digital exclusion, rendering them unable to access online advice and guidance."],[12,"While the local authority's crisis response team had a 2-hour response time and some individuals reported timely responses to support requests, others experienced long waits. For example, some people said they waited over 7 months without communication from the local authority."]],"m":"However, others said they faced challenges when their face-to-face assessments were cancelled and replaced with telephone assessments and reviews. This led to feelings of detachment from their care and support and a perception that the process was not truly person-centred. Information in nonEnglish languages was not easily accessible, especially on the local authority's website. Information in nonEnglish languages was not easily accessible, especially on the local authority's website. This lack "},{"q":[[20,"Leaders told us young carers were supported by Children’s Social Care, and they delivered support through young carers groups and activities, however, it was not clear how adults social care linked in with this."],[24,"Partners were concerned that people were signposted to digital solutions but did not have access to online services and therefore could not access these support resources."],[26,"Therefore, the reasons suggested that the majority of cancellations for direct payments were for legitimate reasons and not through a lack of support from the local authority."]],"m":"Despite these efforts, staff and people expressed concerns around a lack of support for young carers. Leaders told us young carers were supported by Children’s Social Care, and they delivered support through young carers groups and activities, however, it was not clear how adults social care linked in with this. The delays were not aligned with the prevent, reduce and delay agenda and focus was needed by the local authority to ensure adequate and timely OT assessments. However, temporary resourc"},{"q":[[28,"Conversely, Asian individuals across all age groups were underrepresented, while working-age Black adults were notably over-represented in assessment activities."],[29,"An analysis of the data revealed that adult social care services were not equally accessed by all."],[29,"However, the strategy lacked specific details regarding targeted initiatives undertaken by the local authority to support the underrepresented communities identified."]],"m":"However, they did not outline specific steps for improving the quality of data on these latter aspects. Conversely, Asian individuals across all age groups were underrepresented, while working-age Black adults were notably over-represented in assessment activities. An analysis of the data revealed that adult social care services were not equally accessed by all. However, the strategy lacked specific details regarding targeted initiatives undertaken by the local authority to support the underrepr"},{"q":[[33,"The JSNA was used by the local authority to identify health inequalities, gaps in services and identifying unmet needs."],[34,"Additionally, a significant portion of the population provided unpaid care, with 7.7% of residents offering support, many of whom provided over 50 hours per week. It also found that family carers often lacked adequate support and resources, impacting their ability to provide care effectively."],[34,"Furthermore, over 57,000 residents reported disabilities that limited their daily activities."]],"m":"The JSNA was used by the local authority to identify health inequalities, gaps in services and identifying unmet needs. Additionally, a significant portion of the population provided unpaid care, with 7.7% of residents offering support, many of whom provided over 50 hours per week. It also found that family carers often lacked adequate support and resources, impacting their ability to provide care effectively. Furthermore, over 57,000 residents reported disabilities that limited their daily acti"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[54,"Although these policies and procedures were detailed and informative, they lacked specificity regarding individual responsibilities and contingency procedures if the designated person was unavailable."],[55,"Although the local authority had introduced a 'waiting well' process/approach, it was not yet embedded across adult social care and thus did not effectively mitigate this risk."],[55,"Partners also reported that information conveyed through the local authority systems during hospital discharge could be inconsistent and lack detail rendering them inaccurate of the person’s care needs which could impact the support they received post discharge."]],"m":"Although these policies and procedures were detailed and informative, they lacked specificity regarding individual responsibilities and contingency procedures if the designated person was unavailable. Although the local authority had introduced a 'waiting well' process/approach, it was not yet embedded across adult social care and thus did not effectively mitigate this risk. Partners also reported that information conveyed through the local authority systems during hospital discharge could be in"},{"q":[[59,"However, all alerts had to be signed off by team leaders, who were responsible for risk assessing and ensuring the immediate safety of individuals, though the procedure was not documented."],[60,"While leaders referred to the MAPP detailed on the safeguarding adults board website, staff did not reference it when asked about policies and procedures, however they did refer to their line manager for support."],[60,"There was significant responsibility placed on team leaders for managing safeguarding alerts, but they could not demonstrate robust oversight for ensuring the safe and effective management of safeguarding alerts and enquiries within teams."]],"m":"However, the local authority did not have localised procedures or staff guidance for ‘in-house’ safeguarding enquiries or for causing other agencies to undertake enquiries. However, all alerts had to be signed off by team leaders, who were responsible for risk assessing and ensuring the immediate safety of individuals, though the procedure was not documented. Conversely, if something was assigned as high risk, it was unclear if immediate action was taken. Leaders stated that guidance for assigni"},{"q":[[69,"While the governance structure shared with us did not detail each group's specific responsibilities, these were outlined within certain strategies. For instance, the Adult Social Care Operational Strategy specified that the directors for adult social care were the strategy owners, reporting to corporate governance structures, including the Learning and Improvement Board."],[69,"However, the new strategy did not evaluate the outcomes or impact from the previous strategy and there was no evidence submitted in relation to this, therefore we were also unable to assess the progress and impact made from the previous strategy."],[70,"However, there was limited evidence that leaders were effectively following up actions from these scrutiny meetings. For example, in the papers for 14th November 2024, a discussion on \"deep dive into race equity\" identified that White, Black, and Dual Heritage working-age adults were disproportionately more likely to be the subject of a contact, whereas Asian working-age adults were less likely."]],"m":"While the governance structure shared with us did not detail each group's specific responsibilities, these were outlined within certain strategies. However, the new strategy did not evaluate the outcomes or impact from the previous strategy and there was no evidence submitted in relation to this, therefore we were also unable to assess the progress and impact made from the previous strategy. However, there was limited evidence that leaders were effectively following up actions from these scrutin"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Leicestershire":[{"q":[[16,"People were contacted and informed of the potential delays in having an assessment and were given contact details of services to support them to remain well whilst they waited. They were advised to contact the local authority should their needs change and staff confirmed they gave people this advice when they checked in with them."],[17,"Data provided by the local authority in April 2025 showed of the 224 out of county placements, 186 were completed within the 12 months, and 37 were outstanding with 32 being overdue between 1 and 6 months."],[21,"People were given help, advice and information about how to access services, facilities and other agencies for help with non-eligible care and support needs, however the local authority were not able to report on the effectiveness of this offer."]],"m":"A partner agency had conducted a survey about people's experiences of accessing adult social care. This found that pathways and processes were not always clear; people did not always know where the process started and said they became frustrated at having to repeat their story multiple times. Staff told us there was sometimes internal movement of staff between localities to meet changes in demand, however they were concerned at the time of the assessment that staffing capacity was not sufficient"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[36,"Avoiding non-essential hospital admissions and promoting early discharges aimed to ensure people with a learning disability were not caused undue distress."],[37,"Occupational Therapy teams took into consideration different communities for example some cultures did not use a shower or bath, so they have added low down taps or equipment to assist with washing."],[37,"Housing leaders told us the local authority had a good understanding of homelessness across the county, for example they reported in the Blaby district council had 98 families in temporary accommodation, these inequalities were noted in the Joint Strategic Needs Assessment."]],"m":"Avoiding non-essential hospital admissions and promoting early discharges aimed to ensure people with a learning disability were not caused undue distress. Occupational Therapy teams took into consideration different communities for example some cultures did not use a shower or bath, so they have added low down taps or equipment to assist with washing. Housing leaders told us the local authority had a good understanding of homelessness across the county, for example they reported in the Blaby di"},{"q":[[41,"The local authority and Health and Wellbeing board had recognised gaps in the provision of residential care to meet complex needs, and a 16% reduction in nursing home beds across 2023."],[43,"Staff noted a lack of day service choice for people needing learning disability and mental health services, particularly in Hinckley and Bosworth."],[45,"People experienced delays in availability of supported living, with data from August 24 showing some people were waiting 47 weeks from the date the referral was received to the date the person moved in."]],"m":"Leaders understood on gaps in provision for people needing support with their mental health. The JSNA feedback from unpaid carers survey identified low levels of satisfaction in services with 62% of respondents saying there was insufficient range of services to meet the physical needs of the person they cared for, and 54% said there was insufficient services to meet their mental health needs. The local authority and Health and Wellbeing board had recognised gaps in the provision of residential c"},{"q":[[52,"Staff shared that the process of applying for funding was particularly difficult due to a 28-day referral window which often did not allow sufficient time to gather and upload the necessary information."],[54,"Staff managing crisis interventions valued the strong multi-disciplinary approach but noted this support did not extend to learning disability and autism teams which, may have put people at a disadvantage."],[54,"Staff shared how the “Worcestershire judgement” regarding section 117 aftercare had caused some delays to agreements regarding supporting people."]],"m":"Staff shared that the process of applying for funding was particularly difficult due to a 28-day referral window which often did not allow sufficient time to gather and upload the necessary information. Although staff had reported this issue had been escalated, it was unclear if it had been formally raised with system leaders. Staff managing crisis interventions valued the strong multi-disciplinary approach but noted this support did not extend to learning disability and autism teams which, may "},{"q":[[56,"Leaders told us that safety was a priority for the local authority, and it understood the risks to people across their care journeys, for example planned and unplanned interruptions in service provision, being ‘lost’ between services due to gaps in access/ referral criteria, timely planning for transition between services, agencies or across local authority boundaries, funding disputes leading to delays in provision etc."],[56,"However, these were not always fully effective, for example, significant waiting times and numbers were evident at the time of our assessment despite active work to reduce this."],[57,"However, there were capacity pressures across the approved mental health practitioner (AMHP) service due to limited staffing resource."]],"m":"Leaders told us that safety was a priority for the local authority, and it understood the risks to people across their care journeys, for example planned and unplanned interruptions in service provision, being ‘lost’ between services due to gaps in access/ referral criteria, timely planning for transition between services, agencies or across local authority boundaries, funding disputes leading to delays in provision etc. However, these were not always fully effective, for example, significant wa"},{"q":[[66,"More recently, there was increasing risk presented through potential licence revocation for overseas workforce, with potential continuity risks care provision should workers be unable to continue working in the UK."],[68,"Cases that were not allocated to a locality team remained the responsibility of the safeguarding team There were risk management processes in place to reduce risks to people’s wellbeing and swift action was taken to address immediate risks."],[70,"Further work was required to understand the length of time taken for a decision in the remaining 50%, and the risks this presented to people as this was not clear from the data available."]],"m":"More recently, there was increasing risk presented through potential licence revocation for overseas workforce, with potential continuity risks care provision should workers be unable to continue working in the UK. There was a high number of DoLS applications made to the local authority and there was a backlog in assessments and authorisations. We were told there were various reasons for delays in allocating DoLS applications which included, the level of demand versus capacity, the risk level, a"},{"q":[[74,"Members received regular updates on adult social care performance and delivery of Care Act duties through in person meetings with senior leaders and written reports."],[74,"Members visited local care services to look at commissioning activity and service delivery and hear from people about their experiences and outcomes. They told us this brings them closer to the strategic direction of adult social care."],[77,"The local authority used information about risks, performance, inequalities, and outcomes to shape the Adults and Communities strategy 2020-24, with the thread of driving performance and improving outcomes for the people of Leicestershire County Council."]],"m":""},{"q":[[80,"Staff gave examples of improvement suggestions; however, the staff members we spoke with were not informed if these were considered or included in the strategy."],[80,"Staff told us they had ongoing access to learning and support so they could deliver their Care Act duties safely and effectively. They told us they felt valued and supported in their roles, however some staff said that workloads were not always manageable."],[81,"The local authority and system partners demonstrated a commitment to understanding peoples experiences and learn how to shape services to reduce some of the challenges faced by unpaid carers however it was not clear at the time of our assessment what changes had been made to improve the experience of unpaid carers."]],"m":"Staff gave examples of improvement suggestions; however, the staff members we spoke with were not informed if these were considered or included in the strategy. Staff told us they had ongoing access to learning and support so they could deliver their Care Act duties safely and effectively. They told us they felt valued and supported in their roles, however some staff said that workloads were not always manageable. However, it was unclear how the impact of learning was evidenced or embedded and h"}],"Lincolnshire":[{"q":[[11,"However, these methods were not always successful, and people’s feedback indicated there was more to do to ensure people received a timely response when they contacted different parts of the service such as locality teams."],[17,"Local authority data showed in November 2025, 218 people were waiting for a financial assessment, reflecting that there remained delays in outcomes of assessments for people."],[17,"We heard some examples from staff and people where delays to financial assessments had resulted in people being in debt due to not being able to consider the financial implications of accepting support services."]],"m":"However, these methods were not always successful, and people’s feedback indicated there was more to do to ensure people received a timely response when they contacted different parts of the service such as locality teams. Leaders told us strengths-based approaches were not always used consistently. Other partners told us they were not always included in people’s annual reviews of their support, which meant they were unable to provide feedback on how the person’s care supported them. Feedback fr"},{"q":[[22,"The local authority had been impacted by the sudden liquidation of its equipment supplier which had impacted delivery times for necessary equipment, and although an alternate service had been commissioned, staff told us this had led to backlogs which were still being worked through."],[22,"The median wait time was 27 days; however, this was calculated from assessments which had been started and therefore did not adequately capture the experience of people who were still waiting for assessment and how long they had waited."],[22,"Data provided by the local authority in August 2025 showed 98.4% people were assessed within 4 weeks of referral to the local authority, however, staff told us the data was not wholly reliable as their manual systems recorded on spreadsheets indicated people had actually waited longer."]],"m":"The local authority had taken steps to identify people with needs for care and support that were not being met. Partners told us about limited transport for people who lived in rural or coastal areas which impacted their ability to access support. While some people had fed back improvements in quality of life and wellbeing, some people could not always access equipment and minor home adaptations to maintain their independence and continue living in their own homes. The local authority had been i"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[34,"The majority of these services were located in the West of the county and this made it more difficult to access for those living elsewhere in the area."],[34,"The JNSA informed that there were gaps in identifying autistic people, due to the shortage of skilled professionals in autism and the lack of pathways for autistic people. In response to this, the local authority had introduced an Autism Partnership Board as highlighted in their ‘All Age Autism Strategy’ (2023-2028)."],[39,"However, rurality and limited availability of care home beds sometimes challenged the team resulting in people with mental health needs being taken to hospital when a communitybased alternative would have been preferable."]],"m":"Staff told us local provision for more complex care, such as complex mental health, was sometimes limited. The majority of these services were located in the West of the county and this made it more difficult to access for those living elsewhere in the area. Staff and partners also told us it was difficult to access supported accommodation for people with learning disabilities and people with mental health needs. Partners told us the county was heavily reliant on residential and nursing care, wh"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[50,"Some operational challenges were identified in EDT with staff telling us they often only had access to limited information due to care records sometimes being incomplete or difficult to navigate."],[51,"Front line staff explained gaps in service provision created risks for people across pathways and transitions. For example, a lack of transport services impacted people being able to access the right service when they needed them, and staff reported limited interpreter availability which created barriers in accessing support with communication when needed."],[54,"The local authority told us it had been undertaking improvement work in this area in the past 12 months as reporting did not, at that time, allow for straightforward identification of reviews relating specifically to out-of-county placements."]],"m":"Some operational challenges were identified in EDT with staff telling us they often only had access to limited information due to care records sometimes being incomplete or difficult to navigate. Staff also worked across two different IT systems which were not connected. Staff told us this caused delays in understanding needs and required additional clarification and created risk during urgent decision making. However, staff told us handovers were not always operationally effective. Front line s"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Liverpool":[{"q":[[14,"Staff told us the new model had allowed them to understand risks and utilise team strengths, however this process was very new, and it was not known or evidenced how sustainable it could be."],[16,"However, some unpaid carers told us they were informed of services and provisions that were not readily accessible."],[20,"The local authority explained that the median time for a financial assessment to commence from date of referral was 2 days or less. They also explained that the reason for delays in completion of some financial assessments were due to circumstances beyond their control, for example when assessments involved Court of Protection applications."]],"m":"Assessments, reviews, and care planning arrangements were not always timely, which the local authority recognised and had made improvements to reduce waiting times for assessments and reviews. Providers told us approaches to waiting times were inconsistent and sometimes responses were timely but on other occasions there were delays which had a detrimental impact on people. Providers told us approaches to waiting times were inconsistent and sometimes responses were timely but on other occasions t"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[40,"The local authority also commissioned accommodation-based harm reductions services for those not yet able stop misuse."],[40,"Partners told us there were gaps in services for people with mental health needs, care home placements specifically for people with dementia, nursing home placements and availability of accessible housing."],[43,"The local authority had recognised that there were not sufficient services for unpaid carers and were in the process of changing their Carers Voucher Scheme."]],"m":"The local authority also commissioned accommodation-based harm reductions services for those not yet able stop misuse. Partners told us there were gaps in services for people with mental health needs, care home placements specifically for people with dementia, nursing home placements and availability of accessible housing. Support for people with mental health conditions in the community was limited and the local authority told us supported accommodation in the area was good but they had identif"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[56,"Staff and partners told us the establishment of daily system calls have provided the opportunity to resolve any problems at an early stage and prevent delays in discharge. These changes have improved caseload management and co-ordination and management of pathways, increased resources to enable the strengths-based approach to be fully embedded and created an integrated discharge process via the Transfer of Care Hub."],[57,"If support was not immediately required, communication was sent to the relevant community team on the electronic recording system for them to take necessary actions the following working day."],[58,"Some unpaid carers and parents told us the process was not automatic, and they had to make a referral to adult services themselves."]],"m":"Staff and partners told us the establishment of daily system calls have provided the opportunity to resolve any problems at an early stage and prevent delays in discharge. Staff and partners told us the establishment of daily system calls have provided the opportunity to resolve any problems at an early stage and prevent delays in discharge. These changes have improved caseload management and co-ordination and management of pathways, increased resources to enable the strengths-based approach to "},{"q":[[62,"Staff told us when poor quality referrals were received and/or and they could not speak to the referring professional; the staff liaised with the quality assurance team and/or the Integrated Care Board who would then work with professionals in relation to this matter."],[65,"Recognising the challenge of managing demand with limited resource the local authority had created a DoLs team in March 2024 and implemented the ADASS prioritisation tool, introduced a robust system of oversight, implemented a waiting well approach."],[68,"The local authority had a standard operating procedure, process map and guidance to explain the criteria for section 42 enquiries and actions to take when the criteria was not met."]],"m":"Staff told us they did not always receive quality safeguarding referrals, however there were mandatory fields on the forms to ensure they must speak to the adult at risk. Staff told us when poor quality referrals were received and/or and they could not speak to the referring professional; the staff liaised with the quality assurance team and/or the Integrated Care Board who would then work with professionals in relation to this matter. Recognising the challenge of managing demand with limited re"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Tower Hamlets":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[38,"We also heard about creative joint working to support people who wanted to be Shared Lives carers but didn’t have a spare bedroom to access similar one-bedroomed properties with a neighbouring vacant property where a person requiring support could live to promote their independence while still having access to nearby support."],[40,"To address some gaps in respite support, a service had commenced in 2024 to provide support for people with a learning disability to enable their unpaid carers to have a break. This commissioned service supported people in long term, short term and emergency situations."],[41,"Local authority data demonstrated waits for residential and nursing care were not impacted by seeking out of area placements, with most people having placements arranged within 7 days."]],"m":"We also heard about creative joint working to support people who wanted to be Shared Lives carers but didn’t have a spare bedroom to access similar one-bedroomed properties with a neighbouring vacant property where a person requiring support could live to promote their independence while still having access to nearby support. However, feedback we received from some unpaid carers indicated there was not sufficient support, with one person highlighting the carer retreats as supportive to their wel"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[50,"Staff told us at times such as Christmas they checked in with all people on the waiting list to ensure their safety over periods where the local authority would have limited staff."],[51,"Staff told us about coordinated pathways to support young people moving to adult services including a dedicated staff team; however, the target was for people to be allocated at age 17.5, which people told us left limited time to plan for their future."],[53,"Brokerage teams liaised with host local authorities before any out of area placements were made, and placements were not made where providers were rated inadequate or requires improvement by the Care Quality Commission (CQC)."]],"m":"Staff told us at times such as Christmas they checked in with all people on the waiting list to ensure their safety over periods where the local authority would have limited staff. Staff told us about coordinated pathways to support young people moving to adult services including a dedicated staff team; however, the target was for people to be allocated at age 17.5, which people told us left limited time to plan for their future. Staff told us contingency measures were now in place for support t"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Waltham Forest":[{"q":[[14,"There were multiple ways of contacting the local authority’s care and support services via telephone, email or webform, but people told us it was not easy to access an assessment, and they said this could put them off seeking help. For example, two voluntary and community sector (VCS) partners told us people were sometimes unable to find the right information about assessments."],[14,"The team was staffed by administrative staff rather than qualified social work staff and they were not equipped with the knowledge or tools, to make decisions about Care Act eligibility nor were they expected to do so."],[14,"For other people whose primary need was unclear, staff in the Multi Agency Safeguarding Hub (MASH) were responsible for triaging all new adult social care requests where a Care Act 2014 assessment was indicated, with management oversight. This triaging and internal referral process meant there was duplication of some activity, and the need for people to have to repeat their stories."]],"m":"There were multiple ways of contacting the local authority’s care and support services via telephone, email or webform, but people told us it was not easy to access an assessment, and they said this could put them off seeking help. The team was staffed by administrative staff rather than qualified social work staff and they were not equipped with the knowledge or tools, to make decisions about Care Act eligibility nor were they expected to do so. For other people whose primary need was unclear, "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[42,"Despite these innovations, some services remained commissioned using traditional time-and-task models, which limited person-centred approaches."],[43,"Delays in hospital discharge-related home care were monitored via Integrated Discharge Hub (IDH) reports, and a Bridging Service initiated care when no alternative was available."],[44,"We heard securing out-of-hours care and respite placements could be challenging, often causing delays for people in receiving a service."]],"m":"Feedback from staff and leaders indicated that further work to understand capacity and demand across different types of care and support was needed to inform planning and resource requirements. They also planned to review supported living provision to increase capacity and meet more complex needs, with intentions to co-produce these developments, although they had not yet defined specific capacity requirements. Despite these innovations, some services remained commissioned using traditional time"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[59,"Other young people who were eligible for adult social care but did not have either a learning disability or mental health needs were referred instead to the adult care management team."],[60,"Young people with no recourse to public funds, were funded by the Council until either a Home Office decision or until first payment of benefits/wages, ensuring that the young person was not without money."],[61,"Although funding decisions between agencies weren’t always easy, disagreements did not delay the provision of care and support."]],"m":"Other young people who were eligible for adult social care but did not have either a learning disability or mental health needs were referred instead to the adult care management team. Young people with no recourse to public funds, were funded by the Council until either a Home Office decision or until first payment of benefits/wages, ensuring that the young person was not without money. Some staff however told us they could not always respond quickly to unexpected issues in out-of-area placemen"},{"q":[[62,"Oversight of safeguarding enquiries had been unclear in some teams, and there were examples of inconsistencies in decision making about whether concerns met the threshold for a S42(2) enquiry."],[64,"Whilst staff across teams demonstrated confidence in raising concerns, and applying Making Safeguarding Personal principles, it was unclear if staff involved in safeguarding work were suitably skilled and supported through training to undertake safeguarding duties effectively."],[68,"The local authority had 2 safeguarding concerns waiting for allocation at the end of June 2025. They told us the MASH team did not have a waiting list, so these were concerns received on the last day of the month which would be dealt with the next day."]],"m":"Oversight of safeguarding enquiries had been unclear in some teams, and there were examples of inconsistencies in decision making about whether concerns met the threshold for a S42(2) enquiry. The local authority did not have their own operational policy but said they worked to the Pan-London Adult Safeguarding procedures, which set out timescales for completion of the various adult safeguarding stages: response to initial concern within 1 day, planning within 5 days, outcomes within 20 days, an"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Luton":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[24,"One person reported poor communication following an OT assessment when works were passed to the adaptation team, leading them to have to retell their ‘story’ again despite the AP and OT being placed in the adaptation team during this time."],[25,"Staff spoke about having files of community resources to be able to share with people they worked with, and that people who were digitally excluded could contact the service via telephone to get advice and information, however people described long waits of over an hour when they tried to call the service."],[26,"Carers who reported accessing a recently launched smart phone application spoke positively about having access to advice and information 24 hours a day, however this did not impact those digitally excluded."]],"m":"There was not always evidence of recovery work supporting people to regain independence and also showed a lack of floating support and adequate accommodation in Luton had been a barrier in supporting the ‘Home First’ agenda. There were some delays in people accessing specialist equipment and minor home adaptations to maintain their independence and continue living in their own homes. Staff told us there were no backlogs in processing housing adaptations and that this improvement had been achieve"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[35,"In contrast, we were told a cared for person was unable to access day services due to nobody in the provision being able to speak their language."],[36,"People had access to a range of local support options that were safe, effective, affordable and high-quality to meet their care and support needs, however feedback we received highlighted a lack of accommodation-based services to support people with high-level needs."],[37,"The local authority was reviewing their sitting service at the time of assessment, acknowledging the lack of flexibility which did not always meet carers’ needs."]],"m":"Leaders told us that following some analysis it was identified that there was a lack of diversity in the people accessing extra care facilities, therefore targeted work was being undertaken to promote the provision more widely in minority communities. Following feedback from partners and unpaid carers, leaders told us that people felt that the sitting service available to carers wasn’t as culturally competent as people wanted it to be. In contrast, we were told a cared for person was unable to a"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[52,"We received mixed feedback about arrangements to support safe transitions for people moving from child to adult services."],[54,"Contingency and emergency preparedness to address provider failure in Luton was governed by a multi-agency framework, with coordination primarily managed through the Bedfordshire Local Resilience Forum (BLRF) and Luton Council's Civil Protection Team. This framework ensured that local authorities, emergency services, and health agencies (NHS) worked together to prepare for and respond to incidents."],[55,"Partners told us that contingency planning for people with care and support needs wasn’t consistent or always comprehensively considered by workers completing Care Act and carers assessments, leaving people at risk if unpaid carers found themselves in a position of being unable to provide support."]],"m":"Partners told us that contingency planning for people with care and support needs wasn’t consistent or always comprehensively considered by workers completing Care Act and carers assessments, leaving people at risk if unpaid carers found themselves in a position of being unable to provide support."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Hillingdon":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[31,"Senior leaders recognised hearing the voice of people/communities was an ongoing area for development across the local authority. They had co-production boards but acknowledged the need to widen participation, so they didn’t just hear from the same people."],[34,"Staff and leaders were mindful of, and working to address, any accessibility issues or limitations on services offered which might impact more on one group than another. They gave examples of providing additional written guidance to help older people use assistive technology and speaking to catering staff in care establishments about limited menus."],[34,"Staff also reported instances where delays in safeguarding investigations being completed were caused by delays of 4 weeks in providing a suitable advocate. They noted that this usually took 1-2 weeks."]],"m":"Senior leaders recognised hearing the voice of people/communities was an ongoing area for development across the local authority. They had co-production boards but acknowledged the need to widen participation, so they didn’t just hear from the same people. Staff told us there were not enough services for the LGBTQIA+ community, but there was a project to set up access in the local library for advice and signposting services. Staff told us there were not enough services for the LGBTQIA+ community"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Hounslow":[{"q":[[10,"Others raised concerns about assessments and care planning, for example about the transparency of decision making, and a lack of support provided as a result the assessments."],[11,"In March 2023, 291 people had waited over 28 days for an assessment, including 99 people waiting for a Care Act Assessment, and 105 people waiting for an OT (Occupational Therapy) Assessment."],[12,"Data provided by the local authority showed longer delays for assessments to be completed in some specialist teams, for example Mental Health team and the Adults with a Learning Disability or Autistic People Team, and in some Locality teams."]],"m":"Others raised concerns about assessments and care planning, for example about the transparency of decision making, and a lack of support provided as a result the assessments. By contrast, other people told us very clearly this was not their experience. Hounslow told us they were reviewing the way the data is captured as they felt the current system did not accurately reflect their approach. However, some people waited longer for completion of a full assessment. In March 2023, 291 people had wait"},{"q":[[18,"The Short Breaks service provided respite up to 20 hours per 4-week period, and other types of respite provision appeared limited."],[19,"People could not always access equipment and minor adaptations in a timely manner to maintain their independence and continue living in their own homes. It was noted a proportion of people waiting for an OT assessment were closed without receiving a service, because by the time they were assessed their needs had changed and they no longer needed anything."],[20,"The local authority had an Improvement Plan to address long waiting times, but waits were still prolonged, particularly for those with more complex needs, or where more specialist equipment needed to be ordered or trialled before a final determination could be reached."]],"m":"Therefore, it was a way in which people with care and support needs that were not being met could be identified. The Short Breaks service provided respite up to 20 hours per 4-week period, and other types of respite provision appeared limited. Another carer told us the Short Breaks respite service was insufficient, and not flexible enough. One partner organisation told us of insufficient daytime provision for people living with dementia and said that it was a real concern for carers. People coul"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Lewisham":[{"q":[[15,"We were informed that staff did not have access to the data dashboard however, performance data was communicated and disseminated to frontline staff, through management."],[20,"The local authority had introduced an appeals process in April 2024, which had limited uptake with only 2 appeals lodged since implementation."],[21,"Partners recognised referrals to advocacy had increased; however, they believed many eligible people still did not access Care Act advocacy. They particularly highlighted reduced involvement at assessment reviews, and they felt this was an area of unmet need."]],"m":"While staff described clear referral processes and that screening was risk-based, there was no formal, documented process for assessing risk in place at the time of our assessment. While staff described clear referral processes and that screening was risk-based, there was no formal, documented process for assessing risk in place at the time of our assessment. This gap has been recognised, and work was underway to develop a separate, standalone document. However, other people said they didn’t fee"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[34,"Staff also collected personal data, however there was an acknowledgement by staff of the gaps to systematic analysis of safeguarding themes or trends across specific population groups. This limited the local authority’s ability to proactively address emerging inequalities."],[36,"Partners raised concerns about disproportionate numbers of Black people receiving mental health assessments and Black men facing homelessness and there was a lack of understanding about the needs of this specific group. They also felt there was a lack of urgency and action related to the needs of this group."],[41,"The local authority had inclusion and accessibility arrangements in place so people could engage with the local authority, however these were not always effective. For example, feedback from partners and staff indicated challenges in accessing translated information and interpreters."]],"m":"Staff also collected personal data, however there was an acknowledgement by staff of the gaps to systematic analysis of safeguarding themes or trends across specific population groups. This limited the local authority’s ability to proactively address emerging inequalities. Partners raised concerns about disproportionate numbers of Black people receiving mental health assessments and Black men facing homelessness and there was a lack of understanding about the needs of this specific group. Partne"},{"q":[[43,"However, there remained a number of gaps in service delivery, with identified gaps in fully understanding and meeting the needs across the community."],[44,"Other staff recognised the lack of nursing home beds and the challenges of tailoring the current service model to the needs of the young age demographic."],[52,"Care and support were not always sufficient to meet demand; people did not consistently receive services when and where they needed them."]],"m":"However, there remained a number of gaps in service delivery, with identified gaps in fully understanding and meeting the needs across the community. Other staff told us about gaps in current service, specifically citing insufficient employment support for people with learning disabilities or autism, including supported apprenticeships. Other staff recognised the lack of nursing home beds and the challenges of tailoring the current service model to the needs of the young age demographic. Senior "},{"q":[[63,"Although some partners participated in forums such as the recently revived Learning Disability Programme Board, attendees felt their involvement lacked meaningful influence, with some subgroups viewed it as largely symbolic."],[65,"There was a need to strengthen oversight of data relating to Carers’ Assessments undertaken within the Mental Health Service, as these were recorded on a separate system. As a result, the local authority did not have full visibility of the volume of assessments completed by Mental Health."],[65,"Consequently, assessments could not be tracked back to people’s initial contacts. This created potential weaknesses in the consistency of oversight and governance relating to partnership working."]],"m":"Although some partners participated in forums such as the recently revived Learning Disability Programme Board, attendees felt their involvement lacked meaningful influence, with some subgroups viewed it as largely symbolic. Partners also noted the loss of the former Voluntary Action Group (VAL) and said that emerging plans for new infrastructure organisations lacked clarity and did little to strengthen the sector. While partners acknowledged the Health and Wellbeing Board was moving forward fro"},{"q":[[70,"Staff previously identified a gap in the mental health safeguarding pathway for referrals that did not go through the Multi Agency Safeguarding Hub (MASH Changes had been made and these referrals were now sent to a general email inbox (with a specific email address), which ensured referrals were picked up."],[74,"The records we reviewed did not yet show the impact of these changes, with a referral to the team being received after the person turned 17."],[74,"Partners’ feedback included that some families were unaware of resources and support for young people transitioning to adult services, although they did note some progress such as the development of an autistic hub. This indicated some further development was needed to ensure the sharing of information about transitions support."]],"m":"Information sharing protocols did not always support safe, secure, and timely sharing of personal information in ways that protected people’s rights and privacy. Staff previously identified a gap in the mental health safeguarding pathway for referrals that did not go through the Multi Agency Safeguarding Hub (MASH Changes had been made and these referrals were now sent to a general email inbox (with a specific email address), which ensured referrals were picked up. The use of different systems a"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[89,"Staff told us decisions were based on professional judgment and were not supported by a formal approach."],[89,"The local authority shared the ‘Waiting Well’ process which outlined risk assessments took place, but this document did not include information on how to assess risk effectively for staff. This potentially increased the likelihood of not assessing risks consistently at the front door."],[89,"However, without a clear framework and reliable performance metrics, it remained difficult to assess accountability and effectiveness in the delivery of mental health services under this arrangement, this current challenge limited the local authority’s ability to actively monitor and manage risks for people accessing the service."]],"m":"Staff told us decisions were based on professional judgment and were not supported by a formal approach. The local authority shared the ‘Waiting Well’ process which outlined risk assessments took place, but this document did not include information on how to assess risk effectively for staff. The local authority shared the ‘Waiting Well’ process which outlined risk assessments took place, but this document did not include information on how to assess risk effectively for staff. This potentially "},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Merton":[{"q":[[13,"The maximum length of time a person waited for a review was 296 days. This was a reduction from November 2024, when 412 people were waiting for a review of their Care Act needs, and the maximum wait was 1352 days."],[13,"There were no waits for reablement support from occupational therapists (OT) and people did not wait to be allocated care and support by the brokerage team."],[13,"The OT team were working towards expanding the capacity of existing resources to reduce the time people waited. For example, staff assessed and supported people two days per week in the local authority’s on-site Occupational Therapy Assessment Centre, and the team aimed to expand this usage to enable more people to be assessed on-site where possible and help meet their needs."]],"m":"However, leaders were aware some people still did not find it easy to contact the local authority for this support and told us work to improve accessibility to adult social care at the front-door was in the planning stages. The maximum length of time a person waited for a review was 296 days. The maximum length of time a person waited for a review was 296 days. This was a reduction from November 2024, when 412 people were waiting for a review of their Care Act needs, and the maximum wait was 135"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[26,"Leaders and staff understood the profile and demographics of local population. For example, they were aware of the disparity in health inequalities and deprivation between the East and West of the borough."],[28,"While arrangements for overseeing progress towards these objectives were laid out in this strategy, staff leading this work told us there was a lack of clarity around their roles and responsibilities. They said this meant they often undertook additional work outside of their remits which drew them away from being able to oversee work aligned with the strategy, indicating an area of focus for local authority leaders."],[29,"Leaders told us diversity at a senior leadership level had increased as a result of improved recruitment approaches. They said they were committed to making recruitment approaches more equitable, for example by exploring why people were not successful at interview and amending recruitment processes to make them more inclusive."]],"m":"While arrangements for overseeing progress towards these objectives were laid out in this strategy, staff leading this work told us there was a lack of clarity around their roles and responsibilities. While arrangements for overseeing progress towards these objectives were laid out in this strategy, staff leading this work told us there was a lack of clarity around their roles and responsibilities. They said this meant they often undertook additional work outside of their remits which drew them "},{"q":[[32,"However, leaders were aware that, at the time of our assessment, not everyone could access the care and support they needed when, where, and how they needed it due to gaps in provision for certain groups."],[33,"Though emergency amendments could be put in place outside this forum and retroactively agreed to ensure people did not wait for urgent changes to their care packages, the lack of flexibility built into the process for nonemergency care package amendments indicated an area of focus for leaders."],[35,"Plans for the development had been coproduced with partners and people who drew on services to meet their specialised needs, indicating leaders were actively working towards addressing existing gaps in provision."]],"m":"However, leaders were aware that, at the time of our assessment, not everyone could access the care and support they needed when, where, and how they needed it due to gaps in provision for certain groups. Though emergency amendments could be put in place outside this forum and retroactively agreed to ensure people did not wait for urgent changes to their care packages, the lack of flexibility built into the process for nonemergency care package amendments indicated an area of focus for leaders. "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Redbridge":[{"q":[[10,"The local authority had identified through case audits that strengths-based principles were not being applied consistently across locality teams and had refreshed its strengthsbased training for all staff."],[12,"For annual reviews, the local authority reported 798 people had reviews which were overdue in August 2025, with a maximum wait time of 896 days, which means a person had waited nearly 2 and a half years past when an annual review of their care was due."],[13,"However, the eligibility criteria were not available on the local authority’s website to provide a fully transparent criteria for people who may be looking to see if they were eligible for care and support under the Care Act."]],"m":"Staff told us about unclear pathways for people who would benefit from the mental health team, with some locality staff joint working alongside staff from the mental health team, and other people who were supported solely by the mental health team. The local authority had identified through case audits that strengths-based principles were not being applied consistently across locality teams and had refreshed its strengthsbased training for all staff. Although these were not in place at the time "},{"q":[[17,"People gave us mixed feedback about the reablement service, with some people telling us about a positive experience which helped them maintain their independence, and another person telling us the service was not personcentred."],[18,"Reablement was not routinely considered when people were accessing the local authority for assessment. This meant that people were not offered the opportunity to develop their independence before completion of Care Act assessments or consideration of long term needs."],[18,"Local authority data demonstrated that in the 12 months to July 2025, 56 people were referred for reablement support following screening at the local authority front door from 4270 Conversation 1s which were completed. It was not clear from local authority data whether those people were accepted into the reablement service and not every person who had a Conversation 1 would have contacted the local authority requesting support."]],"m":"Leaders told us there was more to do in promoting this preventative approach, and that the VCSFE sector in Redbridge was not as strong as in other parts of inner London, limiting the full reach and impact of preventive services, particularly for seldom heard populations. However, most unpaid carers told us they had to identify the carer’s centre themselves and were not signposted to this by the local authority. People gave us mixed feedback about the reablement service, with some people telling "},{"q":[[22,"However, it meant the local authority did not fully understand of how safeguarding processes were experienced by people and whether they were inclusive."],[23,"A survey undertaken by partners had found that LGBT+ individuals did not feel confident in expressing their identity to professionals."],[23,"The survey also found lack of practitioner confidence in supporting gender identity. To address this, the local authority’s Health and Wellbeing Board had arranged training, alongside partners, to increase practitioner confidence in discussing and meeting people’s diverse needs including sexuality and gender identity."]],"m":"However, it meant the local authority did not fully understand of how safeguarding processes were experienced by people and whether they were inclusive. A survey undertaken by partners had found that LGBT+ individuals did not feel confident in expressing their identity to professionals. The survey also found lack of practitioner confidence in supporting gender identity. The survey also found lack of practitioner confidence in supporting gender identity. To address this, the local authority’s Hea"},{"q":[[26,"Where gaps in provision had been identified through feedback such as respite services, these had been addressed through investment in additional services. For example, a carer’s organisation was funded to provide respite for older unpaid carers and support people with a learning disability."],[29,"Local authority data demonstrated there were no waits for care caused by lack of availability and that once funding was authorised support was arranged quickly through the brokerage system."],[30,"The local authority did not have clear arrangements to monitor the quality and impact of the care and support services being commissioned for people, but it supported improvements where needed."]],"m":"Where gaps in provision had been identified through feedback such as respite services, these had been addressed through investment in additional services. However, people also told us their choice of services was limited by cost. The local authority commissioned with a variety of services including outcome focussed day services, but people told us where they requested specialised services, they were told they must trial more cost-effective services first, which people felt did not meet their spe"},{"q":[[34,"Through the Section 75 review the local authority was acting where it identified joint services were not delivering the best outcomes for people. This included amendments which were due to be made to the hospital discharge service shortly after our assessment, and a review of the reablement service which was in scoping and due to take place towards the end of the Section 75 review."],[35,"Relationships with the sector varied in strength, with examples of productive collaboration alongside feedback that some partnerships lacked depth, consistency, and sustainability."],[35,"Some groups described inconsistent engagement from senior leaders and a lack of regular opportunities to connect with the local authority to discuss joint work."]],"m":"Through the Section 75 review the local authority was acting where it identified joint services were not delivering the best outcomes for people. Through the Section 75 review the local authority was acting where it identified joint services were not delivering the best outcomes for people. This included amendments which were due to be made to the hospital discharge service shortly after our assessment, and a review of the reablement service which was in scoping and due to take place towards the"},{"q":[[37,"The local authority used data to have oversight of waiting lists and had adopted a waiting well approach to ensure people were given regular updates and risk re-assessed while they awaited a Care Act assessment."],[38,"Staff also told us that when working with partners they were not consistently using the local authority’s core case management system and often recorded statutory information on the NHS system instead. This meant that Care Act assessments and safeguarding data were not always visible to the local authority in real time, limiting the ability for it to monitor care journeys, track risk, and ensure timely safeguarding action."],[39,"The local authority did not use a named worker approach, where people have a consistently allocated social worker, but staff told us they would work with people for as long as it took for them to be stable and safe, with examples provided where staff were involved for 3 years to ensure people’s ongoing safety."]],"m":"The local authority used data to have oversight of waiting lists and had adopted a waiting well approach to ensure people were given regular updates and risk re-assessed while they awaited a Care Act assessment. Staff also told us that when working with partners they were not consistently using the local authority’s core case management system and often recorded statutory information on the NHS system instead. Staff also told us that when working with partners they were not consistently using th"},{"q":[[43,"Any concerns received into ASH relating to people open to the mental health teams were sent to mental health social workers for screening and action which meant they were not subject to the same process and BRAG prioritisation as concerns managed by ASH."],[43,"Local authority data excluded concerns which were being actioned by the mental health teams suggesting a lack of governance and oversight."],[46,"There was not clarity on what constituted a safeguarding concern and when S42 safeguarding enquiries were required."]],"m":"Any concerns received into ASH relating to people open to the mental health teams were sent to mental health social workers for screening and action which meant they were not subject to the same process and BRAG prioritisation as concerns managed by ASH. Local authority data excluded concerns which were being actioned by the mental health teams suggesting a lack of governance and oversight. However, leaders told us there was a lack of data available to the SAB which limited its ability to hold p"},{"q":[[52,"Clear and effective governance, management and accountability arrangements were not consistently present at all levels within the local authority to provide visibility and assurance on delivery of Care Act duties."],[52,"Staff within integrated teams recorded information on health systems and not always on the local authority system which led to data reporting not fully identifying where Care Act duties were being delivered, reducing the governance of these."],[52,"We were also told about a lack of data relating to safeguarding, which impacted the ability of the Safeguarding Adults Board (SAB) to provide rigorous scrutiny to how well people were being kept safe."]],"m":"Clear and effective governance, management and accountability arrangements were not consistently present at all levels within the local authority to provide visibility and assurance on delivery of Care Act duties. Leaders told us about strong working relationships between partners; however, this was not underpinned with data to inform on effective service delivery. Staff within integrated teams recorded information on health systems and not always on the local authority system which led to data "},{"q":[[56,"The local authority had a workforce action plan which identified training priorities for the year 2025-2026 including professional curiosity, reflective practices and key legislation for adult social care."],[56,"The local authority was piloting use of technology enabled care devices within extra care housing, supported living and care homes following funding from the Department for Health and Social Care (DHSC)."],[57,"Coproduction was not embedded throughout the local authority’s work although the local authority was taking steps to increase its coproduction and include the voices of people in service planning."]],"m":"Coproduction was not embedded throughout the local authority’s work although the local authority was taking steps to increase its coproduction and include the voices of people in service planning."}],"Richmond upon Thames":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Southwark":[{"q":[[16,"Staff regularly re-assessed people’s risks while they waited, to check if their needs had changed. This provided opportunities for signposting to other services, prevention and enablement support."],[18,"Many were unaware what services were available to them, and some did not know how to access support."],[14,"387 people were waiting for an assessment by the older people and people with physical disability (OPPD) team. The OPPD median waiting time was 89 days, and the maximum wait was 157 days, beyond the internal target of 28 days."]],"m":"Some progress was being made to reducing the backlog of people waiting for assessments, but many people were still waiting too long, which presented risks to their safety and wellbeing. The local authority took action to reduce waiting times for assessments and reduce risks to people's wellbeing while they waited for an assessment. The local authority had systems to mitigate risks while people waited for assessments. Staff regularly re-assessed people’s risks while they waited, to check if their"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[70,"Some staff reported delays in sharing information with social workers and missed joint visits – including for cases involving identified risks."],[72,"The EDT did not have access to emergency funds and there were examples of staff using their own money in emergencies, for example to provide food to people in crisis."],[72,"Assessments were completed over a few sessions to ensure all needs were considered and the assessment was not rushed, but this did not always provide sufficient time for planning after assessment before the young person turned 18."]],"m":"Some staff reported delays in sharing information with social workers and missed joint visits – including for cases involving identified risks. Staff also told us information sharing was a challenge and needed to improve as information was not always easily available. Some staff reported the local authority’s electronic records system was not user friendly as it took a long time to complete assessment and safeguarding forms. The EDT did not have access to emergency funds and there were examples "},{"q":[[78,"There was limited awareness of training completion gaps in the local Safeguarding Adults Board (SAB), which had not identified training uptake as a local concern."],[81,"Although the SAB received quarterly performance reports, there was scope to improve SAB oversight of safeguarding performance and improve its connection with frontline practice as this was not sufficiently scrutinised. For example, review of safeguarding training uptake."],[83,"At the time of our assessment the local authority did not have a waiting list for s42 enquiry initial review and immediate risks were mitigated to ensure the person was safe."]],"m":"There was limited awareness of training completion gaps in the local Safeguarding Adults Board (SAB), which had not identified training uptake as a local concern. Senior leaders recognised some learning methods were not effective and staff were not accessing them. Additionally, some staff were not clear where these learning needs had come from or why they were doing the training. The local authority did not have an online reporting system or proforma. There were risks associated with this proces"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Sutton":[{"q":[[8,"People with needs which could not be fully resolved at that stage were progressed through to a locality or specialist team for additional assessment."],[11,"Data provided by the local authority in January 2025 showed people already in receipt of care and support were not reviewed in a timely manner, for example, 467 people had annual reviews that were overdue."],[12,"The median wait for an overdue review was 220 days and the maximum wait was 985 days."]],"m":"People with needs which could not be fully resolved at that stage were progressed through to a locality or specialist team for additional assessment. However, some partners told us there was a lack of understanding and application, for example, when working with people with substance misuse issues providers told us people fed back their intervention with social workers had been deficit based with a lack of appreciation for achievements. Some frontline staff told us they lacked confidence when un"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[50,"Leaders were also aware of the risk of interruptions to people’s care for reasons such as provider failure or people becoming unable to fund their care."],[51,"Partner feedback also indicated the local authority did not always share information in a timely manner and this impacted on partners’ ability to support people at risk of potential harm. For example, partners did not always receive updates or outcomes regarding assessments or safeguarding referrals from the local authority, meaning they lacked awareness around whether the risk to the person was being mitigated."],[53,"However, partner feedback indicated some young people and their families or carers were not adequately prepared by the local authority and experienced a sudden shift in the level of care from the point at which the young person turned 18."]],"m":"Leaders were also aware of the risk of interruptions to people’s care for reasons such as provider failure or people becoming unable to fund their care. However, staff told us they did not consistently receive updates about shared cases from other teams or local authority departments where they could not access the information on the systems. Partner feedback also indicated the local authority did not always share information in a timely manner and this impacted on partners’ ability to support p"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Manchester":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[76,"The process of raising a concern was clear, but some providers said they would value more support to understand safeguarding thresholds and said the responses could be inconsistent."],[79,"One strategic partner however shared feedback gathered that safeguarding learning and strategies were not delivered in an effective way to reach all communities, volunteer and third sector agencies."],[82,"The DASS completed audits, including a sample of cases where risk remained and where “desired outcomes were not met” and we heard that the quality assurance framework was being strengthened in relation to consistent application of the safeguarding threshold across teams."]],"m":"The process of raising a concern was clear, but some providers said they would value more support to understand safeguarding thresholds and said the responses could be inconsistent. A leader told us safeguarding cases sometimes stayed open longer, and safeguarding plans were not always delivered in good time or reached the individual due to workflow, so there were plans to review this. Some partners told us they had accessed online training and resources but said it was not always easy to know w"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Medway":[{"q":[[11,"The local authority had recognised that an increase in demand for adult social care services and staff shortages had resulted in a backlog of assessments."],[16,"The reasons given by the local authority for outstanding financial assessment delays included shortage of staff since October 2023, delays in information being provided by people, and delays in response to information requests from social workers. To address this, new staff were being trained to undertake financial assessments."],[16,"The Customer Relations team explored ways to make the complaints process more accessible to adults with disabilities, people who needed support to make a complaint, and where English was not a first language."]],"m":"In contrast some people had not always felt valued and had waited over 4 weeks for a visit from staff since an initial phone call from the local authority. Other providers told us local authority staff did not always have appropriate knowledge of the people they were working with. The local authority had recognised that an increase in demand for adult social care services and staff shortages had resulted in a backlog of assessments. In the short term the local authority had accessed funding to a"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[29,"However, we saw conversation 3 of the Care Act 3 eligibility assessment and eligibility guidance did not prompt practitioners to consider people's needs in relation to protected characteristics."],[31,"One project supporting people for whom English was not their first language, set up language cafes to support people, share information and signpost them to other services."],[31,"Some teams made phone calls to people waiting for services, however it was not evident if this was the standard practice across all teams or if this was an initiative by some teams."]],"m":"The local authority used the voluntary and community sector to reach out, but there was a lack of protocols for sharing data and information. However, there was limited understanding of new communities coming into Medway. The local authority recognised there had been a lack of processes to capture data on equality diversity and inclusion and was now included in future plans. However, we saw conversation 3 of the Care Act 3 eligibility assessment and eligibility guidance did not prompt practition"},{"q":[[34,"The local authority was proud of their multi-disadvantaged network and multi-disciplinary drug and alcohol service team. These were developed following identifying gaps in the market and worked to support people in the community."],[34,"The multi-disadvantaged network was a joint multi-disciplinary group consisting of the Integrated Care Board (ICB), outreach nurses, housing, mental health, nighttime support, and others. It brought together separate initiatives to bridge the gap to people who found it difficult to access services."],[35,"In the main, people had access to a range of local support options to meet their care and support needs, however there were gaps in service provision."]],"m":"The local authority was proud of their multi-disadvantaged network and multi-disciplinary drug and alcohol service team. These were developed following identifying gaps in the market and worked to support people in the community. The multi-disadvantaged network was a joint multi-disciplinary group consisting of the Integrated Care Board (ICB), outreach nurses, housing, mental health, nighttime support, and others. It brought together separate initiatives to bridge the gap to people who found it "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[58,"The local authority was an active partner in Safeguarding Adult Reviews (SARs) and other serious incident enquiries; however, learning was not always embedded into systems, processes, and practice."],[58,"Local authority data showed the number of DoLS referrals awaiting allocation was 364 as of 1 September 2024. To address this, the local authority had employed additional staff who were not yet in post, and they were still actively recruiting."],[59,"The median waiting time for review and allocation was 35 days, however some safeguarding concerns requiring section 42 enquiry had waited over a year for allocation."]],"m":"Feedback received across system partners highlighted some had concerns with a lack of engagement with providers in the safeguarding process, including minimal information sharing, no lessons learned, and no outcomes following an enquiry or concern. However, other staff said they did not offer a formal consultation service with providers around safeguarding. However, the majority of providers raised concerns there was a lack of safeguarding processes. The local authority was an active partner in "},{"q":[[63,"However, there were shortfalls remained with the safeguarding processes which the local authority had started to address."],[63,"The local authority’s political and executive leaders were informed about the potential risks facing adult social care which were reflected in the corporate risk registers, for example the backlogs of safeguarding enquiries and shortage of nursing home beds."],[63,"People did not receive a consistent experience of adult social care in areas such as assessment, care planning and reviews."]],"m":"However, there were shortfalls remained with the safeguarding processes which the local authority had started to address. The local authority’s political and executive leaders were informed about the potential risks facing adult social care which were reflected in the corporate risk registers, for example the backlogs of safeguarding enquiries and shortage of nursing home beds. People did not receive a consistent experience of adult social care in areas such as assessment, care planning and revi"},{"q":[[67,"The local authority demonstrated efforts to address gaps in shared learning. For example, they held open days for local people to learn about the services on offer."],[70,"The impact these forums had on practice improvement had not yet been analysed."],[71,"Data from the Local Government Social Care Ombudsman showed 50% of complaints were upheld, however any recommendations for remedies were not always carried out in a timely way."]],"m":"The local authority demonstrated efforts to address gaps in shared learning. However, a recent open day was not very well attended, and it was felt this was due to a lack of advertisement. The impact these forums had on practice improvement had not yet been analysed. Data from the Local Government Social Care Ombudsman showed 50% of complaints were upheld, however any recommendations for remedies were not always carried out in a timely way. An unpaid carer we spoke to told us they had recently r"}],"Middlesbrough":[{"q":[[11,"People’s experiences of care and support had not always ensured their human rights were respected and protected, that they were involved throughout in decisions and their protected characteristics under the Equality Act 2010 were understood and incorporated into care planning. For example, some people told us they felt the assessments conducted were financially driven and did not focus on people or their needs."],[11,"Staff told us that leaders listened to them when processes were not working smoothly, and more resources had been allocated. For example, when the Continuing Healthcare fast-tracking process was causing delays, leaders arranged for a dedicated nurse to be a point of contact and drop-in sessions were organised to discuss referrals quickly."],[13,"Overall, the experiences of the unpaid carers we spoke with was negative. For example, some unpaid carers told us they had been given no choice in the options of support they were given. Some unpaid carers said their responsibilities now and, in the future, had not been considered."]],"m":"However, most people we spoke with told us they did not receive a timely or accurate response. People’s experiences of care and support had not always ensured their human rights were respected and protected, that they were involved throughout in decisions and their protected characteristics under the Equality Act 2010 were understood and incorporated into care planning. Staff told us that leaders listened to them when processes were not working smoothly, and more resources had been allocated. As"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[27,"However, the strategy was still in draft format and the action plan describing what the local authority would do next was not completed."],[28,"With the strategy being in draft, the process of how the local authority was formally assuring itself they were engaged with all communities, and that those communities were accessing services in a culturally sensitive way was not fully embedded."],[31,"However, considering the population of ethnically diverse people in the area stood at 17.6% of the total, information and advice for these groups were not immediately apparent."]],"m":"However, the strategy was still in draft format and the action plan describing what the local authority would do next was not completed. Leaders told us they had recognised the gap in addressing inequalities in adult social care, and in response to this, a draft strategy had been produced to tackle the issue. With the strategy being in draft, the process of how the local authority was formally assuring itself they were engaged with all communities, and that those communities were accessing servi"},{"q":[[36,"The current framework of home care providers was contracted on a task-based spot arrangement. This was not in line with commissioning for positive outcomes whereby providers have flexibility to deliver services in a way that meets people's preferences."],[37,"There were shortfalls identified by people, staff, and leaders in some aspects of adult social care and support. For example, people told us there was a lack of specialist dementia provision including respite and sitting services offered by the local authority."],[39,"The issue was the availability of domiciliary care agencies willing to provide this service which caused delays for people waiting for this type of support."]],"m":"The current framework of home care providers was contracted on a task-based spot arrangement. This was not in line with commissioning for positive outcomes whereby providers have flexibility to deliver services in a way that meets people's preferences. There was not always sufficient care and support available to meet demand and people could not always access it when, where and how they needed it. There were shortfalls identified by people, staff, and leaders in some aspects of adult social care"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[68,"Within the Adult Social Care directorate, governance, accountability, and management of risk arrangements were in place; however, they were not all fully embedded to provide the level of assurances which all leaders within the local authority should expect to have."],[68,"Due to the scant nature of the information provided to us about waiting list times for initial assessments and reviews, it was not possible to ascertain if any themes, trends, or incidents were responsible for delays. Indeed, because no targets were provided for any of the waiting lists, it was not possible to know what, if at all, the target times were, let alone being met."],[70,"However, due to the prioritisation of the recovery plan and the investment in children’s services, the interim Chief Executive Officer (CEO) recognised they were not as aware of the risk level in relation to Adult Social Care and their Care Act responsibilities."]],"m":"Within the Adult Social Care directorate, governance, accountability, and management of risk arrangements were in place; however, they were not all fully embedded to provide the level of assurances which all leaders within the local authority should expect to have. In relation to the delivery of their Care Act duties, the data provided to us by the local authority in June 2024, showed people waited on average 2.4 days for an initial assessment. Due to the scant nature of the information provided"},{"q":[[76,"The local authority's Autism strategy did not include what they planned to do to meet the recommendations of the project."],[78,"While the South Tees strategy was scheduled for a refresh, they had started to undertake internal initiatives to address these gaps, however these were not yet fully embedded into practice."],[78,"Some providers told us the local authority had not collaborated with them to help innovate their services. They told us they felt the local authority did not know what they wanted from services within the area, and because of this they felt they could not model their services towards the needs of the local population."]],"m":"Staff and leaders told us this was an area of improvement but there were no clear plans in place on how to improve this. Partners shared that even though they were invited to meetings to share information sometimes this felt like a tick box exercise, where change was not seen on the ground. The local authority's Autism strategy did not include what they planned to do to meet the recommendations of the project. The local authority had identified gaps in their current approach to working with care"}],"Milton Keynes":[{"q":[[12,"Staff also shared that managers monitor waiting lists closely, immediate needs are met during initial contact, and support workers help bridge gaps in care."],[13,"The support offer included (but was not limited to) a one-off direct payment (subject to eligibility), respite care, annual short breaks, and access to support services such as advice and Admiral Nurses."],[18,"However, in other parts of the service, advocacy did not appear to be consistently embedded, and according to staff feedback, referrals could depend on the absence of family support."]],"m":"However, some people and partners reported concerns around communication, especially during staff changes or absences and some people said the support that they received felt taskfocused and their needs were not fully met. Staff also shared that managers monitor waiting lists closely, immediate needs are met during initial contact, and support workers help bridge gaps in care. The support offer included (but was not limited to) a one-off direct payment (subject to eligibility), respite care, ann"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Newcastle":[{"q":[[14,"In November 2025, there were 871 people with overdue reviews."],[15,"This, alongside the local authority’s All Age Carers Strategy findings showed that while some unpaid carers appreciated services, many unpaid carers’ needs were not fully met, leaving them at risk of poor health and isolation."],[27,"When a person could not take part in conversations about their care and support and had no one to help them, the local authority had access to a commissioned advocacy service to support their involvement in assessments and plans."]],"m":"However, some partners and people told us coordination was not always consistent, and some people needed to repeat their situation to different staff which they felt negatively impacted continuity of care. Senior leaders told us the target was 28 days, and this target had not been met earlier in the year due to staffing shortages in teams supporting people with learning disabilities and autistic people, as well as the additional time sometimes needed to involve the right people in a person’s car"},{"q":[[30,"A redesigned Adult Social Care Point model improved continuity and reduced delays for people accessing support."],[35,"The introduction of MDT led triage at the front door was also cited as an improvement, supporting earlier decision making and prioritisation, although this had not yet translated into waits meeting the 28 day target."],[36,"Delivering home adaptations required coordination between adult social care, housing services and external providers, and delays showed that people did not always experience support as a single, joinedup service following assessment and ongoing improvements had not yet resulted in reduced waiting times for people."]],"m":"However, others told us access for short break support for their loved one was inconsistent and often dependent on charity involvement. However, others told us access for short break support for their loved one was inconsistent and often dependent on charity involvement. This increased the risk of unpaid carer breakdown. A redesigned Adult Social Care Point model improved continuity and reduced delays for people accessing support. However, staff told us short-term teams did not consistently offe"},{"q":[[43,"There was limited strategic and operational understanding of tailored outreach for these groups, and staff could not give examples of culturally specific services. This created a risk that unmet needs were not identified and people were excluded from advice, information, and preventative support."],[45,"Evidence through tracking of people’s records, and staff feedback showed gaps in linking equality information was not consistently informing care planning. For example, templates were in place to record protected characteristics, completion was inconsistent and cultural or individual needs were not always reflected in assessments."],[45,"Improvements were underway, including a toolkit to support better recording, but reporting remained limited at the time of the assessment."]],"m":"However, staff told us these communities were not seen within adult social care and their level of integration in Newcastle was unclear. Senior leaders told us some communities did not access adult social care in numbers they expected, and further work was underway across the city to work with those communities to understand the reasons for this. While some targeted and culturally specific activity was identified, many staff were unable to describe how some ethnic minority communities were engag"},{"q":[[49,"However, some gaps in the care market showed the local authority did not always use this information in ways that were clearly understood by staff and partners, or that visibly prepared for future needs. For example, although people had shared their views on day opportunities, some partners told us they were not yet aware of detailed objectives, timescales or delivery plans for future changes."],[49,"In several areas, senior leaders acknowledged that the JSNA gave them a wealth of knowledge, but there were gaps in translating this into clear, commissioned provision or service development plans."],[50,"While not yet consistent across all services, partners, staff and people with lived experience contributed meaningfully to decisions in several major programmes so decisions reflected shared priorities. For example, the local authority identified in its Market Position Statement a growing need for increased extra care provision based on demographic data and the JSNA."]],"m":"However, some gaps in the care market showed the local authority did not always use this information in ways that were clearly understood by staff and partners, or that visibly prepared for future needs. Staff also told us current day opportunities were not always meeting people’s needs, describing issues such as increased isolation following previous day centre closures. AA partner felt there were limited safe community spaces for autistic adults; the local authority highlighted that it operate"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[68,"Joint meetings with health, mental health and housing professionals were used to plan ahead for safe discharge, transitions and continuity of care aiming for risks to be identified early, equipment and support were in place, and people did not become lost between services."],[69,"However, staff acknowledged that after 8pm the service was limited to emergency responses only, and that reduced placement availability overnight could affect options in some situations."],[74,"Contingency planning, also referred to as emergency planning, was available to outline the support required if an unpaid carer, commissioned service, or the local authority became unable to provide care; however, practice was inconsistent, and individual arrangements were not always in place."]],"m":"In contrast, another person said equipment and practical support at home helped them maintain their independence but long waits for suitable housing and limited communication during these periods left them feeling unsure whether risks were being monitored. Some people told us new arrangements did not match their assessed needs or that they felt excluded from decisions that affected them. Therefore, although risks were identified in some situations, people did not always experience the consistent"},{"q":[[79,"The local authority had developed an accessible course for people whose first language was not English and collaborated with partners on initiatives such as domestic abuse, mental capacity and alcohol use, needs relating to substance use training, and stigma education supported by people with lived experience."],[83,"Partners told us progress was made, including improvements to treatment pathways, trauma-informed engagement, workforce training, and public awareness initiatives, all informed by learning from the SARs to address gaps in partnership working."],[88,"However, despite this progress, audits and partner feedback showed Making Safeguarding Personal was not always consistently applied."]],"m":"There was a risk that people’s wellbeing was not central to decision making in all situations. The local authority had developed an accessible course for people whose first language was not English and collaborated with partners on initiatives such as domestic abuse, mental capacity and alcohol use, needs relating to substance use training, and stigma education supported by people with lived experience. Partners told us progress was made, including improvements to treatment pathways, trauma-info"},{"q":[[92,"The local authority had established governance, quality assurance and performance arrangements, with regular oversight through leadership meetings, dashboards, safeguarding boards and contract monitoring. These structures provided a clear framework for managing statutory duties, but their impact on people was inconsistent."],[94,"Staff felt able to raise concerns and did not describe a blame‑based culture, though some said communication about strategic decisions and service change could be clearer."],[95,"While controls were in place, underlying pressures linked to workforce capacity and market stability had not reduced. As a result, the impact of risk continued to be experienced by people, including delays in accessing timely assessments and support, and variation in the consistency of provision depending on workforce capacity and service availability."]],"m":"The local authority had established governance, quality assurance and performance arrangements, with regular oversight through leadership meetings, dashboards, safeguarding boards and contract monitoring. These structures provided a clear framework for managing statutory duties, but their impact on people was inconsistent. While processes were defined, assurance tended to focus more on activity and finance than on quality, equity and lived experience, meaning at the time of the CQC assessment as"},{"q":[[99,"Senior leaders told us digital, practice and workforce developments were being delivered across multiple workstreams and had not yet been brought together in a way that demonstrated co-ordinated and systematic learning. They told us this would be addressed through their forthcoming three‑year adult social care plan."],[101,"Some people also told us there were limited opportunities for groups to share learning about what worked well, and communication was inconsistent, which affected confidence co‑production was effectively used to improve future activity. However, this was not everyone’s experience."],[101,"Partners shared views through board meetings and routine engagement, though some reported limited shared learning in relation to safeguarding, reducing opportunities to strengthen practice across the wider sector."]],"m":"While there were clear examples of progress, strategic organisational learning was not yet consistently aligned or embedded across adult social care. Senior leaders told us digital, practice and workforce developments were being delivered across multiple workstreams and had not yet been brought together in a way that demonstrated co-ordinated and systematic learning. Senior leaders told us digital, practice and workforce developments were being delivered across multiple workstreams and had not y"}],"Newham":[{"q":[[11,"The local authority told us the waiting time of 726 was an isolated concern and was due to factors such as lack of engagement and complex needs."],[12,"We heard how joint funding was used between health and adult social care to fund therapies for people who were medically ready but were unable to be discharged due to other factors such as limited care capacity or housing. This helped people to maintain their physical skills in a hospital setting and reduced the risk of deconditioning whilst awaiting discharge."],[12,"The local authority told us that most of the reviews overdue by 365 days were currently assigned to the learning disabilities over 25’s team."]],"m":"Although median waiting lists were low assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely and up to date. The local authority told us the waiting time of 726 was an isolated concern and was due to factors such as lack of engagement and complex needs. We heard how joint funding was used between health and adult social care to fund therapies for people who were medically ready but were unable to be discharged due to other factors such as limited care capacity or housing. We heard how"},{"q":[[22,"A partner told us although they were commissioned by the local authority to provide preventative services, funding often did not allow for long or meaningful pieces of work to be carried out, limiting impact."],[26,"People could not always access information and advice on their rights under the Care Act and ways to meet their care and support needs, including for unpaid carers and people who fund or arrange their own care and support."],[26,"Some carers we spoke to told us they did not know how to contact the carers' support service and had not been given information about carers' support from the local authority."]],"m":"A partner told us although they were commissioned by the local authority to provide preventative services, funding often did not allow for long or meaningful pieces of work to be carried out, limiting impact. People could not always access information and advice on their rights under the Care Act and ways to meet their care and support needs, including for unpaid carers and people who fund or arrange their own care and support. Some carers we spoke to told us they did not know how to contact the"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[38,"The local authority had identified gaps in supported living and extra care options to support people throughout their life course including people with more complex needs."],[40,"There were not always sufficient care and support available to meet demand, and people could not always access it when, where and how they needed it."],[42,"Staff told us any discussions with health regarding funding did not impact the person and their service provision."]],"m":"The provision for residential care and nursing homes was limited in Newham. The local authority had identified gaps in supported living and extra care options to support people throughout their life course including people with more complex needs. There were not always sufficient care and support available to meet demand, and people could not always access it when, where and how they needed it. The local authority often relied on out of borough placements due to the limited care home capacity wi"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[51,"There were examples of working closely with partners and how collaborative working between adult social care, health and public health and other partners meant learning was shared to meet joint goals and outcomes. For example, we heard how learning from a Safeguarding Adult Review in relation to a fire incident resulted in joint working with the fire brigade to identify fire risks in people's homes."],[52,"However, one unpaid carer told us they did not feel included or involved and as a result their cared for person lost out on services when they turned 18 years old."],[53,"The transformation of the transitions team was still underway during our assessment and therefore the local authority was unable to identify any improvements in practice this has made to date."]],"m":"However, one unpaid carer told us they did not feel included or involved and as a result their cared for person lost out on services when they turned 18 years old. The transformation of the transitions team was still underway during our assessment and therefore the local authority was unable to identify any improvements in practice this has made to date. Funding decisions or disputes with other agencies did not lead to delays in the provision of care and support. Funding decisions or disputes wi"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[62,"However, there was instability within the local authority and adult social care leadership team."],[62,"The Director of Adult Social Services (DASS) had been in an interim role for the past three years, an advert for a new permanent DASS was live during our assessment."],[62,"Following our assessment the Chief Executive of Newham Council stepped down from their role and the Mayor of Newham has decided not to run for Mayor in the next cabinet election."]],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Norfolk":[{"q":[[12,"People’s feedback showed this work had not yet led to consistent improvement in people’s experiences. For example, people told us the information, advice and support about accessing assessment offered by the local authority not always helpful and did not lead to positive outcomes for the people seeking support."],[15,"People sometimes faced delays to Care Act assessments and reviews, but the local authority had undertaken work to improve this over the previous two years."],[18,"SACE data did not include unpaid carers supported by the commissioned carers service, which made up the majority of unpaid carers in the county."]],"m":"However, other people said it was sometimes difficult to access information about assessments. People’s feedback showed this work had not yet led to consistent improvement in people’s experiences. People sometimes faced delays to Care Act assessments and reviews, but the local authority had undertaken work to improve this over the previous two years. However data also showed some people had waited up to 252 days, but the local authority monitored these and told us there were external factors tha"},{"q":[[22,"The local authority was in the process of enhancing its approach to prevention. This meant there had been a recent increase in the services on offer, but there were gaps in preventative provision in some parts of the county while this work was being implemented."],[22,"The local authority’s 2024-2029 ‘Promoting Independence’ strategy was underway and we saw evidence of work progressing to enhance prevention, including addressing gaps in services."],[25,"People were not always able to access reablement when they needed it, but the local authority was building capacity in its reablement services."]],"m":"The local authority was in the process of enhancing its approach to prevention. This meant there had been a recent increase in the services on offer, but there were gaps in preventative provision in some parts of the county while this work was being implemented. The local authority had a broad prevention offer but were working to address identified gaps in it. The local authority’s 2024-2029 ‘Promoting Independence’ strategy was underway and we saw evidence of work progressing to enhance prevent"},{"q":[[30,"The local authority had taken steps to understand its local population profile and demographics, but there were gaps in the data they gathered around sexuality or marital status which could impact on their ability to understand the experiences of people."],[30,"The local authority understood its local population profile and demographics in most areas, but gaps in data meant there were areas where analysis could not take place."],[33,"There was a corporate equality strategy in place which was overseen by leaders, however leaders said the department was not always represented at EDI performance board meetings where actions were monitored at a corporate level."]],"m":"The local authority had taken steps to understand its local population profile and demographics, but there were gaps in the data they gathered around sexuality or marital status which could impact on their ability to understand the experiences of people. The local authority understood its local population profile and demographics in most areas, but gaps in data meant there were areas where analysis could not take place. The local authority did not have enough data around sexuality or marital sta"},{"q":[[40,"There were gaps in provision across all service types, which the local authority were working to address."],[41,"Dashboards did not capture the reason people were placed out of area, including people’s wishes to move back home."],[42,"Local authority data for the 12 months to February 2025 showed there were 97 instances of people having to wait for supported living and 26 occasions where people waited for residential services for autistic people or people with a learning disability."]],"m":"The local authority had developed a good understanding about local need and were using this to address gaps in capacity which had been a longstanding challenge. The local authority told us how preventative approaches had meant despite their data showing the volumes of people of working age seeking support having risen by 62.4%, the number of people requiring longer term support had been significantly lower at 19.6%. This indicated a higher proportion of people were not requiring ongoing care in "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[55,"There was a governance structure in place which routinely monitored data and quality measures to understand performance when it came to safety and learning was shared across teams and at a senior level."],[55,"However, the gaps in care provision sometimes impacted on safety in the system because it meant people remained placed in inappropriate placements longer than the notice periods within contracts."],[55,"Care and support interventions were planned and organised with people, together with partners and communities, in ways that ensured continuity in care. This included referrals, admissions, and discharge, as well as transitions between services."]],"m":"However, the gaps in care provision sometimes impacted on safety in the system because it meant people remained placed in inappropriate placements longer than the notice periods within contracts. Funding decisions or disputes with other agencies did not lead to delays in the provision of care and support."},{"q":[[64,"People’s experiences of making safeguarding personal (MSP) were mixed. Partners said people they worked with did not always receive feedback or a response to safeguarding concerns that were raised. Partners described more than one example of local authority staff not speaking to the person subject to potential abuse, or requesting their desired outcomes, in a timely way."],[59,"However, the feedback we received showed responses to these concerns were not always timely."],[64,"Staff followed a nationally recognised tool to understand risk but our findings showed work to reduce waiting times had not yet achieved the local authority’s ambitions."]],"m":"Whilst some partners told us about positive experiences of safeguarding where they received a timely response and learning was shared, other partners described not always receiving a response or any outcome to referrals they had made. Whilst some partners told us about positive experiences of safeguarding where they received a timely response and learning was shared, other partners described not always receiving a response or any outcome to referrals they had made. They said this meant there wer"},{"q":[[66,"There had been significant progress made in addressing challenges, but these were not yet consistently embedded."],[67,"Action plans and data showed the local authority had made progress in all of these areas but had not yet fully achieved their ambitions."],[67,"Data showed improvement trajectory in waiting lists for assessments and safeguarding as well as reduced delays in finding care provision as strategies were implemented in these areas."]],"m":"There had been significant progress made in addressing challenges, but these were not yet consistently embedded. Action plans and data showed the local authority had made progress in all of these areas but had not yet fully achieved their ambitions. Data showed improvement trajectory in waiting lists for assessments and safeguarding as well as reduced delays in finding care provision as strategies were implemented in these areas. However, the improvements in people’s experiences were not yet sus"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"North East Lincolnshire":[{"q":[[11,"Unpaid carers highlighted limited support with respite and future planning, alongside delays and repeated conversations, often caused by inconsistent approaches across frontline teams."],[13,"Front door services supported people while they waited, including access to prevention and befriending services."],[14,"Barriers also included misinformation, being told they were not eligible, and difficulty accessing support through SPA or frontline teams."]],"m":"Unpaid carers highlighted limited support with respite and future planning, alongside delays and repeated conversations, often caused by inconsistent approaches across frontline teams. One unpaid carer described a lengthy assessment process which involved five best interest meetings over six months. This was due to an inconsistent understanding of consent and best interest. However, care plan reviews showed that while people were asked strength-based questions, their responses were not consisten"},{"q":[[21,"While the strategy outlined a clear vision for aligning health and social care, there is limited publicly available evidence to confirm whether these plans have been fully realised as the strategy nears its end."],[22,"However, the success of the supported employment service led to a long waitlist, prompting the development of foundational skills placements, internships, and a care coordinator role to manage waiting lists and improve service, which had a positive impact."],[25,"Similarly, urgent community responses from the Single Point of Access (SPA) provided same-day equipment as part of integrated assessments. This ensured people leaving hospital or in urgent need were not delayed by equipment access, reducing risk and supporting recovery."]],"m":"While the strategy outlined a clear vision for aligning health and social care, there is limited publicly available evidence to confirm whether these plans have been fully realised as the strategy nears its end. A health partner confirmed the local authority’s proactive involvement of the VCSE in hospital discharge planning, especially when care was refused, by connecting people to VCSE partners. This helped prevent readmission and ensured people were not left without support, which promoted rec"},{"q":[[33,"A senior leader told us frontline teams worked with people from diverse ethnic backgrounds, including the growing ‘white other’ population, and collaborated with VCSE organisations to improve outreach. This helped ensure people from underrepresented groups were not excluded from accessing support."],[34,"Staff told us they used shared records and screening tools to identify protected characteristics, enabling them to adapt engagement and connect people to culturally appropriate services, including translation support. These examples showed staff were responsive to diverse needs, even when people did not meet eligibility criteria, which promoted inclusion and reduced barriers to care."],[34,"Some told us protected characteristics were not part of contract monitoring, and more funding was needed to regenerate deprived areas and address health inequalities. This suggested a need for stronger alignment between the local authority’s strategy and partner agency practices, to ensure equitable service delivery and inclusive commissioning."]],"m":"A senior leader told us frontline teams worked with people from diverse ethnic backgrounds, including the growing ‘white other’ population, and collaborated with VCSE organisations to improve outreach. This helped ensure people from underrepresented groups were not excluded from accessing support. Staff told us they used shared records and screening tools to identify protected characteristics, enabling them to adapt engagement and connect people to culturally appropriate services, including tran"},{"q":[[37,"Although diagnostic support was provided quickly, services and support systems had not yet been fully aligned, and the strategy was not yet fully implemented."],[37,"Other strategies, such as the carers and autism strategies, identified rising numbers in these groups and gaps in service provision. These strategies were coproduced and included action plans to improve and develop services tailored to these needs."],[38,"The VCSE and commissioned providers shared information with the local authority when they identified a need or gap in provision. For example, in Immingham, where deprivation was high, a local provider offered a community kitchen to increase engagement."]],"m":"Although diagnostic support was provided quickly, services and support systems had not yet been fully aligned, and the strategy was not yet fully implemented. Other strategies, such as the carers and autism strategies, identified rising numbers in these groups and gaps in service provision. Other strategies, such as the carers and autism strategies, identified rising numbers in these groups and gaps in service provision. These strategies were coproduced and included action plans to improve and d"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[72,"However, some staff were unclear about recent SAR learning or how it had been shared. This indicated that while mechanisms for sharing learning were in place, further work was needed to embed learning consistently across teams and ensure accessibility."],[74,"Although the person did not qualify for ongoing support under the Care Act, the safeguarding practitioner followed up with relevant partners and teams. This demonstrated a commitment to safeguarding people regardless of eligibility."],[75,"Additionally, safeguarding data trends in North East Lincolnshire did not align with national patterns, prompting further analysis by the Safeguarding Adults Board and the Director of Adult Social Services."]],"m":"A senior leader told us vulnerable adults were prioritised even when they did not meet Care Act eligibility. However, some frontline staff shared with us they were not always sure how to raise concerns, which meant we could not be assured all concerns had been recorded in a timely and consistent way. However, some staff were unclear about recent SAR learning or how it had been shared. However, some staff were unclear about recent SAR learning or how it had been shared. This indicated that while "},{"q":[[82,"One CiC experienced staffing challenges during mid-2024, leading to delays in reviews."],[82,"Deprivation of Liberty Safeguards (DoLS) backlog was placed on the risk register, and the DASS oversaw a matrix approach to prioritisation."],[85,"A political member told us they regularly requested and received updates on current issues, including delays in administering the Disabled Facilities Grant. They said their involvement helped streamline aspects of the process, resulting in reduced waiting times for people needing adaptations."]],"m":"However, unpaid carers told us changes led by senior leadership were not always reflected in frontline practice. Carers said senior leadership at the local authority listened to them and valued their opinions, but they felt changes made at a strategic level did not always translate into operational delivery. One CiC experienced staffing challenges during mid-2024, leading to delays in reviews. Deprivation of Liberty Safeguards (DoLS) backlog was placed on the risk register, and the DASS oversaw "},{"q":[],"m":""}],"North Lincolnshire":[{"q":[[10,"Social Care Navigators received initial first contact with people and gathered essential information to support a safe and coordinated pathway from first contact to assessment, with triage assessments also carried out by qualified staff as appropriate, although they did not usually transfer calls directly to staff that carried out assessment and prioritisation, and people were advised someone from the duty team would contact them back."],[13,"The local authority had funded 5 posts within care at home services who were able to undertake a review of people’s care and support needs. This reduced waiting times and meant people did not have to repeat their story, helping changes to support happen more quickly."],[16,"Navigators signposted people to universal services and community groups when they did not request or appear to need a formal Care Act assessment, helping people access practical support without entering statutory pathways."]],"m":"Social Care Navigators received initial first contact with people and gathered essential information to support a safe and coordinated pathway from first contact to assessment, with triage assessments also carried out by qualified staff as appropriate, although they did not usually transfer calls directly to staff that carried out assessment and prioritisation, and people were advised someone from the duty team would contact them back. Some protected characteristic information was recorded withi"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[31,"Although Joint Strategic Needs Assessment (JSNA) data identified Scunthorpe as North Lincolnshire’s most ethnically diverse area, senior leaders told us data gaps limited their understanding and monitoring of inequalities."],[31,"To address the data gaps and better understand and monitor inequalities the local authority was drawing on a range of additional resources, such as, the Director of Public Health’s Annual Report, lived experience feedback and commissioned research. This would support a broader understanding of inequalities where quantative data was limited."],[36,"Data provided by the local authority highlighted that only 4% of people accessing services were from a minority ethnic background (including White Other) which was not reflective of their communities."]],"m":"Although Joint Strategic Needs Assessment (JSNA) data identified Scunthorpe as North Lincolnshire’s most ethnically diverse area, senior leaders told us data gaps limited their understanding and monitoring of inequalities. To address the data gaps and better understand and monitor inequalities the local authority was drawing on a range of additional resources, such as, the Director of Public Health’s Annual Report, lived experience feedback and commissioned research. This would support a broader"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[55,"Partners told us the provision of step-down bed facilities such as in house care home provision had facilitated safe transitions from hospital to community care and had improved system flow and reduced delays in hospital discharges."],[57,"Whilst other unpaid carers told us during hospital admission, they did not receive assistance from the Carer Support Worker despite being in a vulnerable situation."],[57,"Contingency planning, also referred to as emergency planning, was available to outline the support required if an unpaid carer, commissioned service, or the local authority became unable to provide care; however, practice was inconsistent, and individual arrangements were not always in place."]],"m":"People told us there were structured pathways following hospital discharge, with assessments promptly arranged, which supported continuity and reduced delays in accessing post-discharge care. Partners told us the provision of step-down bed facilities such as in house care home provision had facilitated safe transitions from hospital to community care and had improved system flow and reduced delays in hospital discharges. Whilst other unpaid carers told us during hospital admission, they did not "},{"q":[[61,"The local authority used a data dashboard to support systematic monitoring of concerns, outcomes, and abuse trends, providing valuable insights for identifying patterns and informing strategic interventions. This approach ensured that safeguarding was not limited to just individual people but also considered the wider impact on local people at risk of harm and abuse."],[65,"The local authority used a 3-stage check on duty for safeguarding: does the person have a need for care and support, are they experiencing, or are at risk of abuse or neglect and, because of their care and support needs, are they are unable to protect themselves from harm or risk of harm."],[67,"In some records, outcomes were primarily recorded at the conclusion of the safeguarding process, with limited evidence of how progress towards those outcomes had been reviewed or tracked throughout the enquiry."]],"m":"However, in some records we reviewed, the rationale for decisions and risk management was not always fully evidenced within the recording. The local authority used a data dashboard to support systematic monitoring of concerns, outcomes, and abuse trends, providing valuable insights for identifying patterns and informing strategic interventions. This approach ensured that safeguarding was not limited to just individual people but also considered the wider impact on local people at risk of harm an"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"North Northamptonshire":[{"q":[[11,"The impact of the restructuring had not yet been measured so it was not possible to assess its effectiveness. This reorganisation formed part of the \"Moving Forward with Place\" programme, aimed at streamlining service delivery and improving outcomes for residents."],[12,"The Care Home Review Team contributed to this by restructuring team roles and coordinating joint visits with quality officers, which improved efficiency and reduced backlogs. Despite this progress, staff continued to report concerns about delays in reviews."],[12,"The application of the waiting well guidance was inconsistent across teams."]],"m":"The impact of the restructuring had not yet been measured so it was not possible to assess its effectiveness. The impact of the restructuring had not yet been measured so it was not possible to assess its effectiveness. This reorganisation formed part of the \"Moving Forward with Place\" programme, aimed at streamlining service delivery and improving outcomes for residents. Assessments and care planning arrangements were not always timely or up to date. People and partners expressed concerns regar"},{"q":[[20,"Co-production and sustainability were embedded within the strategy, though not yet fully embedded in practice."],[21,"The local authority had created an Occupational Therapy Strategy to improve early intervention and co-produced care; however, this was still in draft and had not yet been implemented."],[21,"There was limited evidence that the local authority consistently monitored how such outcomes aligned with Care Act wellbeing duties or contributed to reducing long-term care needs. This restricted its ability to evaluate and evidence the effectiveness of prevention across the system."]],"m":"Co-production and sustainability were embedded within the strategy, though not yet fully embedded in practice. The local authority had created an Occupational Therapy Strategy to improve early intervention and co-produced care; however, this was still in draft and had not yet been implemented. There was limited evidence that the local authority consistently monitored how such outcomes aligned with Care Act wellbeing duties or contributed to reducing long-term care needs. There was limited eviden"},{"q":[[31,"Equality Screening Assessments and self-assessments demonstrated an awareness of disparities and the intent to deliver inclusive care, but these were not consistently applied across adult social care."],[31,"Equality Impact Assessments (EIAs) were not routinely involving people with lived experience and were infrequently published, which reduced opportunities for broader consultation and application in service design."],[31,"Some proposals associated with these assessments had not yet progressed to commissioning and the corporate equality team were not directly aligned with adult social care, instead providing a more corporate function. They had little involvement in the creation of new adult social care strategies."]],"m":"The local authority was working towards understanding its local population profile and demographics. It did not consistently analyse equality data for people who used social care or use it to identify and reduce inequalities in people’s care and support experiences and outcomes. In addition, staff said formal processes to incorporate community voice into service planning were limited, reducing opportunities for meaningful engagement with seldom-heard populations. Equality Screening Assessments a"},{"q":[[37,"Where gaps were identified, such as limited evening and weekend support for younger adults, or the need for specialised accommodation, the local authority undertook planning and development activity, including consultation with assessment teams and people with lived experience to commission services to meet local needs. For example, at the time of the assessment, there were 2 new extra care schemes in development."],[37,"While this flexible approach allowed some responsiveness, it was not underpinned by a dedicated commissioning framework for culturally specific services, and provision was largely reactive rather than strategically embedded."],[39,"Partner feedback indicated that delays in hospital discharge had occurred due to limited capacity, particularly in relation to pathway 3 residential services."]],"m":"Where gaps were identified, such as limited evening and weekend support for younger adults, or the need for specialised accommodation, the local authority undertook planning and development activity, including consultation with assessment teams and people with lived experience to commission services to meet local needs. While this flexible approach allowed some responsiveness, it was not underpinned by a dedicated commissioning framework for culturally specific services, and provision was largel"},{"q":[[45,"Some staff reported barriers that limited the local authority’s ability to deliver integrated, person-centred services in full alignment with its partners."],[46,"While overarching governance structures were established, such as the Adults Services and Health Partnership Board and the monthly quality board, evidence of the local authority’s own review and accountability mechanisms remained limited. This lack of evaluative oversight limited the local authority’s ability to demonstrate the effectiveness of its partnership arrangements."],[47,"The local authority had only just begun to embed formal mechanisms for evaluating the effectiveness of partnership arrangements. As a result, there was limited available evidence regarding the impact of these initiatives."]],"m":"Partners felt this limited the local authority’s ability to co-produce solutions and respond to changing needs. The local authority had taken some steps to engage people with lived experience in shaping local objectives, however, this was not yet consistent or embedded across all adult social care sectors. People who had participated in public engagement groups described limited opportunities to share their views and said they would welcome more consistent involvement. Some staff reported barrie"},{"q":[[51,"The overall structure of care pathways was not clearly defined, which made it challenging to understand the routes people might follow throughout their care journey."],[51,"Notably, the absence of out-of-hours Approved Mental Health Professionals (AMHPs) was recorded as the highest risk, followed by limited review capacity."],[51,"The risk register did not consistently articulate the potential impact of these risks on people, nor did it detail sufficient mitigating actions. For example, the suggested approach to address the shortfall in review capacity involved senior leadership teams identifying priority areas for focused attention, a valuable step, though not a direct mitigation measure."]],"m":"The overall structure of care pathways was not clearly defined, which made it challenging to understand the routes people might follow throughout their care journey. Notably, the absence of out-of-hours Approved Mental Health Professionals (AMHPs) was recorded as the highest risk, followed by limited review capacity. The risk register did not consistently articulate the potential impact of these risks on people, nor did it detail sufficient mitigating actions. A 'waiting well' protocol existed t"},{"q":[[56,"In 2024, fewer than 20 of the 80 audits sent to practitioners were returned, and in August 2024, only two responses were submitted. This limited the local authority’s ability to develop a representative understanding of safeguarding performance."],[56,"While the audit forms addressed aspects such as making safeguarding personal, they did not capture whether communication and outcomes had been shared with the original referrer or the individual at risk, representing an area for future consideration."],[58,"The local authority demonstrated a clear understanding of what constituted a safeguarding concern under Section 42 of the Care Act 2014, and when a statutory enquiry was required. However, this understanding was not always applied consistently or in a timely manner across all teams."]],"m":"In 2024, fewer than 20 of the 80 audits sent to practitioners were returned, and in August 2024, only two responses were submitted. This limited the local authority’s ability to develop a representative understanding of safeguarding performance. While the audit forms addressed aspects such as making safeguarding personal, they did not capture whether communication and outcomes had been shared with the original referrer or the individual at risk, representing an area for future consideration. Whi"},{"q":[[63,"Oversight of adult social care matters was instead conducted through the existing health scrutiny panel. It was noted that political leaders, both longstanding and newly appointed, required additional support to deepen their understanding of the complexities, responsibilities, and associated risks within the adult social care system."],[63,"The full implementation of the new system was not expected until 2027."],[64,"A local authority Information Governance Framework was in place, although the information asset register was still under development, which limited full visibility of data holdings."]],"m":"While strategic documents had been produced, there were limited accompanying process plans or review benchmarks to support their implementation and evaluation and implementation appeared to take several years. Oversight of adult social care matters was instead conducted through the existing health scrutiny panel. It was noted that political leaders, both longstanding and newly appointed, required additional support to deepen their understanding of the complexities, responsibilities, and associat"},{"q":[[68,"Staff reported access to learning opportunities was not consistent across the workforce."],[69,"People with lived experience noted that they were not regularly invited to participate in service planning, and staff acknowledged that engagement often occurred late in the process. This observation was supported by the Principal Social Worker’s Annual Report for 2023–24, which identified the need for earlier and more structured involvement."],[70,"Processes for collecting, analysing, and acting on feedback varied, which limited the extent to which service improvements were systematically informed by lived experience or frontline insight."]],"m":"Staff reported access to learning opportunities was not consistent across the workforce. Some staff reported limited access to adult social care-specific training for out of hours training and noted that AMHP supervision sessions were limited. Co-production was not yet consistently embedded across all areas of adult social care. People with lived experience noted that they were not regularly invited to participate in service planning, and staff acknowledged that engagement often occurred late in"}],"North Somerset":[{"q":[[13,"The Local authority acknowledged that waiting times were an area for improvement and were actively working to reduce delays and mitigate risks that impacted peoples’ wellbeing during long waiting periods. They had trialled new tools to reduce waiting times. These included Magic Notes, which aimed to improve productivity, and an AI Chatbot, which gave people access to advice and information 24/7."],[14,"Therefore, although delays in assessments and support remained an ongoing issue, the local authority had taken proactive steps to address these challenges and support individuals while they waited for services."],[14,"Initiatives such as the ‘Waiting Well’ project, which followed best practice guidelines, supported people to maintain their wellbeing and independence during long waits. This showed the local authority’s commitment to improving outcomes for people accessing adult social care services and reducing risks associated with delayed assessments."]],"m":"Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always carried out in a timely manner and reviews were not always up to date. Waiting lists led to delays in people and unpaid carers receiving appropriate support. Waiting lists led to delays in people and unpaid carers receiving appropriate support. These delays increased the risk of people requiring higher levels of care, including alternative placements in care homes. The extent of the delays for assessments and reviews was reflected in the "},{"q":[[22,"Local authority data showed, in the 12 months prior to December 2024, demand for domiciliary care priority (1) pathways, showed that 14% of delays in pathway (1) discharges were caused by challenges in sourcing care. This included both bedded and non-bedded care, funded by the local authority or privately by people."],[27,"There was a TEC strategy that staff, senior leaders and partners were proud of. It was limited to 3 years due to the local authority’s awareness of ever evolving technology."],[30,"Although, this information was not routinely available at all points in the process to ensure people had the best opportunity to consider how direct payments may be of benefit to them. For example, frontline teams told us people were less likely to be given this information when they first contacted the local authority, or on review of a commissioned package of care."]],"m":"Local authority data showed, in the 12 months prior to December 2024, demand for domiciliary care priority (1) pathways, showed that 14% of delays in pathway (1) discharges were caused by challenges in sourcing care. Local authority data showed, in the 12 months prior to December 2024, demand for domiciliary care priority (1) pathways, showed that 14% of delays in pathway (1) discharges were caused by challenges in sourcing care. This included both bedded and non-bedded care, funded by the local"},{"q":[[33,"Senior leaders and staff acknowledged the impact of inequalities in the North of the borough and utilised data intelligence to help inform the strategic planning for adult social care, combined with insights from community groups and provider feedback, reports and surveys to help identify gaps in care provision."],[34,"However, people’s experiences of accessing these services was not always positive, which created barriers accessing support. For example, a person, whose first language was not English, was supported by commissioned carers who could not speak the person’s native language."],[34,"A review identified challenges in the Single Point of Access (SPA), such as IT limitations and gaps in staff training."]],"m":"Senior leaders and staff acknowledged the impact of inequalities in the North of the borough and utilised data intelligence to help inform the strategic planning for adult social care, combined with insights from community groups and provider feedback, reports and surveys to help identify gaps in care provision. However, staff did not have the required confidence when gathering data information about sexuality and gender identity. Partners told us staff were not always proactive in gathering inf"},{"q":[[41,"The local authority had identified several gaps in service provision. For example, there was a gap in the home care market for people with a high level of mental health needs, learning disabilities and a need for more specialist care homes."],[42,"We heard the efforts to achieve this involved a multi-faceted approach, proactively identifying and addressing gaps in service provision."],[42,"Another area of focus was the development of a framework for commissioning services to support people with multiple or high levels of need or risk around mental health and learning disabilities needs. This addressed a gap in the existing domiciliary care market."]],"m":"The local authority recognised this as a priority area, Staff gave us positive feedback about the development of bespoke accommodation with home support, following targeted commissioning to address gaps in the market. Some partners informed us strategic commissioning did not always consider or prioritise the needs of carers. Some people described working with staff to choose accommodation with support which met their needs as being a supportive experience, for others choice for their cared for p"},{"q":[[48,"The Health and Wellbeing Strategy was being re-written (led by public health on behalf of the local authority), with work underway to ensure greater representation of adult social care in the refreshed strategy, with closer alignment with the health and wellbeing board."],[53,"A smaller partner told us if they were not in attendance at meetings they would often get missed from the agenda."],[54,"There was a frustration amongst some partners that larger organisations were offered contracts and the funding from the local authority was often directed toward specific priority areas, such as housing associations which the smaller charities did not regard as VCFSE sector."]],"m":"A smaller partner told us if they were not in attendance at meetings they would often get missed from the agenda. There was a frustration amongst some partners that larger organisations were offered contracts and the funding from the local authority was often directed toward specific priority areas, such as housing associations which the smaller charities did not regard as VCFSE sector."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[62,"The local authority identified there was no formal process to support people at risk of significant harm when they have capacity and potentially fall outside of s.42 Care Act duties."],[67,"However, during our CQC assessment staff outside of the safeguarding team could not identify any specific learning that had taken place."],[69,"There was mixed feedback about how the local authority ensured there were clear rationales and outcomes from initial enquiries, including those which did not progress to a Section 42 enquiry."]],"m":"The local authority identified there was no formal process to support people at risk of significant harm when they have capacity and potentially fall outside of s.42 Care Act duties. Another partner told us they had suggested a face-to-face session between staff to gain a better understanding of the safeguarding work the local authority carried out. However, this had not yet been acted upon and they told us there remained communication challenges between health and social care staff. Staff told "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"North Tyneside":[{"q":[[16,"Both carers were allocated to Carer Wellbeing Workers, although the assessments had not yet concluded."],[17,"For those people unable to access information and advice online, the local authority’s front door Gateway service offered a Local Welfare Provision service for non-eligible needs, including information about fuel poverty, foodbanks, and homelessness."],[13,"Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely or up to date. Data provided by the local authority showed waiting times for Care Act assessment and reviews often exceeded the local authority’s target timescales. As of February 2025, there were 117 people waiting for Care Act assessments, with a maximum waiting time of 106 days."]],"m":"Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely or up to date. Long waits were influenced by increased reassessments, a rise in safeguarding alerts, and the prioritisation of high-risk cases over scheduled reviews for those deemed at lower risk. Partners told us Care Act reviews were sometimes completed without service providers or the families of the people receiving support being contacted to be part of process. They felt this meant people who lacked capacity to make their own"},{"q":[[23,"About 10% of the borough’s population lacked internet access, with older and disabled individuals being the most affected by this digital exclusion."],[27,"The local authority’s ‘Select and Direct’ service offered quick access to frequently needed equipment and minor adaptations, helping to eliminate unnecessary delays for occupational therapy assessments."],[29,"The service played an important role in assisting the North-east Ambulance Service as the first responder for falls, helping to prevent long waits for help and reducing the need for hospital admissions."]],"m":"About 10% of the borough’s population lacked internet access, with older and disabled individuals being the most affected by this digital exclusion. Senior leaders told us about specialist support offered by the Mental Health Reablement team and the Learning Disability Enablement team who supported people living with multiple complex needs to live more independent lives through intensive, time-limited support to gain or regain independent living skills, preventing and reducing long-term needs fo"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[60,"People told us North Tyneside co-produced, planned and organised how to meet their care and support needs, together with people, partners, and communities. This was done in ways which improved their safety across their care journeys and ensured continuity in care."],[61,"Feedback specific to young people with learning disabilities highlighted gaps in support around finding employment and travel training programmes."],[61,"People told us of confusion about the assessment process for the transition between services after the initial 6 weeks support offer into longer-term support, leading to delays in packages of care and lack of involvement in decision making around which providers supported them."]],"m":"Feedback specific to young people with learning disabilities highlighted gaps in support around finding employment and travel training programmes. People told us of confusion about the assessment process for the transition between services after the initial 6 weeks support offer into longer-term support, leading to delays in packages of care and lack of involvement in decision making around which providers supported them. Funding decisions or disputes with other agencies did not lead to delays i"},{"q":[[65,"Staff understanding of safeguarding data dashboards varied, with some telling us they were unaware of them. This led to confusion around performance outcomes and a lack of communication about themes and trends."],[67,"Further learning in September 2024 identified systemic gaps and areas for improvement for partners within the system including how the escalation of people's risky behaviour was recorded and responded to, how incidents of people's sexual safety and risks were responded to, particularly in the context of a lack of mental capacity and the timeliness of out-of-hours services."],[67,"Learning from the second SAR highlighted gaps in partnership working, pre-admission and assessment checks."]],"m":"The local authority’s systems, processes, practices to make sure people were protected from abuse and neglect were not always effective. Oversight of the length of time safeguarding enquiries took to resolve was not always robust and investigations took longer to resolve than expected. Staff understanding of safeguarding data dashboards varied, with some telling us they were unaware of them. This led to confusion around performance outcomes and a lack of communication about themes and trends. Fu"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"North Yorkshire":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Northumberland":[{"q":[[12,"Northumberland allocated all Care Act assessments to social workers within 5 working days but had a \"small\" waiting list for carers assessments, and a waiting list for overdue assessments."],[13,"The median time between a review becoming overdue and the review being completed was 37 calendar days."],[14,"To ensure people were kept safe, there was continuous contact from adult services during the period where the review was overdue."]],"m":"However, we noted that the strengths-based approach to assessment and support was not always consistent. However, when homecare and long-term support was put in place involving other care agencies, the same approach was not always adopted. Northumberland allocated all Care Act assessments to social workers within 5 working days but had a \"small\" waiting list for carers assessments, and a waiting list for overdue assessments. Leaders noted there should be more oversight of all overdue assessments"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[31,"People using services felt there were shortfalls with the sensory offer as there was limited provision for adults with hearing impairment."],[32,"However, we saw examples of information on equipment and adaptations which did not indicate to people they could request the information in another language."],[33,"Partners highlighted where information was not available in every language, and where there were only digital forms of information, accessibility was an issue as some people were excluded from having access to services."]],"m":"People using services felt there were shortfalls with the sensory offer as there was limited provision for adults with hearing impairment. However, we saw examples of information on equipment and adaptations which did not indicate to people they could request the information in another language. Partners highlighted where information was not available in every language, and where there were only digital forms of information, accessibility was an issue as some people were excluded from having acc"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Nottingham":[{"q":[[10,"People could contact the local authority’s care and support services in multiple ways, for example, through an online form, by telephone or through community-based partner agencies and hubs referrals. However, this was not always easy for people, and people and partners told us getting in touch with the local authority via the telephone was difficult and the telephone number often rang out, or they were placed on hold for a long time."],[13,"A waiting well tool was being implemented to ensure consistent support to people who were on a waiting list, however, this was not yet in place at the time of our assessment."],[14,"The Nottingham City Health and Care Point (front door) team provided people with advice, information and signposting to other services and facilities in the area for help with care and support needs that were not eligible under the Care Act."]],"m":"People could contact the local authority’s care and support services in multiple ways, for example, through an online form, by telephone or through community-based partner agencies and hubs referrals. However, this was not always easy for people, and people and partners told us getting in touch with the local authority via the telephone was difficult and the telephone number often rang out, or they were placed on hold for a long time. However, staff and partner feedback was that care was not alw"},{"q":[[18,"However, staff also told us there was a gap in some areas such as employment support services in Nottingham where there used to be a dedicated team to support people."],[20,"Data provided by the local authority showed 75% people who accessed mental health reablement did not go on to require any further support, demonstrating its impact in supporting people to maintain their independence."],[20,"Some staff said there were waiting times to access reablement from the community, whilst mental health reablement currently had an 8 to 12 week wait for allocation. Staff told us this meant they were using external care home providers instead, to provide care."]],"m":"However, staff also told us there was a gap in some areas such as employment support services in Nottingham where there used to be a dedicated team to support people. Data provided by the local authority showed 75% people who accessed mental health reablement did not go on to require any further support, demonstrating its impact in supporting people to maintain their independence. However, there were 61.00% of people who received reablement during the year, who previously were not receiving serv"},{"q":[[28,"Due to a lack of data being captured it was difficult for the local authority to fully monitor people’s experiences and ensure all services provided were culturally appropriate."],[29,"However, due to the lack of services, it was likely people would not always have access to culturally competent care, thereby creating an inequality of access for people."],[30,"Although over 17% of people in Nottingham did not speak English as their first language, staff and partners told us information was not always readily accessible in different languages."]],"m":"Its ability to analyse equality data was still developing, which limited how effectively it could identify and reduce inequalities in access, experience and outcomes. Staff and partners described the work of community connectors and community champions in engaging seldom heard groups such as Roma, Muslim and African communities, to hear the voices of these communities and identify gaps in service provision. Over 17% of residents did not speak English as their first language. Data collected by ad"},{"q":[[33,"However, people did not always feel they had a choice over services."],[34,"Despite a strong respite offer for unpaid carers, commissioning decisions did not always reflect best practice. For example, the extra care market within Nottingham was described as having inconsistent levels of care provided and low usage."],[39,"The local authority recognised services were experiencing difficulties in continuing to be sustainable. It was noted from the local authority’s risk register the latest annual uplift in fees for providers was relatively small and this combined with rising costs for services was a risk to sustainability."]],"m":"Despite clear intention and effort by staff to develop their understanding of the local population and care needs, these restrictions meant the impact for people was limited. However, people did not always feel they had a choice over services. Despite a strong respite offer for unpaid carers, commissioning decisions did not always reflect best practice. There were not enough services for young people with a disability, general supported living, mental health services for people with complex need"},{"q":[[44,"When the local authority worked in partnerships with other agencies, there were not always clear arrangements for governance, accountability, monitoring, quality assurance and information sharing."],[45,"Care Providers told us there was a lack of communication about changes from the local authority, which led to uncertainty and confusion. They said provider forums had stopped without any explanation, and any collaboration in recent years was facilitated by the Nottingham Care Agency (NCA)."],[46,"For teams who were not co-located some staff told us relationships with partners varied and there was some inconsistency."]],"m":"When the local authority worked in partnerships with other agencies, there were not always clear arrangements for governance, accountability, monitoring, quality assurance and information sharing. Examples were shared about how this impacted on people, including delays in people being discharged from hospital and care being reduced so providers could not meet people’s needs. Partnership working between the local authority and providers was not always effective. Care Providers told us there was a"},{"q":[[51,"However, some actions were not completed and were in progress and staff were unclear of developments which meant risks remained. For example, a Rapid Review of Mental Health Social Care and Approved Mental Health Professional (AMHP) Services (July 2025) had been carried out by a national improvement partner."],[53,"When people moved between care services, this was not always planned and organised with people and partners to ensure safety."],[54,"The local authority made improvements to its processes in June 2025, by introducing a daily approval forum to agree care and support plans and reduce delays in hospital discharges."]],"m":"The local authority’s processes to manage risks to people across their care journeys, were not always effective and some risks were not identified and managed proactively. However, some actions were not completed and were in progress and staff were unclear of developments which meant risks remained. However, staff told us they were not aware of any progress and had not been kept up to date. However, staff told us they were not aware of any progress and had not been kept up to date. They also tol"},{"q":[[62,"Feedback from providers also included, not having a named contact for concerns and having to go through the front door service with long waits on the telephone."],[64,"Whilst some staff teams spoke about reviewing 7-minute briefings and attending learning sessions following SARs, other staff teams told us of lack of opportunities in this area and noted it as an area for improvement."],[69,"There were delays in assessing Deprivation of Liberty Safeguards (DoLS) applications which increased the risk of people’s liberty being restricted unnecessarily."]],"m":"Feedback from providers also included, not having a named contact for concerns and having to go through the front door service with long waits on the telephone. However, staff feedback demonstrated learning was not shared consistently across the organisation. Whilst some staff teams spoke about reviewing 7-minute briefings and attending learning sessions following SARs, other staff teams told us of lack of opportunities in this area and noted it as an area for improvement. The local authority sh"},{"q":[[72,"The Local Government Association (LGA) Peer review report dated July 2025 found a clear lack of overarching strategic vision and narrative which was exacerbated by the frequent changes in leadership."],[73,"Experienced internal staff had stepped into Head of Service roles through secondments and whilst this gave a level of continuity and organisational knowledge, these were not permanent appointments. This new leadership structure was to be aligned with the local authority’s new Target Operating Model (TOM), which aimed to redefine roles and responsibilities to enhance adult social care’s capacity and resilience."],[76,"Nottingham Peer Review Final Report July 2025 documented consistently hearing from people using services, staff and partners that they did not know what was happening in adult social care in Nottingham."]],"m":"The Local Government Association (LGA) Peer review report dated July 2025 found a clear lack of overarching strategic vision and narrative which was exacerbated by the frequent changes in leadership. The review said this had led to rapidly shifting priorities, inconsistent evidence, data and transformation projects that felt disjointed. One partner told us they experienced high levels of frustration due to poor communication, high staff turnover, and lack of strategic engagement from the local a"},{"q":[[79,"Co-production was not embedded throughout the local authority’s work, however there was action and commitment to drive and improve this."],[80,"The new framework ensured a random selection of people’s cases and managers audited records from teams which were not within their line management structure."],[83,"A positive example of improvements following feedback, was the introduction of personal budgets with an aim to give unpaid carers choice and control about how they took breaks from caring responsibilities. This offer was introduced after a commissioning review found the carers respite service did not meet everybody’s needs and parents and unpaid carers were involved in developing the offer."]],"m":"Co-production was not embedded throughout the local authority’s work, however there was action and commitment to drive and improve this. Staff told us the local authority Carers Strategy had been co-produced with unpaid carers; however, examples of wider engagement were more limited. Partners told us they were not aware of any wider coproduction activities and the local authority were not proactive in this area. The new framework ensured a random selection of people’s cases and managers audited "}],"Nottinghamshire":[{"q":[[14,"In December 2025, 2687 peoples’ annual reviews were overdue and of these, 944 reviews were overdue by more than 18 months."],[15,"Waiting times across districts and teams varied significantly and it was unclear how the local authority re-allocated resources to manage rising demand across the different areas. For example, in December 2025, waits for Care Act assessments in one district were significantly longer than in another district."],[17,"In December 2025, 101 unpaid carers were waiting for an assessment, with 60 of these people having waited more than 28 days."]],"m":"The new structure underpinned an ‘all-age’ approach to supporting adults with Care Act needs and staff told us they were not structured by specialty, rather an all-age ‘team around the adult’ approach. The new structure underpinned an ‘all-age’ approach to supporting adults with Care Act needs and staff told us they were not structured by specialty, rather an all-age ‘team around the adult’ approach. Through this new structure, allocations were made based on staff experiences and skills and some"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[49,"There were embedded governance arrangements and multi-agency forums through which the local authority could support partnership working."],[50,"We heard there was a lack of coordination between system partners and internally in the local authority which resulted in the families of people requiring care and/or support being asked the same questions repeatedly by different agencies or departments within the local authority."],[50,"Some partners also told us it had become progressively more difficult to get in touch with the local authority through the customer service number, with social workers sometimes taking 3 to 4 days to return calls."]],"m":"We heard there was a lack of coordination between system partners and internally in the local authority which resulted in the families of people requiring care and/or support being asked the same questions repeatedly by different agencies or departments within the local authority. Some partners also told us it had become progressively more difficult to get in touch with the local authority through the customer service number, with social workers sometimes taking 3 to 4 days to return calls."},{"q":[[55,"The local authority also acknowledged the risk of people becoming ‘lost’ between services due to gaps in access or referral criteria."],[56,"In the short-term, staff said they were sometimes able to agree temporary uplifts in peoples’ packages of care to support the cared-for person’s unpaid carer, but this was not a sustainable approach. This indicated out of hours support for people with Care Act needs and unpaid carers required review and improvement by the local authority."],[58,"In December 2025, 37 people were waiting for a Care Act assessment so they could be discharged, and no-one waited more than 28 days."]],"m":"The local authority also acknowledged the risk of people becoming ‘lost’ between services due to gaps in access or referral criteria. In the short-term, staff said they were sometimes able to agree temporary uplifts in peoples’ packages of care to support the cared-for person’s unpaid carer, but this was not a sustainable approach. In the short-term, staff said they were sometimes able to agree temporary uplifts in peoples’ packages of care to support the cared-for person’s unpaid carer, but thi"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[72,"Although there were consistent approaches to quality assurance and governance across most adult social care areas, we found a lack of auditing practices in place for an out of hours team that operated across both children’s and adults services."],[73,"We also found where there were shortfalls in delivery of Care Act duties, risk mitigations were in place, and these were effective in keeping people safe. For example, staff and leaders told us about recruitment and retention challenges within the local authority’s social workforce and how this could impact on its ability to fulfil its Care Act duties."],[75,"Within the local authority, more work was needed to ensure clarity of strategic decisions and direction within the workforce. This was because some staff lacked clarity about the need for strategic redesign of adult social care services into an all-age approach. For example, some told us the redesign aimed to reduce barriers between the local authority’s ‘living well’ and ‘aging well’ teams."]],"m":"Although staff described immediate line management as supportive in the main, staff feedback indicated senior leaders lacked visibility. Some staff told us leaders were doing more to be visible and transparent regarding decision making, but most said they did not feel heard by senior leaders at the time of the CQC assessment. We did not see evidence that leaders had yet planned to address specific wellbeing and support issues flagged during the CQC assessment. Although there were consistent appr"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Oldham":[{"q":[[9,"However, some people’s care records did not reflect the same level of person-centred and strength-based support. For example, some care records lacked detail about the person’s strengths or the views of the person receiving care or their family."],[12,"A dedicated review team had been introduced in October 2025 to address the backlog of reviews."],[13,"People waited an average of 70 days for a visual impairment specialist assessment and 98 days for a hearing assessment from the sensory team, while waits for dual sensory assessments were approximately 2 weeks."]],"m":"However, some people’s care records did not reflect the same level of person-centred and strength-based support. Leaders said the significant backlog of people waiting for their needs to be reviewed was due to the fact that many people’s long-standing care plans had been developed under a ‘deficit-based’ model rather than the current approach towards strengths-based approach. A dedicated review team had been introduced in October 2025 to address the backlog of reviews. Leaders said the review te"},{"q":[[20,"Additionally, people we spoke with said training programmes that supported people with learning disabilities to access employment had closed, which limited their options to enter the workforce."],[22,"Other unpaid carers told us the lack of available resources in the borough meant they were unable to take planned breaks away to support their wellbeing and prevent burnout. For example, an unpaid carer said access to respite was inconsistent even when booked far in advance for a planned break, resulting in them needing to access respite out of area."],[24,"People experienced lengthy waits for home adaptations: 297 people were waiting for adaptations in October 2025. This was a small reduction from the peak of 339 people in May 2025; however, maximum waiting times had increased steadily from 1958 days in March 2024 to 2445 days in July 2025."]],"m":"Staff particularly highlighted a lack of preventative resources for people with mental health needs and said this was leading to people they supported eventually requiring crisis support to keep them safe. Additionally, people we spoke with said training programmes that supported people with learning disabilities to access employment had closed, which limited their options to enter the workforce. Other unpaid carers told us the lack of available resources in the borough meant they were unable to"},{"q":[[29,"Additionally, local authority data showed disparities in overdue care reviews by ethnicity, age and service type."],[29,"Leaders were aware of gaps in data recording, for example relating to people’s disabilities, nationalities and religion, and they were working towards addressing this through upgrades to the local authority’s digital recording system."],[32,"Additionally, staff made use of relay interpretation services to communicate with people who were both sensorydeprived and did not speak English. This demonstrated creativity within the workforce to ensure the people they supported could participate in care and support planning and decisions regardless of their communication needs."]],"m":"Additionally, local authority data showed disparities in overdue care reviews by ethnicity, age and service type. Leaders were aware of gaps in data recording, for example relating to people’s disabilities, nationalities and religion, and they were working towards addressing this through upgrades to the local authority’s digital recording system. Additionally, staff made use of relay interpretation services to communicate with people who were both sensorydeprived and did not speak English. Addit"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Oxfordshire":[{"q":[[14,"There were concerns from providers that people were either not receiving an assessment prior to discharge or that the documentation about the referrals sent through to them were not completed thoroughly or did not provide an accurate account of people’s care needs."],[15,"In the past, Oxfordshire County Council had significantly long delays for people awaiting assessment and reviews."],[20,"The data provided in July 2024 showed people waited no longer than 8 weeks for a carers assessment with the average waiting time being 6 weeks."]],"m":"On occasions when providers became involved only virtual assessments had been carried out, these did not contain any risk assessments so providers where unaware of any risks they might face ahead of their visits to people’s homes. However, partners identified that, in general, there were long waiting lists for advocacy support which they said impacted people accessing timely support. The local authority told us that it had completed an audit of 50 cases referred to advocacy between April 24 and "},{"q":[[24,"VCS partners told us that they felt valued for the work they delivered that was commissioned by the local authority However, they felt the local authority did not always recognise the additional services and support for people who were not eligible under the Care Act."],[27,"The partnership approach had allowed collaborative working, and all agreed further examination of the data was needed as available data did not allow them to drill down far enough into issues."],[28,"However, some people did not receive adequate support for rehabilitation when discharged from the hospital. For example, some received regular physiotherapy when in hospital, but this stopped when they were discharged, and no provision was in place for it to continue in the community."]],"m":"VCS partners told us that they felt valued for the work they delivered that was commissioned by the local authority However, they felt the local authority did not always recognise the additional services and support for people who were not eligible under the Care Act. However, it was clear that there was a gap in the support offered. The partnership approach had allowed collaborative working, and all agreed further examination of the data was needed as available data did not allow them to drill "},{"q":[[36,"Partners described how this problem was more significant for people living in villages and rural parts of the county unable to access services."],[37,"Partners felt the local authority did not provide or commission services to adequately support domestic abuse for LGBTQIA+ people."],[37,"The local authority acknowledged that information on sexual orientation was not routinely collected, and they were working to ensure all protected characteristics were recorded in systems to have a better understanding of people who use adult social care services."]],"m":"The local authority also had plans to improve data in areas including ethnicity and religion as it was identified there was a lack of data to ensure community needs were understood. Limited local transport provision had prevented some people from being able to take part in social activities, which in turn had increased isolation. Partners described how this problem was more significant for people living in villages and rural parts of the county unable to access services. However, senior leaders "},{"q":[[41,"The local authority had identified that 13,636 people were providing over 50 hours of unpaid care per week in Oxfordshire, many of whom were not in good health."],[42,"The authority had identified the gaps in accommodation and support for people with complex needs including serious mental illness, including psychosis, acquired brain injury, poor physical health and long-term health conditions, addictions, and dual diagnosis."],[45,"The majority of placements were because the authority was unable to identify a provider within Oxfordshire who could meet a person’s specialist requirements. This was more prevalent in people under 65 and people with learning disability and autism diagnoses."]],"m":"The local authority had identified that 13,636 people were providing over 50 hours of unpaid care per week in Oxfordshire, many of whom were not in good health. However, partners felt the data from JSNA was not always used to help providers understand what was needed in the future. The authority had identified the gaps in accommodation and support for people with complex needs including serious mental illness, including psychosis, acquired brain injury, poor physical health and long-term health "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Peterborough":[{"q":[[12,"Some partners told us that care plans and the timing of care calls did not always reflect people’s needs. For example, people with a hearing impairment required more time to communicate with domiciliary care staff during their visits."],[15,"The local authority had a plan in place to ensure minimal waiting times for individuals and to work through their backlog of individuals awaiting review, including identifying outstanding reviews during any interaction with adult services, for example during a hospital admission and completing a review at the time."],[19,"Although unpaid carers were not always clear on the difference between a carer’s conversation and a carer’s assessment they did tell us that they received support from the commissioned carers support service."]],"m":"Some partners told us that care plans and the timing of care calls did not always reflect people’s needs. The local authority had a plan in place to ensure minimal waiting times for individuals and to work through their backlog of individuals awaiting review, including identifying outstanding reviews during any interaction with adult services, for example during a hospital admission and completing a review at the time. Partners told us there were gaps in services for unpaid carers in Peterboroug"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[47,"The local authority recognised that their data around protected characteristics was not comprehensive and required more focus and work was happening to address this."],[47,"Staff provided an example of the Community Champion Network who were involved in a Regeneration Project, and after listening to the voices of marginal groups, along with data and findings from a consultation, decided to review the planned footpath colours to ensure they did not impede people with visual impairments."],[48,"We heard people from seldom heard groups, such as refugees and asylum seekers, did not always engage with the local authority due to fear, difficulty with the process, and receiving the correct information."]],"m":"The local authority recognised that their data around protected characteristics was not comprehensive and required more focus and work was happening to address this. Staff provided an example of the Community Champion Network who were involved in a Regeneration Project, and after listening to the voices of marginal groups, along with data and findings from a consultation, decided to review the planned footpath colours to ensure they did not impede people with visual impairments. We heard people "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Plymouth":[{"q":[[12,"Pathways and processes did not always ensure that people’s support was planned and coordinated across different agencies and services."],[12,"Usage of Trusted Assessors was highlighted by partners as an area which could be improved further. It was felt assessors did not always know about the range of services available in Plymouth and some partners had experience of assessments which were not always accurate."],[18,"The local authority did not currently have any appeals relating to Care Act Assessments, however there was an appeals process in place."]],"m":"Assessment and care planning was not always strengths based. Feedback from some staff, and in some records we reviewed, demonstrated assessments and reviews did not always focus on people’s strengths, but more on deficits and risks. Pathways and processes did not always ensure that people’s support was planned and coordinated across different agencies and services. Usage of Trusted Assessors was highlighted by partners as an area which could be improved further. It was felt assessors did not alw"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[39,"If a package of care could not be brokered, other options such as the use of a direct payment were considered."],[43,"Data from October to December 2024 showed there were no delays for homecare, with a 7 day average waiting time for services to begin."],[45,"Although there was a lack of services for people who had alcohol related brain injuries there was currently a discussion between the local authority and a local organisation to develop this area further."]],"m":"Senior local authority staff told us this document was considered the guiding north star for them, as everything came from this plan, however some staff felt there could be a lack of clarity around the understanding of the JSNA and how it fitted into the overall plan. If a package of care could not be brokered, other options such as the use of a direct payment were considered. Data from October to December 2024 showed there were no delays for homecare, with a 7 day average waiting time for servi"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[59,"A number of staff teams commented on the lack of consistency of access to IT systems across teams where there were primarily 2 systems used, one for health and one for social care."],[60,"Staff did not end their involvement until it was evident the young person was settled."],[61,"Some staff felt there could be better discussions with locality teams however when people were admitted to hospital, as cases were closed to the locality teams, but sometimes this was for such a short period of time, it did not feel to be in the person’s best interests."]],"m":"A number of staff teams commented on the lack of consistency of access to IT systems across teams where there were primarily 2 systems used, one for health and one for social care. Staff did not end their involvement until it was evident the young person was settled. Some staff felt there could be better discussions with locality teams however when people were admitted to hospital, as cases were closed to the locality teams, but sometimes this was for such a short period of time, it did not feel"},{"q":[[67,"In addition, alerts on the local authority IT system recognised when a community DoLS had been authorised, also highlighting when an order was not in place."],[73,"Work was underway with staff, supported by managers and the PSW, to improve when people were asked about their desired outcome in safeguarding enquiries as in 13% of cases, it was documented people were not asked."],[72,"National data from the Safeguarding Adults Collection for 2023/24 showed 34.62% of individuals lacking capacity were supported by an advocate, family, or friend. This was significantly worse than the England average (83.38%)."]],"m":"Other providers felt this could be less responsive however as they had not always got a response when they left a message. In addition, alerts on the local authority IT system recognised when a community DoLS had been authorised, also highlighting when an order was not in place. Other partners had been told referrals made did not meet the local authority safeguarding thresholds, which had meant they could feel more reluctant to refer in future. Some staff disagreed if safeguarding referrals did "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Portsmouth":[{"q":[[10,"If they were not known to the local authority, this would be referred to the Response Team or Helpdesk to review and identify any risk."],[16,"There were 13 unpaid carers awaiting an overdue review."],[18,"The local authority had clear guidance in place for staff to follow to support people with non-eligible needs to ensure appropriate signposting, advice and guidance was provided using a preventative approach for people who were not eligible for care and support under the Care Act."]],"m":"However, staff told us this could be inconsistent and could delay their ability to provide a timely response to people whilst waiting for translator services, particularly in crisis situations, for example, when an interpreter or translator is required for Mental Health Act assessments (MHA) urgently. If they were not known to the local authority, this would be referred to the Response Team or Helpdesk to review and identify any risk. Social workers were based at the hospital and worked closely "},{"q":[[27,"Staff told us that Direct Payments were not being used as much as they should be due to the lack of knowledge and understanding around them."],[28,"Some staff told us that the lack of confidence had been a barrier in improving the uptake of direct payments. For example, staff shared that they often had to rely on the direct payment officers for support and guidance around the direct payments process and when obtaining information on the availability of personal assistants."],[28,"Staff told us that there was limited data analysis such as themes and trends available amongst people receiving direct payments in Portsmouth."]],"m":"Delays in the provision of equipment were reported, the local authority told us this was attributed to the liquidation of a supplier. People were also reprioritised if their needs changed, and staff took steps to mitigate risks for people while they waited for assessments and equipment. Staff feedback indicated that while interpretation and translation options were available, these did not consistently meet people’s needs in all circumstances. Staff feedback indicated that while interpretation a"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[37,"Leaders identified there was a lack of services in Portsmouth to support people with complex mental health needs."],[39,"Care homes in Portsmouth did not support flexible respite and would only prebook respite for a fixed time of around 2 to 4 weeks. This meant the respite provision did not always meet people’s needs."],[40,"The current sitting service provision offered 6 hours per week in which a provider would provide support in the person’s own home, this did not include personal care or going out into the community."]],"m":"However, some information required updating, this meant people’s needs may have changed over recent years, and the JSNA did not fully reflect this and therefore could not accurately support any future commissioning plans. Leaders identified there was a lack of services in Portsmouth to support people with complex mental health needs. Care homes in Portsmouth did not support flexible respite and would only prebook respite for a fixed time of around 2 to 4 weeks. Care homes in Portsmouth did not s"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[53,"Senior leaders told us that they had identified that the adult social care recording system did not fully meet current service needs. This was reflected on the adult social care risk register due to the volume and retention of special category data within the existing system, which increased exposure to data security and information-sharing risks."],[54,"While another person told us they felt their family member was not provided with enough information or support when transitioning from children to adult services."],[55,"Another partner told us that there were no clear transition processes in place, and that people were not always provided with information about what to expect once their child turns 18. They said parents often had to seek out information for themselves, and it was hard to understand the different pathways for transition throughout services such as health and adult social care as the age for transition could vary."]],"m":"Senior leaders told us that they had identified that the adult social care recording system did not fully meet current service needs. Senior leaders told us that they had identified that the adult social care recording system did not fully meet current service needs. This was reflected on the adult social care risk register due to the volume and retention of special category data within the existing system, which increased exposure to data security and information-sharing risks. Partners told us"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Reading":[{"q":[[10,"The DASS commissioned an independent review of waiting times, safeguarding and workforce with results shared with the DASS in February 2024. This report noted high numbers of people waiting, however it was unclear if the report had a clear line of sight of the methodology and granular waiting time details."],[12,"Local authority data showed an improvement trajectory in completion of reviews, with an 18% reduction in overdue reviews between July 2024 and November 2024."],[12,"In mental health, overdue reviews had come down from 70% to 57% being overdue since February 2023 to December 2024."]],"m":"The local authority counted their waiting lists as numbers of people who were “known to need an intervention, until the intervention ended”, which includes people waiting assessments. This methodology has been unclear to understand. The DASS commissioned an independent review of waiting times, safeguarding and workforce with results shared with the DASS in February 2024. This report noted high numbers of people waiting, however it was unclear if the report had a clear line of sight of the method"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[32,"The local authority recognised its workforce did not reflect the diverse demographics of its population, however the strategy continued to commit to recruiting staff who not only reflect the local demographic need but also demonstrate the values the local authority were seeking, and it didn't reflect the wider workforce."],[33,"The local authority had supported a member of staff to undertake learning through a master's programme to investigate what the local authority can do to understand this issue better and improve the cultural suitability of mental health placements on offer. This gap in understanding impacts on these minority groups identified by the AMHP team and they hoped the work would improve their offer for minority ethnic groups."],[34,"People gave varying feedback regarding suitable communication methods with the local authority, for example formats they could read and a reliance for online access which some people did not have access to."]],"m":"The local authority recognised its workforce did not reflect the diverse demographics of its population, however the strategy continued to commit to recruiting staff who not only reflect the local demographic need but also demonstrate the values the local authority were seeking, and it didn't reflect the wider workforce. The local authority had supported a member of staff to undertake learning through a master's programme to investigate what the local authority can do to understand this issue be"},{"q":[[39,"We heard some people were provided with choices when planning their care, but we also heard from unpaid carers and partners that provision for unpaid carers and from community services was sometimes limited."],[39,"The high number of people accessing mental health provision, resulted in a limited number of people being moved out of area."],[42,"The local authority proactively worked with 16 framework providers, both inside Reading and Greater Reading, with the tender limited to organisations within a 45-minute drive from the local authority offices."]],"m":"We heard some people were provided with choices when planning their care, but we also heard from unpaid carers and partners that provision for unpaid carers and from community services was sometimes limited. The high number of people accessing mental health provision, resulted in a limited number of people being moved out of area. People told us respite availability to support Carers felt limited. The local authority proactively worked with 16 framework providers, both inside Reading and Greater"},{"q":[[44,"Staff told us it was challenging to understand how funding would be applied for people with needs which exceed that of adult social care, however they were aware leaders were supporting decision making at higher levels to ensure decisions were not delayed."],[45,"Health partners told us about the Berkshire West discharge improvement group who prioritised projects for discharge improvement to maximise outcomes for people, reducing unnecessary delays in hospital settings."],[47,"Local authority leaders told us the absence of a senior leader in the ICB contributed to the lack of clear focus on priorities, however the local authority had built collaborative and meaningful partnerships regarding the preventative approach and planning integrated services with wider partners, including the acute trust and Mental Health trust, police and environmental health."]],"m":"Staff told us it was challenging to understand how funding would be applied for people with needs which exceed that of adult social care, however they were aware leaders were supporting decision making at higher levels to ensure decisions were not delayed. Health partners told us about the Berkshire West discharge improvement group who prioritised projects for discharge improvement to maximise outcomes for people, reducing unnecessary delays in hospital settings. Local authority leaders told us "},{"q":[[51,"As part of a safeguarding improvement plan, implemented across 2024, the local authority redesigned the method of safeguarding referrals and alerts reaching the safeguarding team as they could not be assured there was sufficient knowledge and skills across the customer fulfilment centre."],[52,"Although all young people in transition were known about through the forum some young people in transition did not start to interact with adult social care until they were nearly 18, due to the capacity of the team to achieve the assessment targets."],[55,"We also saw consideration of contingency in Care Act assessments, however, contingency planning for unpaid carers was not always consistent."]],"m":"As part of a safeguarding improvement plan, implemented across 2024, the local authority redesigned the method of safeguarding referrals and alerts reaching the safeguarding team as they could not be assured there was sufficient knowledge and skills across the customer fulfilment centre. Although all young people in transition were known about through the forum some young people in transition did not start to interact with adult social care until they were nearly 18, due to the capacity of the t"},{"q":[[59,"The focus for the future was to hear more from the people receiving services, which the board felt was currently limited."],[61,"However, the delays in responding to section 42 enquiries resulted in people being exposed to risks."],[63,"Further knowledge was being sought around the management of safeguarding concerns and no further action (NFA) decisions on a monthly basis to ensure issues were not missed and principles were broadly applied across the local authorities in the partnership."]],"m":"A strategic safeguarding lead post (currently held by a locum) had led on the operational transformation which has been welcomed by all safeguarding partners. It was unclear at the time of the assessment of the sustainability of this layer of leadership; however, the local authority told us the post holder role had extended. The focus for the future was to hear more from the people receiving services, which the board felt was currently limited. Staff told us that there was an ample training offe"},{"q":[[65,"There was evidence of governance, management and accountability arrangements at all levels in the local authority. These provided visibility and assurance on quality, sustainability and risks to the local authority when carrying out its Care Act duties."],[65,"The Striving for Excellence programme brought together the adult social care improvement plan, staff and stakeholder engagement events, strategic transformation priorities and communications, visibility, and cultural alignment."],[69,"Evidence to advise staff when remedial actions were needed was noted if plans were not achieved in a timely manner."]],"m":"Evidence to advise staff when remedial actions were needed was noted if plans were not achieved in a timely manner."},{"q":[[70,"The local authority had a focus on staff growth and had measures in place to support quality improvement to ensure services continued to improve outcomes for people."],[70,"The outcomes from this review were sense checked against safeguarding audits completed under the Safeguarding Adults Board quality assurance framework."],[73,"We heard from staff that refresher training on EDI was mandatory, and teams were encouraged to undertake training that did not relate to their roles to increase knowledge and widen skillsets."]],"m":"We heard from staff that refresher training on EDI was mandatory, and teams were encouraged to undertake training that did not relate to their roles to increase knowledge and widen skillsets."}],"Redcar and Cleveland":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[17,"Despite these the local authority’s strategies such as the Adult Social Care Prevention Strategy did not include plans to engage with these groups to ensure any unmet needs for prevention or care and support were identified and considered in strategy and planning."],[18,"Staff also told us whilst there had been an increase in commissioned support for people with mental health needs, there were still gaps in provision including a lack of nursing care support for working aged people and specialist autism service provision. This showed there were missed opportunities to provide mental health prevention support, and these people were being disproportionately affected."],[23,"We found there was a lack of public information available for this group of people. This was an area of development for the local authority."]],"m":"However, they did not provide evidence to show this. Despite these the local authority’s strategies such as the Adult Social Care Prevention Strategy did not include plans to engage with these groups to ensure any unmet needs for prevention or care and support were identified and considered in strategy and planning. Staff also told us whilst there had been an increase in commissioned support for people with mental health needs, there were still gaps in provision including a lack of nursing care "},{"q":[[27,"The local authority had recently established an Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) working group; this was in early stages and there was not yet evidence of the impact of this group."],[27,"There was not yet a planned approach to finding and coproducing with these groups. This example indicated more work was needed to proactively identify the preventative, and care and support needs of some seldom heard communities within Redcar and Cleveland so their needs could be factored into strategic planning and operational delivery."],[28,"Staff had limited understanding of cultural diversity within the area and how they could carry out their Care Act 2014 duties in these communities."]],"m":"The local authority had limited understanding of its local population profile and demographics but recognised reducing inequality and improving outcomes was a continued area for development. Partners told us the local authority did consult with them regarding strategy and policy however, they did not provide adequate time for response. However, these were preliminary screening documents, and some did not provide indepth analysis. However, these were preliminary screening documents, and some did "},{"q":[[31,"The local authority had shown improvements in collecting data but were not yet using available data (for example, the JSNA data) to its full potential to understand the care and support needs of people and communities. This was an area for development."],[31,"The local authority’s Market Position Statement aligned with these ambitions as there were plans in place to move away from the traditional models of care, although these had not yet resulted in service changes."],[35,"Where processes and standards did not meet local authority standards, the Quality Assurance Team agreed an action plan with the care provider accompanied by follow up visits as required to ensure the action plan was completed and standards were maintained."]],"m":"The local authority had shown improvements in collecting data but were not yet using available data (for example, the JSNA data) to its full potential to understand the care and support needs of people and communities. The local authority had shown improvements in collecting data but were not yet using available data (for example, the JSNA data) to its full potential to understand the care and support needs of people and communities. This was an area for development. The local authority’s Market"},{"q":[[39,"The “Welcome Home” service supported people where funded social care support was not be needed, but where short-term 1-1 support to help older people to settle back in at home, and reintegrate into the community, helped prevent deterioration in their health and mental wellbeing."],[39,"Frontline staff told us however, they did not always receive accurate information about this route of discharge such as who had been referred to it and where, which led to people not always receiving timely communication from the local authority."],[41,"Senior leaders, staff and partners told us about the negative impact of the lack of partnership working with the CHC team."]],"m":"The “Welcome Home” service supported people where funded social care support was not be needed, but where short-term 1-1 support to help older people to settle back in at home, and reintegrate into the community, helped prevent deterioration in their health and mental wellbeing. Frontline staff told us however, they did not always receive accurate information about this route of discharge such as who had been referred to it and where, which led to people not always receiving timely communication"},{"q":[[43,"The views of people who drew on care and support services, partners and staff were listened to and considered, although formal methods of co-production were not yet embedded."],[45,"Staff told us the children’s team arranged ‘preparing for adulthood’ events which members of the adults Learning Disability Team attended. These events covered local education settings to raise awareness of the process and help identify any young people who were not already known to the local authority."],[45,"However, staff and people told us transitions led to some people being left without support as they were viewed as not having eligible needs under the Care Act 2014. This highlighted there were gaps in preventative support options available to young people."]],"m":"The views of people who drew on care and support services, partners and staff were listened to and considered, although formal methods of co-production were not yet embedded. Staff told us the children’s team arranged ‘preparing for adulthood’ events which members of the adults Learning Disability Team attended. These events covered local education settings to raise awareness of the process and help identify any young people who were not already known to the local authority. However, staff and p"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[56,"Political leaders had also implemented a task and finish group for unpaid carers in response to recognised gaps in services."],[57,"However, they did not identify themes from this work despite emerging patterns we found, such as those people who were transitioning from children to adult services experiencing a lack of support."],[58,"As data monitoring and forecasting were in their infancy at the time of the assessment, this had a direct impact upon strategic planning and service development. This meant strategies were broad and planning for future service development was not happening at a detailed enough level to be tailored to people’s needs as these were often not identified."]],"m":"Political leaders were knowledgeable and proactive, for example, they told us they requested the development of an Autism Strategy as this was not currently in place. Political leaders had also implemented a task and finish group for unpaid carers in response to recognised gaps in services. There were some areas of work which were designed to collate peoples’ lived experiences, but these were limited, and evidence showed inconsistency in people’s experience of adult social care. However, they di"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Rochdale":[{"q":[[10,"An external agency had been contracted to complete 480 overdue reviews and progress was monitored through regular meetings and transformation initiatives."],[14,"However, some unpaid carers reported not knowing where to find information, and quality assurance audits performed by the local authority found that 9 out of 26 carers could not identify advice or support available to them. This suggests that while systems and partnerships existed to provide help and advice, it was not consistently reaching people who may need it."],[16,"The process was streamlined following public consultation, and financial assessment backlogs were reduced from 650 cases to just 2, meaning people received quicker information on what they may need to pay."]],"m":"Others reported concerns, including poor assessment quality, lack of choice in care arrangements, and delays in funding. Staff said the front door approach reduced duplication, so people didn’t have to repeat their stories and shared systems improved coordination across partners. Another person said their initial assessment and a recent review were completed within three months, but there were delays in their financial and occupational therapy assessments. Another person said their initial asses"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Rotherham":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[30,"The local authority understood its local population profile and demographics and drew on a breadth of strategies and intelligence, though the impact of this work was not yet well established and required further embedding."],[31,"The JSNA provided granular data on sub-populations such as Black and Minority Ethnic and Roma communities, which enabled local partners to tailor planning and delivery of services to match specific demographic trends, but engagement with the Roma community was still in its early stages, required further development, and had not yet demonstrated sufficient impact."],[32,"The DASS told us about the way Adult Care is learning and changing its approach where officers had engaged with local communities and attended a community group for new mothers, whose first language was not English, and ensured advice and information was available in their preferred language, recognising that most communications are often in English then ensured future communications were offered in a variety of languages."]],"m":"The local authority understood its local population profile and demographics and drew on a breadth of strategies and intelligence, though the impact of this work was not yet well established and required further embedding. The JSNA provided granular data on sub-populations such as Black and Minority Ethnic and Roma communities, which enabled local partners to tailor planning and delivery of services to match specific demographic trends, but engagement with the Roma community was still in its ear"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Greenwich":[{"q":[[14,"Unpaid carers said they were unable to continue with activities and hobbies they enjoyed due to the limited availability and quality of respite services."],[17,"Independent advocacy support was not always timely in helping people to participate fully in Care Act 2014 assessments and care planning processes."],[17,"Data provided by the local authority showed whilst people were contacted within 5 days of referrals, their support needs were not resolved in a timely way. For example, in July-September 2025 4% of referrals for Independent Mental Capacity Assessment advocacy were resolved within 4 weeks against a target of 50% and 75% of these referrals were not resolved within 8 weeks."]],"m":"Assessment, review and care planning arrangements were not always timely and up to date. Unpaid carers said they were unable to continue with activities and hobbies they enjoyed due to the limited availability and quality of respite services. However, some partners told us funding for these services was limited and community organisations were often expected to find their own funding to enable ongoing support. However, decisions and outcomes were not always timely. Independent advocacy support w"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[31,"Partners told us frontline teams lacked the specialist knowledge to support people at risk from abuse who were from diverse ethnic backgrounds and LGBTQ+ groups were underrepresented in mental health support services."],[32,"A key takeaway from the report was the current service provision did not meet people’s culturally sensitive needs."],[35,"People’s experience was online translations services were poorly accessed by frontline teams assessing people’s needs in their home, and whilst there were different languages available on the website, information about adult social care was still not accessible enough to people whose first language was not English."]],"m":"Partners told us frontline teams lacked the specialist knowledge to support people at risk from abuse who were from diverse ethnic backgrounds and LGBTQ+ groups were underrepresented in mental health support services. Whilst it was recognised information regarding protected characteristics formed part of the contract monitoring arrangements, partners told us reporting templates were outdated and frontline teams did not always understand the needs of diverse communities. Partners also told us how"},{"q":[[37,"Notably, the number of residents aged 65 and over had risen by 15.6%. This led to an increased demand for services for age-related conditions such as limited mobility and mental health issues such as dementia which were contributing to the increased need for long term support from health and social care services."],[38,"The soon to be published Public Health annual report for 2025 would focus on health inequalities, but this was not available at the time of our assessment of the local authority."],[40,"The local authority’s Provider Market Workforce Position Statement identified a number of commissioning challenges, including diverse stakeholder priorities, shared understanding of what mattered to local communities, fragmented services, and limited opportunities for innovation."]],"m":"Notably, the number of residents aged 65 and over had risen by 15.6%. This led to an increased demand for services for age-related conditions such as limited mobility and mental health issues such as dementia which were contributing to the increased need for long term support from health and social care services. The soon to be published Public Health annual report for 2025 would focus on health inequalities, but this was not available at the time of our assessment of the local authority. People"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[87,"However, people told us coproduction was not always embedded throughout the local authority’s work."],[87,"However, whilst there was a clear priority from the local authority to coproduce strategic approaches and operational services, this was not always in place yet."],[90,"Concerns were raised about inconsistent application of equality, diversity and inclusion policies and an underrepresentation of minority groups within leadership roles."]],"m":"However, people told us coproduction was not always embedded throughout the local authority’s work. People told us the local authority did not listen to their voice until recently. However, whilst there was a clear priority from the local authority to coproduce strategic approaches and operational services, this was not always in place yet. However, people and partners told us it was not working effectively. People told us attendance was poor as agreed actions were not being completed by the loc"}],"Kensington and Chelsea":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Kingston upon Thames":[{"q":[[14,"In contrast, the learning disability team faced significant backlogs due to staffing shortages and recruitment challenges."],[14,"The local authority acknowledged that waiting times for Care Act reassessments and reviews were an area for improvement and were actively working to reduce delays and mitigate risks that impacted people’s wellbeing, during long waiting periods."],[15,"The median wait time for an assessment was 13 days, but in some cases, carers waited up to 157 days."]],"m":"The person did not feel rushed or pressured into making decisions. However, some raised concerns about forward planning, the transparency of eligibility decisions, and delays in reassessment. The local authority explained that individuals with stable care and support needs were not routinely allocated a social worker. In addition to these measures, the local authority had clear guidance for managing the waiting list, and senior staff carried out weekly reviews of overdue cases. In contrast, the "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[42,"Staff reported that the provision was improving, but there were still gaps in services for people with drug and alcohol addictions and those who were homeless."],[43,"Where care could not be sourced within the borough, the brokerage team explored placements within the Southwest London or Surrey areas."],[47,"There were no provider handbacks to the local authority between September 2023 – September (2024), but four of the providers left the market who were not commissioned by the council."]],"m":"On completing the fair cost of care exercise, the local authority acknowledged that it was not able to provide the median rate identified and had a discussed this with providers. Some providers reported that they had limited influence within the sessions and said that involvement in market-shaping activities was limited. Staff reported that the provision was improving, but there were still gaps in services for people with drug and alcohol addictions and those who were homeless. Partners reported"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Windsor and Maidenhead":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[28,"We heard examples from voluntary groups about how the local authority met people’s cultural needs as well as the needs of people where English was not their first language."],[29,"However, the local authority was aware of gaps, such as we heard there was a limited offer for people from the lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender or queer (LGBTQ) community."],[29,"We heard positive examples of staff working with people to overcome stigma, but also heard that there were sometimes limited options for people who identified as LGBTQ, including intersectional groups such as offers for older people or autistic people who identified as LGBTQ."]],"m":"We heard examples from voluntary groups about how the local authority met people’s cultural needs as well as the needs of people where English was not their first language. However, the local authority was aware of gaps, such as we heard there was a limited offer for people from the lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender or queer (LGBTQ) community. We heard positive examples of staff working with people to overcome stigma, but also heard that there were sometimes limited options for people who iden"},{"q":[[36,"The local authority’s adult social care strategy identified a need for a focused strategy for commissioning services for people with a learning disability and autistic people; work had started but the strategy was not expected to be published until the end of 2024."],[36,"Staff and leaders described recent difficulties finding the right provision for working-age adults with complex care needs, including dementia. The local authority had also identified this as a gap but had not yet implemented a strategy to address it."],[37,"The Market Positioning Statement (2024-27) identified further work to shape the local provider market, but the full impacts of this work had not yet been realised in areas such as direct payments and complex care for working age adults."]],"m":"In some areas there was tangible evidence of commissioning practices having a positive impact but in other areas strategies were either under development or had not yet been fully embedded. In other areas, the local authority recognised a need to improve but had not yet achieved their ambitions. The local authority’s adult social care strategy identified a need for a focused strategy for commissioning services for people with a learning disability and autistic people; work had started but the st"},{"q":[[46,"Working groups had led to the development of toolkits to support staff and partners but some of these had not yet had time to embed and improve outcomes for people receiving support."],[46,"Staff and leaders told us the joint ownership of this funding could lead to delays in approval but also described a positive relationship with health colleagues, where professional challenge was encouraged."],[46,"Providers told us there was good partnership working but did say that at times there could be delays to payment where people’s care was jointly funded, such as care provided jointly by health and social care under section 117 of the Mental Health Act 1983."]],"m":"Working groups had led to the development of toolkits to support staff and partners but some of these had not yet had time to embed and improve outcomes for people receiving support. Staff and leaders told us the joint ownership of this funding could lead to delays in approval but also described a positive relationship with health colleagues, where professional challenge was encouraged. Providers told us there was good partnership working but did say that at times there could be delays to paymen"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[54,"The analysis identified delays in processing referrals, inconsistencies in what was progressed as a safeguarding and extended time taken to complete enquiries."],[58,"Staff said waiting lists for safeguarding case allocation were lower than they had been previously but did say at times there could be delays in allocating cases to the community teams."],[59,"The availability of advocacy support could be limited, and staff described how it was prioritised for higher-risk cases. This meant there was a risk people may not have their voices heard in cases which were not deemed to be higher-risk because they did not have the same access to advocacy services."]],"m":"The analysis identified delays in processing referrals, inconsistencies in what was progressed as a safeguarding and extended time taken to complete enquiries. However, local authority data showed performance was not yet where they wanted it to be. Staff said waiting lists for safeguarding case allocation were lower than they had been previously but did say at times there could be delays in allocating cases to the community teams. The availability of advocacy support could be limited, and staff "},{"q":[[61,"However, at the time of this assessment many leaders were in interim roles and some of the actions to improve governance, such as implementing new IT systems, had not yet completed."],[61,"The local authority had identified gaps in overall governance which were planned to be addressed by bringing statutory functions in-house after six years of these being managed by a local authority trading company."],[64,"Whilst these roles had been involved in setting the interim strategy, they did not sit alongside directors at a senior level within the organisational structures."]],"m":"However, at the time of this assessment many leaders were in interim roles and some of the actions to improve governance, such as implementing new IT systems, had not yet completed. The local authority had identified gaps in overall governance which were planned to be addressed by bringing statutory functions in-house after six years of these being managed by a local authority trading company. We saw evidence of increased monitoring of quality and data, but some of the actions had not yet taken "},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Salford":[{"q":[[9,"Data provided by the local authority in November 2025 showed over the previous 12 months approximately 30% of contacts had been progressed to a Care Act assessment. It was unclear how many contacts resulted in advice and information being provided and how many were placed on a waiting list."],[10,"There was also no formal monitoring or internal assurance audits of the quality of contacts and responses to ensure people were consistently directed to the right team or service."],[20,"People were given help, advice and information about how to access services, facilities and other agencies for help with non-eligible care and support needs. However, this was not actively monitored or audited to ensure people were given consistent, quality advice and support."]],"m":"People did not have access to adult social care in community venues or face to face and there were no online self-assessment options at the time of our assessment. Processes to respond to incoming requests and information did not provide assurances that people’s needs were responded to in the most appropriate ways. Data provided by the local authority in November 2025 showed over the previous 12 months approximately 30% of contacts had been progressed to a Care Act assessment. It was unclear how"},{"q":[[25,"At the time of our assessment, despite senior leaders having awareness of the challenges being faced by people in the local area, we saw limited evidence that the changes made within adult social care, for example to the front door, had resulted in any positive impact for people and carers."],[25,"How peoples’ needs could be prevented, reduced or delayed was not considered at every interaction with a person. This was reflected in audits undertaken by an external commissioned partner of people’s care records in 2025 which found, in only 25% of cases community options had been considered ahead of formal care being explored."],[26,"While there were some services that supported the local authority’s prevention duty, there were shortfalls particularly in waiting times for occupational therapy assessments which significantly impacted people’s outcomes."]],"m":"During the assessment, CQC found these improvements had not yet been made. At the time of our assessment, despite senior leaders having awareness of the challenges being faced by people in the local area, we saw limited evidence that the changes made within adult social care, for example to the front door, had resulted in any positive impact for people and carers. The local authority's efforts to support those at risk did not sufficiently promote healthier lives or reduce future care needs. How "},{"q":[[35,"The local authority had limited regard to its Public Sector Equality Duty (Equality Act 2010) in the way it delivered its Care Act functions, and there was no local strategy within adult social care to reduce inequalities in people’s access to care and support."],[35,"The local authority did not provide a comprehensive action plan accompanying the strategy to explain how they planned to take action to address their identified priorities."],[35,"Staff within adult social care teams could name a small number of seldom heard communities, however, their knowledge of culturally competent practice was limited. For example, we did not hear about examples of working with LGBTQ+ people and those experiencing homelessness."]],"m":"The local authority had limited regard to its Public Sector Equality Duty (Equality Act 2010) in the way it delivered its Care Act functions, and there was no local strategy within adult social care to reduce inequalities in people’s access to care and support. The local authority did not provide a comprehensive action plan accompanying the strategy to explain how they planned to take action to address their identified priorities. The local authority lacked priority and focus to demonstrate what"},{"q":[[41,"We found there were significant gaps in provision, which were impacting negatively on people’s experiences. For example, staff told us the local authority had undertaken work to address provision for people with complex dementia needs, piloting a specialist rate that showed positive outcomes and was planned for wider rollout."],[41,"However, the scale of demand was unclear because the local authority data on complex dementia was largely anecdotal. This led to further exploration through brokerage and engagement with providers to understand barriers, such as funding, skills, or environmental factors, and to build a more accurate data picture for future commissioning."],[41,"There was no local prevalence data or an analysis of specific local needs included, and no action plan was included therefore it was unclear whether the strategy had met its aims."]],"m":"We found these shortfalls had led to overly restrictive care provision that was time and task based, rather than based on outcomes, and was being provided with a reliance on residential and nursing placements. Senior leaders recognised this, however, there had been limited market shaping activity at the time of our assessment. However, other strategies were missing and staff told us data availability and quality issues had directly impacted on the local authority’s ability to understand local ne"},{"q":[[49,"People told us they had inconsistent experiences when receiving support from integrated teams within adult social care."],[51,"We saw limited evidence the local authority monitored and evaluated the impact of its partnership working on the costs of social care and the outcomes for people."],[52,"Operational integration was evident through front-line multi-professional teams with access to both health and local authority systems and these arrangements were well embedded at a middle management level, however strategic intention and direction was unclear."]],"m":"People told us they had inconsistent experiences when receiving support from integrated teams within adult social care. However, staff told us some services such as the hospital discharge team faced barriers such as social workers being based off-site which led to delays in people being assessed as they had to travel into the hospital. A senior leader told us although there was no formal forum between the two areas, housing worked closely with social care colleagues. Senior leaders told us the i"},{"q":[[55,"The local authority understood where there were risks to people’s wellbeing across their care journeys, however, these risks were not consistently assessed across adult social care teams."],[57,"We also heard from partners there was a lack of coordination within mental health hospital discharges as there was no integrated discharge team. They told us about examples of issues with people discharged home without a discharge meeting or discharge planning."],[59,"We heard some people experienced delays in their care relating to partnership funding which impacted on their experiences and outcomes. For example, one provider told us people were often delayed from being discharged from hospital into a residential or nursing home due to delays in funding agreements."]],"m":"The local authority understood where there were risks to people’s wellbeing across their care journeys, however, these risks were not consistently assessed across adult social care teams. Standardised waiting well processes were not in place and there had been a rise in complexity and volume of work. Staff told us within some teams they risk rated referrals and contacted people whilst waiting, however, this was not a standardised process. Staff told us within some teams they risk rated referrals"},{"q":[[61,"Staff within the market management team also received information about quality concerns that could result in a safeguarding concern although there was no clear guidance in place as to what constituted a quality concern."],[62,"We heard from staff there was a lack of consistency and communication with other teams about how the approach was being rolled out."],[62,"Operational staff guidance was not yet embedded for safeguarding decision making, and a draft version was being piloted within the safeguarding team."]],"m":"Staff within the market management team also received information about quality concerns that could result in a safeguarding concern although there was no clear guidance in place as to what constituted a quality concern. We heard partners knew how to refer safeguarding concerns; however, they were not confident with using the new online portal and would often ask social workers to raise concerns on their behalf. We heard partners knew how to refer safeguarding concerns; however, they were not co"},{"q":[[72,"Additionally, it was not clear where risk had been considered to recruiting to registered roles. It was also not clear the delegated functions to health partners were enabled as part of the s75 agreement to consider the impact of these decisions on people’s experiences and outcomes or within the context of the local authority’s legal obligations under the Care Act or Mental Capacity Act."],[76,"The absence of waiting well policies, risk rating of those who were waiting and poor data quality, culminated in an environment where it was difficult for senior leaders to have overall oversight of risks to people requiring adult social care support and meant it was difficult to plan and resource effectively. Senior leaders told us they were not assured of risk within data or safeguarding."],[74,"Reports were submitted but not discussed or acted upon and the data which reports relied on was inaccurate. This was reflective of our findings as we did not see the remaining resolutions had been actioned and reasons given were the s75 agreement was being terminated in March 2026."]],"m":"We found people at risk of harm and significant shortfalls leading to risk of poorer outcomes and reduced quality of life for people and unpaid carers in the local authority. Staff had been negatively affected by senior leadership instability and their lack of visibility. Some staff told us it had been difficult to access senior leaders, and they hadn’t felt listened to by leadership. Senior leaders told us they had held regular updates to staff as information became available but given the anno"},{"q":[[80,"We found that where the local authority had delegated Care Act duties to the mental health partner, training around the Care Act was ongoing and had only been in place for 6 months. This was despite staff who were not social workers, for example nurses, carrying out Care Act assessments and was concerning as the delegated arrangements had been in place since 2016."],[81,"However, it was too soon to say what the impact of this work was, as the carers’ strategy had not yet been finalised at the time of our assessment."],[82,"The document was produced in 2022 and was therefore of limited value as population data and need used to inform workforce planning was now several years out of date. It described career pathways in social work, however made no mention of other professionals, such as occupational therapists or registered nurses."]],"m":"We found there was a clear lack of resource and pace needed to prioritise shortfalls around prevention, early intervention, strength based approaches and equity in people’s experiences. The absence of a clear, embedded practice framework and a limited quality assurance system reduced opportunities for learning and continuous improvement within the local authority. Some staff told us there was limited access to training and what was available wasn’t always specific to the needs of teams, only gen"}],"Sandwell":[{"q":[[12,"Further feedback from the local authority was the introduction of trusted triage was designed to reduce unnecessary referrals to therapy teams and avoid delays in meeting people’s needs."],[14,"Although timeliness had improved significantly over the last 2 years, there could still be a wait for an assessment and these delays were not specific to certain teams."],[14,"Also, people in short term placements did not always receive a full Care Act assessment before the end of the placement which hindered post placement planning."]],"m":"However, some people had less positive experiences, for example, having to tell their story again with each change of social worker, another person struggled to get a call back from the local authority and someone else felt their assessment was completed with a lack of empathy. Further feedback from the local authority was the introduction of trusted triage was designed to reduce unnecessary referrals to therapy teams and avoid delays in meeting people’s needs. Partners told us some people did n"},{"q":[[28,"The demand for these services had increased from 2024 and response times from contact assessments to STAR assessments were quick with a median response of the same day which meant people continued to receive support and risks were reduced while they waited for a full Care Act assessment."],[30,"Challenges in recruitment of occupational therapists and waiting lists had created a back log in terms of Disabled Facilities Grant funding awards and delays for contractors, however, recent extra capacity had resulted in cases coming through more quickly than the previous year."],[31,"People using services fed back there had been inconsistent information provided to them at times, for example, in one case about dementia care, which had left people feeling unclear about their options."]],"m":"Some partners felt more services could be offered by the local authority to work in more of a preventative way and that they did not always prioritise prevention, being more reactive to crisis. The demand for these services had increased from 2024 and response times from contact assessments to STAR assessments were quick with a median response of the same day which meant people continued to receive support and risks were reduced while they waited for a full Care Act assessment. Challenges in rec"},{"q":[[36,"Staff understood which ethnic groups accessed care in Sandwell and which did not, for example, Black people showed the highest relative usage of adult social care long term services with Asian people being below the level expected."],[38,"In one study undertaken, the local authority identified lack of communication between ethnic minority groups, service commissioners and providers and that understandings, expectations and provision did not align. This was reflected in some feedback we received."],[38,"The Better Care Fund was being repurposed for this work, however truly accessible services for all were not yet in place, but there were firm plans to work towards this."]],"m":"Staff understood which ethnic groups accessed care in Sandwell and which did not, for example, Black people showed the highest relative usage of adult social care long term services with Asian people being below the level expected. In one example, a member of care staff attending dementia training fed back positively as dementia was not something that was recognised within their culture. In one study undertaken, the local authority identified lack of communication between ethnic minority groups,"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Sefton":[{"q":[[13,"A team was in place to contact people who had waited longer than 28 days for assessment."],[13,"However, at the time of our assessment these teams were not yet fully staffed."],[14,"Data provided by the local authority (January 2025), showed 1306 people were overdue an annual review, with a median wait time of 171 days and a maximum wait time of 1781."]],"m":"People told us sometimes it was difficult to get through or to speak to the right person when using the contact centre telephone number. We saw in assessment notes, consideration of mental capacity to make decisions was not always discussed at assessment and could rely on a family member or unpaid carer acting as advocate. Care Act assessments and annual reviews were not timely. A team was in place to contact people who had waited longer than 28 days for assessment. However, at the time of our a"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[54,"The local authority had strengthened oversight and governance in operational and professional practice and senior leadership capacity, with an Associate Director of Quality and Safeguarding within Sefton Place ICB working alongside a Senior Manager for Assurance and Safeguarding Adults within the local authority to develop strong and safe systems."],[55,"A voluntary sector partner told us they worked with adult social care on discharge pathways, they told us people and unpaid carers did not always know where to go after a person has been discharged."],[56,"We heard mixed views about how people experienced transition, particularly around timeliness in preparation, we heard planning could wait until people reached 17 years or who had been asked to contact adult social care at the end of their education."]],"m":"A voluntary sector partner told us they worked with adult social care on discharge pathways, they told us people and unpaid carers did not always know where to go after a person has been discharged."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Sheffield":[{"q":[[10,"While the local authority did provide in-person support through clinics, walk ins and outreach, feedback suggested these options were not always well-known or easy to navigate for everyone."],[11,"While many Care Act assessments were completed within the local authority’s expected timescales, some people told us they experienced delays in receiving a Care Act assessment."],[22,"When a person could not fully take part in conversation about their needs for care and support and they had no one to help them, the local authority had access to a commissioned advocacy service to support the person’s involvement in their assessment and plans."]],"m":"Partners representing people with care and support needs told us some people felt arrangements lacked a person-centred approach, and accessibility was a concern. While the local authority did provide in-person support through clinics, walk ins and outreach, feedback suggested these options were not always well-known or easy to navigate for everyone. There was a three-step model to promote strength based approaches in assessment however whilst staff acknowledged strengths were considered, we foun"},{"q":[[31,"However, delays in specialist services and fragmented therapy offers presented barriers."],[32,"People were not limited to telephone screening or clinic appointments; people could still wait for a home visit depending on preference and level of need. For example, people living with dementia might not be asked to come to a clinic if the unfamiliar environment would not be supportive to their assessment."],[34,"The local authority’s backlog in Disabled Facilities Grant (DFG) applications for major adaptations was an unintended consequence of reducing assessment waits at pace with a reported 120% increase. In response, senior leaders introduced improvements to the framework for major adaptations, including increasing the number of contractors to address the backlog."]],"m":"In contrast, unpaid carers told us they faced barriers to accessing support, including delays and lack of awareness of arrangements to prevent, delay or reduce needs for care and support. Despite these positive developments, some staff and senior leaders told us that early joint planning between housing and adult social care was not always consistent, and people were often identified at crisis point. Informal routes into voluntary and paid work were common, but structured supported employment pa"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[48,"Staff highlighted gaps in service commissioning, noting that residential care options were often too restrictive, while supported living did not always provide sufficient help leaving some people without appropriate support."],[50,"There were limited adapted properties beyond those recorded through Disabled Facilities Grants, and temporary accommodation was largely reliant on hotels."],[54,"Between June and August 2025, data provided by senior leaders showed that people waited a median of 3 days from referral to start date for home care, 1 day for residential care, 6 days for nursing care, and 34 days for supported living."]],"m":"Staff highlighted gaps in service commissioning, noting that residential care options were often too restrictive, while supported living did not always provide sufficient help leaving some people without appropriate support. Staff also raised concerns about delays in system navigation, with some people remaining without suitable provision for extended periods. Despite strategic intent, access to short breaks remained inconsistent, with carers and staff reporting stress and delays in securing res"},{"q":[[63,"Progress in developing integrated approaches within mental health services had been slower than in other areas, particularly in reducing delays in hospital discharge."],[64,"However, there was limited evidence of formal evaluation to understand the impact of these partnerships on social care outcomes."],[65,"Despite successes, there were areas where the impact of partnership working was less clear or inconsistent. For example, people waiting to be discharged from mental health hospital were not progressing at the same pace despite joined up meetings in place."]],"m":"Carers valued the commissioned carers service, but some reported that the support they received was not always consistent or joined up. Carers valued the commissioned carers service, but some reported that the support they received was not always consistent or joined up. This highlighted the need for the local authority to strengthen its coordination of care and support to ensure carers and people with care and support needs receive timely, effective, and person-centred services. Progress in dev"},{"q":[[70,"Most service areas had a duty function to respond to immediate risks identified for people not yet allocated to a named worker or when a named worker was not available."],[71,"However, staff told this was not consistently provided. For example, one staff group described several critical safety issues affecting both staff and people using services, which they attributed to limitations in information sharing and system access."],[74,"Contingency planning, also referred to as emergency planning, was used to outline the support required if an unpaid carer, commissioned service, or the local authority became unable to provide care."]],"m":"However, another person felt deterioration of their needs could have been prevented through appropriate risk support which suggested a gap in preventative and responsive care planning. Most service areas had a duty function to respond to immediate risks identified for people not yet allocated to a named worker or when a named worker was not available. However, some partners who represented people told us they were not informed of partnership agreements to support hospital discharges and recommen"},{"q":[[77,"Staff outside the MASH did not always have the same access to partner agencies, and there was inconsistency in how expertise within the MASH was used for people already known to services."],[78,"However, staff identified areas for improvement, particularly in delivering training to providers where practice concerns had been raised. These included inappropriate use of restrictive measures and decisions made under the Mental Capacity Act without clear competence, which risked inconsistent protection for adults at risk."],[86,"Processes did not consistently support staff to inform relevant agencies of the outcomes of safeguarding enquiries, even when this information was important for the ongoing safety of the adult at risk or for preventing harm to others. Staff told us while they could contact agencies by email or informally, this relied on staff remembering to do it manually rather than being embedded within formal procedures or checks."]],"m":"However, partners representing people told us there were inconsistent processes. Care providers were not represented at SAB but had local authority representation within operational processes. Staff outside the MASH did not always have the same access to partner agencies, and there was inconsistency in how expertise within the MASH was used for people already known to services. Staff told us the MASH approach supported timely action planning and improved communication with professionals, even wh"},{"q":[[93,"Oversight of some waiting lists remained inconsistent, which affected the level of assurance around outcomes for individuals."],[93,"The CQC found there could be blockages of communication, and not all frontline staff were clear about how the local authority was working towards reducing risks where there were shortfalls in carrying out Care Act duties."],[96,"Staff and partners told us there were challenges related to insufficient workforce capacity and the journey between services was sometimes fragmented, affecting continuity of care. These issues highlighted strategic gaps in risk management and workforce planning, which affected the local authority’s ability to deliver timely care and support."]],"m":"Oversight of some waiting lists remained inconsistent, which affected the level of assurance around outcomes for individuals. While scrutiny was embedded, feedback loops were not always effective in demonstrating how outcomes for people and unpaid carers had improved as a result. However, in terms of engagement with frontline staff there was more to be done to connect with staff that did not volunteer to be involved in project work. The CQC found there could be blockages of communication, and no"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Shropshire":[{"q":[[10,"There was not an option for people to complete a Care Act self-assessment online, however the local authority was exploring this as part of their digital transformation work."],[12,"The local authority provided information about the pre-service agreement they had with providers which set out responsibilities and action, if individual needs were not being met by the level of support indicated in the support plan."],[13,"The local authority’s own data (submitted in March 2024) showed 810 reviews were overdue and of these, 79% were overdue by less than 6 months."]],"m":"There was not an option for people to complete a Care Act self-assessment online, however the local authority was exploring this as part of their digital transformation work. One person told us their care plan did not fully take account of their needs, and another person told us that the process of arranging suitable care provision for them had taken a long time and had been disjointed. The social workers had said they were unaware of the existence of the other relative until this was flagged by"},{"q":[[22,"We were not informed of how people accessed the service nor any outcomes of the impact of this for them."],[24,"Staff also told us there was a system in place to monitor how long people were waiting on the telephone and there was no concern about long waiting times."],[25,"The local authority website did not provide accessible information for people who communicated in non-English languages beyond the landing page of the website."]],"m":"We were not informed of how people accessed the service nor any outcomes of the impact of this for them. Staff also told us there was a system in place to monitor how long people were waiting on the telephone and there was no concern about long waiting times. The local authority website did not provide accessible information for people who communicated in non-English languages beyond the landing page of the website."},{"q":[[29,"There were a number of outreach programmes for example, the Ethnic Minority Outreach Programme was used to improve understanding about the barriers to health and well-being support and gaps in services."],[30,"A voluntary partner said whilst the local authority was engaged in raising awareness of LGBTQ+ issues, they had not always taken the opportunity to embed LGBTQ+ and older people’s issues into strategic partnership boards."],[31,"There were also outreach teams who could provide support to access services in face-to-face settings for people who could not go online."]],"m":"There were a number of outreach programmes for example, the Ethnic Minority Outreach Programme was used to improve understanding about the barriers to health and well-being support and gaps in services. A voluntary partner said whilst the local authority was engaged in raising awareness of LGBTQ+ issues, they had not always taken the opportunity to embed LGBTQ+ and older people’s issues into strategic partnership boards. Senior leaders told us that whilst they could use data to identify any the "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[52,"A partner organisation told us they felt the local authority system was not joined up in terms of planning for a young person's future at the point of transition into adult services, or if they had a learning disability. They told us they were able to raise their concerns with the local authority through involvement in strategic forums and the Making it Real Board."],[54,"Care providers told us on one occasion due to flooding, a paid carer was unable to get to the person receiving care and the local authority assisted with this and ensured the person receiving care was supported."],[54,"However, people told us this was not always completed and person-centred information about what should happen in an emergency was sometimes missing."]],"m":"A partner organisation told us they felt the local authority system was not joined up in terms of planning for a young person's future at the point of transition into adult services, or if they had a learning disability. A partner organisation told us they felt the local authority system was not joined up in terms of planning for a young person's future at the point of transition into adult services, or if they had a learning disability. They told us they were able to raise their concerns with t"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Slough":[{"q":[[12,"However, there were some people who had waited significantly longer (up to 119 days) for various reasons."],[12,"The median wait time was 155 days which was well over the 8-week target. This could mean some people were not receiving care and support most appropriate to their needs."],[14,"For 1 carer, the Care Act assessment documentation confirmed that a carers assessment had been explained and offered, however, when we spoke with the carer, they were not aware of carers assessments and the support available."]],"m":"A partner told us it was good that people could still contact the local authority by telephone and speak to someone. They said they didn’t wait long for calls to be answered and were always directed to the right department. However, there were some people who had waited significantly longer (up to 119 days) for various reasons. The median wait time was 155 days which was well over the 8-week target. This could mean some people were not receiving care and support most appropriate to their needs. "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[40,"Staff looked at market shaping, performance, service delivery and any gaps in the community."],[42,"However, there had been consideration about reopening some centres to bridge the gaps in service provision."],[42,"Data provided by the local authority in March 2025 showed there was no wait for supported accommodation due to the lack of capacity."]],"m":"Staff told us there was a lack of supported housing and plans to redevelop accommodation for adults with a learning disability had been halted and were now being developed into general needs accommodation. Staff looked at market shaping, performance, service delivery and any gaps in the community. There was not always sufficient care and support available to meet demand and people could not always access it when, where and how they needed it. Staff told us there were shortfalls in many aspects o"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[63,"Care and support were not always planned and organised with people, together with partners and communities in ways which improved their safety across their care journeys and ensured continuity in care. This included referrals, admissions and discharge, and where people were moving between services."],[64,"Problems had arisen in the past when the Special Educational Needs (SEN) team ended ECHPs, and people did not understand their rights under the Care Act 2014. This had led to people not understanding transitions to adult social care, and their child (transitioning to adult social care) did not have an education placement through an ECHP."],[64,"If the person did not meet their eligibility of having a diagnosed learning disability, the referral would be triaged to the locality social work teams."]],"m":"People were informed of who to contact should their situation change, while they waited. The East Berkshire Continuing Health Care Verification and Dispute policy described in detail the respective responsibilities of the Integrated Care System (ICS) and the local authority whilst awaiting the outcome of a dispute. This was designed to ensure no-one was left without appropriate support if statutory bodies were unable to agree on respective responsibilities. Care and support were not always plann"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[79,"Other partners highlighted a lack of permanent social work staff, claiming nearly all senior staff in the local authority were in an interim post which was affecting services as strategies were not getting completed because of staff turnover."],[80,"Slough Borough Council did not have a dedicated scrutiny panel for adult social care."],[83,"The Slough Adult Carers Strategy 2023-26 was developed with input from carers and partners, identifying key challenges such as the negative impact of caring on physical and mental health, financial hardship, inconsistent recognition of carers by healthcare services, the growing need for respite support, and the importance of peer support for isolated carers."]],"m":"Many staff had worked at the local authority for several years; turnover was not a major concern, as leaders said their teams were now stable. Other partners highlighted a lack of permanent social work staff, claiming nearly all senior staff in the local authority were in an interim post which was affecting services as strategies were not getting completed because of staff turnover. Slough Borough Council did not have a dedicated scrutiny panel for adult social care. The Slough Adult Carers Stra"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Solihull":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Somerset":[{"q":[[9,"The local authority did not have data on how people were signposted, but staff were confident that they knew what services were available to signpost people correctly. This was in part due to their relationships with the local community and village agents, whose role was as information and advice workers embedded in local communities."],[10,"In the sampled cases we reviewed, we found Care Act assessments written with medical language and a deficit focus, which did not promote a strength-based view or support the individual’s voice to be present in the assessment."],[16,"When contacting the local authority, people were given advice, information and signposting to other services and facilities in the area for help with care and support needs that were not eligible under the Care Act."]],"m":"The local authority did not have data on how people were signposted, but staff were confident that they knew what services were available to signpost people correctly. The local authority did not have data on how people were signposted, but staff were confident that they knew what services were available to signpost people correctly. This was in part due to their relationships with the local community and village agents, whose role was as information and advice workers embedded in local communit"},{"q":[[20,"In some areas of this there were delays and restrictions on services due to lack of capacity which limited the opportunities to promote independence."],[22,"To develop this further, the local authority and commissioned partners were building dashboards to provide more insight into preventative work and outcomes but at time of our assessment expected outcomes were not modelled as part of a programme of strategic oversight."],[23,"Somerset’s reablement services and intermediate care beds were provided as part of the discharge to assess model and were not generally available for community referrals."]],"m":"In some areas of this there were delays and restrictions on services due to lack of capacity which limited the opportunities to promote independence. To develop this further, the local authority and commissioned partners were building dashboards to provide more insight into preventative work and outcomes but at time of our assessment expected outcomes were not modelled as part of a programme of strategic oversight. Somerset’s reablement services and intermediate care beds were provided as part o"},{"q":[[31,"At the time of the assessment, the local authority was looking at expanding resources, so the service was not reliant on one member of staff."],[32,"There was evidence that some work was underway, and groups like farmers were identified as having poor mental health, but this was not quantified or linked to a strategy. This meant work that went on, like engagements at farmers’ markets, was unable to show impact."],[33,"Staff referred to farming communities, the Gypsy Roma and Traveller community and faith communities but only gave limited examples of how they worked to ensure they specifically targeted their needs."]],"m":"There was also an acknowledgement among lead managers of the need to do more to understand which communities were being served by social care and any gaps as this was not currently analysed. At the time of the assessment, the local authority was looking at expanding resources, so the service was not reliant on one member of staff. There was a lack of an equality lens on adult social care strategic planning, which meant the local authority did not understand who was more likely to experience ineq"},{"q":[[38,"There was also some evidence of engagement of people with lived experience in the Working Together board, but at the time of the assessment, the membership was not diverse enough to be representative."],[41,"However, we heard from both staff and partners that there was a lack of appropriate supported living for people and a lack of housing support for people with addiction or stepping down from services, which sometimes resulted in people being placed in unsuitable accommodation."],[43,"When these standards were not met, there were measures in place to suspend services until improvements had been made."]],"m":"Some partners informed us that the local authority had engaged them to understand the market, but others reported that they had limited input and influence. There was also some evidence of engagement of people with lived experience in the Working Together board, but at the time of the assessment, the membership was not diverse enough to be representative. People told us that the hourly rate given within the direct payment was not enough to fund some micro provider in Somerset and in some cases p"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[56,"Performance against the two-day discharge target had been inconsistent since July 2025, though incremental improvement was noted."],[57,"The local authority had oversight of people who were placed out of area through a dashboard that highlighted the overdue reviews for people and where they were placed."],[57,"In an assurance report to the Safeguarding Adults Board in July 2024, the local authority had 106 out-of-area reviews that were overdue, and 45 of these were for people with learning disabilities."]],"m":"Performance against the two-day discharge target had been inconsistent since July 2025, though incremental improvement was noted. The local authority had oversight of people who were placed out of area through a dashboard that highlighted the overdue reviews for people and where they were placed. In an assurance report to the Safeguarding Adults Board in July 2024, the local authority had 106 out-of-area reviews that were overdue, and 45 of these were for people with learning disabilities. The l"},{"q":[[61,"Staff in the contact team were not social care professionals but had received training which included the SSAB risk decision making tool. They also had access to the safeguarding team through a team's chat for advice and support as needed to ensure safe and consistent decision were about how to deal with safeguarding concerns were raised with them."],[61,"However, contact centre workers we spoke with were not aware of a standardised method of recording rationale and decision making, or whether a referral should be sent to the safeguarding team."],[62,"Overall, the Section 42(1) process was inconsistent, which meant the local authority would struggle to be assured about the quality of Section 42(1) decisions."]],"m":"Staff in the contact team were not social care professionals but had received training which included the SSAB risk decision making tool. Staff in the contact team were not social care professionals but had received training which included the SSAB risk decision making tool. They also had access to the safeguarding team through a team's chat for advice and support as needed to ensure safe and consistent decision were about how to deal with safeguarding concerns were raised with them. However, co"},{"q":[[68,"There were some legacy issues within governance from the changes; for example, some of the corporate equality actions were not completed due to structural changes and resultant service demands."],[69,"There was a lack of a long-term manager in the safeguarding team, which was filled as an interim at the time of assessment to support stability in the team."],[72,"Some commitments in the strategy were not included in the midterm review, and it was unclear what progress had been made."]],"m":"There were some legacy issues within governance from the changes; for example, some of the corporate equality actions were not completed due to structural changes and resultant service demands. There was a lack of a long-term manager in the safeguarding team, which was filled as an interim at the time of assessment to support stability in the team. While both strategies had clear and well-articulated aims, delivery plans were not consistently presented as fully resourced or clearly owned across "},{"q":[[74,"However, it was not clear which actions of the strategy were yet to be completed."],[77,"We saw examples of engagement and co-design activity, but this was not fully embedded throughout all the local authority’s work."],[78,"Staff told us they were learning from Safeguarding Adult Reviews (SARs) and there had been a focus recently on self-neglect, which evidenced some learning was completed, but there was a lack of information about more recent SAR’s and how lessons were changing practice."]],"m":"However, it was not clear which actions of the strategy were yet to be completed. We saw examples of engagement and co-design activity, but this was not fully embedded throughout all the local authority’s work. The evidence presented looked like work had been done on the website, but this did not evidence how specific concerns were resolved, with people reporting the website was not always clear. Staff told us they were learning from Safeguarding Adult Reviews (SARs) and there had been a focus r"}],"South Gloucestershire":[{"q":[[9,"There was out of hours provision from 5pm (4.30pm on Fridays) until 8.30am who responded to urgent needs and worked closely with daytime teams to ensure that people were not disadvantaged if their needs arose out of office hours."],[11,"If people were not known to the local authority, they could receive their assessment through the commissioned carers support service."],[12,"In general, unpaid carers waited longer for a Care Act assessment than people who were being cared for."]],"m":"There was out of hours provision from 5pm (4.30pm on Fridays) until 8.30am who responded to urgent needs and worked closely with daytime teams to ensure that people were not disadvantaged if their needs arose out of office hours. Staff also told us that 1534 people were awaiting review, most of which were under 12 months old and nearly half were less than 3 months overdue. If people were not known to the local authority, they could receive their assessment through the commissioned carers support"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[30,"People we spoke with however said their experience of accessing information and guidance was mixed and it was not always easy to find on the local authority’s website."],[31,"A partner organisation told us that the lack of offices where people could see local authority staff made it very difficult for lots of people to get the timely support they needed because using the telephone or internet were barriers to their communication."],[31,"Another organisation said they felt the local authority do not always consider accessibility needs of individuals when looking at forums. They said they could increase the number of people getting to a venue if they considered using more satellite venues and if they would stay in those places over time, they might get more feedback rather than through a form or link, which they reported was a barrier to some disabled people."]],"m":"People we spoke with however said their experience of accessing information and guidance was mixed and it was not always easy to find on the local authority’s website. A partner organisation told us that the lack of offices where people could see local authority staff made it very difficult for lots of people to get the timely support they needed because using the telephone or internet were barriers to their communication. Another organisation said they felt the local authority do not always con"},{"q":[[35,"Provider feedback to a local authority survey in late 2023/early 2024 highlighted a possible gap in some providers' understanding of the population’s diverse needs and how they could meet these, although other providers were able to share multiple examples of ways in which they were able to provide personalised care that considered individuals' specific cultural or other needs."],[39,"In April 2024, 15 older people waited a mean average of 17 days for homecare, although most waited less, and a small number waited more."],[39,"People of working age with specialist needs waited longer, and in April 2024 13 people were waiting an average of 65 days."]],"m":"Leaders said they were developing a dementia strategy, but this was not yet published. Leaders said they were developing a dementia strategy, but this was not yet published. They recognised they needed to increase local housing and care options for all groups in the community. Provider feedback to a local authority survey in late 2023/early 2024 highlighted a possible gap in some providers' understanding of the population’s diverse needs and how they could meet these, although other providers we"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[56,"Where formal social care support was not needed there was a Welcome Home Service provided by the local voluntary and community sector."],[58,"Staff however reported challenges in supporting young people who were no longer under the Child and Adolescent Mental Health Service but were not eligible for the adult mental health Recovery team."],[60,"Other staff told us they had good working relationships with adult social team colleagues, but said the processes that health and social care used were not always aligned."]],"m":"Staff however said that colleagues in different parts of the system did not always have equal access to the same patient information system, which could impact on the effectiveness of planning for complex discharges. Where formal social care support was not needed there was a Welcome Home Service provided by the local voluntary and community sector. Pathways where a person was not going into long term care, also considered whether there was an unpaid carer who needed support, and consideration o"},{"q":[[66,"Data also showed that 12 (1%) concerns received took over 30 days to be responded to, but this did not mean they had not been reviewed."],[68,"Staff told us reports regarding DoLS data were under development, and they were unable to report median waiting times."],[70,"One person told us that they had raised a complaint about a commissioned service, but did not feel they were listened to or that changes had been made as a result."]],"m":"Data also showed that 12 (1%) concerns received took over 30 days to be responded to, but this did not mean they had not been reviewed. Staff told us reports regarding DoLS data were under development, and they were unable to report median waiting times. One person told us that they had raised a complaint about a commissioned service, but did not feel they were listened to or that changes had been made as a result."},{"q":[[72,"The local authority had governance, management and accountability arrangements at all levels of the organisation, but these were not always effective in providing visibility and assurance on the delivery of Care Act duties, sustainability and potential risks to delivery."],[72,"There were many activities undertaken in relation to supporting people to live healthier lives, but many of these were not currently being monitored to understand their effectiveness, especially in terms of outcomes for people. It was not clear for example what outcomes the Village Agents scheme was being monitored against, even though this flagship project was mentioned by many staff as a key initiative."],[73,"The data that the local authority currently had available did not always measure helpful metrics such as median waiting times for Deprivation of Liberty Safeguards applications, or the time taken to complete a safeguarding s42 enquiry investigation."]],"m":"The local authority told us they knew there were gaps in processes, policies and guidance on some of their specific transitions and these had been addressed in their draft development/improvement plan. The local authority had governance, management and accountability arrangements at all levels of the organisation, but these were not always effective in providing visibility and assurance on the delivery of Care Act duties, sustainability and potential risks to delivery. Leaders could not always e"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"South Tyneside":[{"q":[[14,"Some staff expressed concerns around long-standing delays in people accessing Care Act (2014) assessments, reviews and financial assessments."],[15,"Leaders explained that they had changed how they reported their overdue annual reviews. This separated reviews into different categories according to the primary legislation under which they were required. This included Care Act 2014, Continuing Healthcare, S117 aftercare and Deprivation of Liberty Safeguards (DoLS) and provided line of sight on the numbers requiring review and waiting times for each category."],[15,"In April 2024, the local authority reported having 1781 overdue annual reviews."]],"m":"Some staff expressed concerns around long-standing delays in people accessing Care Act (2014) assessments, reviews and financial assessments. Some teams felt that only having the opportunity to be ‘reactive’ in their workload led to a lack of focus on being ‘preventative’ in their work. Leaders explained that they had changed how they reported their overdue annual reviews. Leaders explained that they had changed how they reported their overdue annual reviews. This separated reviews into differen"},{"q":[[23,"There was not a time limit on the LAC intervention, and staff provided case examples of increased wellbeing and confidence in the community which had led to less need for health and social care services."],[23,"Feedback from unpaid carers highlighted gaps in consistency, accountability, and carer support."],[23,"We were told by some carers that they faced fragmented processes, lack of assessments, and minimal engagement in care planning."]],"m":"There was not a time limit on the LAC intervention, and staff provided case examples of increased wellbeing and confidence in the community which had led to less need for health and social care services. Feedback from unpaid carers highlighted gaps in consistency, accountability, and carer support. We were told by some carers that they faced fragmented processes, lack of assessments, and minimal engagement in care planning. Unpaid carers told us that they didn’t feel that future responsibilities"},{"q":[[31,"The JSNAA noted the diversity of people requiring support had increased and that people from the LGBTQ+ community in later life reported poorer health than the general population and had worse experiences of care. It did not include information related to some groups including ethnicity and LGBTQ+, (which was available within the Local Authority’s Data Observatory) focusing more on breakdown of population by gender."],[32,"The strategy highlighted that 5.6% of residents were from ethnic minority communities and 2.34% of households did not have English as a first language."],[34,"Staff involved in carrying out Care Act (2014) duties were not consistently able to demonstrate a good understanding of the range of cultural diversity within the borough, despite the range of languages and communities identified in the EDIB strategy."]],"m":"The JSNAA noted the diversity of people requiring support had increased and that people from the LGBTQ+ community in later life reported poorer health than the general population and had worse experiences of care. It did not include information related to some groups including ethnicity and LGBTQ+, (which was available within the Local Authority’s Data Observatory) focusing more on breakdown of population by gender. The strategy highlighted that 5.6% of residents were from ethnic minority commun"},{"q":[[39,"Despite the local authority’s commissioned pathways already in place, such as Preparing for Adulthood, leaving care arrangements, hospital discharge, and sensory impairment services, partners identified gaps in mental health services, sensory impairment and support for children moving into adult services."],[40,"To support communication for people whose first language was not English, the council signposted to a Newcastle-upon-Tyne Interpreting Service for professionals to arrange interpreting for residents, who provided on-demand and pre-booked interpreting service and operated 24-hour hours a day."],[40,"The local authority commissioning approaches and market position statement supported the aims of the ‘Living Better Lives’ strategy to develop the market to increase people’s independence, support people in their communities and fill the gaps in provision. This included planning for the provision for older people with dementia where they expect future demand to grow."]],"m":"The local authority’s self-assessment told us that their Joint Strategic Needs and Asset assessment (JSNAA) identified gaps in provision for specific groups, including individuals living with dementia and those requiring tailored care. The local authority’s self-assessment told us that their Joint Strategic Needs and Asset assessment (JSNAA) identified gaps in provision for specific groups, including individuals living with dementia and those requiring tailored care. In response, the Living Bett"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[55,"A lack of suitable local supported living properties was identified in the local authority’s Market Position Statement and was being actively addressed."],[56,"Additionally, there was an effort to ensure clear communication and robust data sharing between children’s and adult services to avoid gaps in continuity. For example, there was a Multi-Agency Transitions Forum and Multi-Agency Post-16 Panel meeting regularly to track all young people approaching adulthood with Special Educational Needs and Disabilities (SEND) or complex needs."],[57,"The local authority’s Market Position Statement (MPS) identified that the current market provided a lack of choice for residents with mental health needs, learning disabilities and/ or autism; resulting in an overreliance on residential care."]],"m":"Senior leaders told us a full review of the process had taken place, and they were assured that people’s wellbeing was not impacted by this process and gave examples of using case recording systems in this way. People told us there were gaps in support when people were transitioning from children to adult services. A lack of suitable local supported living properties was identified in the local authority’s Market Position Statement and was being actively addressed. A lack of suitable local suppo"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[65,"Some staff described recent engagement as having had limited impact, however, leaders told us that there had been consistent efforts in staff engagement, which was reflected in the results of a staff survey undertaken in 2025 which provided positive feedback from many employees."],[67,"However, some staff told us that oversight was lacking, and clear quality assurance arrangements were not in place."],[68,"The local authority was capturing equalities data but had recognised there was more to do to consistently collect equality data and use it to influence strategy and reduce inequalities in people’s care and support experiences and outcomes. This was one of the newly identified equality objectives in the ‘Equality, Diversity, Inclusion and Belonging’ Strategy (EDIB) 2024-2027))."]],"m":"Some staff described recent engagement as having had limited impact, however, leaders told us that there had been consistent efforts in staff engagement, which was reflected in the results of a staff survey undertaken in 2025 which provided positive feedback from many employees. However, some staff told us that oversight was lacking, and clear quality assurance arrangements were not in place."},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Southampton":[{"q":[[12,"A new rota system gave staff more flexibility through rotating duties, which they welcomed, but staff noted it didn’t reduce workload."],[14,"Others expressed frustration when they did not receive copies of assessments, Assessments lacked key information (for example support needs in relation to medication), or assessments were difficult to understand without support."],[15,"Feedback from staff suggested inconsistent approaches and some teams relying on the person to contact them."]],"m":"The local authority could not consistently demonstrate that requests for support and assistance were dealt with appropriately and in a timely manner. A new rota system gave staff more flexibility through rotating duties, which they welcomed, but staff noted it didn’t reduce workload. Staff had completed mandatory Oliver McGowan training on providing care and support to people with learning disabilities and autistic people but nevertheless told us they felt in need of additional specialist traini"},{"q":[[24,"A senior leader acknowledged that capacity in the Connect Team to deal with high volumes of requests for assistance, triage, and undertake assessments limited their ability to explore personalised responses in the community to meet lower-level needs and reach out to the voluntary sector."],[27,"The local authority used analysis from the Southampton Data observatory to identify people with needs for care and support that were not being met. This informed some decisions for example commissioning a refuge which could accommodate people with complex needs and developing self-contained refuge accommodation for families with older teenage sons."],[29,"Waiting times and numbers had risen since July, but the longest wait in the past year was 132 days, showing significant reduction in their backlog."]],"m":"Coordination of use and promotion these initiatives, was limited, meaning that they were effective when used but not consistently considered by frontline staff. A senior leader acknowledged that capacity in the Connect Team to deal with high volumes of requests for assistance, triage, and undertake assessments limited their ability to explore personalised responses in the community to meet lower-level needs and reach out to the voluntary sector. Local authority staff said there was a good respit"},{"q":[[36,"Another leader acknowledged that they needed to do more outreach, especially to newer communities such as those from Eastern Europe, which lacked organised groups and were harder to reach."],[38,"Limited cultural inclusion, the impact of unconscious bias, the impact of protected characteristics and use of interpreters were identified as key themes from SARs in the annual Safeguarding Adults board report 2023/ 24."],[38,"The Principal Social worker was incorporating EDI into training and practice expectations but impact of this was not yet evident."]],"m":"There were some shortfalls and areas for development in reducing barriers to care and support, inequalities in experience and outcomes. The local authority did not demonstrate how resources were targeted to meet the needs of those with multiple sources of disadvantage and minimise inequity of outcomes and experience. Another leader acknowledged that they needed to do more outreach, especially to newer communities such as those from Eastern Europe, which lacked organised groups and were harder to"},{"q":[[45,"The local authority did not articulate a clear plan for how/when stated developments would be achieved. For example, in the ASCAP it was identified that 95 more nursing placements were required by 2025 and 118 by 2030."],[46,"Although local authority support for carers was reaching 15.6% of carers who self-identified in the census in 2021staff were aware that there were others not yet receiving support."],[48,"Care and support met demand, but some people with complex needs, such as people with advanced dementia or behavioural support needs, needed out-of-area placements because of a lack of specialist provision in the city."]],"m":"People had access to a range of safe, effective, affordable, and high-quality local support options to meet their care and support needs, although these were not always within the City boundary. The local authority did not articulate a clear plan for how/when stated developments would be achieved. The local authority was therefore unable to evidence that they were strategically shaping their care market to have sufficient capacity to meet current and future demand. Although local authority suppo"},{"q":[[56,"The local authority noted that the latest data available was from 2023-4 so did not demonstrate impact of this first year of work."],[59,"Other staff and managers described limited visibility between local authority and health systems which reduced their ability to collaborate across teams and caused delays on cases."],[59,"Partners told us that despite a shared desire for integrated working, the end of the Section 75 agreement for mental health services had led to fragmented collaboration and uncertainty about long-term commitment. They said this was because working arrangements were not aligned to enable seamless and effective care."]],"m":"The local authority noted that the latest data available was from 2023-4 so did not demonstrate impact of this first year of work. Other staff and managers described limited visibility between local authority and health systems which reduced their ability to collaborate across teams and caused delays on cases. We heard that where there was a lack of individual consent to share information with other professionals, this complicated case management for people with both health and social care needs"},{"q":[[65,"People who used services and partners did not always feel listened to or that their views were considered during transitions."],[65,"People who needed short term rehabilitation in a care home setting however were more likely to face discharge challenges, due to a lack of capacity regionally, and any such service in Southampton itself."],[66,"After they became involved, they continued support carrying out an initial adult services review 6-8 weeks after the young person’s 18th birthday, before handover to a long-term adult’s team. This ensured continuity and minimised gaps in care."]],"m":"Many risks were identified and managed proactively, but others were not meaning that some people experienced delays to transitions due to a lack of local provision to meet specific needs, or due to delayed involvement by social care. The effectiveness of these processes in keeping people safe was not routinely monitored. Staff and managers told us that where individuals had not consented, the lack of access to shared systems in mental health services meant that not all members of the team who mi"},{"q":[[71,"Although the 2024 safeguarding process pathway indicated a “safeguarding hub practitioner” would help filter the high volume of referrals, to identify safeguarding concerns, it was not clear whether this was consistently happening and effective under the Connect Team."],[71,"Staff did not describe filtering and the Connect team process pathway did not evidence this either. This might mean staff were delayed in identifying which emails contained safeguarding concerns to prioritise these."],[71,"Emails and contacts recorded on the local authority’s database were triaged in date order as they were not prioritised before they reached the First Contact team, after which there was a secondary wait for review as a concern."]],"m":"We were not assured that there were effective systems, processes and practices in place to ensure people were protected from abuse and neglect, or that concerns were always dealt with in a timely manner. The local authority was unable to demonstrate that they had full oversight of safeguarding risk and how it was being managed. Although the 2024 safeguarding process pathway indicated a “safeguarding hub practitioner” would help filter the high volume of referrals, to identify safeguarding concer"},{"q":[[82,"Governance, management, and accountability arrangements were not always clear and effective in providing visibility and assurance on the delivery of Care Act duties, quality, sustainability, and risks to delivery."],[83,"Strategic oversight of the consistent quality of work undertaken within and across teams was limited although a renewed focus on audits had commenced since May 2025."],[87,"Staff told us data was actively used to allocate resources and inform strategic decisions across Adult Social Care (ASC) however as described above this did not always mitigate risk – for example it was not used to address waiting times."]],"m":"Governance, management, and accountability arrangements were not always clear and effective in providing visibility and assurance on the delivery of Care Act duties, quality, sustainability, and risks to delivery. At the time of our assessment however these were not sufficiently progressed to have the impact intended, to effectively provide assurance of people’s experiences of care and support, or the outcomes they achieved. Risks to full implementation had been identified and actions to mitigat"},{"q":[[93,"One leader noted they were aware that many people did not have access to the internet, which created a significant barrier to accessing services and information."],[94,"The local authority commissioned an external organisation to support their Ambitious Futures transformation and improvement programme, but the directorate risk assessment identified that data issues and unreliable tools left by this third party, unfamiliarity with how to adjust the tools and a lack of a service level agreement for fixes had created more risk to sustaining change after their departure."],[94,"The local authority said they were committed to being a learning organisation, who actively sought feedback from those who use services, their workforce, and their partners. They said they used these insights to inform their improvement activity but practices to manage and correct identified concerns were not yet embedded."]],"m":"A senior leader said they wanted to better understand whether waiting times and quality were due to gaps in performance management and inconsistent supervision support. A senior leader said they wanted to better understand whether waiting times and quality were due to gaps in performance management and inconsistent supervision support. They said that newer line managers may not have received the level of training and support they needed to do this effectively. Staff also referred to thematic aud"}],"Southend-on-Sea":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[43,"There wasn’t always sufficient care and support available to meet demand, and people experienced delays in accessing some specialist services."],[46,"As the local authority did not commission Continuing Health Care funded placements or community packages of care this was an area of focus primarily for health partners."],[49,"However, data provided by the local authority showed homecare workforce numbers currently exceeded demand and frontline teams were collaborating with providers to retain staff and support them to move to other adult social care roles to ensure their skills and experience were not lost."]],"m":"There wasn’t always sufficient care and support available to meet demand, and people experienced delays in accessing some specialist services. Staff and leaders identified delays in discharge to assess placements due to the length of time needed to complete housing adaptations to meet people’s needs, and partners noted a shortage of social housing options available for rough sleepers. Feedback from people and unpaid carers was mixed, with some people telling us they felt included in decisions ab"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[59,"People told us the care provider market did not always meet their needs due to the lack of range, quality and availability of specialist services in Southend-on-Sea."],[59,"The Corporate Register recognised the provider market concerns and the risk to the local authority of being unable to meet its statutory responsibilities under the Care Act 2014 leading to care not being provided in a timely manner."],[60,"Feedback from people with lived experience of transitions was mixed, with some concerns raised around safe hospital discharges and delays in access to the equipment required to keep people safe."]],"m":"People told us the care provider market did not always meet their needs due to the lack of range, quality and availability of specialist services in Southend-on-Sea. The Corporate Register recognised the provider market concerns and the risk to the local authority of being unable to meet its statutory responsibilities under the Care Act 2014 leading to care not being provided in a timely manner. Feedback from people with lived experience of transitions was mixed, with some concerns raised around"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"St Helens":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[29,"St Helens understood the health needs of the local population and worked closely with internal and external partners to understand current and future service demand in the area."],[31,"However, staff and leaders told us the data gathered was not always effectively used to improve the experiences and outcomes for all people who were more likely to receive poor care and support."],[32,"Staff and leaders told us they could access translation support through the communications service which provided sign language interpreters, lip-speakers, and deafblind interpreters as well as a range of languages spoken by people whose first language was not English."]],"m":"However, staff and leaders told us the data gathered was not always effectively used to improve the experiences and outcomes for all people who were more likely to receive poor care and support. Staff and leaders told us they could access translation support through the communications service which provided sign language interpreters, lip-speakers, and deafblind interpreters as well as a range of languages spoken by people whose first language was not English."},{"q":[[34,"However, at the time of the assessment it was unclear what impact these changes were having on the engagement with these and other seldom heard groups such as the small communities of people from Eastern Europe, refugees, and the Roma, Gypsy, and Traveller community in St Helens."],[38,"Whilst the local authority had clear plans to address gaps in service provision, at the time of the assessment there was not always sufficient care and support available to meet demand, or to allow people to access services when, where and how they needed it."],[40,"The main reason for delayed discharge was cited as due to a lack of capacity within the Borough to meet dementia care and more complex care needs, representing a 14.0% delay with requests for bed-based provision and had increased use of out-of-borough placements to meet people’s care and support needs."]],"m":"Partners told us due to the high levels of deprivation within the Borough the care market had a limited proportion of self-funders, with the Office for National Statistics (ONS) data for 2022 suggesting 85% of care service placements in St Helens were state funded. However, at the time of the assessment it was unclear what impact these changes were having on the engagement with these and other seldom heard groups such as the small communities of people from Eastern Europe, refugees, and the Roma"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[55,"However, people did not always feel safe in St Helens, particularly during transitions."],[56,"People told us of a lack of engagement with older carers and families supporting people with learning disabilities who were living at home. For example, people felt Contact Cares was set up to support immediate need, and not future contingency planning."],[57,"Key areas of concern included inconsistent responses from mental health services, with some appearing to work more cohesively than others, access to crisis support lines, and homecare staff who were not trained to support people with complex mental health feeling unsupported."]],"m":"However, people did not always feel safe in St Helens, particularly during transitions. People told us of a lack of engagement with older carers and families supporting people with learning disabilities who were living at home. However, whilst most people told us their experiences of local authority support during transitions was positive, there were some occasions where people felt there was not enough communication between children and adult services leading to delays in recognising when young"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Staffordshire":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Stockport":[{"q":[[11,"Discussions about ‘best value’ could be challenging, though they did not always lead to the cheapest option."],[11,"However, others reported being given little notice of reviews, which limited their ability to prepare."],[12,"Data provided by the local authority showed 125 people were waiting for an assessment in October 2025, with a median waiting time of 16 days and maximum waiting time of 162 days. In relation to reviews, 470 people were waiting in October 2025 with a median waiting time of 84 days and a maximum waiting time of 654 days."]],"m":"Discussions about ‘best value’ could be challenging, though they did not always lead to the cheapest option. However, others reported being given little notice of reviews, which limited their ability to prepare. Some partners told us in their experience there could be delays in reviews taking place. The local authority reported that reducing waiting for people remained a challenge in the context of their workforce, reporting a lack of time and workload pressures. Unpaid carers had to contact the"},{"q":[[21,"The local authority worked with people, partners and the local community to make available a range of services and resources to promote independence, and to prevent, delay or reduce the need for care and support."],[29,"Some people did not find information on the local authority website easy to navigate."],[30,"Staff confirmed there used to be a backlog of people waiting but due to improvements in processes this was no longer the case."]],"m":"Some people did not find information on the local authority website easy to navigate. Staff confirmed there used to be a backlog of people waiting but due to improvements in processes this was no longer the case."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[68,"The REaCH Team could also provide a 3-night service to assess overnight risk and needs, which was supplemented by the private care market during periods of high operational pressure, when REaCH Team capacity could be limited."],[69,"Staff liaised with schools and colleges to attend Education Health and Care Plan reviews, but they had not always been able to attend in the past due to staff shortages. This had been raised with managers and additional resources had been agreed."],[70,"For another person there had been a 5 month wait for allocation, with the assessment starting 6 months before the young person turned 18. This indicated that people sometimes experienced delays in the allocation of a social worker."]],"m":"However, emergency support options for people with higher levels of need or mental health issues were more limited, there were no out of hours equipment arrangements and sourcing equipment repairs could be challenging. Where emergency provision was not available, hospital admission was sometimes the only option. Although receiving multiple voicemails was not a regular occurrence, this approach did not provide assurance that those messages with the most risk attached would receive the fastest res"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Stockton-on-Tees":[{"q":[[14,"Over this period, 52% of people waited 28 days or less for a Care Act assessment."],[14,"Similarly, leaders were aware that increasing numbers of overdue annual Care Act reviews indicated more work was required to ensure peoples’ needs were being met in a timely manner."],[15,"Additionally, although people were being actively supported by social workers, formal reviews in the last 12 months were not always undertaken and recorded."]],"m":"Some people said they were not always clear about referral pathways or how to ask for support from the local authority. Some work had been undertaken to reshape the front door, but this was a work in progress and the impact had not yet come to fruition. There was a lack of clarity amongst staff we spoke with about the next steps or timescales for the work. However, some people said they did not feel they had choice over how they received their care, for example, in relation to the care placement"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[28,"Local authority leaders understood the demographic profile of the area, and they had insight into inequalities and barriers to social care experienced by people in the borough. For example, there was a recognition of the disparity in life expectancy of people living in different neighbourhoods in the borough."],[28,"There was also awareness of the issues affecting specific groups who were at risk of not having their health and social care need met at an early stage."],[30,"Local authority staff involved in carrying out Care Act duties did not always have a strong understanding of cultural diversity within the population, with some unable to demonstrate knowledge of the community profile of the borough. For example, some staff did not recognise that there were seldom, or unheard communities in the borough."]],"m":"Local authority staff involved in carrying out Care Act duties did not always have a strong understanding of cultural diversity within the population, with some unable to demonstrate knowledge of the community profile of the borough."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[50,"Leaders were aware that transition arrangements for young people required greater oversight and coordination across agencies, as the current shortfalls could exacerbate existing risks to this group."],[51,"Policies and feedback from some staff indicated that, although adult services met with children’s services to plan for young people’s support after the age of 18, adult services often did not start to work with young people at an early enough age to allow for adequate planning and preparation for adulthood."],[51,"Some adult social care teams managed to provide support at short notice, but this could not always be achieved."]],"m":"However, some staff told us risks they had raised (for example, a lack of communication with some external partners) were not consistently reflected at senior level as they were not informed about actions taken in response to raising these issues. However, some staff told us risks they had raised (for example, a lack of communication with some external partners) were not consistently reflected at senior level as they were not informed about actions taken in response to raising these issues. This"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[66,"Staff were encouraged to take up training and development opportunities, such as apprenticeships, Assessed and Supported Year in Employment (ASYE) courses and to use nationally recognised CPD tools to track their learning."],[69,"Staff told us that senior leaders usually listened to them when they raised gaps in policies and provision, for example around transition support for young people, and provided them with a response to the issues raised."],[69,"While there was no formal process for recording compliments in place, leaders told us it was practice for staff to notify their manager when they receive a compliment."]],"m":"Staff told us that senior leaders usually listened to them when they raised gaps in policies and provision, for example around transition support for young people, and provided them with a response to the issues raised. While there was no formal process for recording compliments in place, leaders told us it was practice for staff to notify their manager when they receive a compliment."}],"Stoke-on-Trent":[{"q":[[12,"Staff gave good examples of how they empathetically supported people with specific cultural needs and protected characteristics, but this was not uniformly captured in the Care Act assessment."],[13,"There were long waits for annual reviews, and the local authority could not always attend joint reviews with partners, for example s117 reviews. In response the local authority had launched an annual review team in January 2025 and were recruiting to this."],[16,"The local authority did not have a separate Care Act appeals process but did have an adult social care complaints process, and as part of the review of the complaints policy they planned to look at a process for logging Care Act appeals separately to support early resolution."]],"m":"Staff gave good examples of how they empathetically supported people with specific cultural needs and protected characteristics, but this was not uniformly captured in the Care Act assessment. There were long waits for annual reviews, and the local authority could not always attend joint reviews with partners, for example s117 reviews. There were long waits for annual reviews, and the local authority could not always attend joint reviews with partners, for example s117 reviews. In response the l"},{"q":[[23,"There was a risk management process in place for the waiting list, and people with an urgent need could be seen within 10 working days, but people with non-urgent needs waited a year of more, and this was reflected in the feedback we received from people in need of equipment."],[23,"There was no evidence of learning and action from when things did not go according to plan or from Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency notifications."],[24,"The local authority had online information and advice, and its website was partially compliant with the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines standards, which would allow people to access most of the website with accessibility apps on any computer, but online forms were not accessible this way."]],"m":"There was a risk management process in place for the waiting list, and people with an urgent need could be seen within 10 working days, but people with non-urgent needs waited a year of more, and this was reflected in the feedback we received from people in need of equipment. There were operational procedures for equipment and adaptation provision, and the Occupational Therapist came under the same three-step practice framework as the rest of the social care workforce; however, there was a lack "},{"q":[[28,"The Local Authority understood its local population profile and demographics and had a clear Joint Strategic Needs assessment that outlined population profiles."],[29,"The social care workforce was not representative of the demographic profile of the area, but Stoke-on-Trent had made efforts to understand this and put in place actions to address it. They had refreshed their Skills for Care Workforce Race Equality Standard in July 2024, and this showed that 10% of its workforce earning under £40,000 were from an ethnic group, and there was no employee from an ethnic group earning more than £80,000."],[30,"We had some feedback that the d/Deaf community found it hard to access information about social care services for them, and there was a lack of service provision for people who spoke British Sign Language and had care needs."]],"m":"The social care workforce was not representative of the demographic profile of the area, but Stoke-on-Trent had made efforts to understand this and put in place actions to address it. The social care workforce was not representative of the demographic profile of the area, but Stoke-on-Trent had made efforts to understand this and put in place actions to address it. They had refreshed their Skills for Care Workforce Race Equality Standard in July 2024, and this showed that 10% of its workforce ea"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[45,"An automated telephony project called people on the review list for welfare calls to support screening of needs while people waited for review."],[48,"One carer told us that they had to request an assessment for their child when they turned 18 as they had not been offered a Care Act assessment."],[50,"The initial survey showed that carers and families did not know about discharge plans."]],"m":"An automated telephony project called people on the review list for welfare calls to support screening of needs while people waited for review. One carer told us that they had to request an assessment for their child when they turned 18 as they had not been offered a Care Act assessment. The initial survey showed that carers and families did not know about discharge plans. Funding decisions or disputes with other agencies did not lead to delays in the provision of care and support."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Suffolk":[{"q":[[15,"The median waiting time was 12 days and the maximum waiting time was 64 days, against an internal target of 28 days. This indicated most people were assessed in good time, but some people waited for longer periods."],[17,"Local authority data showed 53% of unpaid carers waited more than 28 days for their assessment and 5% waited more than 91 days. This fluctuated over time depending on changes in demand."],[19,"The local authority signposted people to non-statutory services for help with their noneligible care and support needs, including commissioned voluntary, community, faith, and social enterprise (VCFSE) organisations which bridged the gap for people who were not eligible for a funded package of care."]],"m":"Staff were supported in this approach and were not pressured to rush assessments. The median waiting time was 12 days and the maximum waiting time was 64 days, against an internal target of 28 days. This indicated most people were assessed in good time, but some people waited for longer periods. The local authority was working to improve the timeliness of assessments, care planning and reviews. This included actions to reduce risks to people’s wellbeing while they waited. However, some feedback "},{"q":[[28,"An enhanced level of support was provided through quality assurance processes, which strengthened outcomes for people who could not be supported by the Home First model due to capacity constraints. This meant people received the equivalent level of reablement from either the home first team or the commissioned providers."],[29,"A leader told us OT assessment waits could be easily impacted where there was absence as Occupational Therapist roles were more limited."],[29,"A leader told us teams for different geographical areas were flexible in supporting each other based on workload and need. They also told us of an external review being completed for the OT service, which would inform a strategic plan focused on reducing waiting times for people who required major adaptations, although it was not yet possible to assess the impact of this review."]],"m":"There was a system to identify people whose care and support needs were not being met. The local authority was not yet able to show data outcomes for reablement provided by external reablement providers as there was not yet enough outcome data to report percentages for this part of the pathway. An enhanced level of support was provided through quality assurance processes, which strengthened outcomes for people who could not be supported by the Home First model due to capacity constraints. An enh"},{"q":[[38,"The impact of this strategic approach was not yet evidenced at the time of the assessment."],[39,"There were positive examples of outreach and engagement with under-represented or seldom-heard communities to understand experiences and support access to services, but this work was not always well defined, and the impact was not always clear."],[41,"Some partner feedback suggested there was need for accessibility support to be consistent however, with some people with sensory needs receiving letters in standard format from the local authority when this was not their preference, for example."]],"m":"The impact of this strategic approach was not yet evidenced at the time of the assessment. There were positive examples of outreach and engagement with under-represented or seldom-heard communities to understand experiences and support access to services, but this work was not always well defined, and the impact was not always clear. Staff and leaders also gave examples of outreach and engagement, but the impact of this work was not clearly embedded. Some partner feedback suggested there was nee"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Sunderland":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Surrey":[{"q":[[12,"The number of overdue reviews for people with a costed service had decreased by 2.3% since last month, with an overall reduction of 3% since March 2024."],[12,"The length of time people were waiting for a review has increased currently showing 25% of overdue reviews were more than 1 year over their planned date."],[14,"Partners mirrored the comments of unpaid carers also adding that the language used in assessments was not always user friendly for young carers and there could be confusion at times about who was responsible for support to parent carers. They confirmed the local authority commissioned specific services to support carer breaks and incorporating emergency plans into support packages for unpaid carers."]],"m":"Areas of frustration were around not always getting the same person to speak with at the local authority, delays in getting responses and not always feeling they were offered a choice. There remained a backlog for some assessments and reviews, however there had been a focus on these recently and the waiting lists were starting to reduce. The number of overdue reviews for people with a costed service had decreased by 2.3% since last month, with an overall reduction of 3% since March 2024. The len"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[60,"There could be inconsistencies in the approaches to safeguarding and they felt some parts of the safeguarding process were not well understood by staff. For example, someone with mental capacity should be asked what they wanted to happen as part of the process and this did not always take place."],[63,"Work with the Safeguarding Adults Board had introduced new processes which meant section 42 enquiries were sifted out and managed to reduce backlogs. For example, a change was implemented in how incidents were handled and through data cleansing."],[66,"An example was given of a person who was experiencing self-neglect and self-harm, however because the person was not known to adult social care and did not have a commissioned care and support service, the case was not investigated."]],"m":"Where information came in that was not of a safeguarding nature this was reassigned to the relevant place. There could be inconsistencies in the approaches to safeguarding and they felt some parts of the safeguarding process were not well understood by staff. Another key objective was to have a consistent view of gaps in referral processes and put in place effective initiatives to address these. Covert medicines were sometimes given to people when they were refusing to take medicine but lacked t"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Swindon":[{"q":[[11,"Unpaid carers expressed mixed experiences regarding respite care, some had received emergency carers' cards or grants, while others had lacked access to services."],[14,"The local authority’s My Care My Views survey analysis (June-October 2024) found 157 people knew how much money was available for their care, while 35 didn’t, and 71 were unsure."],[15,"By March 2025, waiting times had increased with an average wait of 12 days and a maximum wait of 39 days, affecting 37 people."]],"m":"The local authority’s people survey ‘My Care My Views’ (June-October 2024) analysis, showed many people felt supported and advocated for, yet some reported a lack of involvement in decision-making, delays in decisions, and low levels of social worker engagement. However, there were some gaps in longterm planning and there had been some risks for those awaiting assessments. The local authority had made progress in addressing delays in assessments and reviews, but challenges remained. Some partner"},{"q":[[19,"While some people reported significant delays in obtaining Occupational Therapy (OT) assessments and mobility aids and sometimes waiting over a year, others found that urgent cases were escalated and managed well. For example, a person while waiting a long time for an assessment, had moved into a care home to meet their needs before their assessment was completed."],[21,"We heard about some long delays in sourcing a personal assistant through the local authority’s commissioned organisation."],[21,"We heard the DP team had historically been short-staffed which had led to delays in reviews."]],"m":"While some people reported significant delays in obtaining Occupational Therapy (OT) assessments and mobility aids and sometimes waiting over a year, others found that urgent cases were escalated and managed well. Overall, access to equipment and adaptations was inconsistent. While structured processes and collaborative efforts had led to positive developments, there had been lengthy wait times and sometimes unclear communication. We heard about some long delays in sourcing a personal assistant "},{"q":[[23,"Improvements to the data gaps included providing guidance for teams on data recording, integration of ED&I into Senior Leadership Performance meetings and compliance tracking in the Data Governance forward plan."],[26,"Staff had reported that the forms were not accessible, prompting leaders to adjust the format, replacing star ratings with symbols."],[27,"Staff coordinated interpretation and translation support, ensuring that language barriers did not prevent access to essential services."]],"m":"Improvements to the data gaps included providing guidance for teams on data recording, integration of ED&I into Senior Leadership Performance meetings and compliance tracking in the Data Governance forward plan. Staff had reported that the forms were not accessible, prompting leaders to adjust the format, replacing star ratings with symbols. However, a partner said accessible information standards had not yet been fully embedded and suggested streamlining information-sharing with partners. Staff"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[41,"Two daily calls between the local authority and inpatient teams facilitated real-time information sharing, though digital platforms were not integrated."],[42,"Partners’ feedback was mixed in this area, some said people experienced stress and confusion due to unclear communication from the local authority regarding care arrangements."],[42,"The local authority provided additional evidence after our assessment to show that numbers of AMPHs were not low."]],"m":"Two daily calls between the local authority and inpatient teams facilitated real-time information sharing, though digital platforms were not integrated. Partners’ feedback was mixed in this area, some said people experienced stress and confusion due to unclear communication from the local authority regarding care arrangements. The local authority provided additional evidence after our assessment to show that numbers of AMPHs were not low."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Tameside":[{"q":[[11,"The local authority pathways suggested that all adult social care pathways began at the Community Gateway, which was not always the case."],[13,"Assessments and reviews were not always timely and up-to-date, and waiting times varied across teams. For example, people supported by the Learning Disability Team experienced longer waits than others."],[13,"The local authority introduced the Early Support and Advice Hub (ESAH) in February 2025 and by November 2025 the number of people waiting for an assessment had reduced to 35 with nobody having waited longer than 6 months."]],"m":"Leaders told us the Community Gateway operated as the integrated administrative access point for adult social care and health, and its functions were limited to initial contact handling and basic demographic information gathering. Leaders reported that around 50% of people who contacted the service through ESAH were signposted elsewhere and did not go on to access adult social care. The local authority pathways suggested that all adult social care pathways began at the Community Gateway, which w"},{"q":[[26,"However, as of October 2025, the local authority faced delays in delivering non-high-risk OT and OTSA assessments, with waiting lists of 309 and 399 people respectively, median wait times of 101 and 94 days respectively, and maximum wait times of 1081 and 549 days respectively."],[26,"Staff told us there were ongoing difficulties with the local authority’s equipment provider and stock shortages had caused 2–3 week delays in assessments. They stated commissioners had indicated there were limited options for changing provider, so efforts had focused on improving the existing service."],[27,"While these measures aimed to improve efficiency, the scale of the wait continued to present housing challenges, with staff working to keep people safe through prioritisation and proportionate responses while they waited."]],"m":"However, several partners felt that prevention work had been limited by funding pressures and an overriding focus on statutory duties. However, as of October 2025, the local authority faced delays in delivering non-high-risk OT and OTSA assessments, with waiting lists of 309 and 399 people respectively, median wait times of 101 and 94 days respectively, and maximum wait times of 1081 and 549 days respectively. Partners told us in their experience, there were no concerns with the provision of sma"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[38,"The local authority had begun working with local people and stakeholders to understand the care and support needs of people and communities. However, this engagement had not yet been formally captured and utilised in terms of a clear commissioning strategy."],[40,"Although the MPS outlined a set of priorities, it did not provide a clear commissioning strategy to support their delivery."],[40,"The document identified short-, medium-, and long‑term actions, but it lacked timelines and did not explain how progress would be measured over the 3‑year period covered by the MPS. For example, the MPS identified a requirement for 320 older people’s extra‑care units."]],"m":"The local authority had begun working with local people and stakeholders to understand the care and support needs of people and communities. However, this engagement had not yet been formally captured and utilised in terms of a clear commissioning strategy. A major focus was expanding housing‑based support, including extra‑care and supported living, to address gaps in suitable accommodation for older adults and working‑age disabled people. Although the MPS outlined a set of priorities, it did no"},{"q":[[48,"When the local authority worked in partnerships with other agencies, there were some arrangements for governance, though these were not always clearly documented."],[50,"The local authority monitored and evaluated the impact of its partnership working through established governance arrangements, though this was not consistent across all partnerships or strategies."],[53,"While they were often approached for input into campaigns or consultations, they did not always feel this reflected genuine partnership working."]],"m":"When the local authority worked in partnerships with other agencies, there were some arrangements for governance, though these were not always clearly documented. However, more local structures between health and the local authority were not clearly documented. Although leaders were clearly engaged in joint governance, pathways and structures were not always evident, with both leaders and partners describing arrangements as largely relationship‑based. However, some staff told us integrated worki"},{"q":[[55,"There were policies and strategies to support risk assessment including (but not limited to) risk registers, neglect guidance, inter-agency safeguarding guidance, a domestic abuse strategy and disaster recovery plans."],[55,"Staff were not aware, nor did they refer to formal criteria or protocols regarding risk assessing or outlining when concerns should be escalated to social workers, managers, AMHPs, or emergency services, and staff explained that they relied on their experience to guide decision‑making."],[58,"Others experienced delays, with transition activity beginning later than they expected and adult arrangements not always being in place when they turned 18."]],"m":"There were policies and strategies to support risk assessment including (but not limited to) risk registers, neglect guidance, inter-agency safeguarding guidance, a domestic abuse strategy and disaster recovery plans. Staff were not aware, nor did they refer to formal criteria or protocols regarding risk assessing or outlining when concerns should be escalated to social workers, managers, AMHPs, or emergency services, and staff explained that they relied on their experience to guide decision‑mak"},{"q":[[63,"Their dedicated team managed all applications efficiently and, notably, did not operate a waiting list. They reported that in 2024-2025 the area saw more than 1,000 applications per 100,000 adults."],[65,"There was clarity on what constituted a Section 42 safeguarding concern and when S42 safeguarding enquiries were required, but limited evidence to establish if this was applied consistently."],[65,"There was limited evidence of a clear rationale and outcome from initial enquiries, including those which did not progress to a Section 42 enquiry."]],"m":"Systems, processes, and practices were in place to promote people’s safety and reduce the risk of abuse or neglect, but these were not always applied consistently or communicated clearly. However, there was no formal written guidance detailing local safeguarding pathways or processes. Staff and leaders noted that some referrals did not meet safeguarding thresholds, contributing to the increasing volume of concerns being managed. Staff and leaders noted that some referrals did not meet safeguardi"},{"q":[[69,"However, the register did not capture several identified risks within safe systems; for example, while it noted rising safeguarding referrals, it did not reflect gaps such as the absence of localised documented pathways and guidance for staff."],[71,"Other strategies, such as the Workforce Strategy, People Plan, Adults Business Plan and Corporate Plan, set out ambitions, priorities and governance arrangements but contained limited analytical depth, lacking detailed modelling, Key Performance Indicators (KPIs), timescales, or evaluation measures."],[73,"The People Plan, shaped by feedback from 500 staff, set organisational expectations but similarly lacked detailed actions, outcomes, and implementation timelines, limiting the authority’s ability to fully assure progress."]],"m":"However, some pathways and processes were not yet operating effectively and required further development and embedding. However, the register did not capture several identified risks within safe systems; for example, while it noted rising safeguarding referrals, it did not reflect gaps such as the absence of localised documented pathways and guidance for staff. While some strategies were informed by meaningful analysis of risks, inequalities and outcomes, some key documents and strategies remain"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Telford and Wrekin":[{"q":[[12,"Annual reviews of care and support had also benefited from the local authority’s recent changes in structure and process, with the number of overdue 12-month reviews reduced from over 500 people in March 2024 to 35 people in July 2024."],[16,"Telford and Wrekin did not operate a separate appeals process; however, people with care and support needs, their family and carers were able to appeal using the Adult Social Care complaints process. As part of this process referrals to the Ombudsman was also signposted."],[12,"Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely or up to date, with significant waiting lists for assessments. Figures provided by the local authority showed only 25% of Care Act 2014 assessments were completed within the authority’s target timescale of 28 days, with the longest wait at the time data was submitted to CQC (March 2024) being 294 days (from the date of submission) and 178 people awaiting an assessment."]],"m":"However, people gave us examples of inconsistent communication and lack of engagement meaning the social work practice framework was not always embedded in front line staff practice. However, some people experienced less favourable arrangements, with examples of poor communication, lack of control over decision making, and inconsistency of approach from individual frontline staff. Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely or up to date, with significant waiting lists for a"},{"q":[[24,"Data provided by the local authority showed less than 1% of discharges during the first quarter of 2024-2025 were delayed due to lack of social care service capacity; none of these delays resulted in delays of more than 72 hours."],[25,"Increase demand for occupational therapy assessments as well as staffing shortages meant there were significant waiting lists particularly for more complex assessments, with 39.3% of assessments completed within the local authority’s target of 28 days."],[28,"Despite the lack of understanding and poor uptake of direct payments, where they were used, people told us of positive outcomes and improved control of their care and support needs. For example, people had used payments for yoga sessions, fridge freezers and tumble dryers to improve independence, confidence, and health."]],"m":"Data provided by the local authority showed less than 1% of discharges during the first quarter of 2024-2025 were delayed due to lack of social care service capacity; none of these delays resulted in delays of more than 72 hours. Most people felt listened to by occupational therapists and assistive technologies helped to maintain their independence, however, people found out of hours commissioned support when equipment broke down was not always timely. Further data provided by the local authorit"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[68,"Partners had similar concerns around CoP assessments, citing inconsistencies in understanding of MCA 2005 assessments and a lack of understanding of the application process. For example, partners told us of occasions where people’s rights were inappropriately restricted due to a lack of understanding of the MCA Act 2005 by the frontline social work teams."],[68,"Feedback from people, staff, and partners consistently identified concerns with DoLs applications. They gave examples of some frontline staff’s understanding of people’s rights under the MCA Act 2005 impacting on people’s independence, and identified frontline staff did not always involve people fully in investigations."],[69,"However, due to the poor uptake of MCA 2005 staff training across Adult Social Care in the borough it is unclear people’s capacity was always assessed correctly."]],"m":"However, people told us coproduction in this area was not as strong as in other Adult Social Care partnership boards. However, partners and staff told us safeguarding policies and procedures were not fully embedded and staff did not always respond quickly enough to concerns. Staff and leaders told us about emerging themes, including forced marriages, domestic abuse, pressure ulcers, emotional abuse, neglect (including selfneglect), and modern slavery. In addition to this, the local authority had"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Thurrock":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[35,"Partners questioned whether frameworks such as the Improving Equality Outcomes Board had led to meaningful change, particularly for seldom-heard communities, and the local authority did not have data to support positive impact for this work."],[37,"The offer of translation services on the website was only available in English so it was not clear how people who could not read or speak English would know how to access translation services."],[38,"While initiatives such as co-produced communication tools, translation services, sensory assessments, and sign video systems enhanced accessibility, BSL interpretation delays, limited translated resources, and reliance on family members for interpreting support highlighted areas for improvement."]],"m":"However, the local authority relied on data from GPs to understand the GRT community which was not accurate. Discrepancies were also noted in assessments of asylum seeker populations, with inconsistent reports across departments. Partners questioned whether frameworks such as the Improving Equality Outcomes Board had led to meaningful change, particularly for seldom-heard communities, and the local authority did not have data to support positive impact for this work. Partners stated that BSL acc"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Torbay":[{"q":[[16,"Where needs did not meet Care Act thresholds, people were signposted to appropriate services within the VCSE."],[17,"One example showed a person with limited communication was supported through recognition of their strengths."],[19,"While the Waiting Well pack offered public information on eligibility, it did not include details on financial assessments or support for people lacking capacity."]],"m":"There were some challenges around telephone-based assessments and some delays to approvals and increasing demand. In one example, strong personal and family preferences were noted and acted upon, with the local authority offering reassurance about future care arrangements. Multiple visits were noted, incorporating family and unpaid carer voices. Staff ensured the person’s needs and preferences were represented and respected, even when the person could not communicate with them directly. Partners"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[31,"Staff echoed many of these sentiments, showing a deep awareness of the barriers faced by different groups and a proactive approach to addressing them. They highlighted that many carers, particularly younger ones, did not identify as carers and therefore missed out on support."],[31,"To counter this, staff promoted education and outreach, coded carers on GP registers, ran carers groups in community venues, and engaged with the public at events. These efforts were aimed at ensuring equitable access to support, even for those not yet known to the system."],[34,"The local authority had responded to gaps in engagement by working with the Polish school and recruiting ambassadors from ethnic minority groups, including the Chinese community."]],"m":"Staff echoed many of these sentiments, showing a deep awareness of the barriers faced by different groups and a proactive approach to addressing them. They highlighted that many carers, particularly younger ones, did not identify as carers and therefore missed out on support. To counter this, staff promoted education and outreach, coded carers on GP registers, ran carers groups in community venues, and engaged with the public at events. These efforts were aimed at ensuring equitable access to su"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[70,"However, partners noted operational success sometimes relied too heavily on individual relationships, experiencing some service instability following key personnel changes. They also highlighted gaps in specialist provision in social work teams, particularly in learning disability services, although the local authority had responded by embedding expertise within the Complex Care team."],[71,"Leaders also recognised data gaps in ethnicity and protected groups, showing awareness of inequalities and the need for improved data to inform future planning."],[72,"Strategic documents such as the Devon Plan and One Devon Integrated Care Strategy outlined ambitions for unified digital infrastructure, standardising frontline systems and improving data sharing across health and care. These improvements were still in development and not yet embedded in daily practice."]],"m":"However, some staff noted inconsistent management practices and a disconnect from senior leadership, suggesting there was a need for more consistent leadership across all levels. However, partners noted operational success sometimes relied too heavily on individual relationships, experiencing some service instability following key personnel changes. They also highlighted gaps in specialist provision in social work teams, particularly in learning disability services, although the local authority "},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Trafford":[{"q":[[11,"Pathways and processes did not always ensure people’s support was planned and coordinated across different agencies and services."],[12,"However, specialist autism support was not available in other teams, meaning a lack of a learning disability diagnosis was a barrier to specialist support."],[12,"The local authority acknowledged the need for more targeted support for autistic adults in Trafford and had invested in specialist training for staff across social care teams. They had started work with the Trafford Autism Partnership forum to create links between people and services, address gaps in care provision and prevent duplication."]],"m":"Staff praised these workers but reported that community link officers did not always have the capacity to support people when needed. Pathways and processes did not always ensure people’s support was planned and coordinated across different agencies and services. However, specialist autism support was not available in other teams, meaning a lack of a learning disability diagnosis was a barrier to specialist support. The local authority acknowledged the need for more targeted support for autistic"},{"q":[[23,"However, the local authorities Market Position Statement and staff feedback noted therapy recruitment shortages, missed therapy at discharge, and rising daily demand meant that some people could not access the reablement they needed in a timely way."],[24,"In December 2025, 455 people were waiting for an assessment, with the median wait being 21.5 weeks and the maximum wait being 55 weeks, with 5 people having waited over 52 weeks."],[26,"People could not always access information and advice on their rights under the Care Act and ways to meet their care and support needs. This included unpaid carers and people who funded or arranged their own care and support."]],"m":"Some voluntary sector providers reported that funding was not enough to support prevention required in the community but recently noticed that the local authority had a greater focus on the prevention agenda. However, the local authorities Market Position Statement and staff feedback noted therapy recruitment shortages, missed therapy at discharge, and rising daily demand meant that some people could not access the reablement they needed in a timely way. In December 2025, 455 people were waiting"},{"q":[[32,"There was not always a range of appropriate inclusion and accessibility arrangements in place, which enabled people to engage with the local authority in ways that worked for them."],[29,"The local authority had identified that not all protected characteristics were consistently recorded in Care Act assessments. For example, data on care plans showed only 337 out of 3512 total people had LGBTQ+ status recorded, with further gaps in ethnicity, religion and marital status."],[32,"While the local authority had commissioned contracts with specialist interpreter services, partners highlighted challenges in accessing these consistently, including limited British Sign Language provision, variable interpreter availability, and some gaps in understanding the communication needs of the neurodiverse."]],"m":"The local authority acknowledged current data quality was limited, and lunch-and-learn sessions for staff were held to improve the understanding of the importance of recording data, which we were told improved the completeness of records. There was not always a range of appropriate inclusion and accessibility arrangements in place, which enabled people to engage with the local authority in ways that worked for them. But there was limited self-service of these materials. While the local authority"},{"q":[[35,"The local authority had commissioned two specific pieces of work looking at health and social care needs, and these were in relation to substance misuse and serious violence in partnership with the Community Safety Partnership. These were not used in the Market Position Statements or the Commissioning Strategies, as they were produced after their publication, and were commissioned by other departments in the local authority."],[35,"The resulting Market Position Statement and Commissioning Strategy lacked refinement and focused on broader areas of provision."],[35,"Even were there were individual differences relating to sexuality or ethnicity highlighted in the JSNA, they were not explicitly translated into needs for the council to address through commissioning."]],"m":"The local authority had commissioned two specific pieces of work looking at health and social care needs, and these were in relation to substance misuse and serious violence in partnership with the Community Safety Partnership. These were not used in the Market Position Statements or the Commissioning Strategies, as they were produced after their publication, and were commissioned by other departments in the local authority. The resulting Market Position Statement and Commissioning Strategy lack"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[52,"Additional measures, such as, fast-track minor adaptations, telecare kits, and urgent housing officer support were introduced to reduce delays in discharge and ensure safe transitions home, demonstrating active efforts by the local authority to improve outcomes for people."],[53,"Transitions arrangements for young people moving from children to adult services were not seamless."],[53,"Issues the person experienced included: poor communication, siloed assessments by Occupational Therapists and lack of adequate input from other professionals involved."]],"m":"Where there were shortfalls in practice, the local authority took steps to rectify this and improve people’s experiences and outcomes. Some partners we spoke with highlighted poor communication, and inadequate, siloed assessments and support planning which led to repeated unsafe discharges. Additional measures, such as, fast-track minor adaptations, telecare kits, and urgent housing officer support were introduced to reduce delays in discharge and ensure safe transitions home, demonstrating acti"},{"q":[[56,"Roles and responsibilities for identifying and responding to concerns were not always clear and, in the past, Safeguarding Adults Reviews (SAR) had reported siloed working, however, we heard changes had been made and the timeliness of safeguarding had improved as a result ensuring risks were addressed promptly."],[56,"A lack of progress from within a Children and Adults Safeguarding board had led to the establishment of a standalone Safeguarding Adults Board (SAB) in early 2025 to strengthen safeguarding arrangements."],[59,"Plans to develop a safeguarding and MCA competency framework to address inconsistent levels of staff competency in safeguarding was 30% complete."]],"m":"Roles and responsibilities for identifying and responding to concerns were not always clear and, in the past, Safeguarding Adults Reviews (SAR) had reported siloed working, however, we heard changes had been made and the timeliness of safeguarding had improved as a result ensuring risks were addressed promptly. A lack of progress from within a Children and Adults Safeguarding board had led to the establishment of a standalone Safeguarding Adults Board (SAB) in early 2025 to strengthen safeguardi"},{"q":[[66,"The local authority had taken a realistic approach to improving services, acknowledging that there were many aspects of service to improve, but this could not all be achieved at the same time."],[67,"There were clear governance structures in place with resources to support quality and performance improvement, but there was not always consistency in implementation and action planning. For example, there was variance in some of the governance processes between operations and commissioning, with documentation and planning tools being very different."],[72,"Where the local authority had delegated responsibilities, they had information sharing agreements, but there was a lack of interoperability between systems. This meant for occupational therapy, mental health and carers assessment the local authority did not have direct access to information and to get this the local authority staff had to use manual processes such as calling up colleagues for updates."]],"m":"The local authority had taken a realistic approach to improving services, acknowledging that there were many aspects of service to improve, but this could not all be achieved at the same time. There were still gaps in quality audits of practice, key performance data, customer feedback, and thematic reviews. There were clear governance structures in place with resources to support quality and performance improvement, but there was not always consistency in implementation and action planning. Wher"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Wakefield":[{"q":[[13,"Assessment and care planning arrangements were not consistently timely and up to date."],[14,"Following triage and support to mitigate immediate risks, people with ‘high’ priority referrals waited 8.7 days to be seen for assessment, which was slightly over the local authority’s target of 7 days."],[19,"The local authority told us they were seeing increasing complexity around deprivation of assets, financial abuse, appeals and debt recovery which increased the work for the financial assessment team which led to delays in completing financial assessments."]],"m":"Assessment and care planning arrangements were not consistently timely and up to date. Following triage and support to mitigate immediate risks, people with ‘high’ priority referrals waited 8.7 days to be seen for assessment, which was slightly over the local authority’s target of 7 days. Providers felt the structure for assessments and reviews was complicated and data systems were not shared. Some unpaid carers said communication with the local authority was not good and felt unsupported. Howev"},{"q":[[24,"The reablement offer in Wakefield provided support to people through a hospital to home short-term service, a bridging service that supported people as they waited for a longer term care package, through hospital admission avoidance, night service, therapy, ward-based discharge to assess."],[27,"The minimum was same day delivery and installation, the median wait time was 7 days (5 working days) and maximum was time a people waited was 60 days."],[28,"The group was developing an improvement plan to fill gaps in provision."]],"m":"The local authority had taken steps to identify people with needs for care and support that were not being met. The reablement offer in Wakefield provided support to people through a hospital to home short-term service, a bridging service that supported people as they waited for a longer term care package, through hospital admission avoidance, night service, therapy, ward-based discharge to assess. Senior leaders told us the current system for recording data on the provision of equipment was not"},{"q":[[33,"Staff and senior leaders noted that access to services was not representative of the population which indicated members of Black Asian and Minority ethnic communities may be underserved. This meant that people and their carers may be missing out on the care and support that they needed and may also serve to further deepen pre-existing health and social care inequalities."],[34,"The local authority had strategies and plans in place to ensure that people received fair and equitable services and outcomes were not adversely impacted by their protected characteristics."],[36,"However, access to commissioned support and services for people who were deaf, deafblind or hard of hearing in Wakefield could be difficult as services did not always provide accessible communication methods."]],"m":"Staff and senior leaders noted that access to services was not representative of the population which indicated members of Black Asian and Minority ethnic communities may be underserved. Staff and senior leaders noted that access to services was not representative of the population which indicated members of Black Asian and Minority ethnic communities may be underserved. This meant that people and their carers may be missing out on the care and support that they needed and may also serve to furt"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[59,"Frontline teams described a lack of nursing home provision, which had caused people to either remain in hospital or be moved out of area."],[62,"There was a referral process, with a separate pathway for schools for young people who may not be known to children’s services, however most referrals were received from special educational needs schools and the complex care team in children’s services, the team were not always clear on the pathway and process."],[62,"If a young person was not eligible for care and support under the Care Act, other support, such as employment support would be given to the young person from around the age of 16."]],"m":"There were clear recording processes in place, however, prompts that may have been helpful on lengthy open cases with action updates or on staff recording contact with people in relation to safeguarding follow up were not yet in place. Frontline teams described a lack of nursing home provision, which had caused people to either remain in hospital or be moved out of area. Staff had recently been given access to hospital records which helped to facilitate a fuller social care response, however at "},{"q":[[68,"The referral did not meet the criteria for a SAR but was passed to the Wakefield Domestic Abuse Management Board which undertook an independent audit of this case and reported back to the Board."],[70,"No delays were noted in the safeguarding team, however in the Social Care Direct team there were delays in initial referrals to enquiry allocation and a review was ongoing to understand and improve safeguarding pathways to and mitigate risks caused by delays."],[71,"The DOLS team were working with health partners to develop efficient ways of working ensure that people were not being deprived of the liberty and were looking at ways to ensure this was consistent across all its health partners."]],"m":"However, some partners felt initial online referral was not as accessible to the public and people with sensory needs. The referral did not meet the criteria for a SAR but was passed to the Wakefield Domestic Abuse Management Board which undertook an independent audit of this case and reported back to the Board. There was a clear rationale and outcome from initial enquiries, including those which did not progress to a Section 42 enquiry. No delays were noted in the safeguarding team, however in "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Walsall":[{"q":[[11,"Whilst people could access the local authority’s care and support services through multiple channels, including online, emails, and by telephone, people told us knowledge of where to go for advice and support often relied on word of mouth. This meant people did not always access the information they needed to maintain their health, wellbeing, and independence."],[11,"However, we also heard from people who told us about multiple changes in named workers, assessments that focused on individual tasks rather than the person’s overall needs or existing support networks, and delays in receiving their support plans."],[14,"Whilst we heard of delays in Care Act assessment and reviews, staff and leaders shared examples of how people were kept safe and supported whilst waiting for assessments."]],"m":"Whilst people could access the local authority’s care and support services through multiple channels, including online, emails, and by telephone, people told us knowledge of where to go for advice and support often relied on word of mouth. This meant people did not always access the information they needed to maintain their health, wellbeing, and independence. However, we heard of inconsistent approaches to assessments and reviews. However, we also heard from people who told us about multiple ch"},{"q":[[20,"Unpaid carers told us of a lack of knowledge about the support available to them, often finding out about carers services through friends and family rather than through local authority and partner information sharing."],[24,"The Market Position Statement for Community-Based Services (2025-2040) identified the supply of equipment as an area for improvement due to a recent review which suggested limited and outdated equipment and some evidence of delays in people receiving equipment due to constraints with the infrastructure and operating model."],[26,"There was not always good uptake of direct payments by people using services in Walsall."]],"m":"However, whilst staff spoke confidently about the importance of taking a relational approach when engaging with people to understand what was important to them and what they wanted to achieve, people’s experiences suggested processes were not always embedded in practice. Unpaid carers told us of a lack of knowledge about the support available to them, often finding out about carers services through friends and family rather than through local authority and partner information sharing. The local "},{"q":[[29,"Senior leaders also told us whilst frontline teams were culturally competent the directorate and the local authority was not as representative amongst heads of service and above."],[31,"People told us there were not always appropriate inclusion and accessibility arrangements in place for people to engage with the local authority in ways which worked for them, for example British Sign Language or interpreter services."],[31,"However, people and partners told us the closure of the one-stop-shop had led to negative impacts on people’s access to information and advice as the community hubs were not well known to all communities."]],"m":"Senior leaders also told us whilst frontline teams were culturally competent the directorate and the local authority was not as representative amongst heads of service and above. People also shared a reactive rather than proactive experience of local authority support meaning preventative strategies were not always having the desired impact in some communities. People told us there were not always appropriate inclusion and accessibility arrangements in place for people to engage with the local a"},{"q":[[36,"Walsall’s commissioning strategies and market shaping activity did not always support people’s changing care and support needs. For example, the Market Position Statement for Services for Working Aged Adults identified a 16.2% increase in demand in 2024-2025 compared to the previous year."],[37,"There was sufficient care and support available to meet demand, and people could access it when, where and how they need it. However, this did not apply to all service types. For example, the Market Positions statements identified there was enough capacity in the Supported Living market, but providers could not meet the nature of the needs people in Walsall were presenting."],[40,"The local authority did not always have clear arrangements to monitor the quality and impact of the care and support services being commissioned for people."]],"m":"However, these were not always high-quality or effective. Walsall’s commissioning strategies and market shaping activity did not always support people’s changing care and support needs. A lack of support for people with more specialist needs and a need to identify separate accommodation and care provision to improve the quality monitoring process had been highlighted. Commissioned models of care and support were not always in line with recognised best practice, however new strategies were being "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[50,"The local authority’s corporate Strategic Risk Register (2024-2025) identified a number of risks linked to adult social care. These included transformation and continuous improvement concerns such as embedding change, partner relationships, particularly in the provider market, delays in responding to safeguarding concerns, community cohesion and resilience, and health and social care inequalities, worsened by the cost of living."],[52,"The Directorate Risk Register identified the timeliness of referrals and a lack of market shaping risks to support young people during their transition into adult social care."],[53,"Data presented by the local authority showed 13% of section 117 annual reviews were overdue, with each person being carefully tracked and ‘waiting well’."]],"m":"However, processes were not always effective at keeping people safe. The local authority’s corporate Strategic Risk Register (2024-2025) identified a number of risks linked to adult social care. These included transformation and continuous improvement concerns such as embedding change, partner relationships, particularly in the provider market, delays in responding to safeguarding concerns, community cohesion and resilience, and health and social care inequalities, worsened by the cost of living"},{"q":[[58,"Systems did not currently facilitate 24 hours reporting of safeguarding concerns, however the Emergency Duty Team supported urgent risks."],[59,"There had been 3 rapid SARs in 2025 which did not meet the criteria under the Care Act 2014 for example, a recent rapid review looked at a single agency issue where joint working was not considered a factor in the concerns raised."],[60,"There was a clear rationale and outcome from initial enquiries, including those which did not progress to an s42 enquiry."]],"m":"However, staff and leaders told us of process duplication leading to delays in enquiries, and people told us of inconsistencies in understanding and management of risk. Where they believed this was not the case, they worked with referrers to provide advice and support in appropriate referring. Systems did not currently facilitate 24 hours reporting of safeguarding concerns, however the Emergency Duty Team supported urgent risks. There had been 3 rapid SARs in 2025 which did not meet the criteria"},{"q":[[64,"However, these did not always provide assurance on the delivery of Care Act 2014 duties, quality assurance, sustainability, and risks to service delivery to improve people’s care and support experiences and outcomes."],[65,"Staff received training and ongoing supervision, which enabled them to maintain competency and consistency in applying the Care Act 2014, and processes incorporated feedback questions into assessment and review forms."],[65,"Feedback from people, and partners on inconsistent frontline practice around assessments, case recording, unpaid carers assessments, and knowledge of community support options showed the quality assurance process was not yet embedded in practice."]],"m":"However, these did not always provide assurance on the delivery of Care Act 2014 duties, quality assurance, sustainability, and risks to service delivery to improve people’s care and support experiences and outcomes. Feedback from people, and partners on inconsistent frontline practice around assessments, case recording, unpaid carers assessments, and knowledge of community support options showed the quality assurance process was not yet embedded in practice. Feedback from people, and partners o"},{"q":[[72,"Coproduction was not yet embedded throughout the local authority’s work."],[72,"The group were not yet representative of the diverse local community and did not have input on local authority decision-making or oversight boards."],[72,"In April 2024 a named worker approach was introduced in the learning disability team based on best practice examples and evidence of better of outcomes for people. This enabled frontline teams to build relationships and respond in a timely and proportionate way, so people did not have to keep retelling their story."]],"m":"Coproduction was not yet embedded throughout the local authority’s work. The group were not yet representative of the diverse local community and did not have input on local authority decision-making or oversight boards. In April 2024 a named worker approach was introduced in the learning disability team based on best practice examples and evidence of better of outcomes for people. This enabled frontline teams to build relationships and respond in a timely and proportionate way, so people did no"}],"Wandsworth":[{"q":[[10,"Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely and up to date. Some teams' waiting lists were minimal or non-existent whereas other teams had significant waiting times. The reviewing team and the Occupational Therapy team had longer waiting lists than the locality and specialist teams."],[13,"Data provided by the local authority stated there were currently 0 people waiting for an assessment in hospital, the local authority stated that any delays in hospital discharge were not due to people waiting for a care act assessment and were working with partners to support with hospital discharge challenges."],[13,"The Carers Centre did not carry out carers assessments."]],"m":"If they were not known to the local authority, this would be referred through to the CAST to review and identify any risks. Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely and up to date. Data provided by the local authority stated there were currently 0 people waiting for an assessment in hospital, the local authority stated that any delays in hospital discharge were not due to people waiting for a care act assessment and were working with partners to support with hospital disc"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[31,"However, it was identified that provision for more complex care such as complex mental health, complex learning disabilities, and advanced dementia was limited in Wandsworth."],[35,"There were no delays to hospital discharge and the reablement service was person centred and effective."],[36,"Out of 40 community services, 2 were rated outstanding, 20 were rated good, and 8 were not yet rated by CQC."]],"m":"Leaders identified that whilst they felt their care provision was good and was continually improving, future plans did not fully reflect the projected future needs of the people in Wandsworth. However, it was identified that provision for more complex care such as complex mental health, complex learning disabilities, and advanced dementia was limited in Wandsworth. The local authority told us the reasons for people being placed out of borough were due to being unable to meet complex needs or per"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Warrington":[{"q":[[9,"There was an inconsistent understanding around strength-based practice. For example, staff tended to describe skill sets within their teams rather than strength or asset-based way of working with people and unpaid carers to identify what they could do or had access to meet their needs and improve their individual wellbeing."],[10,"There was evidence of multidisciplinary working with partners, however there were gaps in how health and social care needs could be managed and met interchangeably. For example, the impact on health conditions affecting peoples social care needs such as Parkinsons, Epilepsy and Anxiety was not captured within assessments viewed."],[11,"Another young adult we spoke with had difficulty engaging in the meeting and the information gathered for this individual was clearly collated from the parent. It was not evident that every effort had been made to ensure that person was involved in the assessment as required by the Care Act and therefore did not evidence personcentred practice, human rights, or respect for protected characteristics under the Equality Act 2010."]],"m":"The approach to assessment and care planning was not always person-centred and strength based. There was an inconsistent understanding around strength-based practice. There was evidence of multidisciplinary working with partners, however there were gaps in how health and social care needs could be managed and met interchangeably. The person expressed how grateful they were to have the same social worker, so they did not have to ‘start again’. The person expressed how grateful they were to have t"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[31,"While the local authority understood its local population and profile of demographics, there was limited detail for the local authority’s actions in tackling inequalities within Warrington. This was an area of current development for the local authority and Warrington’s position statement acknowledged this as work in progress to improve and rebuild consistency of approach and strategy for equality, diversity, and inclusion."],[35,"However, at the time of the assessment, there was little evidence for the impact of the local authority proactively engaging with the people and groups where inequalities had been identified and a lack of evidence to show what actions the local authority were taking to address specific risks and issues experienced by seldom heard groups."],[35,"The website did have some restrictions such as not allowing colour changes or contrasting fonts to support the visually impaired and some video streams did not have captions or work with read aloud software."]],"m":"While the local authority understood its local population and profile of demographics, there was limited detail for the local authority’s actions in tackling inequalities within Warrington. While the local authority understood its local population and profile of demographics, there was limited detail for the local authority’s actions in tackling inequalities within Warrington. This was an area of current development for the local authority and Warrington’s position statement acknowledged this as"},{"q":[[40,"There were some gaps in provision of required support services across Warrington particularly for people who had specialist and complex support needs."],[42,"The local authority had an understanding of demand and capacity throughout their commissioned services within Warrington. They collected data and analysed this for commissioning purposes and to identify gaps in the market."],[43,"People at home waiting for a residential care home waited an average of 21 days and those waiting for a nursing home waited an average of 16 days. This meant people were at risk of not having their needs met or carers breakdown at home whilst waiting for 24-hour care and support."]],"m":"There was a detailed description of the current market and an acknowledgement that the current ways of managing the market were not sustainable and they must switch to a model of prevent, reduce and delay. There were some gaps in provision of required support services across Warrington particularly for people who had specialist and complex support needs. Some people told us they had access to activities to reduce social isolation and some people told us they did not have options for activities t"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[55,"Partners told us that the pathways between intermediate care, hospital and community care were not seamless, however, efforts were being made to improve transitions from hospital to home. This included a steering group including leaders from the local authority and broader partners to tackle the challenges identified such as delays for care provision, homelessness and housing issues."],[58,"The preparation for adulthood team did not commission services until the young person turned 18 years old."],[58,"If a child had a social worker and did not qualify for services under the Care Act, they were allocated a personal advisor through children’s services to assist with tasks like applying for passports, benefits, and housing grants, this support was available until age 25."]],"m":"Partners told us that the pathways between intermediate care, hospital and community care were not seamless, however, efforts were being made to improve transitions from hospital to home. Partners told us that the pathways between intermediate care, hospital and community care were not seamless, however, efforts were being made to improve transitions from hospital to home. This included a steering group including leaders from the local authority and broader partners to tackle the challenges iden"},{"q":[[61,"There was a small internal safeguarding and quality team, but they did not triage all new referrals."],[62,"The digital platform the referrals were sent to sat with a corporate team within the local authority that the adult social care department did not have access to without making an IT request, adult social care was not accessing the closed referrals at the time of the CQC assessment."],[64,"The local authority’s website had links to their safeguarding adults’ procedures which were created in 2022 and overdue for review from June 2024."]],"m":"There was a small internal safeguarding and quality team, but they did not triage all new referrals. The digital platform the referrals were sent to sat with a corporate team within the local authority that the adult social care department did not have access to without making an IT request, adult social care was not accessing the closed referrals at the time of the CQC assessment. The local authority’s website had links to their safeguarding adults’ procedures which were created in 2022 and ove"},{"q":[[75,"However, these processes were not always effective. For example, they did not identify the issues around the out of hours service arrangements."],[75,"Whilst senior leaders told us they had regular discussions and involvement about the development of the online referral form they were not aware of the extent of the issues or concerns with the safeguarding processes in Warrington. For example, safeguarding referrals could be closed prior to being triaged or reviewed by a physical person (triaged digitally)."],[76,"Most political and local authority leaders talked about a history of oversubscription for care in Warrington, however, this was not discussed by any frontline staff which suggested that there was more to be done with regards to communicating current areas of focus for adult social care, throughout the workforce."]],"m":"However, these processes were not always effective. Some roles within of the out of hours service were voluntary, and some were not. Whilst senior leaders told us they had regular discussions and involvement about the development of the online referral form they were not aware of the extent of the issues or concerns with the safeguarding processes in Warrington. However, it was evidenced that leaders were not well informed about all risks within adult social care (safeguarding) and more needed t"},{"q":[[82,"Some partners reported that there was a limited training offer from the local authority such as safeguarding and Deprivation of Liberty Safeguards training."],[82,"Several partners told us that co-production was not yet embedded throughout the local authority’s work, although they acknowledged that this was an area that the local authority was working to improve."],[84,"Data from the Local Government Social Care Ombudsman between 1st April 2023 and 31st March 2024 published in July 2024 shows in Warrington there were 7 complaints, 2 were upheld, 1 was not upheld and 4 were closed after initial enquiries."]],"m":"Staff told us that this had not yet happened, and some said they could not remember receiving strength-based practice training. Some partners reported that there was a limited training offer from the local authority such as safeguarding and Deprivation of Liberty Safeguards training. Others reported that they did not have any training offers from the local authority. Several partners told us that co-production was not yet embedded throughout the local authority’s work, although they acknowledged"}],"Warwickshire":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"West Berkshire":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[18,"Work had been done to create a digital resource from the Joint Strategic Needs Assessment to inform the Health and Wellbeing Strategy and address health inequalities. This approach focussed on the current situation but did not include a proactive approach to projecting into the future to understand trends over the next 5 or 10 years to prevent, reduce and delay care needs on this time scale."],[18,"There was a lack of data beneath the general population data to help the local authority understand needs in smaller communities and particularly isolated groups and their current and long-term care needs that may be missed by the wider data."],[18,"We heard from front line teams about their awareness of certain groups, such as recent migrant communities, and travellers but did not always see this reflected in data and this did not enable future demographic pressures of an aging population to be predicted and addressed."]],"m":"While there were examples of individual projects there was not an overarching strategic approach to this work. There was currently a delay in implementation of this because of delays in the setup of the digital system. The local authority had plans in place to progress this, but timescales were not clear. Work had been done to create a digital resource from the Joint Strategic Needs Assessment to inform the Health and Wellbeing Strategy and address health inequalities. This approach focussed on "},{"q":[[24,"However, as noted in the Equality Diversity and Inclusion report it was not clear how the framework informed a more strategic approach to equity."],[24,"The local authority did not always use opportunities to ensure they gathered data to help understand groups of people who might find it difficult to access services or those groups who needs were not being met. For example, data on complaints was broken down by team and by who was making the complaint (a carer or the person receiving care) but was not broken down by protected characteristics."],[24,"Similarly, there was no breakdown of the number of people placed at distance out of area by protected characteristic, to understand if it was the needs of particular groups of people that were not being met within West Berkshire."]],"m":"The report identified a lack of clarity on the action taken by the local authority in relation to addressing equality issues and said better communication in this area was required. However, as noted in the Equality Diversity and Inclusion report it was not clear how the framework informed a more strategic approach to equity. The local authority did not always use opportunities to ensure they gathered data to help understand groups of people who might find it difficult to access services or thos"},{"q":[[28,"While the commissioning team worked closely with adult social care in the provision of care, it was not apparent how they were taking a more strategic approach to deliver on the adult social care strategy."],[29,"However, the feedback from the voluntary organisations was that they didn’t feel there was ongoing involvement and use of their experience to understand needs and develop services."],[30,"There were not always suitable services available for people with a learning disability and autistic people, especially when people had complex and multiple needs such as physical disabilities requiring adapted environments."]],"m":"However, staff and leaders told us there were limited available spaces within residential care which meant people sometimes were placed out of the area. While the commissioning team worked closely with adult social care in the provision of care, it was not apparent how they were taking a more strategic approach to deliver on the adult social care strategy. However, the feedback from the voluntary organisations was that they didn’t feel there was ongoing involvement and use of their experience to"},{"q":[[35,"The local authority worked closely with health professionals operationally within the hospital to flag issues for example, any potential delays in discharge, or identification of unpaid carers who were admitted to hospital to ensure provision for the cared for person."],[36,"The positive strategic relationships, both within Berkshire West and in Health and Wellbeing Board arrangements, provided a basis to support positive future joint programmes and interventions. This was not at a developed stage of maturity, but the early work had been done toward this."],[37,"However, measurement of impact beyond the BCF and hospital discharge was limited as there was still work to be done to develop impactful partnership working with fully integrated joint commissioning across health and care."]],"m":"The local authority worked closely with health professionals operationally within the hospital to flag issues for example, any potential delays in discharge, or identification of unpaid carers who were admitted to hospital to ensure provision for the cared for person. The positive strategic relationships, both within Berkshire West and in Health and Wellbeing Board arrangements, provided a basis to support positive future joint programmes and interventions. This was not at a developed stage of m"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"West Northamptonshire":[{"q":[[10,"The local authority website provided written information, however this was not yet available in alternative formats, such as easy read."],[13,"Annual Care Act reviews were not being undertaken in a timely manner."],[17,"People and partners told us it could be difficult to access support for non-eligible care needs, particularly following a Care Act assessment."]],"m":"People and partners told us it could sometimes be difficult to contact staff, and trying to get in touch by using the telephone could take a long time. The local authority website provided written information, however this was not yet available in alternative formats, such as easy read. The local authority said the reasons for this were delays caused by high demand on community teams, though they told us work had been done to cleanse data and reduce the backlog. Annual Care Act reviews were not "},{"q":[[25,"The local authority took active steps to identify and support people whose care and support needs were not being met, particularly in response to high rates of emergency hospital admissions and hip fractures among those aged 65 and over."],[27,"Data from the local General Hospital showed patients waited an average of 10.1 days for a Pathway 2 bed, prompting a review of assessment criteria and response times."],[32,"However, partners raised concerns that advice was often generic and lacked practical support, particularly for people whose first language was not English or those navigating services independently."]],"m":"However, they also felt that the customer service response to new enquiries needed improvement, as opportunities to prevent, reduce, or delay care needs were not consistently maximised during initial contact. The local authority took active steps to identify and support people whose care and support needs were not being met, particularly in response to high rates of emergency hospital admissions and hip fractures among those aged 65 and over. Where ongoing support was not needed, staff ensured t"},{"q":[[36,"The local authority acknowledged that its workforce did not yet reflect the demographic of the community, particularly in leadership roles, and committed to implementing the Local Government Association’s Diverse by Design principles."],[37,"Community-based services, drop-ins, and partnerships with voluntary groups were used to reduce barriers, though leaders noted that availability didn’t always translate to access."],[38,"Mental health services were concentrated in central Northampton, with limited access in towns like Brackley, Daventry and Towcester, meaning some people had to travel long distances or relocate for support."]],"m":"The local authority acknowledged that its workforce did not yet reflect the demographic of the community, particularly in leadership roles, and committed to implementing the Local Government Association’s Diverse by Design principles. Community-based services, drop-ins, and partnerships with voluntary groups were used to reduce barriers, though leaders noted that availability didn’t always translate to access. Some staff and leaders highlighted a range of inequalities in experience and outcomes."},{"q":[[43,"The Autism Health Needs Assessment showed a rising population of autistic children and adults, with projected increases of 3% and 14% respectively by 2040, but it remained unclear how service capacity would meet future demand."],[43,"The COVID Health Impact Assessment revealed that 14% of respondents continued to experience long COVID symptoms, with gaps in diagnosis and treatment contributing to worsening mental health and pressure on services."],[45,"The local authority’s review of commissioning models found people with acquired brain injury and autism didn’t always have the right support. In response, the 2025 Supported Living framework included dedicated options for both, helping to make services more inclusive."]],"m":"The Autism Health Needs Assessment showed a rising population of autistic children and adults, with projected increases of 3% and 14% respectively by 2040, but it remained unclear how service capacity would meet future demand. The COVID Health Impact Assessment revealed that 14% of respondents continued to experience long COVID symptoms, with gaps in diagnosis and treatment contributing to worsening mental health and pressure on services. While some care providers reported being engaged through "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[72,"Staff also highlighted wider system challenges, such as delays in response times from Section 12 doctors and the police, as well as limited availability in hospitals and designated places of safety. These workforce pressures and system delays limited the AMHP service’s ability to consistently respond to mental health risks, impacting the local authority’s ability to meet Care Act duties around safeguarding and timely intervention."],[72,"While AMHP staff were happy to support colleagues, there was some concern that the regular reliance on this advice could indicate a lack of structure or confidence in managing complex risk within community teams."],[74,"While Pathway 1 worked well, partners reported challenges with delays in Pathways 2 and 3, particularly for people with complex needs or where discharge planning was being coordinated by a community team."]],"m":"Staff noted they could not directly access crisis house placements, which occasionally presented challenges. Staff also highlighted wider system challenges, such as delays in response times from Section 12 doctors and the police, as well as limited availability in hospitals and designated places of safety. Staff also highlighted wider system challenges, such as delays in response times from Section 12 doctors and the police, as well as limited availability in hospitals and designated places of s"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[89,"Governance within the local authority had strengthened following the council’s formation in 2021, but many strategies and systems remained in their early stages and were not yet fully embedded."],[92,"Leaders acknowledged gaps in access for seldom heard groups and had committed to embedding equality, diversity, and inclusion more fully across adult social care."],[93,"The local authority developed its Carers Strategy by working closely with local carers and placing their experiences at the heart of decision-making. It recognised that many carers were not known to services and committed to improving identification and outreach, using tools such as training, reform funding, and support to help carers recognise their role."]],"m":"Governance within the local authority had strengthened following the council’s formation in 2021, but many strategies and systems remained in their early stages and were not yet fully embedded. Leaders acknowledged gaps in access for seldom heard groups and had committed to embedding equality, diversity, and inclusion more fully across adult social care. The local authority developed its Carers Strategy by working closely with local carers and placing their experiences at the heart of decision-m"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"West Sussex":[{"q":[[13,"There were long waiting lists for occupational therapy, transitions assessments and Mental Health Act assessments although they had reduced in the last 6 months."],[13,"The local authority had systems to monitor and manage risks while people waited for assessment or services."],[14,"Care Act reviews were not conducted in a timely fashion, data provided by the local authority showed 6,619 people were waiting for an annual review with a median wait of 236 days and a maximum wait of 1,882 days."]],"m":"However, some people’s relatives told us they were not always involved in the most recent assessments and reviews which were conducted without their knowledge or involvement, which they should have participated in. We saw evidence of strength-based assessments, although these were not always consistently in people’s own words. Staff told us they considered what was available in the community for people and did not try to make people fit into services. Assessment and care planning arrangements we"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[27,"Staff and leaders understood the areas of deprivation in West Sussex and were aware of the seldom-heard groups in the communities."],[27,"Diversity and inclusion were highlighted in the Adult Social Care Strategy as a priority area for the local authority and a key focus in the improvement programme."],[28,"The local authority used the Joint Strategic Needs Assessments (JSNA) to understand groups at a higher risk of poorer health outcomes including those with high vulnerability such as people with severe mental illness, children in care, care leavers and homeless people with multiple complex needs."]],"m":""},{"q":[[34,"There was a lack of capacity for people needing short-to-medium-term accommodation and the local authority was working with a variety of developers and providers in order to innovate housing solutions for people in West Sussex."],[35,"The local authority was undertaking a strategic review to ensure there was sufficient care and support services available for people, however they were aware of the gaps and lack of capacity specifically for people with learning disabilities, autism, and mental health needs."],[37,"People were supported out-of-area if their care and support needs could not be met in West Sussex under an Individual Placement Agreement (IPA)."]],"m":"Most unpaid carers we spoke with did not access any form of respite; however, the short break respite offer was being reviewed. There was a lack of capacity for people needing short-to-medium-term accommodation and the local authority was working with a variety of developers and providers in order to innovate housing solutions for people in West Sussex. The local authority was undertaking a strategic review to ensure there was sufficient care and support services available for people, however th"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Westminster":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Westmorland and Furness":[{"q":[[11,"Although, some Care Act assessments did not evidence which outcomes were non-eligible and how these needs could be met elsewhere."],[12,"A care home review team had been implemented to address overdue reviews for people in residential care homes."],[13,"However, there were delays to people receiving Care Act assessments within this period."]],"m":"People told us their social worker understood what was important to them and they were listened to by their social worker, however, they did not always receive a copy of their support plan when completed. Although, some Care Act assessments did not evidence which outcomes were non-eligible and how these needs could be met elsewhere. Assessment and care planning arrangements were not always timely and up to date. Staff told us the local authority had developed an overdue review dashboard which id"},{"q":[[20,"Some people told us the local authority had focused on prevention and supported people to remain independent at home, while others told us the local authority were not focused on prevention and could do more to support people at an earlier stage."],[23,"OT assessments were not always carried out in a timely manner."],[23,"However, the new waiting safely guidance required OTs to respond within 24 hours, which they told us was not viable due to case load and staff shortages and previously the response time was 72 hours. They had consistently aimed to respond within 72 hours but had often had to provide advice until they could make an in person visit."]],"m":"Some people told us the local authority had focused on prevention and supported people to remain independent at home, while others told us the local authority were not focused on prevention and could do more to support people at an earlier stage. The local authority had taken steps to identify people with needs for care and support that were not being met. OT assessments were not always carried out in a timely manner. However, the new waiting safely guidance required OTs to respond within 24 hou"},{"q":[[29,"It had been recognised by the local authority that the Gypsy, Roma, Traveller community were less likely than people from other communities to access support from statutory services. Partners told us more work was needed to ensure equitable access to service and outcomes for the community."],[29,"Partners told us the farming community traditionally did not come forward to request help and support."],[32,"There were appropriate inclusion and accessibility arrangements in place so that people could engage with the local authority in ways that worked for them, for example British Sign Language or interpretation services."]],"m":"Partners told us the farming community traditionally did not come forward to request help and support."},{"q":[[35,"Partners told us there were a range of services available for people to be referred to, however there was not an equitable number of services across the district."],[37,"Staff told us there was not a specific sitting service commissioned for carers to have respite; however, Shared Lives could provide respite support and capacity had been increased to meet demand."],[39,"Local authority data provided in January 2025 showed 181 people were placed out of area, 33% of people were out of area due to personal choice and 25% of people were out of area due to lack of suitable provision in the area."]],"m":"The local authority had recognised a gap in the market for accommodation for people with complex mental health needs, autism or people that required higher level of specialist support needs. Partners told us there were a range of services available for people to be referred to, however there was not an equitable number of services across the district. Staff told us there was not a specific sitting service commissioned for carers to have respite; however, Shared Lives could provide respite suppor"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[49,"Care plans evidenced that risks were identified and managed accordingly to reduce risks for people. For example, a risk was identified around possible mental health deterioration which could lead to mental health crisis, support plans documented who people could contact to access additional support."],[50,"The local authority had an Urgent Care Team (UCT) which at the time of assessment was jointly hosted with Cumberland and was due to be disaggregated on 1st July 2025."],[52,"The local authority discharge and reablement team held morning meetings with hospital colleagues and discussed individuals identified for reablement. This allowed a multidisciplinary approach to discuss best discharge pathways and challenge any decisions made where they felt people were not appropriate for reablement services."]],"m":"The local authority discharge and reablement team held morning meetings with hospital colleagues and discussed individuals identified for reablement. This allowed a multidisciplinary approach to discuss best discharge pathways and challenge any decisions made where they felt people were not appropriate for reablement services."},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Wigan":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Wiltshire":[{"q":[[10,"When a person needed an assessment, they were directed to preventative services while they waited."],[11,"There was found to be a lack of genuine choice in how to meet the range of needs and reasonable preferences of people who needed care and support services."],[14,"Unpaid carers needs were noted as a priority for staff when carrying out people’s Care Act assessments and they actively made referrals for unpaid carers to be individually assessed to maintain their safety and personal well-being."]],"m":"When a person needed an assessment, they were directed to preventative services while they waited. There was found to be a lack of genuine choice in how to meet the range of needs and reasonable preferences of people who needed care and support services. The local authority controlled how peoples care needs were met by referring them to a care provider that was available to meet their needs. This meant people could not freely decide between a range of options which would give them more control a"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[49,"There was no formal partnership agreement for services across the mental health trust and the local authority."],[49,"We heard health and social care managers met regularly to support service delivery specifically to reduce risks and improve outcomes for people. However, this was not extended to the voluntary sector, which meant some people may not have received the same joined up support as others."],[52,"Partners told us there were gaps in providing support to smaller groups and, how uncertainty of funding and competitive procurement decisions was a challenge for the voluntary sector."]],"m":"There was no formal partnership agreement for services across the mental health trust and the local authority. We heard health and social care managers met regularly to support service delivery specifically to reduce risks and improve outcomes for people. However, this was not extended to the voluntary sector, which meant some people may not have received the same joined up support as others. Partners told us there were gaps in providing support to smaller groups and, how uncertainty of funding "},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[62,"The safeguarding partnership board was attended by the local authority, police, health services and other local organisations, this did not include advocacy or provider services."],[64,"The local authority had identified gaps in support for young adults leaving care who didn’t have care needs that met Care Act thresholds but were posing risks to themselves or others."],[67,"There was concern any immediate risks to people using services were not addressed at the time by care providers as they were not told."]],"m":"The safeguarding partnership board was attended by the local authority, police, health services and other local organisations, this did not include advocacy or provider services. However, there was areas that lacked detail to demonstrate what the local authority was doing to deliver its part of the plan. The local authority had identified gaps in support for young adults leaving care who didn’t have care needs that met Care Act thresholds but were posing risks to themselves or others. However, t"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Wirral":[{"q":[[12,"Staff told us the interface between the teams worked well, although we heard they were not always confident in understanding the remit of their roles."],[13,"People’s experience was inconsistent and there was some confusion around processes following implementation of services or equipment."],[17,"The local authority did not have any Care Act assessment appeals in the last year."]],"m":"Staff told us the interface between the teams worked well, although we heard they were not always confident in understanding the remit of their roles. People’s experience was inconsistent and there was some confusion around processes following implementation of services or equipment. One family told us that a social worker in the service arranged a move into residential care for their loved one when they were unable to arrange this themselves due to living a distance away. In contrast, people to"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[27,"The plan did not specifically reference minority communities, or seldom heard voices, however we were told by partners that a new Council for Voluntary Services (CVS) had been set up by the local authority and it had invited ‘Wirral Change’ to take part."],[28,"An ‘inequality audit’ had been carried out, and it was identified that people from black and ethnic minority (BAME) backgrounds were not accessing drug and alcohol services equally as other groups."],[30,"We heard about a person who had a hearing impairment struggling to engage with the local authority’s financial assessment process. They told the local authority that they didn’t understand the forms and requested a home visit due to their first language being British Sign Language (BSL)."]],"m":"Leaders told us that they are aware of shortfalls in their current data set for them to utilise to inform them of areas of risk and had plans to address this. We were told about people from minority backgrounds accessing a multi-cultural centre in the community, however staff were not able to demonstrate how they reached into these communities to work with them to increase their cultural understanding of needs. The plan did not specifically reference minority communities, or seldom heard voices,"},{"q":[[37,"Staff reported trying to enable people to remain in their own home for as long as possible, however this became difficult when a person’s health deteriorated and the buildings in which they lived in could not accommodate a change in their need, such as a stairlift for example."],[41,"There was a Quality Network Group in place to identify and report any gaps in service delivery to support overarching quality improvement initiatives across the health and social care system."],[42,"We were not made aware of any monitoring tools to anticipate provider failure and service disruption."]],"m":"In contrast, people told us that in relation to domiciliary care, they did not always have choice and control of who supported them, with them being allocated an agency and told who would be providing their care. One person told us that they felt domiciliary staff did not always provide person-centred care or a holistic approach to their care. Leaders told us there were gaps in provision of day activities for young people with complex needs in the community. Leaders told us there were gaps in pr"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[50,"The local authority understands the risks to people across their care journeys, with service risk identified and managed proactively."],[51,"Following the assessment, the local authority told us that whilst pathways are clear to many staff, there can be delays in referrals being sent and an action plan was created in July 2024 to further improve this process."],[51,"A steering group held in April 2024 highlighted that in comparison to children’s services data, adult social care systems were not supporting identification and tracking of young people that were likely to be eligible for support under the Care Act."]],"m":"Following the assessment, the local authority told us that whilst pathways are clear to many staff, there can be delays in referrals being sent and an action plan was created in July 2024 to further improve this process. A steering group held in April 2024 highlighted that in comparison to children’s services data, adult social care systems were not supporting identification and tracking of young people that were likely to be eligible for support under the Care Act."},{"q":[[57,"Leaders were aware that data was not always accurate or easy to interpret and this was a priority to address."],[58,"We were told of vacancies in the DoLS team; however, these posts were not being advertised at the point of assessment."],[60,"There were some delays in completion of S42 enquires due to waiting for information from partners, which at times caused long delays."]],"m":"Leaders were aware that data was not always accurate or easy to interpret and this was a priority to address. We were told of vacancies in the DoLS team; however, these posts were not being advertised at the point of assessment. Red, amber, green (RAG) rating of assessments was used when allocating, although we were not told of plans to address the backlog. Following the assessment, the local authority told CQC that they had recruited agency workers to fill vacant posts and offered overtime to t"},{"q":[[63,"We were told of investment in providing additional staff for the review team, however, the trajectory did not yet show evidence of improved performance."],[64,"Partners told us that data was not easily interpreted, with access to the authority’s performance dashboard being a concern. For example, the WSAPB told us that although they were provided with access, they weren’t able to pick out any themes and trends relating to the safeguarding figures due to data not being clearly presented and analysed."],[64,"Other challenges and risks as identified by the SLT which impacted on the delivery of Care Act duties included financial pressures, staffing levels, partnership working and long waiting lists for assessment and review leading to missed opportunities to regain independence."]],"m":"The local authority’s political and executive leaders were well informed about the potential risks facing adult social care however were not always sighted on improvement trajectories or actions to address these. Despite the data trending toward negative, and leaders telling us that reviews were an ongoing issue and required improvements, this was not listed on the local authority’s risk register. We were told of investment in providing additional staff for the review team, however, the trajecto"},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Wokingham":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}],"Worcestershire":[{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[[39,"While the previous strategy had clear priorities to improve equitable opportunities for people to access care and support, limited work had been undertaken to review and measure the impact of the local authority’s actions."],[39,"Development of the local authority’s new equality strategy was at a very early stage of development with limited information."],[40,"Analysis by the local authority showed gaps including 70.2% of records did not have people’s sexual orientation recorded."]],"m":"While the previous strategy had clear priorities to improve equitable opportunities for people to access care and support, limited work had been undertaken to review and measure the impact of the local authority’s actions. Development of the local authority’s new equality strategy was at a very early stage of development with limited information. However, the local authority did not have sufficient mechanisms to understand its local population profile and demographics in detail. Frontline staff "},{"q":[[47,"Local authority leaders were aware of demographic shifts, the challenge of increasing demand for services, and recognised the local authority was not fully meeting current demand across all service types."],[49,"The local authority commissioned research to understand local vulnerabilities and needs in different Worcestershire communities. It identified gaps in public transport impacted people’s ability to access some services."],[52,"Staff and partners highlighted gaps in shortterm placements for people with high acuity needs, for example people with advanced dementia, complex mental health needs, hoarding and challenging behaviours."]],"m":"Local authority leaders were aware of demographic shifts, the challenge of increasing demand for services, and recognised the local authority was not fully meeting current demand across all service types. The local authority commissioned research to understand local vulnerabilities and needs in different Worcestershire communities. It identified gaps in public transport impacted people’s ability to access some services. There was a mature supported living, residential and nursing care market wit"},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""},{"q":[],"m":""}]};
